msgid "Introduction and Preliminaries"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <chapter><introduction><p>
#: source/ch_intro.ptx:7
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Welcome to Discrete Mathematics.\n"
"      If this is your first time encountering the subject,\n"
"      you will probably find discrete mathematics quite different from other math subjects.\n"
"      You might not even know what discrete math is!\n"
"      Hopefully this short introduction\n"
"      will shed some light on what the subject is about and what you can expect as you move forward\n"
"      in your studies.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:3
#, no-wrap
msgid "What is Discrete Mathematics?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\tdis"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:6
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"crete / dis'krët.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:10
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t<em>Adjective</em>: Individually separate and distinct.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:14
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t<em>Synonyms</em>: separate - detached - distinct - abstract.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tDefining <em>discrete mathematics</em>\n"
"\t\tis hard because defining <em>mathematics</em> is hard.\n"
"\t\tWhat is mathematics?\n"
"\t\tThe study of numbers?\n"
"\t\tIn part yes, but you also study functions and lines and triangles and parallelepipeds and vectors and\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:24
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tOr perhaps you want to say that mathematics is a collection of tools that allow you to solve problems.\n"
"\t\tWhat sort of problems?\n"
"\t\tWell, those that involve numbers,\n"
"\t\tfunctions, lines, triangles,\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:29
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tWhatever your conception of what mathematics is,\n"
"\t\ttry applying the concept of <q>discrete</q> to it, as defined above.\n"
"\t\tSome math fundamentally deals with <em>stuff</em>\n"
"\t\tthat is individually separate and distinct.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:37
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tIn an algebra or calculus class,\n"
"\t\tyou might have found a particular set of numbers\n"
"\t\t(perhaps they constitute the range of a function).\n"
"\t\tYou would represent this set as an interval:\n"
"\t\t<m>[0,\\infty)</m> is the range of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> since the set\n"
"\t\tof outputs of the function are all real numbers 0 and greater.\n"
"\t\tThis set of numbers is NOT discrete.\n"
"\t\tThe numbers in the set are not separated by much at all.\n"
"\t\tIn fact, take any two numbers in the set and there are infinitely many more between\n"
"\t\tthem that are also in the set.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><fn>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "Even larger natural numbers for old ladies who live in shoes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:50
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tDiscrete math could still ask about the range of a function,\n"
"\t\tbut the set would not be an interval.\n"
"\t\tConsider the function that gives the number of children of each person reading this.\n"
"\t\tWhat is the range?\n"
"\t\tI'm guessing\n"
"\t\tit is something like <m>\\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\tMaybe 5 or 6 is in there too.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>\n"
"\t\tBut certainly nobody reading this has 1.32419 children.\n"
"\t\tThis output set <em>is</em> discrete because the elements are separate.\n"
"\t\tThe inputs to the function also form a discrete set because each input is an individual person.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><fn>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:65
#, no-wrap
msgid "Many of the problems discussed in this book are admittedly contrived and clearly fictional, but hopefully you will see how these toy problems can be generalized to actually represent problems that people would care about in reality."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThere are many discrete mathematical objects besides sets of numbers; we will introduce some of these in <xref po4a-id=0>.\n"
"\t\tStudying these discrete <term>structures</term> is the main focus of discrete mathematics and this book.\n"
"\t\tHowever, the reason we want to study these structures is because they provide a way to model <q>real-world</q> problems.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tTo get a feel for the subject, let's consider the types of problems you solve in discrete math.\n"
"\t\tHere are a few simple examples:\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:74
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t<em>Note: Throughout the book you will see <em>Investigate!</em>\n"
"\t\tactivities like this one.\n"
"\t\tAnswer the questions in these as best you can to give yourself a feel for what is coming next.</em>\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:83
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe most popular mathematician in the world is throwing a party for all of his friends.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tTo kick things off, they decide that everyone should shake hands.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAssuming all 10 people at the party each shake hands with every other person\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(but not themselves,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tobviously)\n"
"\t\t\t\t\texactly once, how many handshakes take place?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:94
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAt the warm-up event for Oscar's All-Star Hot Dog Eating Contest, Al ate one hot dog.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBob then showed him up by eating three hot dogs.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tNot to be outdone, Carl ate five.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis continued with each contestant eating two more hot dogs than the previous contestant.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many hot dogs did Zeno (the 26th and final contestant) eat?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many hot dogs were eaten in total?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:105
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAfter excavating for weeks, you finally arrive at the burial chamber.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe room is empty except for two large chests.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOn each is carved a message (strangely in English):\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p><ol><li><sidebyside><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:112
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tExactly one of these chests contains a treasure, while the other is filled with deadly immortal scorpions.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p><ol><li><sidebyside><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:116
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tFor either chest, if the chest's message is true, then the chest contains treasure.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:121
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe problem is, you don't know whether the messages are true or false.  What do you do?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tBack in the days of yore,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tfive small towns decided they wanted to build roads directly connecting each pair of towns.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhile the towns had plenty of money to build roads as long and as winding as they wished,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tit was very important that the roads not intersect\n"
"\t\t\t\t\twith each other\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(as stop signs had not yet been invented).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAlso, tunnels and bridges were not allowed, for moral reasons.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIs it possible for each of these towns to build a road to each of the four other towns without creating any intersections?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:142
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAs you consider the problems above, don't worry if it is not obvious to you what the solutions are.\n"
"\t\tWe are more interested here in what sort of information we need to be able to answer the questions.\n"
"\t\tHow can we represent the situation using individually separate and distinct objects?  Don't read on until you have thought about at least this for each of the questions.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:148
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tReady?  Here are some things you might have thought about:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:152
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe people at the party are individuals.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can consider the <em>set</em> of people.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can also consider sets of pairs of people, since it takes exactly two people to shake hands.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo the question is really, how many pairs can you make using elements from a 10-element set?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:159
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tFor example, if there were three people at the party, conveniently named <m>1</m>, <m>2</m>, and <m>3</m>, then the pairs would be <m>(1,2)</m>, <m>(1,3)</m>, and <m>(2,3)</m>.  Or should we include <m>(2,1)</m>, <m>(3,1)</m>, and <m>(3,2)</m> as well?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:164
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          To count the number of hot dogs eaten, either by an individual or in total, we could use a <term>sequence</term> of integers (whole numbers).  The <m>n</m>th term in the sequence might represent the number of hot dogs eaten by the <m>n</m>th contestant.  We can consider a second sequence, also of integers, that gives the total number of hot dogs eaten by the first <m>n</m> contestants combined.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:167
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The solution to the problem will then be the value of the 26th term in the sequence.  To help us find this, we could consider the rate of growth of the sequences, as well as how these two sequences relate to each other.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:172
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Logic questions also belong under the discrete math umbrella: Each statement can have a <em>value</em> of True or False (and there is nothing in-between).  To answer questions like that of the chests of scorpions, we must understand the structure of the statements, and how the truth values of the parts of the statements interact to determine the truth value of the whole statement.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The last question is about a discrete structure called a <term>graph</term>, not to be confused with a graph of a function or set of points.  We can use a graph to represent which elements of a set (or towns) are related to each other (or connected by a road).  In this case, the question becomes, can we draw a graph with five vertices (towns) and ten edges (roads) such that no two edges intersect?  \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:184
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThe four problems above illustrate the four main topics of this book:\n"
"\t\t<term>combinatorics</term>\n"
"\t\t(the theory of ways things <em>combine</em>;\n"
"\t\tin particular, how to count these ways),\n"
"\t\t<term>sequences</term>, <term>symbolic logic</term>,\n"
"\t\tand <term>graph theory</term>.\n"
"\t\tHowever, there are other topics that are also considered part of discrete mathematics, \n"
"\t\tincluding computer science, abstract algebra,\n"
"\t\tnumber theory, game theory,\n"
"\t\tprobability, and geometry\n"
"\t\t(some of these, particularly the last two,\n"
"\t\thave both discrete and non-discrete variants).\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:199
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tUltimately the best way to learn what discrete math is about is to <em>do</em> it.\n"
"\t\tLet's get started!\n"
"\t\tBefore we can begin answering more complicated\n"
"\t\t(and fun)\n"
"\t\tproblems, we will consider a very brief overview of the types of discrete structures we will be using.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:208
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tEach section of the book will end with a small number of <em>Reading Questions</em> like the ones below.\n"
"\t\t\tThese are designed to help you reflect on what you have read.\n"
"\t\t\tIn particular, the final reading question asks you to ask a question of your own.\n"
"\t\t\tThinking about what you don't yet know is a wonderful way to further your understanding of what you do.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:218
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tRight now, how would you describe what <term>discrete</term> mathematics is about, if you were telling your friends about the class you are in?  Write one or two sentences.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_intro-intro.ptx:227
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tWhat questions do you have after reading this section?  Write at least one question about the content of this section that you are curious about.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <appendix><section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:3 source/peer-instruction.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "Discrete Structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:5 source/sec_structures-relations.ptx:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:8
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA double-six domino set consists of tiles containing pairs of numbers, each from 0 to 6.\n"
"\t\t\tHow many tiles are in a double-six domino set?  How many dominoes are in a double-nine domino set?  How many dominoes are in a double-<m>n</m> domino set?\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSpend a few minutes thinking about the questions above.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThen write 2-3 sentences describing your thoughts.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tYou do not need to find a complete solution, but you should describe what you could try and what you think you might need to do to find a solution.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:25
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe are taking a problem-solving approach to discrete mathematics: We will consider a large variety of questions that have a discrete feel to them, and consider how to answer those questions (and prove that our answers are correct).\n"
"\t\t\tThis is not the only way to study discrete math.\n"
"\t\t\tAnother approach would be to study the tools used to solve the problems.\n"
"\t\t\tIf we were art students, we could study paintbrushes and easels and the composition of paint, which would be interesting for sure, but I think it is more enjoyable to actually paint those happy little trees.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:32
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThat said, understanding your tools does help you use them, so in this section, we will consider some basic tools used in discrete mathematics.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will come back to these throughout our studies and understand them better as we need to.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:43
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" The tools in our subject are called <term>discrete structures</term>.\n"
"\t\t\tThey are the mathematical objects that we use to represent parts of the problems we are solving.\n"
"\t\t\t<q>Structure</q> is a good word here, since these <q>things</q> have fairly rigid constraints that make them what they are, just like an apartment building is going to have different characteristics than an airplane hangar or a suspension bridge (these are types of physical structures, not mathematical structures, just to be overly clear and destroy the metaphor).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#.  In particular, if you need a refresher on sets and functions, or have not played with these much at all yet, they are treated in greater detail in <xref ref="sec_intro-sets" />
#.  and <xref ref="sec_intro-functions" />.
#.       
#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:49
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe structures we will use most in discrete math are <term>sets</term>, <term>functions</term>, <term>sequences</term>, <term>relations</term>, and <term>graphs</term>.\n"
"\t\t\tWe now briefly preview each of these.\n"
"\t\t\tAs we progress through our studies, each will be explored in more detail.\n"
"\t\t\t\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <appendix><section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:58 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:4
#: source/peer-instruction.ptx:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:61
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA <term>set</term> is an <em>unordered</em> collection of elements.\n"
"\t\t\tThis is fairly vague, but unless we want to spend a whole book trying to understand sets more precisely, it will be good enough for us.\n"
"\t\t\tIt is possible to define all of mathematics using just sets (even numbers can be thought of as sets themselves), but this is also not what we will do.\n"
"\t\t\tRather, we want to be able to talk about collections of numbers and other objects, and we will collect them in something we call a <term>set</term>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:68
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe can describe sets by saying exactly what elements are members of the set.\n"
"\t\t\tWe could specify this membership in words (e.g., Let <m>A</m> be the set of all natural numbers less than 10), or by explicitly listing all the elements (e.g.,\n"
"\t\t\t<m>A = \\{3, 5, 7\\}</m>), or using something called <term>set comprehension</term> (also called <term>set builder notation</term>).\n"
"\t\t\tAn example of this is <m>A = \\{x \\in \\N \\st x \\lt 10\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tWe would read that as <q><m>A</m> is the set of natural numbers that satisfy the property that they are less than 10.</q> More precisely, the <m>\\N</m> symbol represents the natural numbers, which is itself a set: <m>\\N = \\{0, 1, 2, 3, \\ldots\\}</m> (this shows another way to describe a set).\n"
"\t\t\tThe <m>\\in</m> symbol means <q>is an element of</q>.\n"
"\t\t\tThe <m>\\st</m> is read <q>such that</q> and tells us that what comes next is the <em>condition</em> that must be true of the set's elements.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><aside><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:79
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        In this book, we define the <term>natural numbers</term> to be the whole numbers starting with 0.\n"
"        Not every book includes 0 in this set.\n"
"        It largely depends on what area of mathematics you study.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:86
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSince there are multiple ways to describe the same set, we should be careful about what it means for sets to be the same or different.\n"
"\t\t\tA set is <em>determined</em> by its membership, so all four of the following describe exactly the same set:\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:91
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:97
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\{1, 2, 1+1, 1+2, 2+2\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t(How many numbers belong to this set? It's not 5.)\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:104
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\{2, 4, 1, 3\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t(All that matters is what elements are in the collection, not what order they were written down in.)\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:111
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\{x \\in \\N \\st x \\lt 5 \\text{ and } x \\ge 1\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThere are lots of things you can <em>do</em> with sets, which we will consider in more detail as we need to.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will see that it is often helpful to build new sets from ones we already have (by taking the <term>union</term> or <term>intersection</term> of sets, for example), to compare sets (asking if one set is a <term>subset</term> of another), and to find the number of elements of a set (called its <term>cardinality</term>).\n"
"\t\t\tWe might also want to match up elements of one set with another: To do this, we might use a <term>function</term>, which we will discuss next.\n"
"\t\t\tAwesomely, we can also use sets themselves to describe functions.\n"
"\t\t\tLet's check it out.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <appendix><section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:127 source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:4
#: source/peer-instruction.ptx:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "Functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:142
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      One way to define a <term>function</term> is as a rule that assigns each input exactly one output.\n"
"\t\t\tThe output is called the <term>image</term> of the input.\n"
"\t\t\tFunctions also come equipped with a <term>domain</term>, the set of all inputs, and a <term>codomain</term>, the set of all allowable outputs.\n"
"\t\t\tYou might also speak of the <term>range</term> of the function, which is the set of all <em>actual</em> outputs, or put another way, the set of all <em>images</em> of elements from the domain.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:149
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe write <m>f:X \\to Y</m> to describe a function with name <m>f</m>, domain <m>X</m> and codomain <m>Y</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThis does not tell us <em>which</em> function <m>f</m> is though.\n"
"\t\t\tTo define the function, we must describe the rule.\n"
"\t\t\tOften this is done with a formula (for example, <m>f(x) = x^2</m> says that each element of the domain is mapped to its square), or in words (like how we just described the squaring function).\n"
"\t\t\tWe could also define a function with a table or a graph.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:157
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe key thing that makes a rule a <em>function</em> is that there is <em>exactly one</em> output for each input.\n"
"\t\t\tThat is, the rule must be a good rule.\n"
"\t\t\tWhat output do we assign to the input 7? There can only be one answer for any particular function.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:163
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSince a function maps one set (the domain) to another set (the codomain), there is an obvious connection between sets and functions.\n"
"\t\t\tThere is another connection worth considering though: The graph of a function is often described as a "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><alert>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:164
#, no-wrap
msgid "set"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:164
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" of points.\n"
"\t\t\tHere is an example.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:170
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tConsider the function <m>f:\\{1,2,3\\} \\to \\{2, 4, 6\\}</m> defined by <m>f(x) = 2x</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIf we wanted to plot a graph of this function, we would draw the points <m>(1,2)</m>, <m>(2,4)</m>, and <m>(3,6)</m> (but we would not connect these points with lines, since we are studying discrete math; the domain only contains three elements, not the infinitely many between them).\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:175
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tSo associated with the function is the set of points <m>\\{(1,2), (2, 4), (3,6)\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn fact, this set of points is associated exactly with this (and only this) function.\n"
"\t\t\t\tSo we can think of this set of points <em>as the function itself</em>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOf course, we are using a mathematical object here: an <term>ordered pair</term>.\n"
"\t\t\tThis is not a set (since sets are unordered).\n"
"\t\t\tHow should we talk about ordered things? We will take this question up in the next section.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:189
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThere is one more important consideration about how we define a function with a rule.\n"
"\t\t\tA <term>closed formula</term> is one in which each output is given by an explicit rule based solely on its input.\n"
"\t\t\tThis is what most of us think of as a formula.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, <m>f(n) = 3n + 1</m> is a closed formula, since to find <m>f(5)</m> (say) we take the input 5 and do something to it: Multiply it by 3 and then add 1.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:197
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWhat else could a formula possibly be? A <term>recursive definition</term> of a function tells us how to compute the output for a given input <em>based on other outputs of the function</em>.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, we might insist that <m>f(n) = 2\\cdot f(n-1)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tIf we also specify an <term>initial condition</term> that <m>f(0) = 3</m>, then we can find <m>f(1) = 2\\cdot 3 = 6</m>, and then <m>f(2) = 2\\cdot 6 = 12</m>, and so on.\n"
"\t\t\tWhat is <m>f(5)</m> here? The only way to answer that is to find <m>f(4)</m>, which means we need to find <m>f(3)</m>, which we could do, since we have computed <m>f(2)</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:204
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tRecursive definitions of functions might be less useful for finding a particular output, but they are often easier to specify for a particular application.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will explore this phenomenon more when we study sequences.\n"
"\t\t\tSpeaking of which...\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <appendix><section><title>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:211 source/ch_sequences.ptx:4
#: source/peer-instruction.ptx:59
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:214
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSometimes we are interested not just in a collection of numbers, but in what <em>order</em> those numbers appear.\n"
"\t\t\tIn such cases, we cannot use <em>sets</em>, since they do not distinguish between the order of their elements.\n"
"\t\t\tInstead, we consider <term>sequences</term>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:222
#, no-wrap
msgid "sequence, finite"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:223
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe will consider both <term>finite</term> and <term>infinite</term> sequences.\n"
"\t\t\tA finite sequence may be something as simple as <m>(1,2,3)</m>; that is a sequence with 3 elements, in that particular order.\n"
"\t\t\tWe might also call this an <term>ordered triple</term>, the same way that <m>(7,3)</m> is an ordered pair.  In general, we could call this an <term><m>n</m>-tuple</term> if it has <m>n</m> elements (we assume that tuples are ordered).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:229
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe key difference between the sequence <m>(1,2,3)</m> and the set <m>\\{1,2,3\\}</m> is that we <q>care</q> about the order.\n"
"\t\t\tThat is, the sequence <m>(1,2,3)</m> is different from the sequence <m>(2,3,1)</m>, while the set <m>\\{1,2,3\\}</m> is identical to <m>\\{2,3,1\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:234
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe will often use sequences as a counting tool.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, a very simple counting question is, <q>How many wheels do 100 cars have?</q> Instead of answering just this one question, we could generalize and ask how many wheels <m>n</m> cars have, and get a sequence of answers.\n"
"\t\t\tThis yields the infinite sequence <m>(4, 8, 12, \\ldots )</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThe order these multiples of 4 appear in is important, since each number in the sequence corresponds to a specific version of the question.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:242
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIt is fine to think of a sequence of numbers as an ordered list.\n"
"\t\t\tWe can refer to the <term>terms</term> simply as\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:247
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tand might refer to the entire sequence as <m>(a_n)_{n\\ge 0}</m> or <m>(a_n)_{n\\in \\N}</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:251
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIf we want to be a little more precise and more abstract, we can think of a sequence as a <em>function</em>.\n"
"\t\t\tThe domain is the natural numbers or some subset of consecutive natural numbers (like <m>\\{1,2,3,4\\}</m>).\n"
"\t\t\tThe codomain is some set.\n"
"\t\t\tWe think of the domain as the positions in the sequence, and the image of those inputs as the terms in the sequence.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:259
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tFor example, we might consider the Fibonacci sequence <m>(f_n)_{n\\ge 1}</m>, which starts <m> 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8,\\ldots</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tWhat is the 4th term in the sequence? We might say <m>f_4 = 3</m> (this is assuming the first 1 is the first term and not the 0th term).\n"
"\t\t\tNote that there can only be one 4th term.\n"
"\t\t\tThere can only be one <m>n</m>-th term for any particular value of <m>n</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tSo for any input (the position of the term), there is only one output (the term).\n"
"\t\t\tIt would be perfectly reasonable to write <m>f(4) = 3</m>, and that really does look like a function.\n"
"\t\t\tBut we like to use subscripts.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:269
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe can also describe the terms in a sequence using a table.\n"
"\t\t\tWe might write something like the following:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:282
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t<m>n</m>\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

