#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:330 source/sec_structures-relations.ptx:3
#, no-wrap
msgid "Relations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:333
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHow are the numbers <m>2</m> and <m>6</m> related? Oh, I know: <m>2 \\lt 6</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tAlso, <m>6</m> is a multiple of <m>2</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThe two numbers are also both even.\n"
"\t\t\tAnd here is another fact: They are <em>not equal</em>.\n"
"\t\t\tAll four of these are examples of <term>relations</term>: less than, multiple of, both even, not equal.\n"
"\t\t\tAnd there are many more (infinitely many) relations for pairs of numbers.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:344
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe examples above are all <term>binary relations</term> in that they relate two elements.\n"
"\t\t\tYou could also consider relations between more than two elements.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, we could consider the relation <q>Pythagorean triple</q> on three numbers that holds precisely if they are the side lengths of a right triangle.\n"
"\t\t\tSo the relation is true of the triple <m>(3,4,5)</m>, but not of <m>(4,5,6)</m> (since <m>3^2 + 4^2 = 5^2</m>, but <m>4^2 + 5^2 \\ne 6^2</m>).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:351
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNotice that we can talk about a pair or triple <em>satisfying</em> a relation.\n"
"\t\t\tWe might say that a pair <em>belongs</em> to the relation.\n"
"\t\t\tThe careful and formal way to define a relation is as a <em>set</em> of ordered pairs (or triples, etc.).\n"
"\t\t\tConsider the (infinite) set of all ordered pairs <m>(a,b)</m> such that <m>a \\lt b</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tEvery element of this set contains numbers for which the relation <q>less than</q> is true, and every pair of numbers for which the relation is true is a pair in the set.\n"
"\t\t\tSo we can say that this set of pairs "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><alert>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:356
#, no-wrap
msgid "is"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:356
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" the relation.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:363
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tRelations can have some standard properties, and deciding whether a particular relation has a given property can often help us understand the relation better.\n"
"\t\t\tThe less-than relation is, for example, <term>irreflexive</term> because there are no elements that are less than themselves.\n"
"\t\t\tIt is also <term>antisymmetric</term> since there are no distinct numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that <m>a \\lt b</m> and <m>b \\lt a</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tIt is also <term>transitive</term> since if <m>a \\lt b</m> and <m>b \\lt c</m> then it must follow that <m>a \\lt c</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThese are just a few examples of relation properties though.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:374
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWhy would we care about these properties? It turns out that some groups of properties happen together frequently, and for such collections of properties, we can prove general results about the relations that satisfy them.\n"
"\t\t\tSo if we can prove that a given relation is <term>reflexive</term>, <term>symmetric</term>, and <term>transitive</term> (whatever those mean), then we know the relation is an <term>equivalence relation</term>, and therefore we know it has a bunch of other properties.\n"
"\t\t\tA large portion of discrete mathematics is about studying particular types of relations.\n"
"\t\t\tOne of my favorites is a relation that gives us a graph.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:382
#, no-wrap
msgid "Graphs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:385
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tConsider the set <m>V = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tWhich pairs of numbers from that set add up to 7? We could have <m>\\{1,6\\}</m> or <m>\\{2, 5\\}</m> or <m>\\{3,4\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tWe can picture the set and the interesting (unordered) pairs (i.e., two-element subsets) as a picture called a graph:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:392 source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:393
#, no-wrap
msgid "The graph of pairs of numbers 1 to 6 that sum to 7"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:397
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOn the other hand, we might want to consider pairs of numbers whose sum is even.\n"
"\t\t\tThen, we would get the following picture.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:402
#, no-wrap
msgid "The graph of pairs of numbers 1 to 6 that have an even sum"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:407
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe call these discrete structures <term>graphs</term>.\n"
"\t\t\tA graph is a type of relation, one that is <term>symmetric</term> (if <m>a</m> is related to <m>b</m>, then <m>b</m> is related to <m>a</m>) and <term>irreflexive</term> (no element is related to itself).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:414
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHowever, we mostly think of graphs as the drawings of dots and lines, or more precisely as a set of <term>vertices</term> together with a set of <term>edges</term>, where each edge is a two-element subset of the vertices.\n"
"\t\t\tNotice that even here, we are using the structure <em>set</em> to define the structure <em>graph</em>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:419
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tGraphs show up in all sorts of real-world applications: In a class, some students are friends with each other, so take the students to be the vertices and the edges to be the friendships.\n"
"\t\t\tIn geography, some countries share a border, so take the countries to be the vertices and connect a pair of vertices with an edge if the countries share a border.\n"
"\t\t\tPerhaps you are planning a trip and want to fly from Denver to Paris.\n"
"\t\t\tIs there a direct flight, or must you stop in Newark? That is, does the graph of flights have an edge between Denver and Paris or only between Denver and Newark and between Newark and Paris? When your Amazon driver delivers packages to 10 houses in your neighborhood, how does her app know in which order to deliver the packages? Graph theory!\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:426
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe study of graphs is a subject in its own right, in which many mathematicians hold doctorate degrees and write hundreds of papers each year.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will scratch the surface of this fascinating subject in <xref po4a-id=0>\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:478
#, no-wrap
msgid "Even More Structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:483
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOur list of structures could go on and on, but we will stop here.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will spend just a little time looking at <term>multisets</term>, which are just like sets except that they can have repeated elements.\n"
"\t\t\tSince this is not a geometry class, we will not consider <term>finite geometries</term>, or <term>designs</term> (which are somewhere between graphs and geometries).\n"
"\t\t\tDiscrete structures are useful in computer science, but we will stop short of studying <term>linked lists</term> or <term>red-black trees</term>.\n"
"\t\t\tAlthough abstract algebra is a fascinating subject, we will not get to <term>groups</term> or <term>rings</term> or <term>metroids</term> or <term>POSets</term> or <term>Boolean algebras</term> or ....\n"
"\t\t\tThese are examples of sets on which we define additional operations and study the algebraic structure of how the sets and operations interact.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_intro-structures.ptx:492
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe point is, discrete mathematics is awesome, and you can spend multiple lifetimes studying it.\n"
"\t\t\tSo what are we waiting for? Let's dive in and solve some problems.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

