#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:40
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWhile walking through a fictional forest,\n"
"\t\t\tyou encounter three trolls guarding a bridge.\n"
"\t\t\tEach is either a <em>knight</em>,\n"
"\t\t\twho always tells the truth, or a\n"
"\t\t\t<em>knave</em>, who always lies.\n"
"\t\t\tThe trolls will not let you pass until you correctly identify each as either a knight or a knave.\n"
"\t\t\tEach troll makes a single statement:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:51
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tTroll 1: If I am a knave, then there are exactly two knights here.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:55
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tTroll 2: Troll 1 is lying.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:59
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tTroll 3: Either we are all knaves, or at least one of us is a knight.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:64
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWhich troll is which?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Spend a few minutes thinking about the Investigate problem above.  What could you conclude if you knew Troll 1 really was a knave (i.e., their statement was false)?  Share your initial thoughts on this.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIn order to <em>do</em> mathematics,\n"
"\t\t\twe must be able to <em>talk</em> and\n"
"\t\t\t<em>write</em> about mathematics.\n"
"\t\t\tPerhaps your experience with mathematics so far has mostly involved finding numerical answers to problems.\n"
"\t\t\tAs we embark towards more advanced and abstract mathematics,\n"
"\t\t\twriting will play a more prominent role in the mathematical process.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:89
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIn fact, the primary goal of mathematics, as an academic discipline in its own right, is to establish general mathematical truths.\n"
"\t\t\tHow can we know whether these facts, perhaps called <em>theorems</em> or <em>propositions</em>, are true?  We construct valid arguments, called <em>proofs</em>, which establish the truth of the statements.\n"
"\t\t\tHere, an argument is not the sort of thing you have with your Mom when you disagree about what to have for dinner.\n"
"\t\t\tRather, we have a technical definition of the term.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "Argument"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:105
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\t\tAn <term>argument</term> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:105
#, no-wrap
msgid " is a sequence of statements, the last of which is called the <term>conclusion</term> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:105
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" and the rest of which are called <term>premises</term>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:110
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\t\tAn argument is said to be <term>valid</term> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:110
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" provided the conclusion must be true whenever the premises are all true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAn argument is <term>invalid</term> if it is not valid; that is, all the premises can be true, and the conclusion could still be false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:115
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAn argument is <term>sound</term> provided it is valid and all the premises are true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tA <term>proof</term> of a statement is a sound argument whose conclusion is the statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><aside><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:122
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOur definitions of <term>argument</term>, <term>valid argument</term>, and <term>sound argument</term> are the same ones used in philosophy, the other primary academic discipline concerned with logic and reasoning.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTo determine whether we have a proof of a statement, we must decide both whether every premise is true, and whether the argument is valid: whether the conclusion <em>follows from</em> the premises.\n"
"\t\t\tHow can we do this?\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:135
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider the following two arguments:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:141
#, no-wrap
msgid "If Edith eats her vegetables, then she can have a cookie."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:145
#, no-wrap
msgid "Edith eats her vegetables."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:148 source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:163
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:171 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:357
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:442 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:558
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:683 source/exercises/logic-conc.ptx:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\therefore</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "Edith gets a cookie."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "Florence must eat her vegetables to get a cookie."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "Florence eats her vegetables."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:164
#, no-wrap
msgid "Florence gets a cookie."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t(The symbol <q><m>\\therefore</m></q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:172
#, no-wrap
msgid "<q>therefore</q>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:174
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tmeans <q>therefore</q>)\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:178
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAre these arguments valid?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:184
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tDo you agree that the first argument is valid but the second argument is not?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe will soon develop a better understanding of the logic involved in this analysis, but if your intuition agrees with this assessment, then you are in good shape.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:189
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNotice the two arguments look almost identical.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tEdith and Florence both eat their vegetables.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn both cases, there is a connection between the eating of vegetables and cookies.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tYet we claim that it is valid to conclude that Edith gets a cookie, but not that Florence does.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe difference must be in the connection between eating vegetables and getting cookies.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe need to be skilled at reading and comprehending these sentences.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tDo the two sentences mean the same thing?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:199
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tUnfortunately, in everyday language we are often sloppy, and you might be tempted to say they are equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBut notice that just because Florence <em>must</em> eat her vegetables, we have not claimed that doing so would be <em>enough</em> (she might also need to clean her room, for example).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn everyday (non-mathematical) practice, you might be tempted to say this <q>other direction</q> is implied.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn mathematics, we never get that luxury.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><remark><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:209
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe arguments in the example above illustrate another important point: Even if you don't care about the advancement of human knowledge in the field of mathematics, becoming skilled at analyzing arguments is useful.\n"
"\t\t\t\tAnd even if you don't want to give your grandmother a cookie.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIf you are <em>using</em> mathematics to solve problems in some other discipline, it is still necessary to demonstrate that your solution is correct.\n"
"\t\t\t\tYou better have a good argument that it is!\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:217
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSince arguments are built up of statements, we must agree on what counts as a statement.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:224
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tA <term>statement</term> is a declarative sentence that is either true or false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:230
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIf the sentences in an argument could not be true or false, there would be no way to determine whether the argument was valid, since validity describes a relationship between the truth values of the premises and conclusions.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:234
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe goal of this section is to explore the different <q>shapes</q> a statement can take.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will see that more complicated statements can be built up from simpler ones, in ways that entirely determine their truth value based on the truth values of their parts.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

