#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:776 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:342
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1341
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg P</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:781
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tFor example, we can use the truth table for <m>P \\imp Q</m> to decide whether the statement, <q>If 5 is even, then 6 is even,</q> is true or false.  Here <m>P</m> is the statement <q>5 is even,</q> and <m>Q</m> is the statement <q>6 is even.</q>  Since 5 is not even, the statement <m>P</m> is false.  Since 6 is even, the statement <m>Q</m> is true.  The truth table tells us that the statement <m>P \\imp Q</m> is true when <m>P</m> is false and <m>Q</m> is true (the 3rd row).  So the statement, <q>If 5 is even, then 6 is even,</q> is true.  (If you don't like that the statement is true, hold on to that thought, and we will hopefully resolve it soon.)\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:788
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNote that for us, <em>or</em> is the\n"
"\t\t\t<term>inclusive or</term>\n"
"\t\t\t(and not the sometimes used <em>exclusive or</em>) meaning that\n"
"\t\t\t<m>P \\vee Q</m> is true when <m>P</m> or <m>Q</m> <em> or both <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m></em> are true.\n"
"\t\t\tAs for the other connectives,\n"
"\t\t\t<q>and</q> behaves as you would expect, as does negation.\n"
"\t\t\tThe biconditional\n"
"\t\t\t(if and only if)\n"
"\t\t\tmight seem a little strange,\n"
"\t\t\tbut you should think of this as saying the two parts of the statements are <em>equivalent</em>\n"
"\t\t\tin that they have the same truth value.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:802
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis leaves only the implication\n"
"\t\t\t<m>P \\imp Q</m> which has a slightly different meaning in mathematics than in ordinary usage.\n"
"\t\t\tHowever, implications are so common and useful in mathematics that\n"
"\t\t\twe must develop a level of fluency with their use which warrants a whole section  (<xref po4a-id=0>).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:811
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tUsing the truth conditions for the logical connectives, determine which statements below are true and which are false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:815
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t17 is prime, and 17 is odd.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:820
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t17 is prime, and 18 is prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:825
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t17 is prime, or 18 is prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:830
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t17 is prime, or 19 is prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:835
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf 17 is prime, then 19 is prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:840
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf 18 is prime, then my favorite number is 17.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:845
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t17 is prime if and only if 19 is prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:850
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t17 is not prime if and only if 19 is not prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:858
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tFirst, let's agree on some facts: 17 really is prime and odd, 18 is not either, and 19 is prime.  \n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:862
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTrue.  Both parts of the conjunction are true, so the entire statement is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:867
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tFalse.  The first part is true, but the second part is false, so the entire statement is false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:872
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTrue.\tThe first part is true, so the entire statement is true.  As soon as we see one true statement in a disjunction, we can stop checking and declare the entire statement true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:877
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTrue.  Since we use the inclusive or, the statement is true when both parts are true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:882
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTrue.  Don't be worried that there isn't a good reason that 17 being prime <em>causes</em> 19 to be prime.  That is not what we mean by a conditional statement.  Since the <q>then</q> part is true, we know that the statement overall is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:887
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTrue.  The <q>if</q> part of the statement is false.  That's all we need.  I bet you don't even know what my favorite number is, and you don't need to.\tThe statement is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:892
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTrue.  Do both parts have the same truth value?  Yes, since they are both true.  So the entire statement is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:897
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTrue as well.  Now both parts are false (since both are the negation of a true statement), so the entire statement is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:906
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe way we define logical connectives and their truth value is very precise and technical.  Often, language is not.  Part of learning how to communicate mathematics is learning the cultural norms of mathematical language and how to translate statements in ordinary language into these technical statements.  This will get easier with practice, so make sure you are talking to lots of people about the math you are studying.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:910
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHere are a few examples of how ordinary language might be difficult to translate.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:916
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIdentify the logical structure of each of the following statements.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:920
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t4 and 5 are both prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:925
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tOnly one of 4 or 5 is prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:930
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tYou must attend every day and do the homework to pass this class.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:935
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tEvery number is even or odd.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:946
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tDo you agree this is the same statement as <q>4 is prime, <em>and</em> 5 is prime</q>?  Notice that it would not make sense to write this as <m>P \\wedge Q</m> where <m>P</m> is <q>4</q> and <m>Q</m> is <q>5 is prime</q>.  But if we let <m>P</m> be the statement, <q>4 is prime,</q> then both parts of the conjunction are statements.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:951
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tAgain, we can't just put what is on one side of the <q>or</q> as a statement.  But if we let <m>P</m> be <q>4 is prime</q> and <m>Q</m> be <q>5 is prime,</q>, then we can write this as <m>(P \\vee Q) \\wedge \\neg (P \\wedge Q)</m>.  That is, either 4 is prime or 5 is prime, and it is not the case that both 4 is prime and 5 is prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:956
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHere is another way you could phrase the same statement: If you pass the class, then it must be the case that you attended every day and that you did the homework.  If we agree that this is just a clearer way to state the original statement, then we could illustrate its structure as <m>P \\imp (Q \\wedge R)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:961
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tNotice that this is not the same as saying, <q>Every number is even, or every number is odd.</q>  Of course, saying, <q>3 is even or odd,</q> <em>is</em> the same as saying, <q>3 is even, or 3 is odd.</q>  Language is confusing!  \n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:964
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tWe don't yet have the logical technology to translate this statement as anything more than <m>P</m>, where <m>P</m> is the statement, <q>Every number is even or odd.</q>  Luckily, that technology is available, starting... now!\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:975
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantifiers and Predicates"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:978
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tDid you know that all mammals have hair?  That every integer is even or odd?  That some odd numbers are not prime?  \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:982
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOur goal is to explore how to write statements such as these in mathematical notation to highlight the logical structure of the statements.  \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:988
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis will require considering a new sort of basic sentence called a <term>predicate</term>, which is like a statement, but contains a <term>free variable</term>.  When you replace that variable with a constant of some sort, then the sentence becomes a statement proper. Think of a predicate as making a claim about the values that are substituted for the <q>placeholder</q> variable(s).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:992
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA predicate can be made into a (true or false) statement by evaluating it at some constant(s), or we can claim that some or all possible constants would make the resulting statement true or false.  This is done using <term>quantifiers</term>. \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:996
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantifiers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1004
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe <term>universal quantifier</term> is written <m>\\forall</m> and is read, <q>for all.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe <term>existential quantifier</term> is written <m>\\exists</m> and is read, <q>there exists</q> or <q>for some.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1011
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe usually write predicates similar to how you write a function, although with capital letters.  For example, we might use the predicate <m>P(x)</m> to represent <q><m>x</m> is prime</q>.  We can then say that <m>P(7)</m> is true (since 7 is prime) and that <m>P(8)</m> is false.  Or using quantifiers, we can (falsely) claim that all numbers are prime by writing <m>\\forall x P(x)</m> or (truthfully) claim that there is at least one prime number, by writing <m>\\exists x P(x)</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1019
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTranslate the statement, <q>Every number is even or odd,</q> into symbols.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1024
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tBefore we even start using symbols, it is helpful to rephrase this in a way that captures the logical structure of the statement.  What is the claim saying?  Given any number, it will either be the case that the number is even, or that the number is odd.  In particular, we are not claiming that either all numbers are even or all numbers are odd. \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1028
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLet's use <m>E(x)</m> to say that <m>x</m> is even, and <m>O(x)</m> to say that <m>x</m> is odd.  Then we can write,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1032
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tto say that <m>x</m> is even or <m>x</m> is odd.  Which <m>x</m> is that true for (according to the claim)?  <em>All</em> of them.  So we write the statement as,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1035
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe added some parentheses to emphasize that the <term>scope</term> of the universal quantifier includes both predicates.  \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1040
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNote that if we incorrectly interpreted the statement as claiming that either all numbers are even or all numbers are odd, we could write that as <m>\\forall x O(x) \\vee \\forall x E(x)</m>.  This is not the same!\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1046
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tJust like we did for propositional logic and the logical connectives, we should decide what it means for a quantified predicate to be true or false.\tWe say <m>\\forall x P(x)</m> is true if <m>P(a)</m> is true no matter what constant <m>a</m> we substitute for <m>x</m>.  And similarly, <m>\\exists x P(x)</m> is true if there is at least one value <m>a</m> for which <m>P(a)</m> is true.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1052
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHowever, we must be careful here.  Consider the statement\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1055
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\tYou would read this,\n"
"\t\t\t\t<q>For every <m>x</m> there is some <m>y</m> such that <m>y</m> is less than <m>x</m>.</q> Note that <m>\\lt</m> is a predicate with two free variables; we have chosen to write it with the symbol between the variables instead of the funky-looking <m>L(y,x)</m> or <m>\\lt\\!(y,x)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1062
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIs this statement true?\n"
"\t\t\t\tThe answer depends on our <term>domain of discourse</term>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWhen we say <q>for all</q> <m>x</m>,\n"
"\t\t\t\tdo we mean all positive integers or all real numbers or all elephants or...?\n"
"\t\t\t\tUsually, this information is implied by the context of the statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn discrete mathematics,\n"
"\t\t\t\twe almost always quantify over the\n"
"\t\t\t\t<em>natural numbers</em>, <m>0, 1, 2,\\ldots </m>, so let's take that for our domain of discourse here.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1073
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tFor the statement to be true,\n"
"\t\t\t\twe need, no matter what natural number we select, for\n"
"\t\t\t\tthere to be some natural number that is strictly smaller.\n"
"\t\t\t\tPerhaps we could let\n"
"\t\t\t\t<m>y</m> be\n"
"\t\t\t\t<m>x-1</m>?\n"
"\t\t\t\tBut here is the problem: what if\n"
"\t\t\t\t<m>x = 0</m>?\n"
"\t\t\t\tThen\n"
"\t\t\t\t<m>y = -1</m>, and that is\n"
"\t\t\t\t<em>not a number!</em>\n"
"\t\t\t\t(in our domain of discourse).\n"
"\t\t\t\tThus we see that the statement is false because there is a number less than or equal to all other numbers.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn symbols,\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1093
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe will explore some rules for working with quantifiers and other connectives in <xref po4a-id=0>.  For now, we will focus on  translating between informal statements in ordinary language and the more precise language of logic.  There is no perfect algorithm for doing this translation, but here are a few useful rules of thumb.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1097
#, no-wrap
msgid "Every blank is blank"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1099
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\tAny statement of the form, <q>Every <m>P</m>-thing is a <m>Q</m>-thing</q> can be written as "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1102
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tExample: all mammals have hair, becomes <m>\\forall x (M(x) \\imp H(x))</m>, where <m>M(x)</m> means <m>x</m> is a mammal, and <m>H(x)</m> means <m>x</m> has hair.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTo make sense of this, think about what we mean by statements like these in terms of sets.  We claim that the set of mammals is contained in, or is a subset of, the set of hairy things.  What we mean by <q><m>A</m> is a subset of <m>B</m></q> is precisely that every element of <m>x</m> is an element of <m>y</m>.  This can also be expressed by saying that <q>if <m>x</m> is an element of <m>A</m>, then <m>x</m> is also an element of <m>B</m>.</q>   \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1111
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some blanks are blank"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1113
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAny statement of the form, <q>Some <m>P</m>-things are <m>Q</m>-things,</q> can be written as \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1119
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tExample: Some cats can swim, becomes <m>\\exists x (C(x) \\wedge S(x))</m>, where <m>C(x)</m> means <m>x</m> is a cat, and <m>S(x)</m> means <m>x</m> can swim.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1124
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAgain, it is helpful to think of how to express such statements in terms of sets.  To say that some cats can swim is to say that there are things that belong both to the set of cats and to the set of swimming things.  Such animals belong to the <em>intersection</em> of these two sets, which you can describe as belonging to the first set <em>and</em> the second set.  Existential statements of this form claim that the intersection of the two sets is not empty.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><title>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1128
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implicit Quantifiers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIt is always a good idea to be precise in mathematics.\n"
"\t\t\t\tSometimes though, we can relax a bit, as long as we all agree on a convention.\n"
"\t\t\t\tAn example of such a convention is to assume that sentences containing predicates with free variables are intended as statements, where the variables are universally quantified.  \n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1139
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tFor example, do you believe that if a shape is a square, then it is a rectangle?  But how can that be true if it is not a statement?  To be a little more precise, we have two predicates: <m>S(x)</m> for <q><m>x</m> is a square</q> and <m>R(x)</m> for <q><m>x</m> is a rectangle</q>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThe <em>sentence</em> we are looking at is\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\tThis is neither true nor false, as it is not a statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\tBut come on!  We all know that we meant to consider the statement,\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1148
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"\t\t\t\tand this is what our convention tells us to consider. We call the resulting statement the <term>universal generalization</term> of the original sentence.  \n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1156
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tGiven a sentence with free variables, the <term>universal generalization</term> of that sentence is the statement obtained by adding enough universal quantifiers to the beginning of the sentence so that all free variables become bound.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1162
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tSimilarly, we will often be a bit sloppy about the distinction between a predicate and a statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\tFor example, we might write, <em>let <m>P(n)</m> be the <em>statement</em>, <q><m>n</m> is prime,</q></em> which is technically incorrect.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIt is implicit that we mean that we are defining <m>P(n)</m> to be a predicate, which for each <m>n</m> becomes the statement, <m>n</m> is prime.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-statements.ptx:1175
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
