#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:3
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implications"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:14
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tExplain the conditions under which an implication is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:20
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIdentify statements as equivalent to a given implication or its converse.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:25
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tExplain the relationship between the truth values of an implication, its converse, and its contrapositive.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:37
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLittle Timmy's Mom tells him, <q>If you don't eat all your broccoli, then you will not get any ice cream.</q>  Of course, Timmy loves his ice cream, so he quickly eats all his broccoli (which actually tastes pretty good). \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:40
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAfter dinner, when Timmy asks for his ice cream, he is told no!  Does Timmy have a right to be upset?  Why or why not?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:48
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tBy far, the most important type of statement in mathematics is the implication.  It is also the least intuitive of our basic molecular statement types.  Our goal in this section is to become more familiar with this key concept.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:53
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\tTo see why this sort of statement is so prevalent, consider the <em>Pythagorean Theorem</em>.  Despite what social media might claim, the Pythagorean Theorem is not "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:53
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".  Okay, sure, that has a variable in it, so we must be using the convention to take the universal generalization,\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:56
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t So <m>1^2 + 5^2 = 2^2</m>???  Okay, fine.  The equation is true as long as <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are the lengths of the legs of a right triangle and <m>c</m> is the length of the hypotenuse.  In other words:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:61
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t<em>If</em> <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are the lengths of the legs of a right triangle with hypotenuse of length <m>c</m>, <em>then</em> <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:65
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tMath is about making general claims, but a claim is rarely going to be true of absolutely <em>every</em> mathematical object.  The way we <em>restrict</em> our claims to a particular type of object is with an implication: <q>Take any object you like, <em>if</em> it is of the right type, <em>then</em> this thing is true about it.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tSimilarly, as we saw in the <xref po4a-id=0> subsection, when we make claims like <q>Every square is a rectangle,</q> we really have an implication: <q>If something is a square, then it is a rectangle.</q> \n"
"\t\t\t "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHere is a reminder of what we mean by an implication.\n"
"\t\t\t "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implication"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><idx><see>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:79
#, no-wrap
msgid "hypothesis"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><idx><see>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "conclusion"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:85
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAn <term>implication</term> (or <term>conditional</term>)\n"
"\t\t\t\tis a molecular statement of the form\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:90
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\twhere <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are statements.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWe say that\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:94
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>P</m> is the <term>hypothesis</term>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t(or <term>antecedent</term>).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:99
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>Q</m> is the <term>conclusion</term>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t(or <term>consequent</term>).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:106
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAn implication is <em>true</em>\n"
"\t\t\t\tprovided <m>P</m> is false or <m>Q</m> is true\n"
"\t\t\t\t(or both),\n"
"\t\t\t\tand <em>false</em> otherwise.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn particular, the only way for\n"
"\t\t\t\t<m>P \\imp Q</m> to be false is for <m>P</m> to be true\n"
"\t\t\t\t<em>and</em> <m>Q</m> to be false.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe definition of truth of an implication can also be represented as a truth table:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><figure><caption>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:122
#, no-wrap
msgid "The truth table for <m>P \\imp Q</m>."
msgstr ""

