#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThis is not equivalent to the original statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\tMaybe I also eat popcorn when I watch TV?  In that case, the original statement would be true, but this one would be false.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:338
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIf I don't eat popcorn, then I don't see a movie.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:344
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tCorrect.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:351
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIt is necessary that I eat popcorn when I see a movie.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:357
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThis is equivalent to the original statement (although here <q>necessary</q> is used in a logical sense).\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:364
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tTo see a movie, it is sufficient for me to eat popcorn.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:370
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tJust because I eat popcorn, doesn't mean I see a movie.\n"
"\t\t\t\tI might eat popcorn in other situations.\n"
"\t\t\t\tSo this is not equivalent to the original statement.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tI only watch a movie if I eat popcorn.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:385
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAnother way of saying this is, <q>I watch a movie only if I eat popcorn.</q>  This is equivalent to the original statement.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:397
#, no-wrap
msgid "Understanding the Truth Table"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:400
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe truth value of the implication is determined by the truth values of its two parts.\n"
"\t\t\tOur definition of the truth conditions for an implication says that there is only one way for an implication to be false: when the hypothesis is true and the conclusion is false.  \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:407
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider the statement:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:412
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIf Bob gets a 90 on the final,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tthen Bob will pass the class.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:418
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThis is definitely an implication:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>P</m> is the statement <q>Bob gets a 90 on the final,</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tand <m>Q</m> is the statement <q>Bob will pass the class.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:424
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSuppose I made that statement to Bob.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn what circumstances would it be fair to call me a liar?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat if Bob really did get a 90 on the final,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tand he did pass the class?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThen I have not lied; my statement is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tHowever, if Bob did get a 90 on the final and did not pass the class,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen I lied, making the statement false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe tricky case is this: What if Bob did not get a 90 on the final?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tMaybe he passes the class, maybe he doesn't.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tDid I lie in either case?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tI think not.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn these last two cases,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>P</m> was false, and the statement <m>P \\imp Q</m> was true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn the first case, <m>Q</m> was true,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tand so was <m>P \\imp Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo <m>P \\imp Q</m> is true when either <m>P</m> is false or <m>Q</m> is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:445
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tJust to be clear,\n"
"\t\t\talthough we sometimes read <m>P \\imp Q</m> as <q> <m>P</m>\n"
"\t\t\t<em>implies</em> <m>Q</m> </q>,\n"
"\t\t\twe are not insisting that there is some <em>causal</em> relationship between the statements <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> (although there might be).\n"
"\t\t\t<q>If <m>x \\lt y</m>, then <m>x+1 \\lt y+1</m>,</q> is a true statement (or at least, its universal generalization is).  We know it is true because we understand how the two parts interact.  If you add 1 to two numbers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, then their order does not change.  But the statement, <q>if <m>1 \\lt 2</m>, then Euclid studied geometry</q> is also a true implication.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:455
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tDecide which of the following statements are true and which are false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBriefly explain.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:460
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf <m>1=1</m>, then most horses have 4 legs.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:466
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf <m>0=1</m>, then <m>1=1</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:472
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf 8 is a prime number, then the 7624th digit of\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\pi</m> is an 8.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:479
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf the 7624th digit of <m>\\pi</m> is an 8, then <m>2+2 = 4</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:488
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAll four of the statements are true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tRemember, the only way for an implication to be false is for the\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<em>if</em> part to be true and the\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<em>then</em> part to be false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:495
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHere both the hypothesis and the conclusion are true,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tso the implication is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIt does not matter that there is no meaningful connection between the true mathematical fact and the fact about horses.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:503
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHere the hypothesis is false and the conclusion is true,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tso the implication is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:510
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tI have no idea what the 7624th digit of <m>\\pi</m> is,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tbut this does not matter.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSince the hypothesis is false,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tthe implication is automatically true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:519
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tRegardless of the truth value of the hypothesis,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tthe conclusion is true, making the implication true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:529
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis is a strange example and isn't really how we use implications anyway.  This strangeness is not just mathematicians being stubborn though.  The truth conditions for implications <em>must</em> be like they are for mathematics to make sense.  Let's see why.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:535
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider the statement, <q>All squares are rectangles,</q> which can also be phrased as, <q>For all shapes, if the shape is a square, then it is a rectangle.</q>  Is this statement true or false?  Are we sure?  What about the following three shapes?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:538
#, no-wrap
msgid "three shapes, a square, a non-square rectangle, and a triangle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:550
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tOf course the statement is true.  A square is a 4-sided plane figure with 4 right angles and 4 equal-length sides, while a rectangle is a 4-sided plane figure with 4 right angles.  \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:554
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHowever, what we mean when we consider a universal statement like this is that, no matter what we <q>plug in</q> for the variable (<q>the shape</q> in this case), the resulting statement is true.  When the statement is about a particular shape, we have an implication <m>P \\imp Q</m>.  This means it must be true that, if the actual shape on the left is a square, then it is a rectangle.  Great.  The shape is a square (<m>P</m> is true) and is a rectangle (<m>Q</m> is true), so yes, the implication is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:558
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIs the implication true of the rectangle in the middle?  Well, that shape is not a square (<m>P</m> is false), and it is a rectangle (<m>Q</m> is true).  But look, we believe that all squares are rectangles, so the statement must be true.  Even of a rectangle.  The only way this works is if <q>false implies true</q> is true!\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:562
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSimilarly, all squares are rectangles is a true statement, even when we look at a triangle.  <m>P</m> is false (the triangle is not a square), and <m>Q</m> is false (the triangle is not a rectangle).  Thankfully, we defined implications to be true in this case as well.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:566
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe have given shapes that illustrate lines 1, 3, and 4 of the truth table for implications (<xref po4a-id=0>).  What shape illustrates line 2?  That would need to be a shape that was a square and was not a rectangle.... Of course we can't find one, precisely because the statement is true!\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:573
#, no-wrap
msgid "Related Statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:576
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAn implication is a way of expressing a relationship between two statements.\n"
"\t\t\tIt is often interesting to ask whether there are other relationships between the statements.\n"
"\t\t\tHere we introduce some common language to address this question.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:582
#, no-wrap
msgid "Converse, Contrapositive, and Inverse"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:588
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tGiven an implication <m>P \\imp Q</m>, we say,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:591
#, no-wrap
msgid "The <term>converse</term> is the statement <m>Q \\imp P</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:595
#, no-wrap
msgid "The <term>contrapositive</term> is the statement <m>\\neg Q \\imp \\neg P</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:599
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThe <term>inverse</term> is the statement, <m>\\neg P \\imp \\neg Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:611
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider the implication, <q>If you clean your room, then you can go to the party.</q>  Give the converse, contrapositive, and inverse of this statement\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:616
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe converse is, <q>If you can go to the party, then you clean your room.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:619
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe contrapositive is, <q>If you can't go to the party, then you don't clean your room.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:622
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe inverse is, <q>If you don't clean your room, then you can't go to the party.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:628
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSymbolically, both the converse and the contrapositive <em>switch</em> the order of the two parts of the statement (or alternatively, think about turning the arrow to point in the other direction).  The contrapositive and the inverse take the <em>negation</em> of both of the statements.  Notice that if you take the converse (switch the order) and then take the contrapositive <em>of</em> that converse (switch the order back and negate both parts) you get the inverse.  So the inverse is nothing more than the contrapositive of the converse.  Or the converse of the contrapositive, which is a fun fact to mention at parties.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:632
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWhen considering statements with quantifiers, we ignore the outside quantifiers when forming the converse, contrapositive, and inverse.  \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:636
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantifiers and the Converse, Contrapositive, and Inverse"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:638
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tA quantified implication <m>\\forall x (P(x) \\imp Q(x))</m> has:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:641
#, no-wrap
msgid "Converse"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:643
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\forall x (Q(x) \\imp P(x))</m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:647 source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1065
#, no-wrap
msgid "Contrapositive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:649
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\forall x (\\neg Q(x) \\imp \\neg P(x))</m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:653
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inverse"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:655
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\forall x (\\neg P(x) \\imp \\neg Q(x))</m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><note><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:664
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIt is unlikely that we would encounter a statement of the form <m>\\exists x (P(x) \\imp Q(x))</m>, since this would be automatically true if there was any <m>x</m> that made <m>P(x)</m> false.  But if we did, the same rules would apply to the converse, contrapositive, and inverse as above: Just ignore the quantifier when swapping and/or negating the parts of the implication.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:670
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tFor example, <q>For all shapes, if the shape is a square, then it is a rectangle</q> (i.e., all squares are rectangles) has the converse, <q>For all shapes, if the shape is a rectangle, then it is a square</q> (so all rectangles are squares).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:675
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIt turns out the Pythagorean Theorem is one such statement.  It is also true that <em>if</em> <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>,\n"
"\t\t\t\t<em>then</em> there is a right triangle with legs of lengths <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> and hypotenuse of length <m>c</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tSo we could have also written the theorem as a biconditional:\n"
"\t\t\t\t<q><m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are the lengths of the legs of a right triangle with hypotenuse of length <m>c</m> <em>if and only if</em>\n"
"\t\t\t\t<m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:674
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWell, that's not true!  There exist shapes that are rectangles and are NOT squares.  Indeed, this is an example of a statement that is true with a false converse.  There are lots of examples of this throughout mathematics.  There are also examples of true implications that have true converses.  You just can't know from the logic.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\tThe contrapositive of <q>For all shapes, if it is a square, then it is a rectangle</q> is <q>For all shapes, if the shape is not a rectangle, then it is not a square.</q>  This is true.  In fact, "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><alert>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid "the contrapositive of a true statement is always true"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"!  \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:688
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSince the contrapositive of an implication\n"
"\t\t\talways has the same truth value as its original implication, it can often be helpful to analyze the contrapositive to decide whether an implication is true.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:695
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTrue or false: If you draw any nine playing cards from a regular deck,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen you will have at least three cards all of the same suit.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIs the converse true?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTrue.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe original implication is a little hard to analyze because there are so many combinations of nine cards.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBut consider the contrapositive: if you <em>don't</em>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\thave at least three cards all of the same suit,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen you don't have nine cards.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIt is easy to see why this is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIf you don't have at least three cards in a suit, you can have at most two cards of each of the four suits,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tfor a total of at most eight cards.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:714
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe converse: If you have at least three cards of the same suit,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen you have nine cards.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis is false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tYou could have three spades and nothing else.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tNote that to demonstrate that the converse\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(an implication)\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tis false, we provided an example where the hypothesis is true\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(you do have three cards of the same suit),\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tbut where the conclusion is false\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(you do not have nine cards).  In other words, we find some example that puts us in row 2 of the implication's truth table.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:729
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tUnderstanding converses and contrapositives can help understand implications and their truth values:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:735
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSuppose I tell Sue that if she gets a 93% on her final,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen she will get an A in the class.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAssuming that what I said is true,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\twhat can you conclude in the following cases:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:745
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSue gets a 93% on her final.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:751
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSue gets an A in the class.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:757
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSue does not get a 93% on her final.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:763
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSue does not get an A in the class.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:772
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNote first that whenever\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>P \\imp Q</m> and\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>P</m> are both true statements,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>Q</m> must be true as well.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tFor this problem, take\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>P</m> to mean\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<q>Sue gets a 93%\n"
"\t\t\t\t\ton her final</q> and\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>Q</m> to mean\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<q>Sue will get an A in the class.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:788
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tWe have <m>P \\imp Q</m> and <m>P</m>, so <m>Q</m> follows.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSue gets an A.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:795
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tYou cannot conclude anything.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSue could have gotten the A because she did extra credit, for example.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tNotice that we do not know that if Sue gets an <m>A</m>,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tthen she gets a 93% on her final.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThat is the converse of the original implication,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tso it might or might not be true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:806
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThe contrapositive of the converse of\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>P \\imp Q</m> is <m>\\neg P \\imp \\neg Q</m>,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\twhich states that if Sue does not get a 93% on the final,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tthen she will not get an A in the class.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tBut this does not follow from the original implication.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tAgain, we can conclude nothing.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSue could have done extra credit.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:818
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tWhat would happen if Sue did not get an A but <em>did</em>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tget a 93% on the final?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThen <m>P</m> would be true, and <m>Q</m> would be false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThis makes the implication <m>P \\imp Q</m> false!\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIt must be that Sue did not get a 93% on the final.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tNotice we now have the implication\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\neg Q \\imp \\neg P</m> which is the contrapositive of <m>P \\imp Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSince <m>P \\imp Q</m> is assumed to be true,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\twe know <m>\\neg Q \\imp \\neg P</m> is true as well.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:835
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAs we said above,\n"
"\t\t\tan implication is not logically equivalent to its converse,\n"
"\t\t\tbut it is possible that both the implication and its converse are true.\n"
"\t\t\tIn this case, when both\n"
"\t\t\t<m>P \\imp Q</m> and\n"
"\t\t\t<m>Q \\imp P</m> are true, we say that\n"
"\t\t\t<m>P</m> and\n"
"\t\t\t<m>Q</m> are equivalent and write <m>P \\iff Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThis is the biconditional we mentioned in <xref po4a-id=0>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:848
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tYou can think of <q>if and only if</q>\n"
"\t\t\tstatements as having two parts:\n"
"\t\t\tan implication and its converse.\n"
"\t\t\tWe might say one is the <q>if</q> part,\n"
"\t\t\tand the other is the <q>only if</q> part.\n"
"\t\t\tWe also sometimes say that <q>if and only if</q>\n"
"\t\t\tstatements have two directions:\n"
"\t\t\ta forward direction <m>(P \\imp Q)</m> and a backward direction\n"
"\t\t\t(<m>P \\leftarrow Q</m>,\n"
"\t\t\twhich is really just sloppy notation for <m>Q \\imp P</m>).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:861
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tLet's think a little about which part is which.\n"
"\t\t\tIs <m>P \\imp Q</m> the <q>if</q>\n"
"\t\t\tpart or the <q>only if</q> part?\n"
"\t\t\tConsider an example.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:870
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSuppose it is true that I sing if and only if I'm in the shower.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe know this means both that if I sing, then I'm in the shower,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tand also the converse, that if I'm in the shower, then I sing.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>P</m> be the statement,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<q>I sing,</q> and <m>Q</m> be,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<q>I'm in the shower.</q> So\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>P \\imp Q</m> is the statement <q>if I sing,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen I'm in the shower.</q> Which part of the if and only if statement is this?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:881
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat we are really asking for is the meaning of <q>I sing\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<em>if</em> I'm in the shower</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tand <q>I sing <em>only if</em>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tI'm in the shower.</q> When is the first one\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(the <q>if</q> part)\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<em>false</em>?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhen I am in the shower but not singing.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThat is the same condition for being false as the statement,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<q>If I'm in the shower,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen I sing.</q> So the <q>if</q> part is <m>Q \\imp P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOn the other hand, to say,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<q>I sing only if I'm in the shower</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tis equivalent to saying <q>If I sing,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen I'm in the shower,</q> so the\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<q>only if</q> part is <m>P \\imp Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:901
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIt is not especially important to know which part is the\n"
"\t\t\t<q>if</q> or <q>only if</q> part,\n"
"\t\t\tbut this does illustrate something very, very important:\n"
"\t\t\t<em>There are many ways to state an implication!</em>\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:910
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tRephrase the implication,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t<q>If I dream, then I am asleep</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tin as many ways as possible.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThen do the same for the converse.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:919
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe following are all equivalent to the original implication:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:923
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tI am asleep if I dream.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:929
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tI dream only if I am asleep.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:935
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIn order to dream, I must be asleep.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:941
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTo dream, it is necessary that I am asleep.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:947
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tTo be asleep, it is sufficient to dream.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:953
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tI am not dreaming unless I am asleep.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:957
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe following are equivalent to the converse (if I am asleep,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen I dream):\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:962
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tI dream if I am asleep.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:968
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tI am asleep only if I dream.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:974
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIt is necessary that I dream in order to be asleep.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:980
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIt is sufficient that I be asleep in order to dream.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:986
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf I don't dream, then I'm not asleep.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:995
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHopefully you agree with the above example.\n"
"\t\t\tWe include the\n"
"\t\t\t<q>necessary and sufficient</q>\n"
"\t\t\tversions because those are common when discussing mathematics.\n"
"\t\t\tLet's agree once and for all what they mean.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1003
#, no-wrap
msgid "Necessary and Sufficient"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1010
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<q> <m>P</m> is necessary for <m>Q</m> </q> means <m>Q \\imp P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1014
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<q> <m>P</m> is sufficient for <m>Q</m> </q> means <m>P \\imp Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1019
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf <m>P</m> is necessary and sufficient for <m>Q</m>, then <m>P \\iff Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1028
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTo be honest, I have trouble with these if I'm not very careful.\n"
"\t\t\tI find it helps to keep a standard example for reference.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1035
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIn a regular deck of cards, the red suits are hearts and diamonds.  The black suits are clubs and spades.  Thus it is true that, after picking a card, if my card is a spade, then my card is black.  \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1039
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tRestate this fact using necessary and sufficient phrasing.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1044
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tFor my card to be a spade, it is necessary that it is black.  However, it is not sufficient for it to be black to say that I am holding a spade (since I could have a club).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1048
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tI can also say that to have a black card, it is sufficient to have a spade.  It is not necessary that I have a spade.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1052
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIt is helpful to think about the amount of evidence you need.  Is knowing that the card is a spade enough evidence to conclude that it is a black card?  Yes, that is sufficient!  Being a spade is a sufficient condition for the card to be black.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1058
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThinking about the necessity and sufficiency of conditions can also help when writing proofs and justifying conclusions.\n"
"\t\t\tIf you want to establish some mathematical fact,\n"
"\t\t\tit is helpful to think what other facts would\n"
"\t\t\t<em>be enough</em>\n"
"\t\t\t(be sufficient)\n"
"\t\t\tto prove your fact.\n"
"\t\t\tIf you have an assumption,\n"
"\t\t\tthink about what must also be necessary if that hypothesis is true.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1074
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIt happens to be true that all mammals have hair.  Which of the following are also true?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1081
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHaving hair is a necessary condition for being a mammal.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1088
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHaving hair is a sufficient condition for being a mammal.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1093
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThis would be saying that as soon as a thing has hair, it is a mammal.  But...tarantulas!\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1100
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIf an animal doesn't have hair, then it is not a mammal.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1105
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThis is the contrapositive of the original statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1112
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tAn animal is a mammal only if it has hair.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1117
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tAnd this is the same as saying if an animal is a mammal, then it has hair.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-implications.ptx:1127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tGive an example of a <em>true</em> implication (written out in words) that has a <em>false</em> converse.\n"
"\t\t\t\tExplain why your implication is true and why the converse is false.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:9
#, no-wrap
msgid " Translate into English: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\forall x (E(x) \\imp E(x +2))</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:12
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\forall x \\exists y (\\sin(x) = y)</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\forall y \\exists x (\\sin(x) = y)</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\forall x \\forall y (x^3 = y^3 \\imp x = y)</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:24
#, no-wrap
