#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:156
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Recall from calculus, if a function is differentiable at a point\n"
"        <m>c</m>, then it is continuous at\n"
"        <m>c</m>, but that the converse of this statement is not true (for example,\n"
"        <m>f(x) = |x|</m> at the point 0).\n"
"        Restate this fact using <q>necessary and sufficient</q> language.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:166
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        It is true that in order for a function to be differentiable at a point\n"
"        <m>c</m>, it is necessary for the function to be continuous at\n"
"        <m>c</m>.\n"
"        However, it is not necessary that a function be differentiable at\n"
"        <m>c</m> for it to be continuous at\n"
"        <m>c</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-implications.ptx:175
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        It is true that to be continuous at a point\n"
"        <m>c</m>, it is sufficient that the function be differentiable at\n"
"        <m>c</m>.\n"
"        However, it is not the case that being continuous at\n"
"        <m>c</m> is sufficient for a function to be differentiable at\n"
"        <m>c</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

