#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rules of Logic"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tUse truth tables to determine whether two statements are logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:22
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tUse truth tables to determine whether a deduction rule is valid.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:28
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tUse logical equivalence and deduction rules to simplify statements and make deductions.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:39
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHolmes always wears one of the two vests he owns: one tweed and one mint green.\n"
"\t\t\t\tHe always wears either the green vest or red shoes.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWhenever he wears a purple shirt and the green vest, he chooses to not wear a bow tie.\n"
"\t\t\t\tHe never wears the green vest unless he is also wearing either a purple shirt or red shoes.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWhenever he wears red shoes, he also wears a purple shirt.\n"
"\t\t\t\tToday, Holmes wore a bow tie.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWhat else did he wear?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:52
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSpend a few minutes thinking about the <em>Investigate!</em> question above.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOf the six statements in the puzzle, only one is atomic.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tUse this atomic statement and one other statement to deduce a new statement about what Holmes might (or might not) be wearing.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tExplain why you think your new statement is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:61
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe atomic statement is, <q>Holmes wore a bow tie.</q>  Only one of the molecular statements has this as one of its <em>atoms</em>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:67
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tLogic studies the ways statements can interact with each other.\n"
"\t\t\tMore precisely, we consider the way the logical form statements can interact.\n"
"\t\t\tThe study of logic does not care about the content of the atomic statements or the meaning of predicates.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, the claims, <q>If spiders have six legs, then Sam walks with a limp,</q> and, <q>If the moon is made of cheese, then cheddar is a type of cheese,</q> are identical from a logical perspective.\n"
"\t\t\tLogic doesn't care about whether Sam is a spider or the culinary makeup of the moon.\n"
"\t\t\tBoth statements have the same form: They are implications, <m>P \\imp Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:76
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOf course, in mathematics we often <em>do</em> know some relationship between various atomic statements.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, we know a relationship between being even and being a multiple of 10.\n"
"\t\t\tThat relationship allows us to make claims such as, <q>If the number I'm thinking of is a multiple of 10, then it is even.</q>  Suppose I also told you that I am now thinking of a number that is not even.\n"
"\t\t\tWe can deduce that I am not thinking of a multiple of 10! Crucially, if we accept the truth of the statements here, we can make this deduction without thinking about the nature of numbers.\n"
"\t\t\tIt can feel very liberating and provide much-needed clarity when trying to understand complicated reasoning if we can separate the content from the logical form of arguments.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:84
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOur goal in this section is to establish some procedures for analyzing how the truth or falsity of statements interact, based on their logical form.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will see that some molecular statements must be true regardless of whether their atomic parts are true or false, while some statements must always be false.\n"
"\t\t\tFor other statements, it can be that two statements are always true or false together, or that whenever one statement is true, another statement must also be true.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:90
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe main method for establishing these relationships will be <term>truth tables</term>.\n"
"\t\t\tThere is a very clear procedure for constructing and analyzing truth tables, but for complicated arguments that contain many atomic statements, the truth tables become very large and unwieldy.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will therefore use truth tables to understand some basic equivalences and deductions that can be applied in a sequence of reasoning to construct larger arguments.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:109
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider the statement, <q>Whenever Holmes wears a purple shirt and the green vest, he chooses to not wear a bow tie.</q>  Let <m>P</m> be the statement, <q>Holmes wears a purple shirt,</q> <m>G</m> be the statement, <q>Holmes wears the green vest,</q> and <m>B</m> be the statement, <q>Holmes wears a bow tie.</q>  Which of the following is the best translation of the statement into propositional logic?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m> (P \\wedge G) \\imp \\neg B </m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:126
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m> (P \\wedge G) \\imp B </m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:134
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m> (P \\vee G) \\imp \\neg B </m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:142
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m> P \\wedge (G \\imp B) </m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:152
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider the statement, <q>Holmes never wears the green vest unless he is also wearing either a purple shirt or red shoes.</q> With <m>P</m> and <m>G</m> as in the previous question, and <m>R</m> being the statement, <q>Holmes wears red shoes,</q> which of the following is the best translation of the statement into propositional logic?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:161
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m> G \\imp (P \\vee R) </m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m> \\neg G \\imp (P \\vee R) </m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:175
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tConsider the case where Holmes does wear a green vest but does not wear a purple shirt or red shoes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThat would make <m>\\neg G</m> false and <m>P \\vee R</m> true, so the implication would be true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tBut in this situation, the original statement would be false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:185
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m> (P \\vee R) \\imp G </m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:191
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tConsider the case where Holmes does not wear a purple shirt or red shoes, and does wear the green vest.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThat would make <m>P \\vee R</m> false and <m>G</m> true, so the implication would be true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tBut in this situation, the original statement would be false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:201
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m> (P \\vee R) \\imp \\neg G </m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:207
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tConsider the case where Holmes does not wear a purple shirt or red shoes, and does wear the green vest.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tThat would make <m>P \\vee R</m> false and <m>\\neg G</m> false, so the implication would be true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tBut in this situation, the original statement would be false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

