#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:276
#, no-wrap
msgid "Truth Tables"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:280
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHere's a question about playing Monopoly:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:285
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIf you get more doubles than any other player, then you will lose, or if you lose, then you must have bought the most properties.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:290
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTrue or false? We will answer this question and won't need to know anything about Monopoly.\n"
"\t\t\tInstead, we will look at the logical <em>form</em> of the statement.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:295
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe need to decide when the statement <m>(P \\imp Q) \\vee (Q \\imp R)</m> is true.\n"
"\t\t\tUsing the definitions of the connectives in <xref po4a-id=0>, we see that for this to be true, either <m>P \\imp Q</m> must be true or <m>Q \\imp R</m> must be true (or both).\n"
"\t\t\tThose are true if either <m>P</m> is false or <m>Q</m> is true (in the first case) and <m>Q</m> is false or <m>R</m> is true (in the second case).\n"
"\t\t\tSo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:298
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"yeah, it gets a bit messy.\n"
"\t\t\tLuckily, we can make a chart to keep track of all the possibilities with a <term>truth table</term>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:303
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe idea is this: On each row, we list a possible combination of T's and F's (Trues and Falses) for each of the propositional variables, and then mark down whether the (molecular) statement in question is true or false in that case.\n"
"\t\t\tWe do this for every possible combination of T's and F's.\n"
"\t\t\tThen we can clearly see the cases in which the statement is true or false.\n"
"\t\t\tFor complicated statements, we will first fill in values for each part of the statement, as a way of breaking up our task into smaller, more manageable pieces.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:310
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSince the truth value of a statement is completely determined by the truth values of its parts and how they are connected, all you need to know is the truth tables for each of the logical connectives, which we have already seen in <xref po4a-id=0>\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:314
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe truth tables we consider here all build off the basic ones, applying the basic rules multiple times.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:320
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tMake a truth table for the statement <m>\\neg P \\vee Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:327
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNote that this statement is not <m>\\neg(P \\vee Q)</m>; the negation belongs to <m>P</m> alone.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe <term>main connective</term> here is the <m>\\vee</m>, which means we will use that truth table <em>last</em>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tFirst, we apply the truth table for <m>\\neg</m>, and then apply the truth table for <m>\\vee</m> using <q>inputs</q> from <m>\\neg P</m> and <m>Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:333
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSince there are two variables, there are four possible combinations of T's and F's.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tPutting this all together gives us the following truth table.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:343 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:526
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg P \\vee Q</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:376
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe added a column for <m>\\neg P</m> to make filling out the last column easier.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe entries in the <m>\\neg P</m> column were determined by the entries in the <m>P</m> column.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThen to fill in the final column, look only at the column for <m>Q</m> and the column for <m>\\neg P</m> and use the rule for <m>\\vee</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:384
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNow let's answer our question about Monopoly.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:390
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAnalyze the statement, <q>If you get more doubles than any other player, then you will lose, or if you lose, then you must have bought the most properties,</q> using truth tables.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:396
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tRepresent the statement in symbols as <m>(P \\imp Q) \\vee (Q \\imp R)</m>, where <m>P</m> is the statement, <q>You get more doubles than any other player,</q> <m>Q</m> is the statement, <q>You will lose,</q> and <m>R</m> is the statement, <q>You must have bought the most properties.</q> Now make a truth table.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:400
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe truth table must contain 8 rows to account for every possible combination of truth and falsity among the three statements.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tHere is the full truth table:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:409 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:891
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1409 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1429
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>R</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:410 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:525
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1231 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1315
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>P \\imp Q</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:411 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1404
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1431 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1529
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>Q \\imp R</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:412
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>(P \\imp Q) \\vee (Q \\imp R)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:489
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe first three columns are simply a systematic listing of all possible combinations of T and F for the three statements (do you see how you would list the 16 possible combinations for four statements?).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe next two columns are determined by the values of <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, and <m>R</m> and the definition of implication.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThen, the last column is determined by the values in the previous two columns and the definition of <m>\\vee</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIt is this final column we care about.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:496
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNotice that in each of the eight possible cases, the statement in question is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo our statement about monopoly is true (regardless of how many properties you own, how many doubles you roll, or whether you win or lose).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:504
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe statement about monopoly is an example of a <term>tautology</term>,\n"
"\t\t\ta statement that is necessarily true based on its logical form alone.\n"
"\t\t\tTautologies are always true, but they don't tell us much about the world.\n"
"\t\t\tNo knowledge about monopoly was required to determine that the statement was true, and thus knowing that the statement is true tells us nothing about monopoly.\n"
"\t\t\tIt is equally true that <q>if the moon is made of cheese, then Elvis is still alive, or if Elvis is still alive, then unicorns have 5 legs.</q>\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:514 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:564
#, no-wrap
msgid "Logical Equivalence"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:517
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tYou might have noticed in <xref po4a-id=0> that the final column in the truth table for <m>\\neg P \\vee Q</m> is identical to the final column in the truth table for <m>P \\imp Q</m>:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:559
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis says that no matter what <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are, the statements <m>\\neg P \\vee Q</m> and <m>P \\imp Q</m> are either both true or both false.\n"
"\t\t\tWe therefore say these statements are <term>logically equivalent</term>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:569
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTwo (molecular) statements <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are <term>logically equivalent</term>  provided <m>P</m> is true precisely when <m>Q</m> is true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThat is, <m> P</m> and <m>Q</m> have the same truth value under any assignment of truth values to their atomic parts.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:574
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe write this as <m>P \\equiv Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:580
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTo verify that two statements are logically equivalent, you can make a truth table for each and check whether the columns for the two statements are identical.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:584
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIn <xref po4a-id=0> we claimed that whenever an implication is true, so is its contrapositive.\n"
"\t\t\tWe can now make this claim as the following theorem.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:591
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAn implication is logically equivalent to its contrapositive.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThat is,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:595 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:771
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:410 source/sec_counting-non-disjoint.ptx:225
#: source/sec_counting-probability.ptx:377
#: source/sec_counting-probability.ptx:462
#: source/sec_counting-probability.ptx:478
#: source/sec_counting-probability.ptx:518
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe simply examine the truth tables.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:644 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:91
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:353 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:374
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg Q</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><proof><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:646
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg Q \\imp \\neg P</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t(Note that we have the truth value combinations in the same order in both tables, so we can easily see that the final columns are identical.)\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:690
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tRecognizing two statements as logically equivalent can be quite helpful.\n"
"\t\t\tRephrasing a mathematical statement can often lend insight into what it is saying, or how to prove or refute it.\n"
"\t\t\tBy using truth tables we can systematically verify that two statements are indeed logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:698
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAre the statements, <q>It will not rain or snow,</q> and, <q>It will not rain and it will not snow,</q> logically equivalent?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:704
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe want to know whether <m>\\neg(P \\vee Q)</m> is logically equivalent to <m>\\neg P \\wedge \\neg Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tMake a truth table which includes both statements:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:713
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg(P \\vee Q)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:714
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg P \\wedge \\neg Q</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:747
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSince the truth values for the two statements are equal in every row, the two statements are logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:753
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNotice that this example gives us a way to <q>distribute</q> a negation over a disjunction (an <q>or</q>).\n"
"\t\t\tWe have a similar rule for distributing over conjunctions (<q>and</q>s):\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:758
#, no-wrap
msgid "De Morgan's Laws"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:763
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe negation of a disjunction or conjunction is logically equivalent to a conjunction or disjunction of negations, respectively.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThat is,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:768
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tand,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:777
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis suggests there might be a sort of <q>algebra</q> you could apply to statements (okay, there is: It is called <em>Boolean algebra</em>) to transform one statement into another.\n"
"\t\t\tWe can start collecting useful examples of logical equivalence and apply them in succession to a statement, instead of writing out a complicated truth table.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:782
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tDe Morgan's laws do not directly help us with implications, but as we saw above, every implication can be written as a disjunction:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:786
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implications are Disjunctions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:797
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tExample: <q>If a number is a multiple of 4, then it is even</q> is equivalent to, <q>A number is not a multiple of 4, or (else) it is even.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:802
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWith this and De Morgan's laws, you can take any statement and <em>simplify</em> it to the point where negations are only being applied to atomic propositions.\n"
"\t\t\tWell, except that you could get multiple negations stacked up.\n"
"\t\t\tBut this can be easily dealt with:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:808
#, no-wrap
msgid "Double Negation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:818
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tExample: <q>It is not the case that <m>c</m> is not odd</q> means <q><m>c</m> is odd.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:823
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tLet's see how we can apply the equivalences we have encountered.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:829
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve that the statements <m>\\neg(P \\imp Q)</m> and <m>P\\wedge \\neg Q</m> are logically equivalent without using truth tables.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:835
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe want to start with one of the statements and transform it into the other through a sequence of logically equivalent statements.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tStart with <m>\\neg(P \\imp Q)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can rewrite the implication as a disjunction, so this is logically equivalent to\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:840
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tNow apply De Morgan's law to get\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:844
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tFinally, use double negation to arrive at <m>P \\wedge \\neg Q</m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:851
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNotice that the above example illustrates that the negation of an implication is NOT an implication: It is a conjunction! We saw this before, in <xref po4a-id=0>, but it is so important and useful, it warrants stating as a theorem.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:855
#, no-wrap
msgid "Negation of an Implication"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:859
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe negation of an implication is a conjunction:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:862
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThat is, the only way for an implication to be false is for the hypothesis to be true "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p><alert>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:863
#, no-wrap
msgid "AND"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:863
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" the conclusion to be false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:869
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTo verify that two statements are logically equivalent, you can use truth tables or a sequence of logically equivalent replacements.\n"
"\t\t\tThe truth table method, although cumbersome, has the advantage that it can verify that two statements are NOT logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:876
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAre the statements <m>(P \\vee Q) \\imp R</m> and <m>(P \\imp R) \\vee (Q \\imp R)</m> logically equivalent?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:882
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNote that while we could start rewriting these statements with logically equivalent replacements in the hopes of transforming one into another, we will never be sure that our failure is due to their lack of logical equivalence rather than our lack of imagination.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo instead, let's make a truth table:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:892
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>(P\\vee Q) \\imp R</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:893
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>(P\\imp R) \\vee (Q \\imp R)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:966
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLook at the fourth (or sixth) row.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn this case, <m>(P \\imp R) \\vee (Q \\imp R)</m> is true, but <m>(P \\vee Q) \\imp R</m> is false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tTherefore the statements are not logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:972
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhile we don't have logical equivalence, it is the case that whenever <m>(P \\vee Q) \\imp R</m> is true, so is <m>(P \\imp R) \\vee (Q \\imp R)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis tells us that we can <em>deduce</em> <m>(P \\imp R) \\vee (Q \\imp R)</m> from <m>(P \\vee Q) \\imp R</m>, just not the reverse direction.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:980
#, no-wrap
msgid "Equivalence for Quantified Statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:983
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAll the examples we have looked at so far have only involved <em>propositional</em> logic, where the basic units of logic are statements that are either true or false.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIt is also possible to say that two statements involving quantifiers and predicates are logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:988
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tSometimes the quantifiers have nothing to do with the equivalence.\n"
"\t\t\t\tFor example,\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:992
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\tAs soon as we replace the <m>x</m> with a constant, we are left with two statements that are logically equivalent based on their propositional form.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:997
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tOther times, the more interesting times, it is exactly the logic of the quantifiers that makes the statements logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWhat is especially interesting here is that we cannot use truth tables to verify these equivalences!\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1002
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tInstead, we need to reason about the domain of discourse as a set.\n"
"\t\t\t\tFor example, let's consider how negation interacts with quantifiers.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1007
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tConsider the claim that <q>all odd numbers are prime.</q>  We might represent this symbolically as <m>\\forall x (O(x) \\imp P(x))</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThe statement clearly is not true, so what <em>is</em> true is that <q>not all odd numbers are prime</q> (i.e., <m>\\neg\\forall x(O(x) \\imp P(x))</m>).\n"
"\t\t\t\tHow do we know?  Easy: 9.\n"
"\t\t\t\tYes, 9 is odd but not prime.\n"
"\t\t\t\tBut is it enough that just one odd number isn't prime?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1015
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tTo dispute a universal claim, you just need <em>one</em> single counterexample.\n"
"\t\t\t\tYou just need to show <em>there exists</em> a number for which the claim is false.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn our case, we have the equivalence,\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1020
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\tIf we ignore the quantifiers for a minute, we are left with\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1025
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\twhich is exactly an example of <xref po4a-id=0>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThe new, interesting part is that when we negated the universal quantifier, we got an existential quantifier.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1030
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tNegating an existential quantifier results in a universal quantifier.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThis makes sense.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIf there does not exist something with a property, then everything does not have that property.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1036
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantifiers and Negation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1042
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\neg \\forall x P(x)</m> is equivalent to <m>\\exists x \\neg P(x)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1046
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\neg \\exists x P(x)</m> is equivalent to <m>\\forall x \\neg P(x) </m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1052
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tSymbolically, we can pass the negation symbol over a quantifier, but that causes the quantifier to switch type.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1056
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAnother way to see why this makes sense: Universal quantifiers are like (possibly infinite) conjunctions since they claim that the property is true of this thing, and that thing, and the other thing,... all things. Existential quantifiers are like (possibly infinite) disjunctions: The property is true of at least one thing, maybe this, or that, or the other, or....\n"
"\t\t\t\tDe Morgan's laws tell us that when we negate a conjunction, we get a disjunction, and when we negate a disjunction, we get a conjunction.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIsn't it great when everything works out as it should?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1064
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSuppose we claim that there is no smallest number.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe can translate this into symbols as\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1068
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t(<q>It is not true that there is a number <m>x</m> such that for all numbers <m>y</m>, <m>x</m> is less than or equal to <m>y</m>.</q>)\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1073
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tHowever, we know how negation interacts with quantifiers: We can pass a negation over a quantifier by switching the quantifier type (between universal and existential).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo the statement above should be <em>logically equivalent</em> to\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1077
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tNotice that <m>y \\lt x</m> is the negation of <m>x \\le y</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThis reads, <q>For every number <m>x</m> there is a number <m> y</m> which is smaller than <m>x</m>.</q> We see that this is another way to make our original claim.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1085
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIt is important to stress that predicate logic <em>extends</em> propositional logic (much like how quantum mechanics extends classical mechanics).\n"
"\t\t\t\tEverything that we learned about logical equivalence and deductions still applies.\n"
"\t\t\t\tHowever, predicate logic allows us to analyze statements at a higher resolution, digging down into the individual propositions <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, etc.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1091
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tTo do this, we need to understand how quantifiers and connectives interact.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWe have already seen something about negations and quantifiers.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWhat about the other connectives?  Let's look at an example exploring how the universal quantifier and disjunctions can (or cannot) work together.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1100
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tConsider the two statements,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1103 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1158
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tAre these logically equivalent?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1110
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThese statements are NOT logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIntuitively, the statement on the left claims that everything is either a <m>P</m>-thing or a <m>Q</m>-thing.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThe statement on the right claims that either everything is a <m>P</m>-thing or that everything is a <m>Q</m>-thing.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThese <em>feel</em> different.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1117
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tTo be sure, we would like to think of predicates <m>P(x)</m> and <m>Q(x)</m> and some domain of discourse such that one of the statements is true and the other is false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tHow about we let <m>P(x)</m> be, <q><m>x</m> is even</q> and <m>Q(x)</m> be, <q><m>x</m> is odd.</q> Our domain of discourse will be all integers (as that is the set of numbers for which even and odd make sense).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1122
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThe statement on the left is true!  Every number is either even or odd.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tBut is every number even?  No.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIs every number odd?  No.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo the statement on the right is false (it is a <em>false or false</em>).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1129
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tInterestingly, the statement on the right implies the statement on the left.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThat is,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1134
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tis always true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThis is similar to a tautology, although we reserve that term for necessary truths in propositional logic.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tA statement in predicate logic that is necessarily true gets the more prestigious designation of a <term>law of logic</term>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1141
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t(or sometimes <term>logically valid</term>,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1142
#, no-wrap
msgid "law of logic"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tbut that is less fun).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1149
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tWe can also consider how quantifiers interact with each other.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1155
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tCan you switch the order of quantifiers? For example, consider the two statements:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1165
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThese statements are NOT logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tTo see this, we should provide an interpretation of the predicate <m>P(x,y)</m> which makes one of the statements true and the other false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1170
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>P(x,y)</m> be the predicate <m>x \\lt y</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIt is true, in the natural numbers, that for all <m>x</m> there is some <m>y</m> greater than that <m>x</m> (since there are infinitely many numbers).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tHowever, there is no natural number <m>y</m> which is greater than every number <m>x</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThus it is possible for <m>\\forall x \\exists y P(x,y)</m> to be true while <m>\\exists y \\forall x P(x,y)</m> is false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe cannot do the reverse of this though.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIf there is some <m>y</m> for which every <m>x</m> satisfies <m>P(x,y)</m>, then certainly for every <m>x</m> there is some <m>y</m> which satisfies <m>P(x,y)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThe first is saying we can find one <m>y</m> that works for every <m>x</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThe second allows different <m>y</m>'s to work for different <m>x</m>'s, but nothing is preventing us from using the same <m>y</m> that works for every <m>x</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIn other words, while we don't have logical equivalence between the two statements, we do have a valid deduction rule:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1187
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\exists y \\forall x P(x,y)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1191
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m> \\therefore</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1192
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\forall x \\exists y P(x,y)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1197
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tPut yet another way, this says that the single statement\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1201
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tis always true; it is a law of logic.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1209
#, no-wrap
msgid " Deductions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1212
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tEarlier, we claimed that the following was a valid argument:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1217
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIf Edith eats her vegetables, then she can have a cookie.\n"
"\t\t\t\tEdith ate her vegetables.\n"
"\t\t\t\tTherefore Edith gets a cookie.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1224
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHow do we know this is valid? Let's look at the form of the statements.\n"
"\t\t\tLet <m>P</m> denote, <q>Edith eats her vegetables</q> and <m>Q</m> denote, <q>Edith can have a cookie.</q> The logical form of the argument is then:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1240 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1324
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1413 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1538
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1561
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\therefore</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1246
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis is an example of a <term>deduction rule</term>,\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1248
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tan argument form that is always valid.\n"
"\t\t\tThis one is a particularly famous rule called "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><h><em>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1249 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1250
#, no-wrap
msgid "modus ponens"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1251
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAre you convinced that it is a valid deduction rule? If not, consider the following truth table:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1288
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis is just the truth table for <m>P \\imp Q</m>, but what matters here is that all the lines in the deduction rule have their own column in the truth table.\n"
"\t\t\tRemember that an argument is valid provided the conclusion must be true given that the premises are true.\n"
"\t\t\tThe premises in this case are <m>P \\imp Q</m> and <m>P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tWhich <em>rows</em> of the truth table correspond to both of these being true? <m>P</m> is true in the first two rows, and of those, only the first row has <m>P \\imp Q</m> true as well.\n"
"\t\t\tAnd lo-and-behold, in this one case, <m>Q</m> is also true.\n"
"\t\t\tSo if <m>P\\imp Q</m> and <m>P</m> are both true, we see that <m>Q</m> must be true as well.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1297
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThink of deduction rules as a sort of <em>one-way</em> form of logical equivalence.\n"
"\t\t\tTwo statements are logically equivalent provided that in every row of the truth table in which the first statement is true, so is the second, and in every row in which the second statement is true, so is the first.\n"
"\t\t\tA deduction only requires the first of these two parts.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1303
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHere are a few more examples.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1309
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tShow that the following is a valid deduction rule.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1320 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1342
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg P \\imp Q</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1325
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m> Q</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1332
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe make a truth table which contains all the lines of the argument form:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t(we include a column for <m>\\neg P</m> just as a helping step to get the column for <m>\\neg P \\imp Q</m>).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1383
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNow look at all the rows for which both <m>P \\imp Q</m> and <m>\\neg P \\imp Q</m> are true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis happens only in rows 1 and 3.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tHey! In those rows <m>Q</m> is true as well, so the argument form is valid (it is a valid deduction rule).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1393
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tDecide whether the following is a valid deduction rule.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1399 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1430
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1524
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>P \\imp R</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1414 source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1432
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1534
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>P \\vee Q</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1421
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLet's make a truth table containing all four statements.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1509
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLook at the second-to-last row.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tHere all three premises of the argument are true, but the conclusion is false.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThus this is not a valid deduction rule.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1515
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhile we have the truth table in front of us, look at rows 1, 3, and 5.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThese are the only rows in which all of the statements <m>P \\imp R</m>, <m>Q \\imp R</m>, and <m>P\\vee Q</m> are true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIt also happens that <m>R</m> is true in these rows as well.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThus we have discovered a new deduction rule we know <em>is</em> valid:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1539
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m> R</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><title>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1547
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantifier deductions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1550
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThere are also deduction rules we could write down for quantifiers.\n"
"\t\t\t\tFor example:\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1557
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\forall x P(x)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1562
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m> \\exists x P(x)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1567
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIf everything is a <m>P</m>-thing, then there must be something which is a <m>P</m>-thing.  These rules cannot be verified with a truth table, and a full treatment of this sort of predicate logic is beyond the scope of this text.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1584
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTo check whether two statements are logically equivalent, you can use a truth table.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tExplain what you would look for in the truth table to conclude that the two statements are logically equivalent.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat would tell you they are <em>not</em> logically equivalent?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1595
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTo check whether a deduction rule is <em>valid</em>, you can use a truth table.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tExplain what you would look for in the completed truth table to say that the deduction rule is valid, and what would tell you the deduction rule is <em>not</em> valid.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-rules.ptx:1605 source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1655
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat questions do you have after reading this section? Write at least one question about the content of this section that you are curious about.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:14
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Make a truth table for the statement <m>(P \\wedge Q) \\rightarrow (P \\vee Q)</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:20
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        If you think about what this statement is saying, it makes sense that it is a tautology (that it is true in every case).\n"
"        The complete truth table is:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>P \\wedge Q</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:30
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>P \\vee Q</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>(P \\wedge Q) \\rightarrow (P \\vee Q))</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:76
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Complete a truth table for the statement <m>\\neg Q \\vee (Q \\rightarrow P))</m>.\n"
"          What can you conclude about <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> if you knew the statement above was false?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:83
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The truth table is:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>Q \\rightarrow P</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:93
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg Q \\vee (Q \\rightarrow P))</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:130
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          If this statement is false, we must be in the third row, making <m>P</m> false and <m>Q</m> true.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:142
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Construct a truth table for the statement <m>Q \\rightarrow (\\neg P \\vee R)</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:148
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The complete truth table is:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:156 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:258
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:443 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:458
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:574 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:684
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:700 source/exercises/logic-conc.ptx:20
#: source/exercises/logic-conc.ptx:154
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>R</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:158
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg P \\vee R</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:159
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>Q \\rightarrow (\\neg P \\vee R)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Determine whether the statements <m>P \\rightarrow (Q \\vee R)</m> and <m>(P \\rightarrow Q) \\vee (P\\rightarrow R)</m> are logically equivalent by completing a truth table for both statements.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:250
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The complete truth table is:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:259 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:433
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:459
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>P \\rightarrow (Q \\vee R)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:260
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>(P \\rightarrow Q) \\vee (P\\rightarrow R)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:329
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Since the two columns are identical, the statements are logically equivalent.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:341
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Determine if the following is a valid deduction rule:\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:348 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:373
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:674 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:701
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>P \\rightarrow Q</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:365
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                The complete truth table is:\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:412
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                There is only one row in which both premises are true (row 4).\n"
"                In this row, the conclusion is also true.\n"
"                Thus the deduction rule is valid.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:426
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  Determine if the following is a valid deduction rule:\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:438 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:460
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg(P \\rightarrow Q)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:450
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  The complete truth table is:\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:529
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  There is only one row in which both premises are true (row 3).\n"
"                  In this row, the conclusion is also true, so the deduction rule is valid.\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:542
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                    Determine if the following is a valid deduction rule:\n"
"                  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:549 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:575
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>(P \\wedge Q) \\rightarrow R</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:554 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:576
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg P \\vee \\neg Q</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:559 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:577
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\neg R</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:566 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:692
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                    The complete truth table is:\n"
"                  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:654
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                    In rows 3, 5 and 7 both of the premises are true, but the conclusion is false.\n"
"                    Thus the deduction rule is not valid.\n"
"                  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:667
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                      Determine if the following is a valid deduction rule:\n"
"                    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:679 source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:702
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>P \\wedge \\neg Q</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:771
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                    There is no row in which both premises are true (indeed, these are contradictory premises; the second is the negation of the first).\n"
"                    Thus every row in which both premises are true (i.e., no row), the conclusion is also true.\n"
"                    Therefore the deduction rule is valid.\n"
"                    (This is an example of how everything follows from a contradiction.)\n"
"                  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:782
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                    Which of the following statements is a <em>law of logic</em>?  That is, which of the following are true no matter what your domain of discourse is and no matter what you interpret the predicates as meaning?  Select all that apply.\n"
"                  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:791
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                        <m>\\forall x (P(x) \\vee \\neg P(x))</m>.\n"
"                      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:799
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                        <m>\\exists x P(x) \\imp \\forall x P(x)</m>.\n"
"                      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:807
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                        <m>\\neg\\forall x P(x) \\imp \\exists x P(x)</m>.\n"
"                      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-rules.ptx:815
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                        <m>\\forall x \\exists y P(x,y) \\iff \\exists y \\forall x P(x,y)</m>.\n"
"                      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:12
#, no-wrap
msgid "        You stumble upon two trolls playing Stratego"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:12
#, no-wrap
msgid ". They tell you: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><blockquote><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Troll 1: If we are cousins, then we are both knaves.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><blockquote><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:20
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Troll 2: We are cousins, or we are both knaves.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:25
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Could both trolls be knights?\n"
"        Recall that all trolls are either always-truth-telling knights or always-lying knaves.  Explain your answer and how you can use truth tables to find it.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:31
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        You could probably reason through the cases by hand, but try making a truth table.  Use two statements, <m>P</m> being <q>we are cousins</q> and <m>Q</m> being <q>we are both knaves</q>.  \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:39
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Next you come upon three trolls, helpfully wearing name tags.\n"
"          They say:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:43
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pat"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:45
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If either Quinn or I are knights, then so is Ryan.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:50
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quinn"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:52
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Ryan is a knight, and if Pat is a knight, then so am I.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:57
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ryan"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:59
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Quinn is a knave, but Pat and I share the same persuasion.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Create a truth table that includes all three statements.  Then use the truth table to determine the persuasion of each troll.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:70
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Consider the statement about a party, <q>If it's your birthday or there will be cake,\n"
"          then there will be cake.</q>\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:75
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Translate the above statement into symbols. Clearly state\n"
"        which statement is <m>P</m> and which is <m>Q</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:81
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Make a truth table for the statement.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:87
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Assuming the statement is true, what\n"
"              (if anything)\n"
"              can you conclude if you know there will be cake?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:95
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Assuming the statement is true, what\n"
"              (if anything)\n"
"              can you conclude if you know there will not be cake?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:103
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you found out that the statement was a lie.\n"
"              What can you conclude?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:114
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            <m>\n"
"            P</m>: It's your birthday; <m>Q</m>: There will be cake. <m>(P \\vee Q) \\imp Q</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:120
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Hint: You should get three T's and one F.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:126
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Only that there will be cake.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:132
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              It's NOT your birthday!\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              It's your birthday, but the cake is a lie.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:150
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Geoff Poshingten is out at a fancy pizza joint and decides to order a calzone. When the\n"
"        waiter asks what he would like in it, he replies, <q>I want either pepperoni or sausage.\n"
"          Also, if I have sausage, then I must also include quail.\n"
"          Oh, and if I have pepperoni or quail, then I must also have ricotta cheese.</q>\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:158
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Translate Geoff's order into logical symbols.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:164
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The waiter knows that Geoff is either a liar or a truth-teller\n"
"              (so either everything he says is false, or everything is true).\n"
"              Which is it?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:172
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        What, if anything,\n"
"              can the waiter conclude about the ingredients in Geoff's desired calzone?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:181
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        You should write down three statements using the symbols <m>P, Q, R, S</m>. If Geoff is a\n"
"        truth-teller, then all three statements would be true. If he was a liar, then all three\n"
"        statements would be false. But in either case, we don't yet know whether the four atomic\n"
"        statements are true or false, since he hasn't said them by themselves. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:187
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A truth table might help, although it is probably not entirely necessary.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:195
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Determine whether the following two statements are logically equivalent: <m>\\neg(P \\imp Q)</m>\n"
"        and <m>P \\wedge \\neg Q</m>. Explain how you know you are correct. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:200
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Make a truth table for each and compare.\n"
"        The statements are logically equivalent.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:210
#, no-wrap
msgid "        Simplify the following statements (so that negation only appears right before variables). "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:211
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>\\neg(P\n"
"        \\imp \\neg Q)</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:215
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            <m>(\\neg P \\vee\n"
"              \\neg Q) \\imp \\neg (\\neg Q \\wedge R)</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:219
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            <m>\\neg((P \\imp \\neg Q) \\vee \\neg (R \\wedge\n"
"        \\neg\n"
"              R))</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:225
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              It is false that if Sam is not a man then Chris is a woman,\n"
"              and that Chris is not a woman.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:235
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P\n"
"            \\wedge Q</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:239
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            <m>(\\neg P \\vee \\neg R) \\imp (Q \\vee \\neg R)</m> or, replacing the\n"
"            implication with a disjunction first: <m>(P \\wedge Q) \\vee (Q \\vee \\neg R)</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>(P\n"
"              \\wedge Q) \\wedge (R \\wedge \\neg R)</m>. This is necessarily false, so it is also\n"
"              equivalent to <m>P \\wedge \\neg P</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:251
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Either Sam is a woman and Chris is a man, or Chris is a woman.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-rules.ptx:262
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Use De Morgan's Laws and any other logical equivalence facts you know to simplify the\n"
"        following statements. Show all your steps. Your final statements should have negations only\n"
"        appear directly next to the sentence variables or predicates (<m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, <m>E(x)</m>, etc.),\n"
"        and no double negations. It would be a good idea to use only conjunctions, disjunctions, and\n"
"        negations. "
