#. type: Content of: <appendix><section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:4 source/peer-instruction.ptx:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "Proofs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:15
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIdentify the logical structure of, and distinguish between, <em>direct proofs</em>, a <em>proof by contrapositives</em>, and a <em>proof by contradictions</em>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:21
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIdentify flaws in an incorrect proof and determine whether they are flaws in logic or mathematical concepts.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:27
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tApply definitions to prove statements using basic proof styles.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:38
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tA <term>mini sudoku puzzle</term> is a <m>4\\times 4</m> grid of squares, divided into four <m>2\\times 2</m> boxes.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThe goal is to fill each square with a digit from 1 to 4, such that no digit repeats in any row, any column, or any box.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:43
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHere is a simple mini sudoku puzzle you can try to solve.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:48 source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t4x4 sudoku with given digits.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tYou might notice that the solution to the above puzzle has its four outside corners all different, and its four middle squares all different.  \n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:67
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe goal of this <em>Investigate!</em> question is to prove that this is not a coincidence:  Suppose a mini sudoku puzzle has all different numbers in its four corners (marked with # below).\n"
"\t\t\t\tProve that the center four squares (marked with * below) must also contain different numbers.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:91
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTry placing numbers into an empty mini sudoku puzzle.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSee if you can break the statement we were asked to prove in the <em>Investigate!</em> activity.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat stops you?  Briefly explain whether you think the statement is true or false, and why.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:101
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAnyone who doesn't believe there is creativity in mathematics clearly has not tried to write proofs.\n"
"\t\t\tFinding a way to convince the world that a particular statement is necessarily true is a mighty undertaking and can often be quite challenging.\n"
"\t\t\tThere is no guaranteed path to success in the search for proofs.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, in the summer of 1742, a German mathematician by the name of Christian Goldbach wondered whether every even integer greater than 2 could be written as the sum of two primes.\n"
"\t\t\tCenturies later, we still don't have a proof of this apparent fact (computers have checked that Goldbach's conjecture holds for all numbers less than <m>4\\times 10^{18}</m>, but no proof that the statement holds for <em>all</em> numbers has been found).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#.  A good place to start might be to study a classic.
#. 			
#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:109
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWriting proofs is a bit of an art.\n"
"\t\t\tLike any art, to be truly great at it, you need some sort of inspiration, as well as some foundational technique.\n"
"\t\t\tJust as musicians can learn proper fingering, and painters can learn the proper way to hold a brush, we can look at the proper way to construct arguments.\n"
"\t\t\t\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:229
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe can view a proof through two distinct but intersecting lenses.\n"
"\t\t\tFirst, we can think about the <em>logical</em> structure of a proof.\n"
"\t\t\tBelow we will consider three styles of proof that vary precisely in their logical structure.\n"
"\t\t\tSecond, we can look at the <em>mathematical content</em> of the proof.\n"
"\t\t\tHow does the proof illustrate understanding of mathematical concepts?  Does it use definitions of mathematical objects correctly?  How do the definitions interact with each other?\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:238
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tRecall that in <xref po4a-id=0> we said that a <term>tautology</term> is a necessarily true statement, but that it doesn't tell us anything interesting.\n"
"\t\t\tSimilarly, if a proof relied entirely on the logical form of the statement it was proving, it wouldn't tell us anything interesting about mathematics.\n"
"\t\t\tThus all the proofs we consider <em>must</em> involve some combining of mathematical concepts in addition to their logical structure.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIn this section, we will see examples of how the interaction between logical and mathematical structure plays out.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will think of the logical structure as the <em>skeleton</em> or <em>scaffolding</em> of the proof and look at the different shapes this skeleton can take.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will then see how the mathematical content of the proof fills in the details of the skeleton, how it adds meat to the bones.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:250
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIt is often challenging to be careful about proofs when the statements we try to prove seem too obvious or familiar.\n"
"\t\t\tWhile we will definitely want to prove simple facts about numbers, like that the sum of two even numbers is even, our familiarity with numbers can make it difficult to take this task seriously.\n"
"\t\t\tSo instead, we will start by proving some facts in what is hopefully a novel setting: mini sudoku puzzles.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:264
#, no-wrap
msgid "Direct Proof"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:268
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe simplest style of proof is <term>direct proof</term>.\n"
"\t\t\tOften all that is required to prove something is a systematic explanation of what everything means.\n"
"\t\t\tYou look at the definitions, carefully explain and <em>unpack</em> their meaning, until you see that the conclusion is true.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:274
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTo illustrate the importance of definitions in a proof, let's give a few careful definitions about mini sudoku puzzles.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:278
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mini Sudoku Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:282
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tA <term>mini sudoku puzzle</term> is a partially filled in <m>4\\times 4</m> grid of squares, divided into four <m>2\\times 2</m> boxes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tEach square can be empty or contain a digit from 1 to 4.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:287
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe say that a mini sudoku puzzle is <term>valid</term> provided no digit from 1 to 4 appears more than once in any row, any column, or any box.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:291
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tA <term>solution</term> to a mini sudoku puzzle is a valid puzzle with no empty squares and every non-empty square of the puzzle unchanged.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:295
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe say that a mini sudoku puzzle is <term>solvable</term> if there is exactly one solution.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:301
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tFirst, let's prove a useful and <q>obvious</q> fact about any valid mini sudoku puzzle.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proposition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:308
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAny solution to a mini sudoku puzzle will have each digit from 1 to 4 appear exactly once in each row, in each column, and in each box, appearing a total of four times.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:314
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThat's obvious, you say!  Isn't that exactly what a valid puzzle is?  Well, a valid completed puzzle, which is what we mean by a solution, right?  Okay, not exactly, since valid means that no digit repeats...\n"
"\t\t\tisn't that the same thing?\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:319
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tYes!  Exactly! Saying this is a direct proof.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p><fn>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:326
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition of <term>solution</term>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p><fn>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:326
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition of <term>valid</term>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:325
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tSuppose you have a solution to a mini sudoku puzzle.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThat means that you have a <m>4 \\times 4</m> grid with each square filled in with a digit from 1 to 4 that is valid.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/> Since the puzzle is <em>valid</em>, no digit repeats in any row, any column, or any box<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"1\"/>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tSince a row contains four numbers that do not repeat, and there are exactly four possible digits, each of those digits must appear exactly once.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThis is true for every row, and for every column, and for every box.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:332
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tFor each digit, since it appears exactly once in four different rows, it appears exactly four times.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThis completes the proof.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><remark><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:339
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe proof contained one key mathematical idea besides just explaining definitions: If four distinct numbers are chosen from a set of four numbers, then all four numbers are chosen.\n"
"\t\t\t\tPerhaps you want to also explain why this is true, or just say it is an example of the pigeonhole principle (we will say what this is soon).\n"
"\t\t\t\tHow much you explain depends on who you are writing the proof for.\n"
"\t\t\t\tA little paranoia when writing proofs is healthy.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><remark><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:346
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIndeed, in most contexts, we wouldn't even need to write out any of the above proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIt would probably be sufficient to say, <q>Clearly this follows from the definitions.</q>  However, we are currently trying to learn how to write proofs, and it can be useful to be overly pedantic so we can focus on the proof structure and the importance of applying definitions.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:352
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tLet's prove something about a particular sudoku puzzle.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:358
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve that for any solution to the mini sudoku puzzle below, if the solution has a 2 in the top-left square (r1c1), then it will contain a 2 in the bottom-right square (r4c4).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:363
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tA mini sudoku puzzle.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:380
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe do not know whether the puzzle is solvable (in fact, it is not), although note that it is valid right now.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat we want to prove is that <em>if</em> 2 is in the top-left square in any particular solution, <em>then</em> that solution contains a 2 in the bottom-right square.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:387
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>S</m> be a solution to the puzzle, and assume that <m>S</m> contains a 2 in the top-left square.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince <m>S</m> is a valid puzzle, no digit repeats in any row, any column, or any box.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLook first at the top row.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince the row already contains 1, 2, and 3, the remaining open square (r1c4) must be a 4.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:394
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tNow look at column 4 (the right-most column).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince we now know the top-right square is a 4, this column already contains 1, 3, and 4.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo the last open square (r4c4) must be a 2.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:400
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThus <m>S</m> contains a 2 in the bottom-right square, which is what we needed to prove.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:407
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tObserve the general form of the argument above.\n"
"\t\t\tWe were trying to prove an implication <m>P \\imp Q</m>: If there was a 2 in r1c1, then there was a 2 in r4c4.\n"
"\t\t\tWe started by assuming <m>P</m> was true.\n"
"\t\t\tFrom that, we deduced something, and from that something we deduced <m>Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThis is exactly what a direct proof of an implication <m>P \\imp Q</m> looks like (we could have had more steps between the <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> as well).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:416
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAssume <m>P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tExplain, explain, "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:417
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
", explain.\n"
"\t\t\t\tTherefore <m>Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:424
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe one additional consideration we must make is that often we are proving a general, universal statement, a statement of the form <m>\\forall x (P(x) \\imp Q(x))</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tTo handle the quantifier, we fix an <em>arbitrary</em> instance of <m>x</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tAbove, we said, <q>Let <m>S</m> be a solution to the puzzle.</q>  Since we made no additional assumptions about <m>S</m> besides that <m>P</m> was true about it, we say that <m>S</m> was an <em>arbitrary</em> solution.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:430
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIf we wanted to prove that all squares are rectangles, we first realize that this is the same as saying, <q>For any shape, if the shape is a square, then it is a rectangle.</q>  In symbols, <m>\\forall x (S(x) \\imp R(x))</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will want to assume <m>P(x)</m> is true and deduce <m>Q(x)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tWhich <m>x</m> do we use?  An arbitrary one, so our proof can be applied to <em>all</em> possible <m>x</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:438
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve that for any mini sudoku puzzle with three empty squares, if the puzzle has a solution, then the puzzle is solvable.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:444
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIs this obvious?  If a puzzle has a solution, then it is solvable, right?  Not at all!  Look again at the <xref po4a-id=0>: Just because a puzzle has a solution doesn't mean that it has exactly one solution (i.e.,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tit is solvable).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBut even if we don't think this needs a proof, let's be paranoid again and use this as an excuse to focus on the logical structure of the proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:450
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNotice that we are proving that the claim is true no matter what mini sudoku puzzle we start with.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe might start with this puzzle:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:456
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t4x4 sudoku with only one missing digit.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:471
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tClearly there is only one way to complete the puzzle: The top row has only one open square, so we can only put one digit in it, and then columns 2 and 3 only have one open square each, so we can fill those uniquely.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:475
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\t\tLooking at a single example can often be helpful when crafting a proof, but proving a general statement with an example is "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><alert>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:475
#, no-wrap
msgid "NEVER"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:475
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" a correct proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:479
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhile there are <em>only</em> around 152.58 billion mini sudoku puzzles (and most of those are not valid, fewer have solutions, and even fewer have exactly one empty square), we don't really want to check all possible puzzles.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo instead, we fix an arbitrary valid mini sudoku puzzle.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe assume that it has a solution and has exactly three open squares.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tFrom this, we prove that there is only one possible solution.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:488
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>P</m> be an arbitrary mini sudoku puzzle.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tAssume <m>P</m> has exactly three empty squares and that <m>S</m> is a solution.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:493
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince <m>P</m> is arbitrary, we don't know how the three empty squares are arranged.  They could all be in different rows, or two could be in the same row, or all three could be in the same row.  \n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:497
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIf all the empty squares are in different rows, then in each row, there is exactly one empty square.  The other three squares are filled with three different digits (since the puzzle is valid), so there is only one choice to fill the empty square.  This number must be the number used in the solution <m>S</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:501
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tNow consider the case where two of the empty squares are in the same row, and the third square is in a different row.  The third empty square's row has three different digits, so there is only one choice for the last square, and it must agree with <m>S</m>.  Once this is filled in, the other two empty squares must be in two different columns, each of which has three filled-in digits.  In each of these columns, the three filled-in digits are different, so there is only one choice for the empty square.  So again, any solution must be exactly <m>S</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:505
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tFinally, if all three empty squares are in the same row, then they are all in different columns.  So using the same argument as we did when the empty squares were in different rows, but using columns instead, we see that <m>S</m> is the only solution.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:509
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe have considered all possible cases, and in each case, <m>S</m> is the only solution, so <m>P</m> is solvable.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:516
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tDirect proof can, of course, be used to prove statements in mathematics too.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:522
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve: For all integers <m>n</m>, if <m>n</m> is even, then <m>n^2</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:528
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe format of the proof will be this: Let <m>n</m> be an arbitrary integer.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAssume that <m>n</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tExplain explain explain.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tTherefore <m>n^2</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:535
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTo fill in the details, we explain what it means for <m>n</m> to be even, and then see what that means for <m>n^2</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe <em>definition</em> that is relevant here is, <q>An integer <m>n</m> is <term>even</term> if there is an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>n = 2k</m>.</q> Here is a complete proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:542
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>n</m> be an arbitrary integer.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSuppose <m>n</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThen <m>n = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tNow <m>n^2 = (2k)^2 = 4k^2 = 2(2k^2)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince <m>2k^2</m> is an integer, <m>n^2</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:555
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve: For all integers <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>, if <m>b</m> is a multiple of <m>a</m>, and <m>c</m> is a multiple of <m>b</m>, then <m>c</m> is a multiple of <m>a</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:561
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tEven if we don't remember exactly what <q>is a multiple of</q> means, we can set up a direct proof for this statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIt will go something like this: Let <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> be arbitrary integers.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAssume that <m>b</m> is a multiple of <m>a</m> and that <m>c</m> is a multiple of <m>b</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tDot dot dot.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tTherefore <m>c</m> is a multiple of <m>a</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:569
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow do we connect the dots? We say what our hypothesis really means and why this gives us what the conclusion really means.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis is where we need the definition of <term><m>b</m> is a multiple of <m>a</m></term>: This means that <m>b = ka</m> for some integers <m>k</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat are we going for? That <m>c = la</m>, for some integer <m>l</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tHere is the complete proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:578
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> be integers.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tAssume that <m>b</m> is a multiple of <m>a</m> and that <m>c</m> is a multiple of <m>b</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo there are integers <m>k</m> and <m>j</m> such that <m>b = ka</m> and <m>c = jb</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tCombining these (through substitution) we get that\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:584
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tBut <m>jk</m> is an integer, so this says that <m>c</m> is a multiple of <m>a</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:594
#, no-wrap
msgid "Proof by Contrapositive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:600
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tRecall that an implication <m>P \\imp Q</m> is logically equivalent to its contrapositive <m>\\neg Q \\imp \\neg P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThere are plenty of examples of statements that are hard to prove directly, but whose contrapositive can easily be proved using a direct proof.\n"
"\t\t\tThis is all that <term>proof by contrapositive</term> does.\n"
"\t\t\tIt gives a direct proof of the contrapositive of the implication.\n"
"\t\t\tThis is enough because the contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original implication.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:608
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe skeleton of the proof of <m>P \\imp Q</m> by contrapositive will always look roughly like this:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:613
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAssume <m>\\neg Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tExplain, explain, "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:614
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" explain.\n"
"\t\t\t\tTherefore <m>\\neg P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:620
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAs before, if there are variables and quantifiers, we set them to be arbitrary elements of our domain.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:626
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve that if a mini sudoku puzzle is solvable, then it is valid.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:632
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tRemember, a puzzle is valid provided no row, column, or box contains a repeated digit.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tA puzzle is solvable if there is exactly one solution, where a solution is a valid puzzle with no empty squares (that doesn't change any previously filled-in square).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:637
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIf we try a direct proof, we would start with an arbitrary puzzle and assume it is solvable.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe could then <q>get</q> the solution, but would need to reason back in time to when the puzzle started.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis seems hard.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOften, when a proof seems to require breaking things apart, it is easier to try the contrapositive.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThat's what we will do.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:647
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>P</m> be an arbitrary mini sudoku puzzle and assume that it is <em>not</em> valid.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThis means that in at least one row, or one column, or one box, some digit appears more than once.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:652
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tNow suppose we have filled in the empty squares but not changed any previously filled-in squares.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThe original row, column, or box that contained a duplicate digit will still contain that duplication, so the resulting completed puzzle will not be valid.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThus no solution to <m>P</m> exists, so <m>P</m> is not solvable.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:659
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe have proved that if a mini sudoku puzzle is not valid, then it is not solvable, which is the contrapositive of what we wanted to prove and so serves as a proof of the original statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:665
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHere are a couple more mathy examples.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:671
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIs the statement, <q>For all integers <m>n</m>, if <m>n^2</m> is even, then <m>n</m> is even,</q> true?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:677
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThis is the converse of the statement we proved in <xref po4a-id=0> above using a direct proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tFrom trying a few examples, this statement appears to be true.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo let's prove it.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:683
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tA direct proof of this statement would require fixing an arbitrary <m>n</m> and assuming that <m>n^2</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBut it is not at all clear how this would allow us to conclude anything about <m>n</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tJust because <m>n^2 = 2k</m> does not in itself suggest how we could write <m>n</m> as a multiple of 2.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:689
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTry something else: Write the contrapositive of the statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe get, for all integers <m>n</m>, if <m>n</m> is odd, then <m>n^2</m> is odd.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis looks much more promising.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:695
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe need a definition of a number being odd. An integer <m>n</m> is <term>odd</term> provided <m>n = 2k+1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOur proof will look something like this:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:700
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>n</m> be an arbitrary integer.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSuppose that <m>n</m> is not even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis means that "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:702
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn other words "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBut this is the same as saying "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:704
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tTherefore <m>n^2</m> is not even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:709
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNow we fill in the details.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:715
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe will prove the contrapositive.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m> n</m> be an arbitrary integer.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSuppose that <m>n</m> is not even, and thus odd.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThen <m>n= 2k+1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tNow <m>n^2 = (2k+1)^2 = 4k^2 + 4k + 1 = 2(2k^2 + 2k) + 1</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince <m>2k^2 + 2k</m> is an integer, we see that <m>n^2</m> is odd and therefore not even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:729
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve that for all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, if <m>a + b</m> is odd, then <m>a</m> is odd or <m>b</m> is odd.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:735
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe problem with trying a direct proof is that it will be hard to separate <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> from knowing something about <m>a+b</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOn the other hand, if we know something about <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> separately, then combining them might give us information about <m>a+b</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe contrapositive of the statement we are trying to prove is: for all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, if <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are even, then <m>a+b</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThus our proof will have the following format:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:742
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAssume that <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are both even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tla la la.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tTherefore <m>a+b</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:749
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHere is a complete proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:755
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tAssume that <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThen <m>a = 2k</m> and <m>b = 2l</m> for some integers <m>k</m> and <m>l</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tNow <m>a + b = 2k + 2l = 2(k+l)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince <m>k + l</m> is an integer, we see that <m>a + b</m> is even, completing the proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:764
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNote that our assumption that <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are even is the negation of <m>a</m> or <m>b</m> is odd.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe used De Morgan's law here.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:771
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tDirect proofs and proofs by contrapositive can be used when proving <em>implications</em>.\n"
"\t\t\tRemember that some statements that are not explicitly written as an implication can be rephrased as one.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:778
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider the statement, <q>For every prime number <m>p</m>, either <m>p = 2</m>, or <m>p</m> is odd.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can rephrase this as, <q>For every prime number <m>p</m>, if <m>p \\ne 2</m>, then <m>p</m> is odd.</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tNow try to prove it.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:784
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tUse the following as the definition of a prime number: An integer <m>p \\gt 1</m> is <term>prime</term> provided it has exactly two factors, namely <m>1</m> and <m>p</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:791
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>p</m> be an arbitrary prime number.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tAssume <m>p</m> is not odd.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo <m>p</m> is divisible by 2.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince <m>p</m> is prime, it must have exactly two divisors, and it has 2 as a divisor, so <m>p</m> must be divisible by only 1 and 2.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tTherefore <m>p = 2</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThis completes the proof (by contrapositive).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:805
#, no-wrap
msgid "Proof by Contradiction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:810
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTake a step back and consider what it would mean if the conclusion of an argument were false.\n"
"\t\t\tThere are two reasons this could happen.\n"
"\t\t\tEither the logic in the argument is faulty, or at least one assumption must be false.\n"
"\t\t\tA <term>proof by contradiction</term> exploits this second possibility.\n"
"\t\t\tWe start with a single assumption and construct a <em>valid</em> proof that leads to a false conclusion; the only possibility is that the single assumption was false.\n"
"\t\t\tThe false conclusion is the <q>contradiction,</q> which just means a necessarily false statement (technically a statement of the form <m>P \\wedge \\neg P</m>).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:819
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe general form of a proof by contradiction to prove a statement <m>P</m> is,\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:824
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tAssume <m>\\neg P</m> (that <m>P</m> is not true).\n"
"\t\t\t\tThis means that..., which tells us..., so we can say...\n"
"\t\t\t\tBut that is a contradiction, so <m>P</m> must in fact be true.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:831
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNote that if we think of this style of argument as a direct proof of something, it is a direct proof of\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:834
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\tSo we have a valid proof of this implication.\n"
"\t\t\tHow can a true implication have a false conclusion?  Recall the truth table for an implication (<xref po4a-id=0>).\n"
"\t\t\tThe only row in which the implication is true but the conclusion is false is row 4, and here the hypothesis is also false.\n"
"\t\t\tSo <m>\\neg P</m> is false, which is to say <m>P</m> is true.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:842
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOnce you start writing proofs by contradiction, it becomes very natural.\n"
"\t\t\tLet's see how with a sudoku proof.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:849
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve that any solution to the mini sudoku puzzle below must contain a 3 in the top-right corner (r1c4).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:854
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t4x4 sudoku puzzle.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:871
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThere are multiple ways we could prove this, which is often the case for proofs by contradiction.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhile we would always start with the same initial assumption (the opposite of what we want to prove), where the contradiction is found can vary.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:878
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>S</m> be a solution to the puzzle, but assume that <m>S</m> does <em>not</em> contain a 3 in the top-right corner.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThen the top row must contain a 3 somewhere else.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIt cannot be in column 1, since it already has a 3.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIt cannot be in column 2, since that square is already filled in (with the 2).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tIt also cannot be in column 3, since if it was, there would be no place to put a three in the bottom row.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo there would be no 3 in the top row, contradicting that <m>S</m> is a solution.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:887
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tTherefore <m>S</m> must contain a 3 in the top-right corner.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:896
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve that there is no solution to the sudoku puzzle below.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:899
#, no-wrap
msgid "A mini sudoku puzzle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:914
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\t\tLook at the logical format of this statement: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:914
#, no-wrap
msgid ".  Using the rules for negation of quantifiers, this is the same as "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:914
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIf we were to prove this directly, we would need to consider all possible solutions and show that they are not valid.  That seems quite challenging.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:919
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tOn the other hand, if we try a proof by contradiction, we get to assume the negation of the statement we are asked to prove.  That is, we can assume that there <em>is</em> a solution.  That is a single solution we can reason about.  Much more manageable.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:924
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSuppose, for the sake of contradiction, that there <em>does</em> exist a solution to the puzzle.  This solution must have a 4 in the bottom-left box.  But the only squares that can hold a 4 are either in row 4 or column 1 (or both).  In either case, this contradicts that there is a 4 already in column 1 and row 4.  \n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:931
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHere are three examples of proofs by contradiction about numbers:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:937
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve that <m>\\sqrt{2}</m> is irrational.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:944
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSuppose not.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThen <m>\\sqrt 2</m> is equal to a fraction <m> \\frac{a}{b}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWithout loss of generality, assume <m>\\frac{a}{b}</m> is in lowest terms (otherwise reduce the fraction).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:953 source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:965
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:957
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThus <m>a^2</m> is even, and as such <m>a</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo <m>a = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>, and <m>a^2 = 4k^2</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe then have,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:969
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThus <m>b^2</m> is even, and as such <m>b</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSince <m>a</m> is also even, we see that <m>\\frac{a}{b}</m> is not in lowest terms, a contradiction.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThus <m>\\sqrt 2</m> is irrational.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:980
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tProve: There are no integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>x^2 = 4y + 2</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:987
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe proceed by contradiction.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo suppose there <em>are</em> integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>x^2 = 4y + 2 = 2(2y + 1)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo <m>x^2</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe have seen that this implies that <m>x</m> is even.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSo <m>x = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThen <m>x^2 = 4k^2</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThis in turn gives <m>2k^2 = (2y + 1)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tBut <m>2k^2</m> is even, and <m>2y + 1</m> is odd, so these cannot be equal.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThus we have a contradiction, so there must not be any integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>x^2 = 4y + 2</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1004
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe <term>Pigeonhole Principle</term>:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1006
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIf more than <m>n</m> pigeons fly into <m>n</m> pigeonholes, then at least one pigeonhole will contain at least two pigeons.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tProve this!\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1014
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tSuppose, contrary to stipulation, that each of the pigeonholes contains at most one pigeon.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThen at most, there will be <m>n</m> pigeons.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tBut we assumed that there are more than <m>n</m> pigeons, so this is impossible.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThus there must be a pigeonhole with more than one pigeon.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1022
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhile we phrased this proof as a proof by contradiction, we could have also used a proof by contrapositive since our contradiction was simply the negation of the hypothesis.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSometimes this will happen, in which case you can use either style of proof.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThere are examples, however, where the contradiction occurs <q>far away</q> from the original statement.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1032
#, no-wrap
msgid "Summary of Proof Styles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1035
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe have considered three styles of proof: direct proof, proof by contrapositive, and proof by contradiction.\n"
"\t\t\tIt can be challenging to decide which style of proof to use on a given problem, and no rule will always tell us what to do.\n"
"\t\t\tOften, there are multiple ways you can proceed in a proof, which is one reason math is so exciting.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1041
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA good starting point when writing proofs is to consider what the initial assumption would be with each style, and what the conclusion you would be looking for is.\n"
"\t\t\tA proof is a little like a kids-menu maze.\n"
"\t\t\tThere is a <em>START</em> and an <em>EXIT</em>, and your goal is to find your way from one to the other.\n"
"\t\t\tSometimes it helps to work your way in from both sides and hopefully meet in the middle.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1048
#, no-wrap
msgid "Starts and Ends Proofs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1051
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\tTo prove an implication <m>P \\imp Q</m>: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1053
#, no-wrap
msgid "Direct"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1056
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tStart: Assume <m>P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1060
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tEnd: Therefore <m>Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1068
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tStart: Assume <m>\\neg Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1072
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tEnd: Therefore <m>\\neg P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1077
#, no-wrap
msgid "Contradiction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1080
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tStart: Assume <m>\\neg (P \\imp Q)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1084
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tEnd: ...which is a contradiction.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1092
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tYou can use a proof by contradiction even if you are not trying to prove an implication, but if the statement is an implication, then assuming <m>\\neg (P \\imp Q)</m> is really powerful.\n"
"\t\t\tRemember, the only way for an implication to be false is for <m>P</m> to be true and <m>Q</m> to be false.\n"
"\t\t\tSo we are actually assuming <m>P \\wedge \\neg Q</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tAha!  <m>P</m> is what we assume in a direct proof.\n"
"\t\t\t<m>\\neg Q</m> is what we assume in a proof by contrapositive.\n"
"\t\t\tSo a <em>proof by contradiction is like doing the other two proofs at the same time, and meeting in the middle!</em>\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1101
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tTo illustrate this, let's prove the fact from the <em>Investigate!</em> activity.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will prove that if a mini sudoku puzzle has all different numbers in its corners (marked with <m>a</m> through <m>d</m> below), then the center four squares (marked with * below) must also contain different numbers (in any solution).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t4x4 sudoku with given digits.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1122
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe will give three proofs, first a direct proof, then a proof by contrapositive, and finally a proof by contradiction.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1128
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tLet <m>P</m> be a sudoku puzzle with all different numbers in its corners: <m>a</m> in the top-left, <m>b</m> in the top-right, <m>c</m> in the bottom-left, and <m>d</m> in the bottom-right.\n"
"\t\t\t\tLet <m>S</m> be any solution to the puzzle.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIn <m>S</m>, whatever digit <m>a</m> is must appear in row 2.\n"
"\t\t\t\tSince <m>a</m> is already in the top-left box, it cannot appear in columns 1 or 2, so it must appear in columns 3 or 4.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIf it is in column 3, then <m>a</m> appears in one of the center squares.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIf not, then it is in column 4.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn this latter case, we ask where <m>a</m> appears in row 3: It cannot be in column 1 or 4, so it must be in column 2 or 3, and thus in one of the center squares.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1141
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe same argument can now be applied to each of the other three outer squares.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThus, in any solution to the puzzle, the center four squares must contain the digits <m>a</m> through <m>d</m>, all different.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1147
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNow we will prove the same statement by contrapositive.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1153
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tLet <m>P</m> be a mini sudoku puzzle with numbers in its corners.\n"
"\t\t\t\tLet <m>S</m> be any solution to the puzzle.\n"
"\t\t\t\tAssume that the center four squares do not contain all different numbers in <m>S</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThen there must be some common number, say <m>n</m>, in two of the center squares.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1160
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tSince <m>n</m> cannot appear twice in a row, it must be that <m>n</m> is in two center squares diagonally from each other: either in r2c2 and r3c3, or in r2c3 and r3c2.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn either case, where could <m>n</m> be in rows 1 and 4?  It cannot be in columns 2 or 3, so it must be in columns 1 and 4.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThis means that <m>n</m> will be in opposite outer corners, meaning that the digits in the four corners are not all different.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1167
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tFinally, we will prove the same statement by contradiction.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1173
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tLet <m>P</m> be a mini sudoku puzzle and assume that the four outer corners are all different, but in some solution <m>S</m>, the center four squares are not all different.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tSuppose <m>a</m>, the digit in the top-left corner, appears twice in the center four squares.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThe only way this can happen is if <m>a</m> appears in r3c2 and r2c3, as shown below.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1183
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\ta partially filled mini sudoku.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1198
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tBut now, where can the fourth <m>a</m> in the go in the solution?  It must be in r4c4, contradicting that the four corners are all different.\n"
"\t\t\t\tAn analogous argument leads to a contradiction for each of the other possible outer corner digits being repeated in the center.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThus, the center four squares must contain all different numbers.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_logic-proofs.ptx:1390
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhich of the following would be the best first line of a <em>direct proof</em> if you wanted to prove the statement, <q>For all sets <m>A</m> of single-digit numbers, if <m>|A| = 6</m>, then <m>A</m> contains an even number.</q>\n"
