#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              There are numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that <m>a+b</m> is even but <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are not both even.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              False.\n"
"              For example, <m>a = 3</m> and <m>b = 5</m>. <m>a+b = 8</m>,\n"
"              but neither <m>a</m> nor <m>b</m> is even.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:141
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              False, since it is equivalent to the original statement.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:147
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              True.\n"
"              Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers.\n"
"              Assume both are even.\n"
"              Then <m>a = 2k</m> and <m>b = 2j</m> for some integers <m>k</m> and <m>j</m>.\n"
"              But then <m>a+b = 2k + 2j = 2(k+j)</m>, which is even.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:157
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              True, since the statement is false.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:168
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For each of the statements below,\n"
"        say what method of proof you should use to prove them.\n"
"        Then say how the proof starts and how it ends.\n"
"        Bonus points for filling in the middle.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:176
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              There are no integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>x</m> is a prime greater than 5 and <m>x = 6y + 3</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              For all integers <m>n</m>,\n"
"              if <m>n</m> is a multiple of 3, then <m>n</m> can be written as the sum of consecutive integers.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:189
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              For all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>,\n"
"              if <m>a^2 + b^2</m> is odd, then <m>a</m> or <m>b</m> is odd.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:201
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Proof by contradiction.\n"
"              Start of proof: Assume, for the sake of contradiction,\n"
"              that there are integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>x</m> is a prime greater than 5 and <m>x = 6y + 3</m>.\n"
"              End of proof:\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:205
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" this is a contradiction,\n"
"              so there are no such integers.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:212
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Direct proof.\n"
"              Start of proof: Let <m>n</m> be an integer.\n"
"              Assume <m>n</m> is a multiple of 3.\n"
"              End of proof: Therefore <m>n</m> can be written as the sum of consecutive integers.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:221
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Proof by contrapositive.\n"
"              Start of proof: Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers.\n"
"              Assume that <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are even.\n"
"              End of proof: Therefore <m>a^2 + b^2</m> is even.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:236
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider the statement, <q>For all integers <m>n</m>,\n"
"        if <m>n</m> is even then <m>8n</m> is even.</q>\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:242 source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:394
#: source/exercises/logic-conc.ptx:670
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Prove the statement.\n"
"              What sort of proof are you using?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:249
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Is the converse true?\n"
"              Prove or disprove.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:261 source/exercises/logic-conc.ptx:689
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Direct proof.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:265
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  Let <m>n</m> be an integer.\n"
"                  Assume <m>n</m> is even.\n"
"                  Then <m>n = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.\n"
"                  Thus <m>8n = 16k = 2(8k)</m>.\n"
"                  Therefore <m>8n</m> is even.\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:278
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The converse is false.\n"
"              That is, there is an integer <m>n</m> such that <m>8n</m> is even but <m>n</m> is odd.\n"
"              For example, consider <m>n = 3</m>.\n"
"              Then <m>8n = 24</m> which is even, but <m>n = 3</m> is odd.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:293
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The game TENZI comes with 40 six-sided dice\n"
"        (each numbered 1 to 6).\n"
"        Suppose you roll all 40 dice.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:299
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Prove that there will be at least seven dice that land on the same number.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:304
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many dice would you have to roll before you were guaranteed that some four of them would all match or all be different?\n"
"              Prove your answer.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:316
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              This is an example of the pigeonhole principle.\n"
"              We can prove it by contrapositive.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:322
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Suppose that each number only came up 6 or fewer times.\n"
"                So there are at most six 1's, six 2's, and so on.\n"
"                That's a total of 36 dice, so you must not have rolled all 40 dice.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:332
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              We can have 9 dice without any four matching or any four being all different:\n"
"              three 1's, three 2's, three 3's.\n"
"              We will prove that whenever you roll 10 dice,\n"
"              you will always get four matching or all being different.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:340
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Suppose you roll 10 dice, but that there are NOT four matching rolls.\n"
"                This means that at most there are three of any given value.\n"
"                If we only had three different values,\n"
"                that would be only 9 dice,\n"
"                so there must be 4 different values,\n"
"                giving 4 dice that are all different.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:359
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that for all integers <m>n</m>, it is the case that <m>n</m> is  even if and only if <m>3n</m> is even.  That is, prove both implications: If <m>n</m> is even, then <m>3n</m> is even, and if <m>3n</m> is even, then <m>n</m> is even. \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:364
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        One of the implications will be a direct proof; the other will be a proof by contrapositive.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:374
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that <m>\\sqrt 3</m> is irrational.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This is really an exercise in modifying the proof that <m>\\sqrt{2}</m> is irrational.  There you proved things were even; here they will be multiples of 3.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:388
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider the statement, <q>For all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>,\n"
"        if <m>a</m> is even and <m>b</m> is a multiple of 3, then <m>ab</m> is a multiple of 6.</q>\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:401
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              State the converse.\n"
"              Is it true?\n"
"              Prove or disprove.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:411
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Part (a) should be a relatively easy direct proof.  Look for a counterexample for part (b).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:420
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove the statement, <q>For all integers <m>n</m>,\n"
"        if <m>5n</m> is odd, then <m>n</m> is odd.</q>\n"
"        Clearly state the style of proof you are using.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:430
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove the statement, <q>For all integers <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>,\n"
"        and <m>c</m>,\n"
"        if <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>, then <m>a</m> or <m>b</m> is even.</q>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:437
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A proof by contradiction would be reasonable here, because then you get to assume that both <m>a</m> <em>and</em> <m>b</m> are odd.  Deduce that <m>c^2</m> is even, and therefore a multiple of 4 (why? and why is that a contradiction?).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:447
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose that you would like to prove the following implication:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

