#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:667
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you are at a party with 19 of your closest friends\n"
"        (so including you, there are 20 people there).\n"
"        Explain why there must be at least two people at the party who are friends with the same number of people at the party.\n"
"        Assume friendship is always reciprocated.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:675
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider the set of <em>numbers</em> of friends that everyone has.  If everyone had a different number of friends, this set must contain 20 elements.  Is that possible?  Why not?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Your friend has given you his list of 115 best Doctor Who episodes\n"
"        (in order of greatness).\n"
"        It turns out that you have seen 60 of them.\n"
"        Prove that there are at least two episodes you have seen that are exactly four episodes apart on your friend's list.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:692
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This feels like the pigeonhole principle, although a bit more complicated.  At least, you could try to replicate the style of proof used by the pigeonhole principle.  How would the episodes need to be spaced out so that no two of your sixty were exactly 4 apart?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/logic-proofs.ptx:705
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you have an <m>n\\times n</m> chessboard, but your dog has eaten one of the corner squares.  You have dominoes that each cover exactly two squares of the board.  Can you cover the remaining squares on the board with non-overlapping dominoes?\n"
"        What needs to be true about <m>n</m>?\n"
"        Give necessary and sufficient conditions\n"
"        (that is, say exactly which values of <m>n</m> work and which do not work).\n"
"        Prove your answers.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

