"            This would be a good start to a direct proof, not a proof by contradiction.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:102
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Arrange some of the statements below to form a correct proof of the following statement: <q>For any sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, if <m>B \\subseteq A \\cap B</m> then <m>B \\subseteq A</m>.</q>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:108
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Suppose <m>B \\subseteq A \\cap B</m>, and let <m>b</m> be an element of <m>B</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:113
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Then <m>b</m> is an element of <m>A \\cap B</m> since <m>B \\subseteq A \\cap B</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Since <m>A \\cap B</m> contains all the elements that are in both <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, <m>b</m> is an element of <m>A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:123
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore <m>B \\subseteq A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:128
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore <m>B \\subseteq A \\cap B</m>\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Then <m>b</m> is an element of <m>B</m> since <m>B \\subseteq A \\cap B</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>b</m> be an element of <m>A \\cap B</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Suppose <m>B \\subseteq A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:148
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Suppose <m>A \\subseteq B</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:158
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that for any sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, <m>(A \\cap B) \\cup A = A</m>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:161
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Arrange the statements below to form a correct proof.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:167
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          First we will prove that <m>(A \\cap B) \\cup A \\subseteq A</m>.  \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:172
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>x</m> be an element of <m>(A \\cap B) \\cup A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Then <m>x</m> is an element of <m>A \\cap B</m>, or <m>x</m> is an element of <m>A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          So in particular, <m>x</m> is an element of <m>A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:187
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore <m>(A \\cap B) \\cup A \\subseteq A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:192
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Second, we will prove that <m>A \\subseteq (A \\cap B) \\cup A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:197
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>x</m> be an element of <m>A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:202
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Then <m>x</m> is an element of <m>(A \\cap B) \\cup A</m>, since <m>x</m> is in <m>A</m> or in the other set.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:207
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore <m>A \\subseteq (A \\cap B) \\cup A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:212
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Since <m>(A \\cap B) \\cup A \\subseteq A</m> and <m>A \\subseteq (A \\cap B) \\cup A</m>, we have <m>(A \\cap B) \\cup A = A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:223
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Let <m>f:X \\to Y</m> be a function and let <m>B \\subseteq Y</m> be a subset of the codomain.  Define the <term>inverse image</term> of <m>B</m> under <m>f</m> to be the set <m>f\\inv(B) = \\{x \\in X \\st f(x) \\in B\\}</m>.  That is, it is all the elements in the domain that are mapped to elements in <m>B</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:227
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that if <m>B_1 \\subseteq B_2</m> are subsets of the codomain, then <m>f\\inv(B_1) \\subseteq f\\inv(B_2)</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:230
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Arrange some of the statements below to form a correct proof.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:236
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Suppose <m>B_1 \\subseteq B_2</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:241
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>a</m> be an element of <m>f\\inv(B_1)</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:246
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          This means that <m>f(a)</m> is an element of <m>B_1</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:251
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Since <m>B_1 \\subseteq B_2</m>, <m>f(a)</m> is an element of <m>B_2</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:256
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          This then means that <m>a</m> is an element of <m>f\\inv(B_2)</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:261
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore <m>f\\inv(B_1) \\subseteq f\\inv(B_2)</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/logic-structures.ptx:266
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>b</m> be an element of <m>B_1</m>.\n"
"        "
