#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:4 source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:4
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:4 source/sec_seq-conc.ptx:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Chapter Summary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:7
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We have considered logic both as its own sub-discipline of mathematics\n"
"      and as a means to help us better understand and write proofs.\n"
"      We noticed that mathematical statements have a particular logical form,\n"
"      and analyzing that form can help make sense of the statement.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:14
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      At the most basic level,\n"
"      a statement might combine simpler statements using\n"
"      <em>logical connectives</em>.\n"
"      We often make use of variables\n"
"      and <em>quantify</em> over those variables.\n"
"      How to resolve the truth or falsity of a statement based on these connectives and quantifiers is what logic is all about.\n"
"      From this, we can decide whether two statements are logically equivalent or if one or more statements (logically) imply another.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:24
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      When writing proofs\n"
"      (in any area of mathematics)\n"
"      our goal is to explain why a mathematical statement is true.\n"
"      Thus it is vital that our argument implies the truth of the statement.\n"
"      To be sure of this,\n"
"      we first must know what it means for the statement to be true,\n"
"      as well as ensure that the statements that make up the proof correctly imply the conclusion.\n"
"      A firm understanding of logic is required to check whether a proof is correct.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:35
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      There is, however,\n"
"      another reason that understanding logic can be helpful.\n"
"      Understanding the logical structure of a statement often gives clues for how to write a proof of the statement.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:41
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This is not to say that writing proofs is always straight-forward.\n"
"      Consider again the <em>Goldbach conjecture</em>:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:48
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Every even number greater than 2 can be written as the sum of two primes.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:53
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We are not going to try to prove the statement here,\n"
"      but we can at least say what a proof might look like,\n"
"      based on the logical form of the statement.\n"
"      Perhaps we should write the statement to highlight the quantifiers and connectives:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:61
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For all integers <m>n</m>,\n"
"        if <m>n</m> is even and greater than 2, then there exist integers <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> such that <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are prime, and <m>n = p+q</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:67
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      What would a direct proof look like?\n"
"      Since the statement starts with a universal quantifier,\n"
"      we would start, ``Let <m>n</m> be an arbitrary integer.\" The rest of the statement is an implication.\n"
"      In a direct proof we assume the <q>if</q> part,\n"
"      so the next line would be,\n"
"      <q>Assume <m>n</m> is greater than 2 and is even.</q>\n"
"      I have no idea what comes next, but eventually,\n"
"      we would need to find two prime numbers <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>\n"
"      (depending on <m>n</m>)\n"
"      and explain how we know that <m>n = p+q</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Or maybe we try a proof by contradiction.\n"
"      To do this, we first assume the negation of the statement we want to prove.\n"
"      What is the negation?\n"
"      From what we have studied we should be able to see that it is,\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:88
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        There is an integer <m>n</m> such that <m>n</m> is even and greater than <m>2</m>,\n"
"        but for all integers <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>,\n"
"        either <m>p</m> or <m>q</m> is not prime, or <m>n \\ne p+q</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_logic-conc.ptx:95
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Could this statement be true?\n"
"      A proof by contradiction would start by assuming it was and eventually conclude with a contradiction,\n"
"      proving that our assumption of truth was incorrect.\n"
"      And if you can find such a contradiction,\n"
"      you will have proved one of the most famous open problems in mathematics.\n"
"      Good luck.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

