#. type: Content of: <appendix><section><title>
#: source/ch_graphtheory.ptx:4 source/peer-instruction.ptx:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "Graph Theory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <chapter><introduction><p>
#: source/ch_graphtheory.ptx:7
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Graph theory has existed as a branch of mathematics for only a short time; the first book on graph theory was published less than 100 years ago.  While the first problem related to what we now call graph theory dates back to 1735, it has been the advent of computers that has shown the subject's true utility.  It is a subject with simple beauty and surprising depth.  Many of the main areas of graph theory can be understood with almost no mathematical prerequisites, yet new research in the subject generates hundreds of peer-reviewed research papers each year.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <chapter><introduction><p>
#: source/ch_graphtheory.ptx:11
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In this chapter, we will explore just a few of the ways you can use graphs and their properties to solve problems that show up in computer science, mathematics, and almost every other applied science.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Problems and Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:14
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Use the language of graph theory to describe properties of graphs.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:20
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Utilize multiple representations of graphs.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:25
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Apply the Handshake Lemma to answer questions about graphs and problems they represent.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:38
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          In the time of Euler, in the town of Königsberg in Prussia,\n"
"          there was a river containing two islands.\n"
"          The islands were connected to the banks of the river by seven bridges\n"
"          (as seen below).\n"
"          The bridges were very beautiful, and on their days off,\n"
"          townspeople would spend time walking over the bridges.\n"
"          As time passed, a question arose:\n"
"          Was it possible to plan a walk so that you cross each bridge once and only once?\n"
"          Euler was able to answer this question.\n"
"          Are you?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "Drawing of a river (blue) running left to right with two islands. There are 7 bridges over the river: two from the top bank to the left island, two from the left island to the bottom bank, and a bridge from the right island to each of the top bank, bottom bank, and left island."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:95
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Graph Theory is a relatively new area of mathematics,\n"
"        first studied by the super famous mathematician Leonhard Euler in 1735.\n"
"        Since then it has blossomed into a powerful tool used in nearly every branch of science and is currently an active area of mathematics research.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:101
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The problem above, known as the\n"
"        <em>Seven Bridges of Königsberg</em>,\n"
"        is the problem that originally inspired graph theory.\n"
"        Consider a <q>different</q> problem: Below is a drawing of four dots connected by some lines.\n"
"        Is it possible to trace over each line once and only once (without lifting your pencil,\n"
"        starting and ending on a dot)?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Three dots aligned in a vertical column left of a single dot on the right.  Lines connect the dot on the right to each dot on the left.  Among the dots on the left, two arced lines connect the bottom dot to the center dot, and two more connect the center dot to the top dot.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:124
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        There is an obvious connection between these two problems.\n"
"        Any path in the dot and line drawing corresponds exactly to a path over the bridges of Königsberg.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Pictures like this dot and line drawing are called <term>graphs</term> (although technically, the picture above is a <term>multigraph</term>).\n"
"        Graphs are made up of a collection of dots called <term>vertices</term>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:136
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        and lines connecting those dots called <term>edges</term>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        When two vertices are connected by an edge,\n"
"        we say they are <term>adjacent</term>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:141
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The nice thing about looking at graphs instead of pictures of rivers,\n"
"        islands, and bridges is that we now have a mathematical object to study.\n"
"        We have distilled the <q>important</q>\n"
"        parts of the bridge picture for the problem.\n"
"        It does not matter how big the islands are,\n"
"        what the bridges are made out of,\n"
"        if the river contains alligators, etc.\n"
"        All that matters is which land masses are connected to which other land masses,\n"
"        and how many times.\n"
"        This was the great insight Euler had.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:154
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We will return to the question of finding paths through graphs in <xref po4a-id=0>.  In this section, we will explore various ways that graphs can be used to represent, or <em>model</em>, real-world problems.  Along the way, we will introduce some basic definitions, terminology, and notation that will be used in the rest of the chapter.\n"
"\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

