#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:433
#, no-wrap
msgid "What is a Graph?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:438
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Before we start studying graphs,\n"
"    we need to agree upon what a graph is. While we almost always think of graphs as pictures\n"
"    (dots connected by lines),\n"
"    this is fairly ambiguous.\n"
"    Do the lines need to be straight?\n"
"    Does it matter how long the lines are or how large the dots are?\n"
"    Can there be two lines connecting the same pair of dots?\n"
"    Can one line connect three dots?\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:449
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    The way we avoid ambiguities in mathematics is to provide concrete and rigorous <em>definitions</em>.\n"
"    Crafting good definitions is not easy, but it is incredibly important. The definition is the\n"
"    agreed-upon starting point from which all truths in mathematics proceed. Is there a graph with\n"
"    no edges? We have to look at the definition to see if this is possible. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:455
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    We want our\n"
"    definition to be precise and unambiguous, but it also must agree with our intuition for the\n"
"    objects we are studying. It needs to be useful: We <em>could</em> define a graph to be a six-legged\n"
"    mammal, but that would not let us solve any problems about bridges. Instead, here is the (now)\n"
"    standard definition of a graph. \n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:463 source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1391
#, no-wrap
msgid "Graph"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:476
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" A <term>graph</term> is an\n"
"      ordered pair <m>G = (V, E)</m> consisting of a nonempty set <m>V</m> (called the <term>vertices</term>)\n"
"      and a set <m>E</m> (called the <term>edges</term>) of two-element subsets of <m>V</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:483
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Strange. Nowhere in the definition is there talk of dots or lines. From the definition, a graph\n"
"    could be "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:486
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
". Here we have\n"
"    a graph with four vertices (the letters <m>a, b, c, d</m>) and five edges (the pairs <m>\\{a,b\\},\n"
"    \\{a,c\\}, \\{b,c\\}, \\{b,d\\}, \\{c,d\\})</m>). "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:492
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Looking at sets and\n"
"    sets of 2-element sets is difficult to process. That is why we often draw a representation of\n"
"    these sets. We put a dot down for each vertex, and connect two dots with a line precisely when\n"
"    those two vertices are one of the 2-element subsets in our set of edges. Thus one way to draw\n"
"    the graph described above is this: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:507
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Four vertices arranged in a square, with edges on the border of the square and one connecting\n"
"      the bottom left vertex to the top right vertex. Vertices are labeled a (top left), b (top\n"
"      right), c (bottom left) and d (bottom right).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:515
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    However we could also have drawn the graph differently.\n"
"    For example either of these:\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:530
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Four vertices arranged in a square. Vertices are labeled a (top left), d (top right), c\n"
"        (bottom left) and b (bottom right). Edges connect a to c and b, d to b and c, and c to b.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:548
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Four vertices arranged in a horizontal row, labeled a, b, c, and d from left to right. Edges\n"
"        connect each vertex to the one on its right. A curved edge connects a to c, and another\n"
"        curved edge connects b to d.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:557
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    We should be careful about what it means for two graphs to be <q>the same.</q> Actually, given our\n"
"    definition, this is easy: Are the vertex sets equal? Are the edge sets equal? We know what it\n"
"    means for sets to be equal, and graphs are nothing but a pair of two special sorts of sets. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:568
#, no-wrap
msgid " Are the graphs below equal? "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:570 source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:378
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:572 source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:644
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:836 source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:864
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:871 source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:893
#: source/practice/gt-paths.ptx:89 source/practice/counting-pascal.ptx:140
#: source/sec_counting-non-disjoint.ptx:532
#: source/exercises/counting-proofs.ptx:532
#: source/exercises/counting-advPIE.ptx:14 source/exercises/seq-growth.ptx:154
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:426 source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:454
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:413 source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:332
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:673
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:699
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:711 source/sec_addtops-numbth.ptx:485
#: source/sec_addtops-numbth.ptx:506 source/sec_addtops-numbth.ptx:623
#: source/exercises/addtops-numbth.ptx:332
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:574
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" No. Here the vertex sets of each graph are equal, which is a\n"
"        good start. Also, both graphs have two edges. In the first graph, we have edges <m>\\{a,b\\}</m>\n"
"        and <m>\\{b,c\\}</m>, while in the second graph we have edges <m>\\{a,c\\}</m> and <m>\\{c,b\\}</m>. Of course, <m>\\{b,c\\} = \\{c,b\\}</m>, so that is not the problem. The issue is that <m>\\{a,b\\} \\ne\n"
"        \\{a,c\\}</m>. Since the edge sets of the two graphs are not equal (as sets), the graphs are\n"
"        not equal (as graphs). "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:582
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Even if two graphs are not <em>equal</em>,\n"
"    they might be <em>basically</em> the same. The graphs in the previous example could be drawn like\n"
"    this: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:599
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Two graphs with three vertices each arranged in a horizontal row. Edges connect vertices to\n"
"      the vertex on either side of it. The graph on the left (G1) has vertices labeled a, b, c from\n"
"      left to right. The graph on the right (G2) has vertices labeled a, c, b from left to right.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:607
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Graphs that are basically the same (but perhaps not equal) are called <term>isomorphic</term>. We will\n"
"    give a precise definition of this term after a quick example: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:612
#, no-wrap
msgid " Consider the graphs: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:624
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Are these graphs the same?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:628
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" The two graphs are NOT equal.\n"
"        It is enough to notice that <m>V_1 \\ne V_2</m> since <m>a \\in V_1</m> but <m>a \\notin V_2</m>.\n"
"        However, both of these graphs consist of three vertices with edges connecting every pair of\n"
"        vertices. We can draw them as follows: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:643
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            A graph with three vertices arranged as a triangle, with edges along the border of the\n"
"            triangle. The vertices are labeled a, b, and c.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:656
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            A graph with three vertices arranged as a triangle, with edges along the border of the\n"
"            triangle. The vertices are labeled u, v, and w.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:664
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Clearly we want to say these graphs are basically the same, so while they are not equal,\n"
"        they will be <em>isomorphic</em>. We can rename the vertices of one graph and get the second\n"
"        graph as the result. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:673
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Intuitively, graphs are <term>isomorphic</term>\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:676
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" if they are basically the same, or better yet, if they are the same\n"
"    except for the names of the vertices. To make the concept of renaming vertices precise, we give\n"
"    the following definitions: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:689
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" An <term>isomorphism</term> between two graphs <m>G_1</m> and <m>G_2</m> is a bijection <m>f:V_1\n"
"      \\to V_2</m> between the vertices of the graphs such that <m>\\{a,b\\}</m> is an edge in <m>G_1</m> if\n"
"      and only if <m>\\{f(a), f(b)\\}</m> is an edge in <m>G_2</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:693
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Two graphs are <term>isomorphic</term> if\n"
"      there is an isomorphism between them. In this case we write <m>G_1 \\isom G_2</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:699
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    An isomorphism is simply a function which renames the vertices.\n"
"    It must be a bijection so every vertex gets a new name.\n"
"    These newly named vertices must be connected by edges precisely when they were connected by\n"
"    edges with their old names.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:710
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Decide whether the graphs <m>G_1 = (V_1, E_1)</m>\n"
"        and <m>G_2 = (V_2, E_2)</m> are equal or isomorphic. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:714
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        <m>V_1 = \\{a,b,c,d\\}</m>, <m>E_1 =\n"
"        \\{\\{a,b\\}, \\{a,c\\}, \\{a,d\\}, \\{c,d\\}\\}</m>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:719
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        <m>V_2 = \\{a,b,c,d\\}</m>, <m>E_2 = \\{\\{a,b\\},\n"
"        \\{a,c\\}, \\{b,c\\}, \\{c,d\\}\\}</m>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:724
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" The graphs are NOT equal, since <m>\\{a,d\\}\n"
"        \\in E_1</m> but <m>\\{a,d\\} \\notin E_2</m>. However, since both graphs contain the same number\n"
"        of vertices and the same number of edges, they <em>might</em> be isomorphic (this is not enough in\n"
"        most cases, but it is a good start). "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:729
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" We can try to build an isomorphism. How about we\n"
"        say <m>f(a) = b</m>, <m>f(b) = c</m>, <m>f(c) = d</m> and <m>f(d) = a</m>. This is definitely a\n"
"        bijection, but to make sure that the function is an isomorphism, we must make sure it <em>respects\n"
"        the edge relation</em>. In <m>G_1</m>, vertices <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are connected by an edge. In <m>\n"
"        G_2</m>, <m>f(a) = b</m> and <m>f(b) = c</m> are connected by an edge. So far, so good, but we\n"
"        must check the other three edges. The edge <m>\\{a,c\\}</m> in <m>G_1</m> corresponds to <m>\\{f(a),\n"
"        f(c)\\} = \\{b,d\\}</m>, but here we have a problem. There is no edge between <m>b</m> and <m>d</m>\n"
"        in <m>G_2</m>. Thus <m>f</m> is NOT an isomorphism. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:738
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Not all hope is lost, however. Just\n"
"        because <m>f</m> is not an isomorphism does not mean that there is no isomorphism at all. We\n"
"        can try again. At this point it might be helpful to draw the graphs to see how they should\n"
"        match up. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:763
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The graph G1 with four vertices arranged in a diamond. The top vertex (a) is connected\n"
"            to the three other vertices (d on the left, b on the right, and c below). There is one\n"
"            additional edge between d and c.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:786
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The graph G2 with four vertices arranged in a diamond. The top vertex (a) is connected\n"
"            to the vertices c below and b on the right. There is two additional edges between d\n"
"            (left) and c and between b and c .\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:795
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Alternatively, notice that in <m>G_1</m>, the vertex <m>a</m> is adjacent to every other vertex.\n"
"        In <m>G_2</m>, there is also a vertex with this property: <m>c</m>. So build the bijection <m>g:V_1\n"
"        \\to V_2</m> by defining <m>g(a) = c</m> to start with. Next, where should we send <m>b</m>? In <m>G_1</m>,\n"
"        the vertex <m>b</m> is only adjacent to vertex <m>a</m>. There is exactly one vertex like this in <m>\n"
"        G_2</m>, namely <m>d</m>. So let <m>g(b) = d</m>. As for the last two, in this example, we have a\n"
"        free choice: let <m>g(c) = b</m> and <m>g(d) = a</m> (switching these would be fine as well). "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:803
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We should check that this really is an isomorphism. It is definitely a bijection. We must\n"
"        make sure that the edges are respected. The four edges in <m>G_1</m> are "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <backmatter><colophon><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:806 source/exercises/counting-proofs.ptx:618
#: source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:189 source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:225
#: source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:250 source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:291
#: source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:304 source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:314
#: source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:326 source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:349
#: source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:426 source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:430
#: source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:458 source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:464
#: source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:518 source/sec_counting-advPIE.ptx:520
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:614 source/backmatter.ptx:27
#, no-wrap
msgid ". "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:808
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Under the proposed\n"
"        isomorphism these become "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:814
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
", which are precisely the edges\n"
"        in <m>G_2</m>. Thus <m>g</m> is an isomorphism, so <m>G_1 \\cong G_2</m>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:828
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" This is not unlike geometry, where we might have more than one copy of a particular\n"
"    triangle. There instead of <em>isomorphic</em> we say <em>congruent</em>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:825
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Sometimes we will talk about a graph\n"
"    with a special name (like <m>K_n</m> or the <em>Petersen graph</em>) or perhaps draw a graph without\n"
"    any labels. In this case, we are really referring to <em>all</em> graphs isomorphic to any copy of\n"
"    that particular graph. A collection of isomorphic graphs is often called an <term>isomorphism class</term>.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:832
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    There are other relationships between graphs that we care about,\n"
"    other than equality and being isomorphic.\n"
"    For example, compare the following pair of graphs:\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:847
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A\n"
"        graph with six vertices arranged in a hexagon, with edges between every possible pair of\n"
"        vertices."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:860
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Four\n"
"        vertices arranged in a diamond, with edges between every possible pair of vertices."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:867
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    These are definitely not isomorphic,\n"
"    but notice that the graph on the right looks like it might be part of the graph on the left,\n"
"    especially if we draw it like this:\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:883
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A graph with six vertices arranged in a hexagon with edges between every pair of vertices. Six\n"
"      edges are drawn in bold, forming a slanted rectangle with an X through it.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:891
#, no-wrap
msgid "    We would like to say that the smaller graph is a <em>subgraph</em> of the larger. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:894
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    We should give a careful definition of this.\n"
"    In fact, there are two reasonable notions for what a subgraph should mean.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:900
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Subgraphs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:909
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We say that <m>G' = (V', E')</m> is a <term>subgraph</term> of <m>G = (V, E)</m>, and write <m>G' \\subseteq G</m>,\n"
"      provided <m>V' \\subseteq V</m> and <m>E' \\subseteq E</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:912
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" We say that <m>G' = (V', E')</m> is an <term>induced\n"
"      subgraph</term> of <m>G = (V, E)</m> provided <m>V' \\subseteq V</m> and every edge in <m>E</m> whose\n"
"      vertices are still in <m>V'</m> is also an edge in <m>E'</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:919
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Notice that every induced subgraph is also an ordinary subgraph,\n"
"    but not conversely.\n"
"    Think of a subgraph as the result of deleting some vertices and edges from the larger graph.\n"
"    For the subgraph to be an induced subgraph,\n"
"    we can still delete vertices,\n"
"    but now we only delete those edges that included the deleted vertices.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:930
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider the graphs:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:946
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The graph G1 consisting of six vertices arranged in a triangle. Starting with the bottom\n"
"            left vertex and traveling around the triangle clockwise, the vertices are labeled a, d,\n"
"            f, e, c, b. Edges connect vertices at the corners of the triangle to vertices along\n"
"            their edges of the triangle. These center vertices are also connected to each of the\n"
"            other center vertices.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:964
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The graph G2 of four vertices: a horizontal row of three, labeled a, b, c, and a vertex\n"
"            labeled d centered between and above a and b. Edges connect a, b, and d (forming a\n"
"            triangle) and a fourth edge connects b to c.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:980
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The graph G3 of five vertices. Vertices a, b, and c are aligned in a horizontal row.\n"
"            Vertices d and f form a line with a in a line that slants up and to the right. There are\n"
"            edges between a and d, between d and f, between d and b, and between b and c.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:997
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The graph G4 with five vertices: a, b, and c form a horizontal row, d and f line up with\n"
"            a in a line slanting up and the to the right. The vertices form a triangle, and edges\n"
"            are arranged to fill in the outside border of the triangle. Vertices d and b are also\n"
"            connected by and edge.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1007
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Here both <m>G_2</m> and <m>G_3</m> are subgraphs of <m>G_1</m>. But only <m>G_2</m> is an <em>induced</em>\n"
"        subgraph. Every edge in <m>G_1</m> that connects vertices in <m>G_2</m> is also an edge in <m>G_2</m>.\n"
"        In <m>G_3</m>, the edge <m>\\{a,b\\}</m> is in <m>E_1</m> but not <m>E_3</m>, even though vertices <m>a</m>\n"
"        and <m>b</m> are in <m>V_3</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1012
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" The graph <m>G_4</m> is NOT a subgraph of <m>G_1</m>, even though\n"
"        it looks like all we did is remove vertex <m>e</m>. The reason is that in <m>E_4</m> we have the\n"
"        edge <m>\\{c,f\\}</m>, but this is not an element of <m>E_1</m>, so we don't have the required <m>E_4\n"
"        \\subseteq E_1</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1024
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Back to some basic graph theory definitions. Notice that all the graphs we have drawn above have\n"
"    the property that no pair of vertices is connected more than once, and no vertex is connected to\n"
"    itself. Graphs like these are sometimes called <term>simple</term>, although we will just call them <em>\n"
"    graphs</em>. This is because our definition of a graph says that the edges form a set of\n"
"    2-element subsets of the vertices. Remember that it doesn't make sense to say a set contains an\n"
"    element more than once. So no pair of vertices can be connected by an edge more than once. Also,\n"
"    since each edge must be a set containing two vertices, we cannot have a single vertex connected\n"
"    to itself by an edge. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1040
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" That said, there are times we want to consider double (or more)\n"
"    edges and single-edge loops. For example, the <q>graph</q> we drew for the Bridges of Königsberg\n"
"    problem had double edges because there really are two bridges connecting a particular island to\n"
"    the near shore. We will call these objects <term>multigraphs</term>. This is a good name: A <em>multiset</em>\n"
"    is a set in which we are allowed to include a single element multiple times. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1051
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" The graphs above are also <term>connected</term>:\n"
"    you can get from any vertex to any other vertex by following some path of edges. A graph that is\n"
"    not connected can be thought of as two separate graphs drawn close together. For example, the\n"
"    following graph is NOT connected because there is no path from <m>a</m> to <m>b</m>: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1064
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A graph consisting of eight vertices arranged in two overlapping diamonds, with edges forming\n"
"      the border of those diamonds. The vertex on the far left is labeled a and the vertex on the\n"
"      far right is labeled b.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><h><m>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1077
#, no-wrap
msgid "K_n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1079
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Vertices in a\n"
"    graph do not always have edges between them. If we add all possible edges, then the resulting\n"
"    graph is called <term>complete</term>. That is, a graph is complete if every pair of vertices is\n"
"    connected by an edge. Since a graph is determined completely by which vertices are adjacent to\n"
"    which other vertices, there is only one complete graph with a given number of vertices. We give\n"
"    these a special name: <m>K_n</m>\n"
"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1087
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>K_n</m>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1089
#, no-wrap
msgid "the complete graph on <m>n</m> vertices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1091
#, no-wrap
msgid "    is the complete graph on <m>n</m> vertices. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1100
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Each vertex in <m>K_n</m> is adjacent to <m>n-1</m> other vertices. We\n"
"    call the number of edges emanating from a given vertex the <term>degree</term> of that vertex. So\n"
"    every vertex in <m>K_n</m> has degree <m>n-1</m>. How many edges does <m>K_n</m> have? One might think\n"
"    the answer should be <m>n(n-1)</m>, since we count <m>n-1</m> edges <m>n</m> times (once for each\n"
"    vertex). However, each edge is incident to 2 vertices, so we counted every edge exactly twice.\n"
"    Thus there are <m>n(n-1)/2</m> edges in <m>K_n</m>. Alternatively, we can say there are <m>{n \\choose 2}</m>\n"
"    edges, since to draw an edge we must choose 2 of the <m>n</m> vertices. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1108
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" In general, if we know\n"
"    the degrees of all the vertices in a graph, we can find the number of edges. The sum of the\n"
"    degrees of all vertices will always be <em>twice</em> the number of edges, since each edge adds to\n"
"    the degree of two vertices. Notice this means that the sum of the degrees of all vertices in any\n"
"    graph must be even! "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1115
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    This is our first example of a general result about all graphs. It seems innocent enough, but we\n"
"    will use it to prove all sorts of other statements. So let's give it a name and state it\n"
"    formally.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><lemma><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1121
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Handshake\n"
"      Lemma"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><lemma><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1132
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        In any graph, the sum of the degrees of vertices in the graph is always twice the number of\n"
"        edges.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1139
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A <em>lemma</em> is a mathematical statement that is primarily of importance in\n"
"    that it is used to establish other results."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1139
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    The handshake lemma<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/> is sometimes called the <em>degree sum formula</em>,\n"
"    and can be written symbolically as "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
". Here we are using the notation <m>d(v)</m> for the\n"
"    degree of the vertex <m>v</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1153
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" One use for the lemma is to actually find the number of\n"
"    edges in a graph. To do this, you must be given the <term>degree sequence</term> for the graph (or be\n"
"    able to find it from other information). This is a list of every degree of every vertex in the\n"
"    graph, generally written in non-increasing order. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1160
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" How many vertices and edges must a graph\n"
"        have if its degree sequence is "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1163
#, no-wrap
msgid "? "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1166
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" The number of vertices is easy to find. It is the number of degrees in the sequence: 7. To find the number\n"
"        of edges, we compute the degree sum \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1170
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
", so the number of edges is half\n"
"        this: 10. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1176
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    The handshake lemma also tells us what is not possible.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        At a recent math seminar, 9 mathematicians greeted each other by shaking hands.\n"
"        Is it possible that each mathematician shook hands with exactly 7 people at the seminar?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1188
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        It seems like this should be possible. Each mathematician chooses one person to not shake\n"
"        hands with. But this cannot happen. We are asking whether a graph with 9 vertices can have\n"
"        each vertex have degree 7. If such a graph existed, the sum of the degrees of the vertices\n"
"        would be <m>9\\cdot 7 = 63</m>. This would be twice the number of edges (handshakes) resulting\n"
"        in a graph with <m>31.5</m> edges. That is impossible. Thus at least one (in fact an odd\n"
"        number) of the mathematicians must have shaken hands with an <em>even</em> number of people at\n"
"        the seminar. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1199
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A <term>proposition</term> is a general statement in mathematics, similar to\n"
"    a theorem, although generally of lesser importance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1198
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" We can generalize the previous example to get\n"
"    the following proposition.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proposition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1206
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        In any graph, the number of vertices with odd degree must be even.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proposition><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1211
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose there were a graph with an odd number of vertices with odd degree. Then the sum of\n"
"        the degrees in the graph would be odd, which is impossible, by the handshake lemma.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1218
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    We will consider further applications of the handshake lemma in the exercises.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#.  The graph in the houses and utilities puzzle is <m>
#. K_{3,3}</m>. 
#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1228
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" One\n"
"    final definition: We say a graph is <term>bipartite</term> if the vertices can be divided into two\n"
"    sets, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, with no two vertices in <m>A</m> adjacent and no two vertices in <m>B</m>\n"
"    adjacent. The vertices in <m>A</m> can be adjacent to some or all of the vertices in <m>B</m>. If each\n"
"    vertex in <m>A</m> is adjacent to all the vertices in <m>B</m>, then the graph is a <term>complete bipartite\n"
"    graph</term>, and gets a special name: <m>K_{m,n}</m>, where <m>|A| = m</m> and <m>|B| = n</m>. \n"
"    \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1239
#, no-wrap
msgid "Named Graphs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1259
#, no-wrap
msgid " Some graphs are used more than others and get special names. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1261
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>\n"
"      K_n</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1264
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" The complete graph on <m>n</m> vertices. \n"
"\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1270
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>\n"
"      K_{m,n}</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1273
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" The complete bipartite graph with sets of <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>\n"
"      vertices. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1275
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>K_{m,n}</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1277
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"the complete bipartite graph of <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>\n"
"      vertices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1285
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>C_n</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1288
#, no-wrap
msgid "      The cycle on <m>n</m> vertices, just one big loop. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1289
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>C_n</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1291
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"the cycle on <m>n</m>\n"
"      vertices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1299
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P_n</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1302
#, no-wrap
msgid "      The path on <m>n+1</m> vertices (so <m>n</m> edges), just one long path. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1303
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P_n</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1305
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"the\n"
"      path on <m>n+1</m> vertices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-intro.ptx:1330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The\n"
"          graph K5: five vertices arranged in a pentagon. Each vertex is connected to each other\n"
"          vertex by an edge."
