#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:10
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If 10 people each shake hands with each other, how many handshakes took place? What does this question have to do with graph theory?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This is asking for the number of edges in <m>K_{10}</m>.\n"
"      Each vertex (person) has degree (shook hands with) 9 (people).\n"
"      So the sum of the degrees is <m>90</m>.\n"
"      However, the degrees count each edge (handshake) twice, so there are 45 edges in the graph.\n"
"      That is how many handshakes took place.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:28
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Among a group of 5 people, is it possible for everyone to be friends with exactly 2 of the people in the group? What about 3 of the people in the group?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:34
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      It is possible for everyone to be friends with exactly 2 people.\n"
"      You could arrange the 5 people in a circle and say that everyone is friends with the two people on either side of them (so you get the graph <m>C_5</m>).\n"
"      However, it is not possible for everyone to be friends with 3 people.\n"
"      That would lead to a graph with an odd number of odd degree vertices which is impossible since the sum of the degrees must be even.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
