#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:507
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We often define graph theory concepts using set theory.\n"
"      For example, given a graph <m>G = (V, E)</m> and a vertex <m>v \\in V</m>, we define\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:511
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      We define <m>N[v] = N(v) \\cup \\{v\\}</m>.\n"
"      The goal of this problem is to figure out what all this means.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:517
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>G</m> be the graph with vertices <m>V</m> and edges <m>E</m> given by\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:520
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"            Find <m>N(a)</m>, <m>N[a]</m>, <m>N(c)</m>, and <m>N[c]</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:527
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            What are the largest and smallest possible values for <m> |N(v)|</m> and <m>|N[v]|</m> (the sizes of these sets) for the graph in part (a)? Explain.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:533
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Give an example of a graph <m>G = (V, E)</m> (probably different from the one above) for which <m>N[v] = V</m> for some vertex <m>v \\in V</m>.\n"
"            Is there a graph for which <m>N[v] = V</m> for <em>all</em> <m>v \\in V</m>? Explain.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:540
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Give an example of a graph <m>G = (V,E)</m> for which <m>N(v) = \\emptyset</m> for some <m>v \\in V</m>.\n"
"            Is there an example of such a graph for which <m>N[u] = V</m> for some other <m>u \\in V</m> as well? Explain.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:547
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Describe in words what <m>N(v)</m> and <m>N[v]</m> mean in general.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:556
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      You should be able to deduce everything directly from the definition.\n"
"      However, perhaps it would be helpful to know that the <m>N</m> stands for <term>neighborhood</term>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:565
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A graph is a way of representing the relationships between elements in a set: An edge between the vertices <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> tells us that <m>x</m> is related to <m>y</m> (which we can write as <m>x \\sim y</m>).\n"
"      Not all sorts of relationships can be represented by a graph, though.\n"
"      For each relationship described below, either draw the graph or explain why the relationship cannot be represented by a graph.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:571
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The set <m>V = \\{1,2, \\ldots, 9\\}</m> and the relationship <m>x \\sim y</m> when <m>x-y</m> is a non-zero multiple of 3.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:577
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The set <m>V = \\{1,2, \\ldots, 9\\}</m> and the relationship <m>x \\sim y</m> when <m>y</m> is a multiple of <m>x</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:583
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The set <m>V = \\{1,2,\\ldots, 9\\}</m> and the relationship <m>x \\sim y</m> when <m>0 \\lt |x-y| \\lt 3</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:592
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Be careful to make sure the edges are not <q>directed.</q> In a graph, if <m>a</m> is adjacent to <m>b</m>, then <m>b</m> is adjacent to <m>a</m>.\n"
"      In the language of relations, we say that the edge relation is <term> symmetric</term>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:601
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Consider graphs with <m>n</m> vertices.\n"
"      Remember, graphs do not need to be <em>connected</em>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:606
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            How many edges must the graph have to guarantee at least one vertex has degree two or more? Prove your answer.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:612
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            How many edges must the graph have to guarantee all vertices have degree two or more? Prove your answer.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:621
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      You might want to answer the questions for some specific values of <m>n</m> to get a feel for them, but your final answers should be in terms of <m>n</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:629
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Prove that any graph with at least two vertices must have two vertices of the same degree.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:635
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Try a small example first: Any graph with 8 vertices must have two vertices of the same degree.\n"
"      If not, what would the degree sequence be?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-intro.ptx:644
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Suppose <m>G</m> is a connected graph with <m>n &gt; 1</m> vertices and <m>n-1</m> edges.\n"
"      Prove that <m> G</m> has a vertex of degree 1.\n"
"    "
