#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Trees"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:15
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Prove basic facts about trees.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:21
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Use theorems about trees to solve problems.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:26
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Identify and construct spanning trees.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:38
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider the graph drawn below.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:50
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            A graph with seven vertices arranged in three rows: two on top, three in the middle, and two on bottom.  The four vertices on top and bottom have edges forming a square.  The vertex in the middle of the middle row is adjacent to the four vertices on top and bottom.  The vertices on the left and right of the middle row are adjacent to the top and bottom vertices closest to them.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:58
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Find a subgraph with the smallest number of edges that is still connected and contains all the vertices.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Find a subgraph with the largest number of edges that doesn't contain any cycles.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:68
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              What do you notice about the number of edges in your examples above?  Is this a coincidence?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:75
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      One very useful and common approach to studying graph theory is to restrict your focus to graphs of a particular kind.\n"
"      For example,\n"
"      you could try to really understand just complete graphs or just bipartite graphs,\n"
"      instead of trying to understand all graphs in general.\n"
"      That is what we are going to do now,\n"
"      looking at <em>trees</em>.\n"
"      Hopefully by the end of this section we will have a better understanding of this class of graph,\n"
"      and also understand why it is important enough to warrant its own section.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:86
#, no-wrap
msgid "Trees and Forests"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:92
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Sometimes this is stated as <q>a tree is an acyclic connected graph;</q> <q>acyclic</q>\n"
"        is just a fancy word for <q>containing no cycles.</q>\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:91
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A <term>tree</term> is a connected graph containing no cycles.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:98
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A <term>forest</term> is a graph containing no cycles.   Note that this means that a connected forest is a tree.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:104
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Does the definition above agree with your intuition for what graphs we should call trees?\n"
"      Try thinking of examples of trees, and make sure they satisfy the definition.\n"
"      One thing to keep in mind is that while the trees we study in graph theory are related to trees you might see in other subjects,\n"
"      the correspondence is not exact.\n"
"      For instance, in anthropology,\n"
"      you might study family trees, like the one below,\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A family tree with \"Me\" at the top, branching down to the left to \"Mom\" and to the right to \"Dad\".  \"Mom\" has branches leading down to \"Maternal Grandma\" and \"Maternal Grandpa\".  \"Dad\" has branches down to \"Paternal Grandma\" and \"Paternal Grandpa\".\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      So far so good,\n"
"      but while your grandparents are (probably) not blood relatives,\n"
"      if we go back far enough,\n"
"      it is likely that they did have\n"
"      <em>some</em> common ancestor.\n"
"      If you trace the tree back from you to that common ancestor,\n"
"      then down through your other grandparent,\n"
"      you would have a cycle, and thus the graph would not be a tree.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:149
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      You might also have seen something called a\n"
"      <em>decision tree</em>\n"
"      (such as the algorithm for deciding whether a series converges or diverges).\n"
"      Sometimes these too contain cycles,\n"
"      as the decision for one node might lead you back to a previous step.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:159
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Both the examples of trees above also have another feature worth mentioning:\n"
"      There is a clear order to the vertices in the tree.\n"
"      The definition of a tree does not include this added structure,\n"
"      although we can impose such a structure by considering\n"
"      <term>rooted trees</term>,\n"
"      where we simply designate one vertex as the <em>root</em>.\n"
"      We will consider such trees in more detail later in this section.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In this section, we will explore some basic properties of trees, which will serve as an excellent introduction to writing proofs about graphs.  We will also consider a special kind of tree, called a <term>spanning tree</term>, which is a tree that includes all the vertices of a connected graph. Finally, we will briefly consider rooted trees.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:187
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Take out a piece of paper and draw 8 vertices in a circle.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><introduction><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_gt-trees.ptx:190
#, no-wrap
msgid "8 vertices in a circle with no edges"
msgstr ""

