#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:362 source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:371
#, no-wrap
msgid "Euler's Formula for Planar Graphs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:365
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      There is a connection between the number of vertices (<m>v</m>),\n"
"      the number of edges (<m>e</m>), and the number of faces (<m>f</m>) in any connected planar graph.\n"
"      This relationship is called Euler's formula.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:374
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For any connected planar graph with <m>v</m> vertices,\n"
"        <m>e</m> edges, and <m>f</m> faces, we have\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:383
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Why is Euler's formula true?\n"
"      One way to convince ourselves of its validity is to draw a planar graph step by step.\n"
"      Start with the graph <m>P_2</m>:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:398
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Two vertices connected by an edge.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:404
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Any connected graph\n"
"      (besides just a single isolated vertex)\n"
"      must contain this subgraph.\n"
"      Now we build up to our graph by adding edges and vertices.\n"
"      Each step will consist of either adding a new vertex connected by a new edge to part of your graph\n"
"      (so creating a new <q>spike</q>)\n"
"      or by connecting two vertices already in the graph with a new edge\n"
"      (completing a circuit).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:424
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A graph with four vertices arranged in a square. The top left vertex is adjacent to the other three vertices, and the top right and bottom right vertices are also adjacent.  A dashed edge leads from the top right vertex of the square to a fifth vertex below and to its right.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:436
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A graph with four vertices arranged in a square. The top left vertex is adjacent to the other three vertices, and the top right and bottom right vertices are also adjacent.  A dashed edge connects the bottom two vertices.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:443
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      What do these <q>moves</q> do?\n"
"      When adding the spike,\n"
"      the number of edges increases by 1, the number of vertices increases by 1,\n"
"      and the number of faces remains the same.\n"
"      But this means that <m>v - e + f</m> does not change.\n"
"      Completing a circuit adds one edge,\n"
"      adds one face, and keeps the number of vertices the same.\n"
"      So again, <m>v - e + f</m> does not change.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:454
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Since we can build any graph using a combination of these two moves,\n"
"      and doing so never changes the quantity <m>v - e + f</m>,\n"
"      that quantity will be the same for all graphs.\n"
"      But notice that our starting graph <m>P_2</m> has <m>v = 2</m>,\n"
"      <m>e = 1</m>, and <m>f = 1</m>, so <m>v - e + f = 2</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:462
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The argument we have outlined above is not quite correct, since we made the unjustified assumption that all graphs can be built up from <m>P_2</m> using only the two moves we described.  To avoid this issue, we can use a minimal criminal argument.  You are asked to do this in the exercises, but the idea is essentially the same as we have here, except that we start with a minimal connected planar graph that does not satisfy the formula, then <em>remove</em> either an edge or a vertex (and its edge) to get a smaller connected planar graph that does satisfy the formula.  But just like the adding moves we have described above, removing an edge or a vertex does not change the quantity <m>v - e + f</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:467
#, no-wrap
msgid "Non-planar Graphs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:472
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For the complete graphs <m>K_n</m>,\n"
"        we would like to be able to say something about the number of vertices,\n"
"        edges, and\n"
"        (if the graph is planar)\n"
"        faces.\n"
"        Let's first consider <m>K_3</m>:\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:482
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many vertices does <m>K_3</m> have?\n"
"              How many edges?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:489
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If <m>K_3</m> is planar, how many faces should it have?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:496
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Repeat parts (1) and (2) for <m>K_4</m>,\n"
"        <m>K_5</m>, and <m>K_{23}</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:501
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        What about complete bipartite graphs?\n"
"        How many vertices, edges, and faces\n"
"        (if it were planar)\n"
"        does <m>K_{7,4}</m> have?\n"
"        For which values of <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are <m>K_n</m> and <m>K_{m,n}</m> planar?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:509
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Not all graphs are planar.\n"
"      If there are too many edges and too few vertices,\n"
"      then some of the edges will need to intersect.\n"
"      The smallest graph where this happens is <m>K_5</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:524
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A copy of K5: five vertices arranged in a pentagon with edges connecting every vertex to every other vertex.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:531
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If you try to redraw this without edges crossing,\n"
"      you quickly get into trouble.\n"
"      There seems to be one edge too many.\n"
"      In fact, we can prove that no matter how you draw it,\n"
"      <m>K_5</m> will always have edges crossing.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><idx><h><m>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:540 source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:541
#, no-wrap
msgid "K_5"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:543
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>K_5</m> is not planar.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:550
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The proof is by contradiction.\n"
"        So assume that <m>K_5</m> is planar.\n"
"        Then the graph must satisfy Euler's formula for planar graphs. <m>K_5</m> has 5 vertices and 10 edges,\n"
"        so we get\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:556
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"        which says that if the graph is drawn without any edges crossing,\n"
"        there would be <m>f = 7</m> faces.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:562
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Now consider how many edges surround each face.\n"
"        Each face must be surrounded by at least 3 edges.\n"
"        Let <m>B</m> be the total number of <em>boundaries</em>\n"
"        around all the faces in the graph.\n"
"        Thus we have that <m>3f \\le B</m>.\n"
"        But also <m>B = 2e</m>,\n"
"        since each edge is used as a boundary exactly twice.\n"
"        Putting this together we get\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:576
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        But this is impossible,\n"
"        since we have already determined that <m>f = 7</m> and <m>e = 10</m>,\n"
"        and <m>21 \\not\\le 20</m>.\n"
"        This is a contradiction, so in fact <m>K_5</m> is not planar.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:584
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The other simplest graph which is not planar is <m>K_{3,3}</m>\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:595
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A drawing of K3,3 with a row of three vertices on top, each adjacent to the three vertices in a row directly below.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:601
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Proving that <m>K_{3,3}</m> is not planar answers the classic houses and utilities puzzle:\n"
"      it is not possible to connect each of three houses to each of three utilities without the lines crossing.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><idx><h><m>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:609 source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:616
#, no-wrap
msgid "K_{3,3}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:621
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>K_{3,3}</m> is not planar.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:628
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Again, we proceed by contradiction.\n"
"        Suppose <m>K_{3,3}</m> were planar.\n"
"        Then by Euler's formula, there will be 5 faces,\n"
"        since <m>v = 6</m>, <m>e = 9</m>, and <m>6 - 9 + f = 2</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:635
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        How many boundaries surround these 5 faces?\n"
"        Let <m>B</m> be this number.\n"
"        Since each edge is used as a boundary twice, we have <m>B = 2e</m>.\n"
"        Also, <m>B \\ge 4f</m> since each face is surrounded by 4 or more boundaries.\n"
"        We know this is true because <m>K_{3,3}</m> is bipartite,\n"
"        so does not contain any 3-edge cycles.\n"
"        Thus\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:648
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        But this would say that <m>20 \\le 18</m>, which is clearly false.\n"
"        Thus <m>K_{3,3}</m> is not planar.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:654
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Note the similarities and differences in these proofs.\n"
"      Both are proofs by contradiction,\n"
"      and both start with using Euler's formula to derive the (supposed) number of faces in the graph.\n"
"      Then we find a relationship between the number of faces and the number of edges based on how many edges surround each face.\n"
"      This is the only difference.\n"
"      In the proof for <m>K_5</m>,\n"
"      we got <m>3f \\le 2e</m> and for <m>K_{3,3}</m> we had <m>4f \\le 2e</m>.\n"
"      The coefficient of <m>f</m> is the key.\n"
"      It is the smallest number of edges that could surround any face.\n"
"      If some number of edges surround a face,\n"
"      then these edges form a cycle.\n"
"      So that number is the size of the smallest cycle in the graph.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:674
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that for technical reasons, the girth of a graph without any cycles (a forest) is defined to be infinity, and in this case, we definitely don't have <m>gf \\le 2e</m>, even though all trees are planar."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:671
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In general, if we let <m>g</m> be the size of the smallest cycle in a graph (<m>g</m> stands for <em>girth</em>,\n"
"      which is the technical term for this) then for any planar graph we have <m>gf \\le 2e</m>.\n"
"      When this disagrees with Euler's formula,\n"
"      we know for sure that the graph cannot be planar.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:679
#, no-wrap
msgid "Polyhedra"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid "regular polyhedron"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:685
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A cube\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:687
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        is an example of a convex polyhedron.\n"
"        It contains 6 identical squares for its faces, 8 vertices,\n"
"        and 12 edges.\n"
"        The cube is a <term>regular polyhedron</term>\n"
"        (also known as a <term>Platonic solid</term>)\n"
"        because each face is an identical regular polygon and each vertex joins an equal number of faces.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:696
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        There are exactly four other regular polyhedra:\n"
"        the tetrahedron, octahedron, dodecahedron,\n"
"        and icosahedron, with 4, 8, 12, and 20 faces respectively.\n"
"        How many vertices and edges do each of these have?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:713
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      An alternative definition for convex is that the internal angle formed by any two faces must be less than <m>180\\deg</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:706
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Another area of mathematics where you might have heard the terms\n"
"      <q>vertex,</q> <q>edge,</q>\n"
"      and <q>face</q> is geometry.\n"
"      A <term>polyhedron</term>\n"
"      is a geometric solid made up of flat polygonal faces joined at edges and vertices.\n"
"      We are especially interested in <term>convex</term>\n"
"      polyhedra, which means that any line segment connecting two points on the interior of the polyhedron must be entirely contained inside the polyhedron.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:717
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Notice that since <m>8 - 12 + 6 = 2</m>, the vertices,\n"
"      edges, and faces of a cube satisfy Euler's formula for planar graphs.\n"
"      This is not a coincidence.\n"
"      We can represent a cube as a planar graph by projecting the vertices and edges onto the plane.\n"
"      One such projection looks like this:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:733
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Eight vertices arranged as a smaller square inside a larger square.  Edges from the perimeters of both squares, and edges connect each vertex of the small square to its closest vertex of the larger square.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:739
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In fact, <em>every</em> convex polyhedron can be projected onto the plane without edges crossing.\n"
"      Think of placing the polyhedron inside a sphere,\n"
"      with a light at the center of the sphere.\n"
"      The edges and vertices of the polyhedron cast a shadow onto the interior of the sphere.\n"
"      You can then cut a hole in the sphere in the middle of one of the projected faces and <q>stretch</q>\n"
"      the sphere to lie down flat on the plane.\n"
"      The face that was punctured becomes the\n"
"      <q>outside</q> face of the planar graph.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:750
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The point is, we can apply what we know about graphs\n"
"      (in particular planar graphs)\n"
"      to convex polyhedra.\n"
"      Since every convex polyhedron can be represented as a planar graph,\n"
"      we see that Euler's formula for planar graphs holds for all convex polyhedra as well.\n"
"      We also can apply the same sort of reasoning we use for graphs in other contexts to convex polyhedra.\n"
"      For example,\n"
"      we know that there is no convex polyhedron with 11 vertices all of degree 3, as this would make 33/2 edges.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:763
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Is there a convex polyhedron consisting of three triangles and six pentagons?\n"
"          What about three triangles,\n"
"          six pentagons, and five heptagons (7-sided polygons)?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:770
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          How many edges would such polyhedra have?\n"
"          For the first proposed polyhedron,\n"
"          the triangles would contribute a total of 9 edges,\n"
"          and the pentagons would contribute 30.\n"
"          However, this counts each edge twice\n"
"          (as each edge borders exactly two faces),\n"
"          giving 39/2 edges, an impossibility.\n"
"          There is no such polyhedron.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:781
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The second polyhedron does not have this obstacle.\n"
"          The extra 35 edges contributed by the heptagons give a total of 74/2 = 37 edges.\n"
"          So far so good.\n"
"          Now how many vertices does this supposed polyhedron have?\n"
"          We can use Euler's formula.\n"
"          There are 14 faces, so we have\n"
"          <m>v - 37 + 14 = 2</m> or equivalently <m>v = 25</m>.\n"
"          But now use the vertices to count the edges again.\n"
"          Each vertex must have degree <em>at least</em> three\n"
"          (that is, each vertex joins at least three faces since the interior angle of all the polygons must be less that <m>180^\\circ</m>),\n"
"          so the sum of the degrees of vertices is at least 75.\n"
"          Since the sum of the degrees must be exactly twice the number of edges,\n"
"          this says that there are strictly more than 37 edges.\n"
"          Again, there is no such polyhedron.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:800
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      To conclude this application of planar graphs,\n"
"      consider the regular polyhedra.\n"
"      We claimed there are only five.\n"
"      How do we know this is true?\n"
"      We can prove it using graph theory.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:810
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          There are exactly five regular polyhedra.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:817
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Recall that all the faces of a regular polyhedron are identical regular polygons and that each vertex has the same degree.\n"
"        Consider four cases, depending on the type of regular polygon.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p><b>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:822
#, no-wrap
msgid "Case 1"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:822
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
": Each face is a triangle.\n"
"        Let <m>f</m> be the number of faces.\n"
"        There are then <m>3f/2</m> edges.\n"
"        Using Euler's formula, we have\n"
"        <m>v - 3f/2 + f = 2</m> so <m>v = 2 + f/2</m>.\n"
"        Now each vertex has the same degree, say <m>k</m>.\n"
"        So the number of edges is also <m>kv/2</m>.\n"
"        Putting this together gives\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:832
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"        which says\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:840
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Both <m>k</m> and <m>f</m> must be positive integers.\n"
"        Note that <m>\\frac{6f}{4+f}</m> is an increasing function for positive <m>f</m>,\n"
"        bounded above by a horizontal asymptote at <m>k=6</m>.\n"
"        Thus the only possible values for <m>k</m> are 3, 4, and 5.\n"
"        Each of these is possible.\n"
"        To get <m>k = 3</m>, we need <m>f = 4</m>\n"
"        (this is the tetrahedron).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:848
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For <m>k = 4</m> we take <m>f = 8</m>\n"
"        (the octahedron).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:851
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For <m>k = 5</m> take <m>f = 20</m>\n"
"        (the icosahedron).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:854
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Thus there are exactly three regular polyhedra with triangles for faces.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p><b>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:858
#, no-wrap
msgid "Case 2"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:858
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
": Each face is a square.\n"
"        Now we have <m>e = 4f/2 = 2f</m>.\n"
"        Using Euler's formula, we get <m>v = 2 + f</m>,\n"
"        and counting edges using the degree <m>k</m> of each vertex gives us\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:868
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Solving for <m>k</m> gives\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:875
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This is again an increasing function,\n"
"        but this time the horizontal asymptote is at <m>k = 4</m>,\n"
"        so the only possible value that <m>k</m> could take is 3.\n"
"        This produces 6 faces, and we have a cube.\n"
"        There is only one regular polyhedron with square faces.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p><b>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:883
#, no-wrap
msgid "Case 3"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:883
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
": Each face is a pentagon.\n"
"        We perform the same calculation as above,\n"
"        this time getting <m>e = 5f/2</m> so <m>v = 2 + 3f/2</m>.\n"
"        Then\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:889
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"        so\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:897
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Now the horizontal asymptote is at <m>\\frac{10}{3}</m>.\n"
"        This is less than 4, so we can only hope to have <m>k = 3</m>.\n"
"        We can do so by using 12 pentagons, getting the dodecahedron.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:901
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This is the only regular polyhedron with pentagons as faces.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p><b>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:905
#, no-wrap
msgid "Case 4"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:905
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
": Each face is an <m>n</m>-gon with <m>n \\ge 6</m>.\n"
"        Following the same procedure as above, we deduce that\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:914
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Notice that you can tile the plane with hexagons.\n"
"        This is an infinite planar graph;\n"
"        each vertex has degree 3.\n"
"        These infinitely many hexagons correspond to the limit as\n"
"        <m>f \\to \\infty</m> to make <m>k = 3</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_gt-planar.ptx:909
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"        which will be increasing to a horizontal asymptote of <m>\\frac{2n}{n-2}</m>.\n"
"        When <m>n = 6</m>, this asymptote is at <m>k = 3</m>.\n"
"        Any larger value of <m>n</m> will give an even smaller asymptote.\n"
"        Therefore no regular polyhedra exist with faces larger than pentagons.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>"
msgstr ""

