#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:10
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Is it possible for a planar graph to have 6 vertices, 10 edges, and 5 faces?\n"
"        Explain.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        No.\n"
"        A (connected) planar graph must satisfy Euler's formula:\n"
"        <m>v - e + f = 2</m>.\n"
"        Here <m>v - e + f = 6 - 10 + 5 = 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:27
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The graph <m>G</m> has 6 vertices with degrees <m>2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5</m>.\n"
"        How many edges does <m>G</m> have?\n"
"        Could <m>G</m> be planar?\n"
"        If so, how many faces would it have?\n"
"        If not, explain.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:36
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        <m>G</m> has 10 edges, since <m>10 = \\frac{2+2+3+4+4+5}{2}</m>.\n"
"        It could be planar,\n"
"        and then it would have 6 faces, using Euler's formula:\n"
"        <m>6-10+f = 2</m> means <m>f = 6</m>.\n"
"        To make sure that it is actually planar though,\n"
"        we would need to draw a graph with those vertex degrees without edges crossing.\n"
"        This can be done by trial and error\n"
"        (and is possible).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

