#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:85
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        I'm thinking of a polyhedron containing 12 faces.\n"
"        Seven are triangles and four are quadrilaterals.\n"
"        The polyhedron has 11 vertices including those around the mystery face.\n"
"        How many sides does the last face have?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:94
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Say the last polyhedron has <m>n</m> edges\n"
"        and also <m>n</m> vertices.\n"
"        The total number of edges the polyhedron has then is <m>(7 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 4 + n)/2 = (37 + n)/2</m>.\n"
"        In particular,\n"
"        we know the last face must have an odd number of edges.\n"
"        We also have that <m> v = 11 </m>.\n"
"        By Euler's formula, we have <m>11 - (37+n)/2 + 12 = 2</m>,\n"
"        and solving for <m>n</m> we get <m>n = 5</m>,\n"
"        so the last face is a pentagon.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

