#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:154
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove Euler's formula using a minimal criminal argument, where minimum means smallest number of edges\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:161
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Suppose there is a graph <m>G</m> with fewest edges that does not satisfy Euler's formula. \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:165
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t       Note that <m>G</m> cannot be a tree, since for any tree, <m>v = e+1</m> and <m>f = 1</m>, so <m>v - e + f = 2</m>.  Therefore, <m>G</m> must contain a cycle.  Pick any edge <m>e_0</m> that is part of a cycle in <m>G</m> and consider the graph <m>G' = G-e_0</m> that you get by removing just the edge <m>e_0</m> from <m>G</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-planar.ptx:169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Since <m>G'</m> has fewer edges than <m>G</m>, it must satisfy Euler's formula.  That is, <m>v' - e' + f' = 2</m>, where <m>v'</m>, <m>e'</m>, and <m>f'</m> are the number of vertices, edges, and faces of <m>G'</m>.  Since <m>G'</m> is obtained by removing a single edge from a cycle in <m>G</m>, we have <m>v' = v</m>, <m>e'=e-1</m>, and <m>f' = f-1</m>.  Therefore, <m>v - (e-1) + (f-1) = 2</m>, so <m>v - e + f = 2</m> as well.  This is a contradiction, so no such graph <m>G</m> can exist.\n"
"       "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
