#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-paths.ptx:48
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A spider is standing on one face of an octahedron (a polyhedron with eight triangular faces).  She wants to crawl along the solid from face to face so that she crosses each edge exactly once.  Is this possible?  If so, how?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_gt-paths.ptx:52
#, no-wrap
msgid "An octahedron"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-paths.ptx:81
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If we start at a vertex and trace along edges to get to other vertices,\n"
"      we create a <em>walk</em> through the graph.\n"
"      More precisely, a <term>walk</term>\n"
"      in a graph is a sequence of vertices such that every vertex in the sequence is adjacent to the vertices before and after it in the sequence.\n"
"      If the walk travels along every edge exactly once,\n"
"      then the walk is called an <term>Euler trail</term>\n"
"      (or <term>Euler walk</term> or <term>Euler path</term>).\n"
"      If, in addition, the starting and ending vertices are the same\n"
"      (so you trace along every edge exactly once and end up where you started),\n"
"      then the walk is called an <term>Euler circuit</term>\n"
"      (or <term>Euler tour</term>).\n"
"      Of course if a graph is not connected,\n"
"      there is no hope of finding such a trail or circuit.\n"
"      For the rest of this section,\n"
"      assume all the graphs discussed are connected.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-paths.ptx:101
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The bridges of Königsberg problem is really a question about the existence of Euler trails.\n"
"      There will be a route that crosses every bridge exactly once if and only if the multigraph below has an Euler trail:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-paths.ptx:114
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Three vertices aligned in a vertical column left of a single vertex on the right.  Edges connect the vertex on the right to each vertex on the left.  Among the vertices on the left, two arced edges connect the bottom vertex to the center vertex, and two more connect the center vertex to the top vertex.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-paths.ptx:120
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This graph is small enough that we could actually check every possible walk that does not reuse edges,\n"
"      and in doing so convince ourselves that there is no Euler trail\n"
"      (let alone an Euler circuit).\n"
"      On small graphs that do have an Euler trail,\n"
"      it is usually not difficult to find one.\n"
"      Our goal is to find a quick way to check whether a graph has an Euler trail or circuit,\n"
"      even if the graph is quite large.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

