#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:35
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Mapmakers in the fictional land of Euleria have drawn the borders of the various dukedoms of the land.\n"
"        To make the map pretty, they wish to color each region.\n"
"        Adjacent regions must be colored differently,\n"
"        but it is perfectly fine to color two distant regions with the same color.\n"
"        What is the fewest colors the mapmakers can use and still accomplish this task?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:62
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          An abstract drawing of a \"map\" of regions separated by slightly curved borders.  The map contains an outer ring of seven regions (all different sizes), an inner ring of five more regions, and a final odd shaped center region.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:68
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Perhaps the most famous graph theory problem is how to color maps.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Given any map of countries, states, counties,\n"
"        etc., how many colors are needed to color each region on the map so that neighboring regions are colored differently?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:79
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Actual map makers usually use around seven colors.\n"
"      For one thing,\n"
"      they require watery regions to be a specific color,\n"
"      and with a lot of colors it is easier to find a permissible coloring.\n"
"      We want to know whether there is a smaller palette that will work for any map.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:87
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      How is this related to graph theory?\n"
"      Well, if we place a vertex in the center of each region\n"
"      (say in the capital of each state)\n"
"      and then connect two vertices if their states share a border,\n"
"      we get a graph.\n"
"      Coloring regions on the map corresponds to coloring the vertices of the graph.\n"
"      Since neighboring regions cannot be colored the same,\n"
"      our graph cannot have vertices colored the same when those vertices are adjacent.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:103
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In general, given any graph <m>G</m>,\n"
"      a coloring of the vertices is called\n"
"      (not surprisingly)\n"
"      a <term>vertex coloring</term>.\n"
"      If the vertex coloring has the property that adjacent vertices are colored differently,\n"
"      then the coloring is called <term>proper</term>.\n"
"      Every graph has a proper vertex coloring.\n"
"      For example,\n"
"      you could color every vertex with a different color.\n"
"      But often you can do better.\n"
"      The smallest number of colors needed to get a proper vertex coloring is called the\n"
"      <term>chromatic number</term>\n"
"      of the graph, written <m>\\chi(G)</m>.\n"
"\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\chi(G)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "the chromatic number of <m>G</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Our goal in this section is to see how graph coloring can be used to solve some problems and to understand some basic properties of graph coloring.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

