#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:195
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        How many different time slots are needed to teach these classes (and which should be taught at the same time)?\n"
"        More importantly,\n"
"        how could we use graph coloring to answer this question?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:202
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The best way to get a feel for the chromatic number is to actually try to color some graphs.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:207
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Find the chromatic number of the graphs below.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:220
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A copy of K6: six vertices arranged in a hexagon, with every vertex adjacent to every other vertex.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:231
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Six vertices arranged in a triangle (with three vertices along each side).  Six edges form the outside of the triangle, and three edges connect the center vertices of each side (in an upside-down triangle).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:242
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A copy of K2,3, with a row of two vertices on a top row, each adjacent to each of three vertices on a bottom row\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:250
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The graph on the left is <m>K_6</m>.\n"
"          The only way to properly color the graph is to give every vertex a different color\n"
"          (since every vertex is adjacent to every other vertex).\n"
"          Thus the chromatic number is 6.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:257
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The middle graph can be properly colored with just 3 colors (Red, Blue,\n"
"          and Green).\n"
"          For example:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:269
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Six vertices arranged in a triangle (with three vertices along each side).  Six edges form the outside of the triangle, and three edges connect the center vertices of each side (in an upside-down triangle).  The bottom row of vertices are labeled R, B, G (left to right), the middle row of vertices are labeled G, R, and to top vertex is labeled B.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:275
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          There is no way to color it with just two colors,\n"
"          since there are three vertices mutually adjacent (i.e., a triangle).\n"
"          Thus the chromatic number is 3.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:281
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The graph on the right is just <m>K_{2,3}</m>.\n"
"          As with all bipartite graphs,\n"
"          this graph has chromatic number 2:\n"
"          color the vertices on the top row red and the vertices on the bottom row blue.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:290
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      It appears that there is no limit to how large chromatic numbers can get.\n"
"      It should not come as a surprise that <m>K_n</m> has chromatic number <m>n</m>.\n"
"      So how could there possibly be an answer to the original map coloring question?\n"
"      If the chromatic number of a graph can be arbitrarily large,\n"
"      then it seems like there would be no upper bound to the number of colors needed for any map.\n"
"      But there is.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:299
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The key observation is that while it is true that for any number <m>n</m>\n"
"      there is a graph with chromatic number <m>n</m>,\n"
"      only some graphs arrive as representations of maps.\n"
"      If you convert a map to a graph,\n"
"      the edges between vertices correspond to borders between the countries.\n"
"      So you should be able to connect vertices in such a way that the edges do not cross.\n"
"      In other words,\n"
"      the graphs representing maps are all <em>planar</em>!\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:310
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      So the question is,\n"
"      what is the largest chromatic number of any planar graph?\n"
"      The answer is the best-known theorem of graph theory:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:319
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Four Color Theorem"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:322
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          If <m>G</m> is a planar graph,\n"
"          then the chromatic number of <m>G</m> is less than or equal to 4.\n"
"          Thus any map can be properly colored with 4 or fewer colors.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We will not prove this theorem.\n"
"      Really.\n"
"      Even though the theorem is easy to state and understand,\n"
"      the proof is not.\n"
"      In fact, there is currently no <q>easy</q>\n"
"      known proof of the theorem.\n"
"      The current best proof still requires powerful computers to check an\n"
"      <em>unavoidable set</em>\n"
"      of 633 <em>reducible configurations</em>.\n"
"      The idea is that every graph must contain one of these reducible configurations\n"
"      (this fact also needs to be checked by a computer)\n"
"      and that reducible configurations can, in fact,\n"
"      be colored in 4 or fewer colors.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:347
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Cartography is certainly not the only application of graph coloring.\n"
"      There are plenty of situations in which you might wish to partition the objects in question so that related objects are not in the same set.\n"
"      For example, you might wish to store chemicals safely.\n"
"      To avoid explosions,\n"
"      certain pairs of chemicals should not be stored in the same room.\n"
"      By coloring a graph\n"
"      (with vertices representing chemicals and edges representing potential negative interactions),\n"
"      you can determine the smallest number of rooms needed to store the chemicals.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:358
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Here is a further example:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:364
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Radio stations broadcast their signal at certain frequencies.\n"
"          However, there are a limited number of frequencies to choose from,\n"
"          so nationwide, many stations use the same frequency.\n"
"          This works because the stations are far enough apart that their signals will not interfere;\n"
"          no one radio could pick them up at the same time.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:372
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Suppose 10 new radio stations are to be set up in a currently unpopulated\n"
"          (by radio stations)\n"
"          region.\n"
"          The radio stations that are close enough to each other to cause interference are recorded in the table below.\n"
"          What is the fewest number of frequencies the stations could use?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:391
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEA"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:395
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEB"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:398
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEC"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:401
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQED"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:404
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:407
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEF"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:410
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEG"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387
#, no-wrap
msgid " KQEH"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:416
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEI"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:387 source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:419
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEJ"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:413
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQEH "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:428
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Represent the problem as a graph with vertices as the stations and edges when two stations are close enough to cause interference.\n"
"          We are looking for the chromatic number of the graph.\n"
"          Vertices that are colored identically represent stations that can have the same frequency.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:434
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          This graph has chromatic number 5.\n"
"          A proper 5-coloring is shown on the right.\n"
"          Notice that the graph contains a copy of the complete graph <m>K_5</m>, so no fewer than 5 colors can be used.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:461
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A drawing of the graph representing the radio stations with edges between vertices if those radio stations interfere with each other.  Vertices are arranged in a ring with KQEA at the top, and proceeding clockwise to KQEB, and so on through KQEH.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:486
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A drawing of the graph representing the radio stations with edges between vertices if those radio stations interfere with each other.  Here each vertex is labeled with a letter representing a color.  From the top vertex and moving around clockwise: R, G, B, B, G, G, Y, R, B, P.  A copy of of the graph K5 is drawn in bold among the edges of the original graph.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:495
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In the example above,\n"
"      the chromatic number was 5, but this is not a counterexample to the <xref po4a-id=0>Four Color Theorem</xref>,\n"
"      since the graph representing the radio stations is not planar.\n"
"      It would be nice to have some quick way to find the chromatic number of a\n"
"      (possibly non-planar)\n"
"      graph.\n"
"      It turns out nobody knows whether an efficient algorithm for computing chromatic numbers exists.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:505
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      While we might not be able to find the exact chromatic number of a graph easily,\n"
"      we can often give a reasonable range for the chromatic number.\n"
"      In other words,\n"
"      we can give upper and lower bounds for the chromatic number.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:512
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This is not very difficult:\n"
"      for every graph <m>G</m>,\n"
"      the chromatic number of <m>G</m> is at least 1 and at most the number of vertices of <m>G</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:518
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      What?\n"
"      You want <em>better</em> bounds on the chromatic number?\n"
"      Well, you are in luck.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:526
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A <term>clique</term>\n"
"      in a graph is a set of vertices all of which are pairwise adjacent.\n"
"      In other words,\n"
"      a clique of size <m>n</m> is just a copy of the complete graph <m>K_n</m>.\n"
"      We define the <term>clique number</term>\n"
"      of a graph to be the largest <m>n</m> for which the graph contains a clique of size <m>n</m>.\n"
"      Any clique of size <m>n</m> cannot be colored with fewer than <m>n</m> colors,\n"
"      so we have a nice lower bound:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:539
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The chromatic number of a graph <m>G</m> is at least the clique number of <m>G</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:555
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      There are special classes of graphs that can be proved to be perfect.\n"
"      One such class is the set of <term>chordal</term> graphs,\n"
"      which have the property that every cycle in the graph contains a\n"
"      <term>chord</term>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:558
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"an edge between two vertices in the cycle which are not adjacent in the cycle.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:549
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      There are times when the chromatic number of <m>G</m> is\n"
"      <em>equal</em> to the clique number.\n"
"      These graphs have a special name;\n"
"      they are called <term>perfect</term>.\n"
"      If you know that a graph is perfect,\n"
"      then finding the chromatic number is simply a matter of searching for the largest clique.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/> However, not all graphs are perfect.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:566
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      For an upper bound, we can improve on\n"
"      <q>the number of vertices</q>\n"
"      by looking at the degrees of vertices.\n"
"      Let <m>\\Delta(G)</m>\n"
"\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:572
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\Delta(G)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:573
#, no-wrap
msgid "the maximum degree in <m>G</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:576
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      be the largest degree of any vertex in the graph <m>G</m>.\n"
"      One reasonable guess for an upper bound on the chromatic number is <m>\\chi(G) \\le \\Delta(G) + 1</m>.\n"
"      Why is this reasonable?\n"
"      Starting with any vertex,\n"
"      it together with all of its neighbors can always be colored in <m>\\Delta(G) + 1</m> colors,\n"
"      since at most we are talking about\n"
"      <m>\\Delta(G) + 1</m> vertices in this set.\n"
"      Now fan out!\n"
"      At any point, if you consider an already colored vertex,\n"
"      some of its neighbors might be colored, some might not.\n"
"      But no matter what,\n"
"      that vertex and its neighbors could all be colored distinctly,\n"
"      since there are at most <m>\\Delta(G)</m> neighbors,\n"
"      plus the one vertex being considered.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:593
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In fact, there are examples of graphs for which <m>\\chi(G) = \\Delta(G) + 1</m>.\n"
"      For any <m>n</m>,\n"
"      the complete graph <m>K_n</m> has chromatic number <m>n</m>,\n"
"      but <m>\\Delta(K_n) = n-1</m>\n"
"      (since every vertex is adjacent to every <em>other</em> vertex).\n"
"      Additionally,\n"
"      any <em>odd</em> cycle will have chromatic number 3, but the degree of every vertex in a cycle is 2.\n"
"      It turns out that these are the only two types of examples where we get equality,\n"
"      a result known as Brooks' Theorem.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:605
#, no-wrap
msgid "Brooks' Theorem"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:609
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Any graph <m>G</m> satisfies <m>\\chi(G) \\le \\Delta(G)</m>,\n"
"          unless <m>G</m> is a complete graph or an odd cycle,\n"
"          in which case <m>\\chi(G) = \\Delta(G) + 1</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:617
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The proof of this theorem is <em>just</em>\n"
"      complicated enough that we will not present it here\n"
"      (although you are asked to prove a special case in the exercises).\n"
"      The adventurous reader is encouraged to find a book on graph theory to find suggestions for how to prove the theorem.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:626
#, no-wrap
msgid "Coloring Edges"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:630
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The chromatic number of a graph tells us about coloring vertices,\n"
"      but we could also ask about coloring edges.\n"
"      Just like with vertex coloring,\n"
"      we might insist that adjacent edges must be colored differently.\n"
"      Here, we are thinking of two edges as being adjacent if they are incident to the same vertex.\n"
"      The least number of colors required to properly color the edges of a graph <m>G</m> is called the\n"
"      <term>chromatic index</term>\n"
"      of <m>G</m>, written <m>\\chi'(G)</m>.\n"
"\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:640
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\chi'(G)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:641
#, no-wrap
msgid "the chromatic index of <m>G</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:648
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Six friends decide to spend the afternoon playing chess.\n"
"          Everyone will play everyone else once.\n"
"          They have plenty of chess sets, but nobody wants to play more than one game at a time.\n"
"          Games will last an hour\n"
"          (thanks to their handy chess clocks).\n"
"          How many hours will the tournament last?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:658
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Represent each player with a vertex and put an edge between two players if they play each other.\n"
"          In this case, we get the graph <m>K_6</m>:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:675
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"           The graph K6: six vertices (arranged in a circle), each adjacent to the other five.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:681
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          We must color the edges; each color represents a different hour.\n"
"          Since different edges incident to the same vertex will be colored differently,\n"
"          no player will be playing two different games (edges) at the same time.\n"
"          Thus we need to know the chromatic index of <m>K_6</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:688
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Notice that for sure <m>\\chi'(K_6) \\ge 5</m>,\n"
"          since there is a vertex of degree 5.\n"
"          It turns out, 5 colors is enough\n"
"          (go find such a coloring).\n"
"          Therefore the friends will play for 5 hours.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:697
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Interestingly, if one of the friends in the above example left,\n"
"      the remaining 5 chessletes would still need 5 hours:\n"
"      the chromatic index of <m>K_5</m> is also 5.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In general, what can we say about the chromatic index?\n"
"      Certainly <m>\\chi'(G) \\ge \\Delta(G)</m>.\n"
"      But how much higher could it be?\n"
"      Only a little higher.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:710
#, no-wrap
msgid "Vizing's Theorem"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:714
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          For any graph <m>G</m>, the chromatic index <m>\\chi'(G)</m> is either\n"
"          <m>\\Delta(G)</m> or <m>\\Delta(G) + 1</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:721
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      At first, this theorem makes it seem like the chromatic index might not be very interesting.\n"
"      However, deciding which case a graph is in is not always easy.\n"
"      Graphs for which <m>\\chi'(G) = \\Delta(G)</m> are called <em>class 1</em>,\n"
"      while the others are called <em>class 2</em>.\n"
"      Bipartite graphs always satisfy <m>\\chi'(G) = \\Delta(G)</m>,\n"
"      so are class 1\n"
"      (this was proved by König in 1916,\n"
"      decades before Vizing proved his theorem in 1964).\n"
"      In 1965 Vizing proved that all planar graphs with\n"
"      <m>\\Delta(G) \\ge 8</m> are of class 1, but this does not hold for all planar graphs with <m>2 \\le \\Delta(G) \\le 5</m>.\n"
"      Vizing conjectured that all planar graphs with\n"
"      <m>\\Delta(G) = 6</m> or <m>\\Delta(G) = 7</m> are class 1;\n"
"      the <m>\\Delta(G) = 7</m> case was proved in 2001 by Sanders and Zhao;\n"
"      the <m>\\Delta(G) = 6</m> case is still open.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><title>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:740
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ramsey Theory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:745
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        There is another interesting way we might consider coloring edges,\n"
"        quite different from what we have discussed so far.\n"
"        What if we colored every edge of a graph either red or blue?\n"
"        Can we do so without, say, creating a <em>monochromatic</em>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:750
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        triangle (i.e., an all red or all blue triangle)?\n"
"        Certainly, for some graphs the answer is yes.\n"
"        Try doing so for <m>K_4</m>.\n"
"        What about <m>K_5</m>? <m>K_6</m>?\n"
"        How far can we go?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:758
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The problem above is not too difficult and is a fun exercise.\n"
"        We could extend the question in a variety of ways.\n"
"        What if we had three colors?\n"
"        What if we were trying to avoid other graphs?\n"
"        Surprisingly, very little is known about these questions.\n"
"        For example,\n"
"        we know that you need to go up to <m>K_{17}</m> in order to force a monochromatic triangle using three colors,\n"
"        but nobody knows how big you need to go with more colors.\n"
"        Similarly, we know that using two colors, <m>K_{18}</m> is the smallest graph that forces a monochromatic copy of <m>K_4</m>,\n"
"        but the best we have to force a monochromatic <m>K_{5}</m> is a range,\n"
"        somewhere from <m>K_{43}</m> to <m>K_{49}</m>.\n"
"        If you are interested in these sorts of questions,\n"
"        this area of graph theory is called Ramsey theory.\n"
"        Check it out.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-coloring.ptx:780
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          True or false: if a graph contains a vertex of degree 5, then the chromatic number of the graph is at least 5.  Explain.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

