#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:269
#, no-wrap
msgid "Relations Generally"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:271
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA graph is a way to represent some ways that different objects are related.\n"
"\t\t\tWe have seen how to use graphs to represent which people are friends, or which classes have time conflicts, or which radio stations are too close to have the same frequency.\n"
"\t\t\tNot all ways in which things can be related can be represented by a graph, however.\n"
"\t\t\tIn this section, we will consider the more general concept of a relation and see how those might be related to graphs.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:278
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tConsider the example of the relation between students and classes that holds when a student is in that class (in a particular semester).\n"
"\t\t\tThis is a relation between two different sets (the students and the classes).\n"
"\t\t\tIf we used a graph to illustrate this relation, the graph would be <em>bipartite</em>, since two students are never related to each other, and two classes are never related to each other.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:284
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA graph is really a set of vertices and a set of edges: <m>G = (V, E)</m>; each element of the set <m>E</m> is a two-element subset of <m>V</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tIf we want to draw attention to the bipartiteness of the graph, we can split up <m>V</m> into its two sets and write <m>G = ((A, B), E)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tIn this notation, for the graph to be bipartite, we want each edge to be a pair <m>(a,b)</m> where <m>a</m> is an element of <m>A</m> and <m>b</m> is an element of <m>B</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tIn other words, each edge is an element of the <term>Cartesian product</term> of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, written,\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:291
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t(Another way to say this is that <m>A \\times B</m> is <q>the set of all ordered pairs of elements from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.</q>)\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><note><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:296
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThere is one subtlety here we should point out: the bipartite graph we have described really has <em>directed edges</em> from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> since we are considering <em>ordered</em> pairs.\n"
"\t\t\t\tOur definition of a graph has edges as two-element <em>subsets</em> of vertices, and subsets are not ordered.\n"
"\t\t\t\tAs long as <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are disjoint sets, there is no confusion here, but we see relations in which <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> share some elements, but we still care about the order.\n"
"\t\t\t\tMore on that soon.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:304
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis example exactly illustrates what a general binary relation is.\n"
"\t\t\tHere is the careful definition.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:311
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tA <term>binary relation</term> is a set of ordered pairs.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe say the binary relation is a <term>relation on sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></term> provided the ordered pairs are a subset of <m>A \\times B</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe say a binary relation is a <term>relation on a set <m>A</m></term> provided the ordered pairs are a subset of <m>A \\times A</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:319
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNote that <m>A \\times A</m> is just the set of all ordered pairs where both coordinates are elements from <m>A</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:325
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider a set <m>A</m> of students and a set <m>B</m> of classes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSay <m>A = \\{\\text{Al, Bob, Cat, Dirk, Eva}\\}</m> and <m>B = \\{\\text{Calculus, Discrete, Statistics}\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tEveryone except Dirk is in Calculus, Bob and Eva are in Discrete, and Al, Cat, and Dirk are in Statistics.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:331
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can define a relation <m>T</m> of <q>is taking</q> <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> that holds of a student and class precisely if that student is taking that class.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWrite this relation as a subset of <m>A\\times B</m> and draw its bipartite graph.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:338
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tTo write the relation precisely, we just give the set of ordered pairs:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:346
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can draw this relation as a bipartite graph:\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:350
#, no-wrap
msgid "A bipartite graph representing the \"is taking\" relation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:361
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tConsider the relation <m>M</m> for <q>is a multiple of</q> on the set <m>A = \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWrite this as a set of ordered pairs.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tDoes this relation create a graph?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:369
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tFirst, let's think about which elements should be related and which should not.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe know that <m>6</m> is a multiple of <m>2</m>, so the relation is true of the pair <m>(6,2)</m>, but <m>6</m> is not a multiple of <m>4</m>, so the pair <m>(6,4)</m> does not satisfy the relation.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tMore precisely, we say that <m>(6,2) \\in M</m> but <m>(6,4) \\notin M</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:375
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLet's list all the elements of <m>M</m>:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis relation is not a graph for two reasons: first, elements are related to themselves, and second, the order of elements in the relation is not symmetric (<m>6</m> is related to <m>2</m>, but <m>2</m> is not related to <m>6</m>, for example).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:384
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can, however, still draw something like a graph to illustrate this relation: Since the direction of the relation matters, we will have <em>directed</em> edges.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis alone would create a <term>directed graph</term>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSince vertices can have edges going to themselves, we would call the structure a <term>multigraph</term>, so the relation can be thought of as a <term>directed multigraph</term>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:390
#, no-wrap
msgid "A directed multigraph for the relation \"is a multiple of\" on the set 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:399
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA binary relation <m>R</m> on sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> can always be <q>turned around</q> to give a relation on sets <m>B</m> and <m>A</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThat is, <m>R</m> says how things in <m>A</m> are related to things in <m>B</m>; those things in <m>B</m> are related to things in <m>A</m>, just in an <em>inverse</em> (backward) way.\n"
"\t\t\tWe will call this new relation the <term>inverse</term> of <m>R</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:407
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tGiven a binary relation <m>R</m>, define <m>R\\inv</m> to be the <term>inverse</term> of <m>R</m> as the set\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:416
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe relation <m>T</m> from <xref po4a-id=0> said that a given student was in a particular class.\n"
"\t\t\tThe inverse relation <m>T\\inv</m> says that a given class has a particular student in it.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, <m>(\\text{Calculus}, \\text{Al})</m> is an element of <m>T\\inv</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tGraphically, there won't be any difference in the picture, although we could put the set of classes on top and students on bottom.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:423
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe relation <m>M</m> from <xref po4a-id=0> gave us that, for example, <m>6</m> is a multiple of <m>2</m>, since <m>(6,2) \\in M</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tFor the inverse, we have <m>(2,6) \\in M\\inv</m>, which means that <m>2</m> is a factor of <m>6</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tFor this example, we have represented a relation as a directed multigraph.\n"
"\t\t\tThe graph of the inverse will look exactly the same, but all the arrows will point in the opposite direction.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:430
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAnother way to create a new relation is to combine two relations.\n"
"\t\t\tSuppose in addition to the <q>is taking</q> relation from <xref po4a-id=0>, we define a relation <q>is taught by</q> that matches up each course with its instructor.\n"
"\t\t\tPerhaps Professor X teaches Calculus.\n"
"\t\t\tIn that case, since Al is taking Calculus, and Calculus is taught by Professor X, we can conclude that Al is taking a class with Professor X.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:439
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tLet <m>R</m> be a relation from set <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> and <m>S</m> be a relation from <m>B</m> to <m>C</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe <term>composition</term> of <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> is\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:449
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNote the order in which we wrote the two relations: it's <m>S \\circ R</m>, not <m>R \\circ S</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThe reason we do this (which might seem backward) is that it agrees with the usual notation for composition of <em>functions</em>.\n"
"\t\t\tIn fact, functions are nothing but a specific type of relation!\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:457
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWrite out the relation <m>P \\circ T</m>, composing the relations of <xref po4a-id=0> and <m>P = \\{(\\text{Calculus}, \\text{Prof X}), (\\text{Discrete}, \\text{Prof L}), (\\text{Statistics}, \\text{Prof X}), (\\text{Statistics}, \\text{Prof S})\\} </m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tNote that here we are saying the statistic course is co-taught by professors X and S.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:464
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe relation we are looking for is a relation between students and professors.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can start with each element of <m>T</m> and <em>extend</em> it by following the course to the professor(s) via <m>P</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo for example, start with Cat, and notice that <m>(\\text{Cat}, \\text{Calculus}) \\in T</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tNow look at what Calculus is related to via <m>P</m>: <m>(\\text{Calculus}, \\text{Prof X}) \\in P </m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThus we can push these together to conclude that <m>(\\text{Cat}, \\text{Prof X}) \\in P \\circ T</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:472
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAn alternative approach would be to simply consider which pairs of students and professors are linked by a common class.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tShould the pair <m>(\\text{Al}, \\text{Prof L})</m> be an element of the composition?  No, because there is no class that is both taken by Al and taught by Professor L.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOn the other hand, we can conclude the <m>(\\text{Al}, \\text{Prof X}) \\in P \\circ T</m> since there is a common course.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIn fact, the common course could be Calculus or Statistics (it doesn't matter how many common middle steps there are, as long as there is at least one).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:479
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHere is the complete relation:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:490
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSomething interesting often happens when you compose a relation with its inverse.\n"
"\t\t\tYou might have seen something like this for <em>functions</em>\n"
"\t\t\tin calculus or algebra: <m>f(x) = e^x</m> has an inverse function <m>f\\inv(x) = \\ln(x)</m>, and we know that <m>f(f\\inv(x)) = e^{\\ln(x)} = x</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tBut functions are relations in which every <q>input</q> has exactly one <q>output</q>, so perhaps it is not surprising that composing with an inverse just gives you the identity function.\n"
"\t\t\tWhen inputs can have multiple outputs, and outputs can have multiple inputs, then we get something more.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:500
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tDescribe the relation <m>T\\inv \\circ T</m> (using our familiar relation <m>T</m> from <xref po4a-id=0>).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat does this tell us about students and classes?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:507
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tBy following the relation, we could start by saying Al is in Calculus, and Calculus is taken by Al, so Al is related to Al.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBut also , Calculus is taken by Bob, so now Al is also related to Bob.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tIs Bob related to Al as well?  Yes, since Bob is taking Calculus and Calculus is taken by Al.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:513
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe composition is telling us which pairs of students have at least one class in common.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThat's almost all pairs of students, except that Dirk is not related to Bob or Eva, since they don't take Statistics, and that is the only class he takes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:518
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tInstead of writing out the relation (which will almost be all of <m>A \\times A</m>), here is the graph representation.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:522
#, no-wrap
msgid "The graph representing the composition of T and its inverse."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:529
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhile we drew a directed multigraph here, the arrows going both ways mean that we really could have drawn just a multigraph.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:536
#, no-wrap
msgid "Properties of Relations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:538
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tFrom this point on, we will just consider relations on a single set (so from a set to itself).\n"
"\t\t\tTo help understand these relations, let's consider some basic properties a relation might or might not have.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:543
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reflexive, Symmetric, and Transitive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:547
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Ler <m>R</m> be a relation on the set <m>A</m>.  We say,\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:551
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>R</m> is <term>reflexive</term> provided <m>(a,a) \\in A</m> for all <m>a \\in A</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:556
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>R</m> is <term>symmetric</term> provided, for all <m>a, b \\in A</m>, if <m>(a,b) \\in R</m> then <m>(b,a) \\in R</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:561
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>R</m> is <term>transitive</term> provided, for all <m>a, b,c \\in A</m>, if <m>(a,b) \\in R</m> and <m>(b,c) \\in R</m>, then <m>(a,c)\\in R</m>. \n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:570
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Let's examine each of these properties carefully.  \n"
"      \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:575
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      It will be helpful to consider a few standard examples of relations on sets as we go.  Most relations we consider here will be written using <em>infix</em> notation, just meaning that we put the relation symbol between the two things it is relating.  For example, the <em>less than</em> relation is almost always written as <m>2 \\lt 6</m> rather than writing <m>(2,6) \\in \\lt</m>.  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:579
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reflexive and non-reflexive relations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:581
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A relation is reflexive when every element is related to itself.  The following are reflexive relations:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:585
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>less-than-or-equal-to</q> relation on any set of numbers.  Is it the case that <m>3 \\le 3</m>?  More importantly, is every number no greater than itself?  Since the answer is yes, this relation is reflexive.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:590
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>within 3</q> relation, that holds of two numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> provided <m>|a-b| \\le 3</m>.  To prove that this is reflexive, we simply note that <m>|a-a| = 0 \\le 3</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:595
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>is a multiple of</q> relation from <xref po4a-id=0>.  Note that the directed multigraph for this relation had loops at every vertex.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        However, these relations are not reflexive:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:606
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>sums to zero</q> relation, that holds on numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> if <m>a+b = 0</m>.  Note that while <m>0 + 0 = 0</m>, so <m>(0,0)</m> is an element of the relation, every other number is not related to itself.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:611
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Any relation that is described by a graph.  Remember, graphs cannot have edges looping back to a single vertex, so the edge relation on a graph is not reflexive.  (A multigraph could be reflexive or not).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:619
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If no element is related to itself (such as in the edge relation for a graph), then we call the relation <term>irreflexive</term>.  Of course, some relations are neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:623
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Checking that a relation is reflexive is relatively easy.  The other two properties are phrased as implications, which makes them a little more complex.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:627
#, no-wrap
msgid "Symmetric and non-symmetric relations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:629
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Essentially, symmetric relations are the ones that work <q>both ways.</q>  More precisely, if <m>a</m> is related to <m>b</m>, then <m>b</m> is also related to <m>a</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:633
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The following relations are symmetric.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:637
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>within 3</q> relation: if <m>|a-b| \\le 3</m> then <m>|b-a| \\le 3</m>.  \n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:642
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>sums to zero</q> relation: if <m>a+b = 0</m>, then certainly <m>b + a = 0</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:647
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              For any graph, the edge relation is symmetric.  Of course, for <em>directed</em> graphs this is usually not true.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:653
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        On the other hand, these relations are not symmetric.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:657
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>\\le</m> is not symmetric.  All we need to do to prove that a relation is not symmetric is to find some <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that <m>a \\le b</m> but <m>b \\not\\le a</m>.  Well, <m>3 \\le 4</m>, but <m>4 \\not\\le 3</m>.  QED.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:662
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>is a multiple of</q> relation is not symmetric.  <m>6</m> is a multiple of <m>2</m> but <m>2</m> is not a multiple of <m>6</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:670
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Relations that are not symmetric could in fact be <term>antisymmetric</term>, meaning the <em>only</em> elements for which both <m>(a,b)</m> and <m>(b,a)</m> are in the relation is when <m>a = b</m>.  Note that there are relations that are neither symmetric nor antisymmetric.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:674
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Using the language of inverse relations, a relation is symmetric if and only if the relation is equal to its inverse.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:678
#, no-wrap
msgid "Transitive and non-transitive relations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:680
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        If <m>a</m> is related to <m>b</m>, and <m>b</m> is related to <m>c</m>, does that mean <m>a</m> is related to <m>c</m>?  If this is true no matter what <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> are, then we say the relation is transitive.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Here are some transitive relations.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:688
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>\\le</m>.  Suppose <m>a \\le b</m> and <m>b \\le c</m>.  Then clearly <m>a \\le c</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:693
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>is a multiple of</q> relation.  This is a good one to write a proof for: Suppose <m>a</m> is a multiple of <m>b</m> and that <m>b</m> is a multiple of <m>c</m>.  Then <m>a = bk</m> for some integer <m>k</m> and <m>b = cj</m> for some integer <m>j</m>.  By substitution, <m>a = cjk</m>, so <m>a</m> is a multiple of <m>c</m>. \n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:699
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        However, the following relations are not transitive.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>within 3</q> relation is not transitive.  All we need to do is find three numbers that fail to meet the condition.  How about <m>1</m>, <m>3</m>, and <m>5</m>?  Here <m>1</m> is within 3 of <m>3</m>, and <m>3</m> is within 3 of <m>5</m>, but <m>|1 - 5| = 4</m> so the relation does not hold of <m>(1,5)</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:708
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <q>sums to zero</q> relation is not transitive.  Notice that we never claimed that <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> need to be different numbers.  Let <m>a = 5</m>, <m>b = -5</m>, and <m>c = 5</m>.  Then <m>a+b = 0</m> and <m>b + c = 0</m>, so the relation holds of <m>(a,b)</m> and <m>(b,c)</m>.  But <m>a + c = 10</m> so the relation does not hold of <m>(a,c)</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:714
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The edge relation for a graph might or might not be transitive.  What would a graph look like if its edge relation was transitive?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:719
#, no-wrap
msgid "Equivalence Relations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:721
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Now we will do something very typical for mathematics: \n"
"        We will look at our most common types of relations, consider what properties these have, and then classify other relations that also have these properties as a specific class of relations.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:726
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The relation we are all most familiar with is <term>equality</term>.  Which properties of relations does the equality relation possess?  Certainly everything is equal to itself, so equality is <em>reflexive</em>.  If <m>a = b</m>, then <m>b = a</m>, so equality is <em>symmetric</em>.  If <m>a = b</m> and <m>b = c</m>, then <m>a = c</m>, so equality is <em>transitive</em>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:730
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        What other relations are <em>reflexive</em>, <em>symmetric</em>, and <em>transitive</em>?  Exactly those relations that behave like equality.  We call such relations <term>equivalence relations</term>.  \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:734
#, no-wrap
msgid "Equivalence Relation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:737
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            A relation that is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive is called an <term>equivalence relation</term>.            \n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><remark><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:744
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Another example of a type of relation that is modeled after a classic relation is a <term>partial order</term>.  This is a relation that is reflexive, <em>antisymmetric</em>, and transitive, just like less-than-or-equal-to.  Perhaps you noticed already that the subset relation, written <m>\\subseteq</m>, feels a lot like <m>\\le</m>.  This is because <m>\\subseteq</m> is also a partial order.  So is the <q>is a multiple of</q> relation we saw above.  \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><remark><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:747
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          There are lots of interesting things we can say about partial orders and the sets they partially order, called <term>partially ordered sets</term> or <term>PoSets</term>.  Another time.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:752
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        None of the examples we have considered so far in this section have been equivalence relations, but they are ubiquitous in mathematics.  They are so common that it is easy to overlook them as anything worth saying something about at all.  Let's see some examples.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:758
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Prove that the relation <m>\\equiv_2</m>, which holds of two integers if their difference is even, is an equivalence relation.  That is, <m>a \\equiv_2 b</m> if and only if <m>b-a = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:763
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            We simply check the three required properties.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:767
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  <m>\\equiv_2</m> is reflexive: for any integer <m>a</m>, we have <m>a - a = 0</m> and <m>0 = 2k</m> for <m>k = 0</m>, so <m>a \\equiv_2 a</m>.\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:772
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  <m>\\equiv_2</m> is symmetric: Fix arbitrary integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, and assume <m>a \\equiv_2 b</m>.  That means that <m>b-a = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.  What about <m>a - b</m>?  Well, we will have <m>a-b = 2(-k)</m>, and <m>-k</m> is an integer, so we have <m>b \\equiv_2 a</m>.\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:777
#, no-wrap
msgid "                  <m>\\equiv_2</m> is transitive: Fix arbitrary integers <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> and assume <m>a \\equiv_2 b</m> and <m>b \\equiv_2 c</m>.  This means that <m>b-a = 2k</m> and <m>c-b = 2j</m> for some integers <m>k</m> and <m>j</m>.  What about <m>c - a</m>?  Well, "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:777
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"                  Since <m>k+j</m> is an integer, we see that <m>a \\equiv_2 c</m> as required.\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:789
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let's call two graphs <q>degree-sequence-equivalent</q> if they have the same degree sequence.  Is this an equivalence relation?\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:794
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Yes it is.  Clearly every graph has the same degree sequence as itself, so the relation is reflexive.  If <m>G_1</m> has the same degree sequence as <m>G_2</m>, then <m>G_2</m> has the same degree sequence as <m>G_1</m>, so the relation is transitive.  Finally, if <m>G_1</m> has the same degree sequence as <m>G_2</m>, which has the same degree sequence as <m>G_3</m>, then they all have the same degree sequence, so <m>G_1</m> has the same degree sequence as <m>G_3</m> (i.e., the relation is transitive).\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:801
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This example is almost too obvious.  That's because we said that two things are related if a well-defined property of those things was <em>equal</em>, and equality satisfies the properties of an equivalence relation.  Our next goal is to try to make better sense of this and see that it is exactly what gives us an equivalence relation.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:808
#, no-wrap
msgid "Equivalence Classes and Partitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:811
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Given <em>any</em> relation <m>R</m>, we can look at the <em>set</em> of elements that are related to a particular element.  For the <q>is taking</q> relation, we can ask what classes Al is taking (i.e., the classes related to Al).  For the <q>is a multiple of</q>, we can ask which numbers 6 a multiple of.  One way to study the relation is to study the sets of things related to each element.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:817
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>R</m> be a relation on the set <m>A</m>, and let <m>a</m> be an element of <m>A</m>.  The <term>relation class</term> of <m>a</m>, written <m>[a]</m> is the set of all elements <m>b</m> such that <m>(a,b) \\in R</m> (the set of <m>b</m> that are related to <m>a</m> by <m>R</m>).  That is,\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:820 source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:230
#: source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:241 source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:618
#: source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:626 source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:688
#: source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:697 source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:704
#: source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:763 source/sec_counting-proofs.ptx:770
#: source/exercises/counting-proofs.ptx:275
#: source/exercises/counting-proofs.ptx:357
#: source/sec_counting-probability.ptx:717 source/sec_seq-basics.ptx:819
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:554 source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:561
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:652 source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:574
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:583 source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:660
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:668 source/sec_addtops-genfun.ptx:621
#: source/sec_addtops-numbth.ptx:1123 source/sec_addtops-numbth.ptx:1133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:824
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            When <m>R</m> is an equivalence relation, we call relation classes <term>equivalence classes</term>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:832
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Find the relation classes for the <q>is a multiple of</q> relation on the set <m>A = \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:837
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            There will be six relation classes since each element has a relation class.  They are:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:846
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            For example, we found <m>[4]</m> by considering all pairs <m>(4,b)</m> that satisfied the relation: 4 is a multiple of 1, 2, and 4, so those are the possible values of <m>b</m> that we find.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:850
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Look back at the directed multigraph for this relation shown in the solution to <xref po4a-id=0>.  What are the relation classes?  They are nothing but the neighbors of each vertex (where neighbor means you follow the arrows in the correct direction).\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:858
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Find the equivalence classes for the <m>\\equiv_2</m> relation on the integers.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:863
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            On no!  Our set is infinite, so we will have infinitely many relation classes? Well, we better get started...\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:867
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            What numbers are related to 1?  Remember, we want integers whose difference with 1 is a multiple of 2.  So 3 for sure.  Also 5, and 7, and -1, and -3, and... all the odds?  Yes, because the difference of any two odd numbers is even.  We can also say that the difference between any two even numbers is even, so the equivalence class of 2 will contain all the even numbers.  So far we have:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:875
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Actually, I think we are done.  While <m>[3]</m>, <m>[4]</m>, <m>[5]</m>, and so on are all completely valid equivalence classes, the elements that are related to <m>3</m> will be exactly the elements related to <m>1</m>, since <m>1 \\equiv_2 3</m>.  This is because the relation is transitive!  If <m>3 \\equiv_2 b</m>, then we know <m>1 \\equiv_2 b</m>.  \n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:879
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            So we have exactly two equivalence classes.  Every integer is in exactly one of these two equivalence classes, and the equivalence class of any integer is exactly the class it belongs to.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:885
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Examine the two examples above carefully.  For the <q>is a multiple of</q> relation, which is NOT an equivalence relation, some elements belong to more than one (different) relation class.  But for <m>\\equiv_2</m>, which is an equivalence relation, every element is in exactly one equivalence class.  This is no accident.  To make sense of this, we will define a new term.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:891
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Given a non-empty set <m>A</m>, a <term>partition</term> of <m>A</m> is a set <m>P</m> of non-empty subsets of <m>A</m> such that every element of <m>A</m> is in exactly one element of <m>P</m>. \n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:897
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        That definition has a lot of symbols and sets involved.  It's really not complicated though: A partition is a way to break up a set into <em>disjoint</em> subsets that <em>cover</em> the whole set.  That the subsets are disjoint means no element is in <em>more than one</em> subset.  That the subsets cover the set means every element is in <em>at least one</em> subset. \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:903
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Give a few different partitions of the set <m>A = \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:908
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            There are so many choices here! One choice is:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:911
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"            That's a partition of <m>A</m> into two subsets.\n"
"            Another partition:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:916
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"            Another:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:918
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
", which happens to be a partition into even and odd numbers.  We also have the trivial partition:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:919
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".  \n"
"            Note: that is a single set inside the set <m>P_4</m>.  \n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:926
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        It is sometimes a little confusing to think of the elements of a partition as subsets since the partition is a set, and a set of sets can be difficult to talk about.  We sometimes call the partition a <em>collection</em> and each of the elements of the partition <em>parts</em> or <em>blocks</em>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:930
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Now the big idea: For any equivalence relation, the equivalence classes form a partition, and for any partition, we can define a relation <q>are in the same subset</q> which will be an equivalence relation.  We have already seen that the equivalence classes of <m>\\equiv_2</m> form a partition.  Let's go the other direction.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:936
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Define an equivalence relation <m>\\sim</m> on the set <m>A = \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}</m> from the partition <m>P = \\{\\{1,4\\}, \\{2,3,5\\}\\}</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:941
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Say two elements of <m>A</m> are equivalent provided they belong to the same element of <m>P</m>.  That is, <m>1 \\sim 4</m> and <m>4\\sim 1</m>, and <m>2 \\sim 3</m>, <m>2 \\sim 5</m>, <m>3 \\sim 5</m>, <m>3\\sim 2</m>, <m>5\\sim 2</m>, and <m>5\\sim 3</m>.  Wait. We also have <m>1\\sim 1</m>, <m>2\\sim 2</m>, and so on, since every number is in the same subset as itself.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:944
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            It is clear from looking that this relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, so is an equivalence relation.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:952
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Given any equivalence relation <m>R</m> on a set <m>A</m>, the equivalence classes form a partition of <m>A</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:956
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Given any partition <m>P = \\{B_1, B_2, \\ldots, \\}</m> of a set <m>A</m>, the relation <m>\\equiv_P</m> defined by <m>a \\equiv_P b</m> if and only if <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> belong to the same block of <m>P</m>, is an equivalence relation.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:960
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Further, the equivalence classes for the equivalence relation formed by a partition are exactly the original partition, and the equivalence relation built by the partition of its equivalence classes is exactly the original equivalence relation.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:971
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Consider the relation <m>R</m> defined on the integers that holds of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> precisely if <m>b-a \\ge 4</m>.  So for example, <m>(2,7) \\in R</m> but <m>(8,6) \\notin R</m>.  Which of the following properties of relations does <m>R</m> have?\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:978
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>R</m> is reflexive.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:983
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                If <m>R</m> were reflexive, then then in particular (2,2) would be in <m>R</m>.  But <m>2-2 = 0</m>, which is not greater than or equal to 4.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:990
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>R</m> is irreflexive.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:995
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Correct.  Since no integer is four or more greater than itself, the relation does not hold of any element with itself.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:1002
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>R</m> is symmetric.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:1007
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                If <m>R</m> were symmetric, then for any <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that <m>b-a \\ge 4</m>, we would also have <m>a-b \\ge 4</m>.  But this is not true.  For example, <m>(2,10)</m> is in <m>R</m> but <m>(10,2)</m> is not.  (We are not using the <em>absolute</em> difference.)\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:1014
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>R</m> is antisymmetric.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:1019
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Correct.  There are no numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that both <m>b-a \\ge 4</m> and <m>a-b \\ge 4</m>, so the hypothesis of the definition of antisemetric is always false, making the entire statement in the definition true.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:1027
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>R</m> is transitive.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:1032
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Correct.  If <m>b-a \\ge 4</m> and <m>c-b \\ge 4</m>, then <m>c-a = (c-b) + (b-a) \\ge 4+4 = 8</m>, so <m>c-a \\ge 4</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-relations.ptx:1042
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Not all graphs have a transitive edge relation.  But do some of them?  If so, give an example and explain why it is transitive.  If not, explain why.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

