#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Matching in Bipartite Graphs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><idx><see>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:14 source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "matching"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Given a bipartite graph,\n"
"      a <term>matching</term> is a subset of the edges for which every vertex belongs to exactly one of the edges.\n"
"      Our goal in this activity is to discover some criteria for when a bipartite graph has a matching.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:25
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Does the graph below contain a matching?\n"
"      If so, find one.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><image><description>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:46
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A bipartite graph with 12 vertices, in two sets of six.  If we call the top vertices a0 through a5, and the bottom vertices b0 to b5 (left to right), then the graph has the following edges: (a0,b0), (a0,b1), (a0,b5), (a1,b0), (a1,b3), (a2,b1), (a2,b2), (a2,b3), (a3,b0), (a3,b5), (a4,b2), (a4,b3), (a4,b4), (a4,b5), (a5,b4).\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:51
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Not all bipartite graphs have matchings.\n"
"      Draw as many fundamentally different examples of bipartite graphs that do NOT have matchings.\n"
"      Your goal is to find all the possible obstructions to a graph having a perfect matching.\n"
"      Write down the <em>necessary</em>\n"
"      conditions for a graph to have a matching\n"
"      (that is, fill in the blank: If a graph has a matching,\n"
"      then "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
").\n"
"      Then ask yourself whether these conditions are sufficient\n"
"      (is it true that if "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:59
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
", then the graph has a matching?).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    We conclude with one more example of a graph theory problem to illustrate the variety and vastness of the subject.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:79
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    What we are calling a <em>matching</em>\n"
"    is sometimes called a <em>perfect matching</em>\n"
"    or <em>complete matching</em>.\n"
"    This is because it is interesting to look at non-perfect matchings as well.\n"
"    We will call those <em>partial</em> matchings.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Suppose you have a bipartite graph <m>G</m>.\n"
"    This will consist of two sets of vertices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> with some edges connecting some vertices of <m>A</m> to some vertices in <m>B</m>\n"
"    (but of course,\n"
"    no edges between two vertices both in <m>A</m> or both in <m>B</m>).\n"
"    A <term>matching of <m>A</m></term>\n"
"    is a subset of the edges for which each vertex of <m>A</m> belongs to exactly one edge of the subset,\n"
"    and no vertex in <m>B</m> belongs to more than one edge in the subset.\n"
"    In practice, we will assume that <m>|A| = |B|</m>\n"
"    (the two sets have the same number of vertices),\n"
"    so this says that every vertex in the graph belongs to exactly one edge in the matching.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:98
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    The standard example for matchings used to be the\n"
"    <em>marriage problem</em>\n"
"    in which <m>A</m> consisted of the men in the town, <m>B</m> the women,\n"
"    and an edge represented a marriage that was agreeable to both parties.\n"
"    A matching then represented a way for the town elders to marry off everyone in the town,\n"
"    no polygamy allowed.\n"
"    We have chosen a more progressive context for the sake of political correctness.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:87
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Some context might make this easier to understand.\n"
"    Think of the vertices in <m>A</m> as representing students in a class,\n"
"    and the vertices in <m>B</m> as representing presentation topics.\n"
"    We put an edge from a vertex <m>a \\in A</m> to a vertex <m>b \\in B</m> if student <m>a</m> would like to present on topic <m>b</m>.\n"
"    Of course, some students would want to present on more than one topic,\n"
"    so their vertex would have degree greater than 1.\n"
"    As the teacher,\n"
"    you want to assign each student their own unique topic.\n"
"    Thus you want to find a matching of <m>A</m>:\n"
"    you pick some subset of the edges so that each student gets matched up with exactly one topic,\n"
"    and no topic gets matched to two students.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:108
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    The question is:\n"
"    when does a bipartite graph contain a matching of <m>A</m>?\n"
"    To begin to answer this question,\n"
"    consider what could prevent the graph from containing a matching.\n"
"    This will not necessarily tell us a condition when the graph\n"
"    <em>does</em> have a matching,\n"
"    but at least it is a start.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#.  \todo{write exercise asking for a proof by induction for this} 
#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    One way <m>G</m> could not have a matching is if there is a vertex in <m>A</m> not adjacent to any vertex in <m>B</m>\n"
"    (so having degree 0).\n"
"    What else?\n"
"    What if two students both like the same topic, and no others?\n"
"    Then after assigning that one topic to the first student,\n"
"    there is nothing left for the second student to like,\n"
"    so it is very much as if the second student has degree 0.\n"
"    Or what if three students like only two topics between them?\n"
"    Again, after assigning one student a topic,\n"
"    we reduce this to the previous case of two students liking only one topic.\n"
"    We can continue this way with more and more students.\n"
"\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    It should be clear at this point that if there is a group of <m>n</m> students who as a group like <m>n-1</m> or fewer topics,\n"
"    then no matching is possible.\n"
"    This is true for any value of <m>n</m>,\n"
"    and any group of <m>n</m> students.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:142
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    To make this more graph-theoretic,\n"
"    say you have a set <m>S \\subseteq A</m> of vertices.\n"
"    Define <m>N(S)</m>\n"
"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:147
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>N(S)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:148
#, no-wrap
msgid "the set of neighbors of <m>S</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:151
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    to be the set of all the <term>neighbors</term>\n"
"    of vertices in <m>S</m>.\n"
"    That is, <m>N(S)</m> contains all the vertices\n"
"    (in <m>B</m>)\n"
"    that are adjacent to at least one of the vertices in <m>S</m>. (In the student/topic graph,\n"
"    <m>N(S)</m> is the set of topics liked by the students of <m>S</m>.) Our discussion above can be summarized as follows:\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "Matching Condition"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:166
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If a bipartite graph <m>G = \\{A, B\\}</m> has a matching of <m>A</m>, then\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:170
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      for all <m>S \\subseteq A</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    This happens often in graph theory.\n"
"    If you can avoid the obvious counterexamples,\n"
"    you often get what you want.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><fn>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:186
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    There is also an infinite version of the theorem, which was proved by Marshal Hall, Jr.\n"
"    The name is a coincidence though as the two Halls are not related.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:175
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Is the converse true?\n"
"    Suppose <m>G</m> satisfies the matching condition\n"
"    <m>|N(S)| \\ge |S|</m> for all <m>S \\subseteq A</m>\n"
"    (every set of vertices has at least as many neighbors as vertices in the set).\n"
"    Does that mean that there is a matching?\n"
"    Surprisingly, yes.\n"
"    The obvious necessary condition is also sufficient.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/> This is a theorem first proved by Philip Hall in 1935.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"1\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:191
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hall's Marriage Theorem"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:199
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Let <m>G</m> be a bipartite graph with sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.\n"
"        Then <m>G</m> has a matching of <m>A</m> if and only if\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:204
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        for all <m>S \\subseteq A</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:210
#, no-wrap
msgid "    There are quite a few different proofs of this theorem "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:210
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" a quick internet search will get you started.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:214
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    In addition to its application to marriage and student presentation topics,\n"
"    matchings have applications all over the place.\n"
"    We conclude with one such example.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:222
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you deal 52 regular playing cards into 13 piles of 4 cards each.\n"
"        Prove that you can always select one card from each pile to get one of each of the 13 card values Ace, 2, 3, "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:223
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
", 10, Jack, Queen,\n"
"        and King.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:229
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Doing this directly would be difficult,\n"
"        but we can use the matching condition to help.\n"
"        Construct a graph <m>G</m> with 13 vertices in the set <m>A</m>,\n"
"        each representing one of the 13 card values,\n"
"        and 13 vertices in the set <m>B</m>,\n"
"        each representing one of the 13 piles.\n"
"        Draw an edge between a vertex <m>a \\in A</m> to a vertex <m>b \\in B</m> if a card with value <m>a</m> is in the pile <m>b</m>.\n"
"        Notice that we are just looking for a matching of <m>A</m>;\n"
"        each value needs to be found in the piles exactly once.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_gt-matchings.ptx:241
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We will have a matching if the matching condition holds.\n"
"        Given any set of card values (a set\n"
"        <m>S \\subseteq A</m>), we must show that <m>|N(S)| \\ge |S|</m>.\n"
"        That is, the number of piles that contain those values is at least the number of different values.\n"
"        But what if it wasn't?\n"
"        Say <m>|S| = k</m>.\n"
"        If <m>|N(S)| \\lt k</m>,\n"
"        then we would have fewer than <m>4k</m> different cards in those piles\n"
"        (since each pile contains 4 cards).\n"
"        But there are <m>4k</m> cards with the <m>k</m> different values,\n"
"        so at least one of these cards must be in another pile,\n"
"        a contradiction.\n"
"        Thus the matching condition holds,\n"
"        so there is a matching, as required.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:7
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Find a matching of the bipartite graphs below or explain why no matching exists.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:25
#, no-wrap
