"          A bipartite graph with six vertices, in two sets of three.  If we call the top vertices a,b,c, and the bottom vertices 1, 2, 3 (left to right), then the graph has the following edges: (a,1), (a,2), (b,1), (b,3), (c,1), (c,3).\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:46
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A bipartite graph with eight vertices, in two sets of four.  If we call the top vertices a,b,c,d and the bottom vertices 1, 2, 3,4 (left to right), then the graph has the following edges: (a,1), (a,3), (a,4) (b,2), (c,1), (c,2), (c,3), (c,4), (d,2).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:68
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            A bipartite graph with ten vertices, in two sets of five.  If we call the top vertices a,b,c,d,e and the bottom vertices 1, 2, 3,4,5 (left to right), then the graph has the following edges: (a,1), (a,2), (a,3), (b,1), (b,3), (c,2), (c,4), (d,3), (d,5), (e,3), (e,4), (e,5).\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:76
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The first and third graphs have a matching, shown in bold\n"
"        (there are other matchings as well).\n"
"        The middle graph does not have a matching.\n"
"        If you look at the three circled vertices,\n"
"        you see that they only have two neighbors,\n"
"        which violates the matching condition <m>\\card{N(S)} \\ge \\card{S}</m>\n"
"        (the three circled vertices form the set <m>S</m>).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:92
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            A bipartite graph with six vertices, in two sets of three.  If we call the top vertices a,b,c, and the bottom vertices 1, 2, 3 (left to right), then the graph has the following edges: (a,1), (a,2), (b,1), (b,3), (c,1), (c,3).  The edges (a,2), (b,3), and (c,1) are highlighted bold.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:101
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            A bipartite graph with eight vertices, in two sets of four.  If we call the top vertices a,b,c,d and the bottom vertices 1, 2, 3,4 (left to right), then the graph has the following edges: (a,1), (a,3), (a,4) (b,2), (c,1), (c,2), (c,3), (c,4), (d,2).  The vertices 1, 3, and 4 are circled.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:113
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            A bipartite graph with ten vertices, in two sets of five.  If we call the top vertices a,b,c,d,e and the bottom vertices 1, 2, 3,4,5 (left to right), then the graph has the following edges: (a,1), (a,2), (a,3), (b,1), (b,3), (c,2), (c,4), (d,3), (d,5), (e,3), (e,4), (e,5).  Of these, the following edges are highlighted bold: (a,1), (b,3), (c,2), (d,5), (e,4).\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:126
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A bipartite graph that doesn't have a matching might still have a\n"
"        <term>partial matching</term>.\n"
"        By this we mean a set of <em>edges</em>\n"
"        for which no vertex belongs to more than one edge\n"
"        (but possibly belongs to none).\n"
"        Every bipartite graph\n"
"        (with at least one edge)\n"
"        has a partial matching,\n"
"        so we can look for the largest partial matching in a graph.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Your <q>friend</q> claims that she has found the largest partial matching for the graph below\n"
"        (her matching is in bold).\n"
"        She explains that no other edge can be added,\n"
"        because all the edges not used in her partial matching are connected to matched vertices.\n"
"        Is she correct?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><image><description>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:158
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A bipartite graph with ten vertices in two sets of five.  Call the top vertices a, b, c, d, e and the bottom vertices 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.  Then the graph has the following edges: a-1, a-3, b-1, b-2, c-1, c-2, c-4, d-5, e-4, and e-5.  Of these, the edges a-1, b-2, c-4, and e-5 are highlighted bold\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:172
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        One way you might check to see whether a partial matching is maximal is to construct an\n"
"        <term>alternating path</term>.\n"
"        This is a sequence of adjacent edges,\n"
"        which alternate between edges in the matching and edges not in the matching\n"
"        (no edge can be used more than once).\n"
"        If an alternating path starts and stops with an edge\n"
"        <em>not</em> in the matching,\n"
"        then it is called an <term>augmenting path</term>.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

