#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:307
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We say that a set of vertices\n"
"        <m>A \\subseteq V</m> is a <term>vertex cover</term>\n"
"        if every edge of the graph is incident to a vertex in the cover\n"
"        (so a vertex cover covers the <em>edges</em>).\n"
"        Since <m>V</m> itself is a vertex cover,\n"
"        every graph has a vertex cover.\n"
"        The interesting question is about finding a\n"
"        <term>minimal</term> vertex cover,\n"
"        one that uses the fewest possible number of vertices.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:319
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Suppose you had a matching of a graph.\n"
"            How can you use that to get a minimal vertex cover?\n"
"            Will your method always work?\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:325
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Suppose you had a minimal vertex cover for a graph.\n"
"            How can you use that to get a partial matching?\n"
"            Will your method always work?\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/gt-matchings.ptx:331
#, no-wrap
