"            <m>\\card{N(S)} \\ge \\card{S}</m> for all <m>S \\subseteq V</m>,\n"
"            then the graph has a matching.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:6
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Hopefully this chapter has given you some sense of the wide variety of graph theory topics as well as why these studies are interesting.\n"
"    There are many more interesting areas to consider, and the list is increasing all the time;\n"
"    graph theory is an active area of mathematical research.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:12
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    One reason graph theory is such a rich area of study is that it deals with such a fundamental concept:\n"
"    Any pair of objects can either be related or not related.\n"
"    What the objects are and what <q>related</q> means varies depending on context,\n"
"    and this leads to many applications of graph theory to science and other areas of math.\n"
"    The objects can be countries,\n"
"    and two countries can be related if they share a border.\n"
"    The objects could be land masses that are related if there is a bridge between them.\n"
"    The objects could be websites that are related if there is a link from one to the other.\n"
"    Or we can be completely abstract:\n"
"    The objects are vertices that are related if there is an edge between them.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:25
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    What question we ask about the graph depends on the application,\n"
"    but often leads to deeper,\n"
"    general and abstract questions worth studying in their own right.\n"
"    Here is a short summary of the types of questions we have considered:\n"
"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:33
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Can the graph be drawn in the plane without edges crossing?\n"
"          If so, how many regions does this drawing divide the plane into?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:40
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Is it possible to color the vertices of the graph so that related vertices have different colors using a small number of colors?\n"
"          How many colors are needed?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Is it possible to trace over every edge of a graph exactly once without lifting your pencil?\n"
"          What other sorts of <q>paths</q> might a graph possess?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:54
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Can you find subgraphs with certain properties?\n"
"          For example,\n"
"          when does a (bipartite) graph contain a subgraph in which all vertices are only related to one other vertex?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><p>
#: source/sec_gt-conc.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    Not surprisingly, these questions are often related to each other.\n"
"    For example,\n"
"    the chromatic number of a graph cannot be greater than 4 when the graph is planar.\n"
"    Whether the graph has an Euler trail depends on how many vertices each vertex is adjacent to\n"
"    (and whether those numbers are always even or not).\n"
"    Even the existence of matchings in bipartite graphs can be proved using paths.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:7
#, no-wrap
