#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:338
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Only if <m>n \\ge 6</m> and is even.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:344
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              None.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:350
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              12.\n"
"              Such a graph would have <m>\\frac{5n}{2}</m> edges.\n"
"              If the graph is planar, then\n"
"              <m>n - \\frac{5n}{2} + f = 2</m> so there would be <m>\\frac{4+3n}{2}</m> faces.\n"
"              Also, we must have <m>3f \\le 2e</m>,\n"
"              since the graph is simple.\n"
"              So we must have <m>3\\left(\\frac{4 + 3n}{2}\\right) \\le 5n</m>.\n"
"              Solving for <m>n</m> gives <m>n \\ge 12</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:368
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        At a school dance, 6 girls and 4 boys take turns dancing\n"
"        (as couples)\n"
"        with each other.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:375
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many couples dance if every girl dances with every boy?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:381
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many couples dance if everyone dances with everyone else (regardless of gender)?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:387
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Explain what graphs can be used to represent these situations.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:398
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              There were 24 couples: 6 choices for the girl and 4 choices for the boy.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:404
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              There were 45 couples:\n"
"              <m>{10 \\choose 2}</m> since we must choose two of the 10 people to dance together.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:411
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              For part (a),\n"
"              we are counting the number of edges in <m>K_{4,6}</m>.\n"
"              In part (b) we count the edges of <m>K_{10}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:424
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Among a group of <m>n</m> people,\n"
"        is it possible for everyone to be friends with an odd number of people in the group?\n"
"        If so, what can you say about <m>n</m>?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:431
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Yes, as long as <m>n</m> is even.\n"
"        If <m>n</m> were odd,\n"
"        then the corresponding graph would have an odd number of odd degree vertices,\n"
"        which is impossible.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:442
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Your friend has challenged you to create a convex polyhedron containing 9 triangles and 6 pentagons.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:447
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Is it possible to build such a polyhedron using\n"
"              <em>only</em> these shapes?\n"
"              Explain.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:455
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              You decide to also include one heptagon\n"
"              (seven-sided polygon).\n"
"              How many vertices does your new convex polyhedron contain?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:463
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Assuming you are successful in building your new 16-faced polyhedron,\n"
"              could every vertex be the joining of the same number of faces?\n"
"              Could each vertex join either 3 or 4 faces?\n"
"              If so, how many of each type of vertex would there be?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:477
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              No.\n"
"              The 9 triangles each contribute 3 edges,\n"
"              and the 6 pentagons contribute 5 edges.\n"
"              This gives a total of 57,\n"
"              which is exactly twice the number of edges,\n"
"              since each edge borders exactly 2 faces.\n"
"              But 57 is odd, so this is impossible.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:489
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Now adding up all the edges of all the 16 polygons gives a total of 64,\n"
"              meaning there would be 32 edges in the polyhedron.\n"
"              We can then use Euler's formula\n"
"              <m>v - e + f = 2</m> to deduce that there must be 18 vertices.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:498
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If you add up all the vertices from each polygon separately,\n"
"              we get a total of 64.\n"
"              This is not divisible by 3, so it cannot be that each vertex belongs to exactly 3 faces.\n"
"              Could they all belong to 4 faces?\n"
"              That would mean there were <m>64/4 = 16</m> vertices,\n"
"              but we know from Euler's formula that there must be 18 vertices.\n"
"              We can write <m>64 = 3x + 4y</m> and solve for <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>\n"
"              (as integers).\n"
"              We get that there must be 10 vertices with degree 4 and 8 with degree 3. (Note that the number of faces joined at a vertex is equal to its degree in graph theoretic terms.)\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:517
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Is there a convex polyhedron that requires 5 colors to properly color the vertices of the polyhedron?\n"
"        Explain.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:523
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        No.\n"
"        Every polyhedron can be represented as a planar graph,\n"
"        and the Four Color Theorem says that every planar graph has chromatic number at most 4.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:533
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        How many edges does the graph <m>K_{n,n}</m> have?\n"
"        For which values of <m>n</m> does the graph contain an Euler circuit?\n"
"        For which values of <m>n</m> is the graph planar?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:540
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        <m>K_{n,n}</m> has <m>n^2</m> edges.\n"
"        The graph will have an Euler circuit when <m>n</m> is even.\n"
"        The graph will be planar only when <m>n \\lt 3</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:550
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The graph <m>G</m> has 6 vertices with degrees <m>1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 5</m>.\n"
"        How many edges does <m>G</m> have?\n"
"        If <m>G</m> was planar, how many faces would it have?\n"
"        Does <m>G</m> have an Euler trail?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:558
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        <m>G</m> has 8 edges\n"
"        (since the sum of the degrees is 16).\n"
"        If <m>G</m> is planar, then it will have 4 faces\n"
"        (since <m>6 - 8 + 4 = 2</m>). <m>G</m> does not have an Euler trail since there are more than 2 vertices of odd degree.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:569
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        What is the smallest number of colors you need to properly color the vertices of <m>K_{7}</m>.\n"
"        Can you say whether <m>K_7</m> is planar based on your answer?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:575
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        <m>7</m> colors.\n"
"        Thus <m>K_7</m> is not planar\n"
"        (by the contrapositive of the Four Color Theorem).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:585
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        What is the smallest number of colors you need to properly color the vertices of <m>K_{3,4}</m>?\n"
"        Can you say whether <m>K_{3,4}</m> is planar based on your answer?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:591
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The chromatic number of <m>K_{3,4}</m> is 2, since the graph is bipartite.\n"
"        You cannot say whether the graph is planar based on this coloring\n"
"        (the converse of the Four Color Theorem is not true).\n"
"        In fact, the graph is <em>not</em> planar,\n"
"        since it contains <m>K_{3,3}</m> as a subgraph.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:603
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that <m>K_{3,4}</m> is not planar.\n"
"        Do this using Euler's formula,\n"
"        not just by appealing to the fact that <m>K_{3,3}</m> is not planar.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:610
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We have that <m>K_{3,4}</m> has 7 vertices and 12 edges\n"
"        (each vertex in the group of 3 has degree 4).\n"
"        Then by Euler's formula we have that\n"
"        <m>7 - 12 + f = 2</m>, so if the graph were planar,\n"
"        it would have <m>f = 7</m> faces.\n"
"        However, since the girth of the graph is 4\n"
"        (there are no cycles of length 3),\n"
"        we get that <m>4f \\le 2e</m>.\n"
"        But this would mean that <m>28 \\le 24</m>, a contradiction.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:626
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A dodecahedron is a regular convex polyhedron made up of 12 regular pentagons.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:631
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Suppose you color each pentagon with one of three colors.\n"
"              Prove that there must be two adjacent pentagons colored identically.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:638
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              What if you use four colors?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:644
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              What if instead of a dodecahedron you colored the faces of a cube?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:652
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For all these questions, we are really coloring the vertices of a graph.\n"
"        You get the graph by first drawing a planar representation of the polyhedron and then taking its planar dual:\n"
"        Put a vertex in the center of each face\n"
"        (including the outside),\n"
"        and connect two vertices if their faces share an edge.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:661
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Since the planar dual of a dodecahedron contains a 5-wheel,\n"
"              its chromatic number is at least 4.\n"
"              Alternatively,\n"
"              suppose you could color the faces using 3 colors without any two adjacent faces colored the same.\n"
"              Take any face, and color it blue.\n"
"              The 5 pentagons bordering this blue pentagon cannot be colored blue.\n"
"              Color the first one red.\n"
"              Its two neighbors\n"
"              (adjacent to the blue pentagon)\n"
"              get colored green.\n"
"              The remaining 2 cannot be blue or green,\n"
"              but also cannot both be red since they are adjacent to each other.\n"
"              Thus a 4th color is needed.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:679
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The planar dual of the dodecahedron is itself a planar graph.\n"
"              Thus by the 4-color theorem,\n"
"              it can be colored using only 4 colors without two adjacent vertices\n"
"              (corresponding to the faces of the polyhedron)\n"
"              being colored identically.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:689
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The cube can be properly 3-colored.\n"
"              Color the <q>top</q> and <q>bottom</q> red,\n"
"              the <q>front</q> and <q>back</q> blue,\n"
"              and the <q>left</q> and <q>right</q> green.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Decide whether the following statements are true or false.\n"
"        Prove your answers.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:709
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If two graphs <m>G_1</m> and <m>G_2</m> have the same chromatic number,\n"
"              then they are isomorphic.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:716
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If two graphs <m>G_1</m> and <m>G_2</m> have the same number of vertices and edges and have the same chromatic number,\n"
"              then they are isomorphic.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:723
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If two graphs are isomorphic,\n"
"              then they have the same chromatic number.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:735
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              False.\n"
"              To prove this,\n"
"              we can give an example of a pair of graphs with the same chromatic number that are not isomorphic.\n"
"              For example,\n"
"              <m>K_{3,3}</m> and <m>K_{3,4}</m> both have chromatic number 2 but are not isomorphic.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:745
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              False.\n"
"              The previous example does not work,\n"
"              but you can easily draw two trees that have the same number of vertices and edges but are not isomorphic.\n"
"              Since all trees have chromatic number 2, this is a counterexample.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:754
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              True.\n"
"              If there is an isomorphism from <m>G_1</m> to <m>G_2</m>,\n"
"              then we have a bijection that tells us how to match up vertices between the graph.\n"
"              Any proper vertex coloring of <m>G_1</m> will tell us how to properly color <m>G_2</m>,\n"
"              simply by coloring <m>f(v_i)</m> the same color as <m>v_i</m>,\n"
"              for each vertex <m>v_i \\in V</m>.\n"
"              That is, color the vertices in <m>G_2</m> exactly how you color the corresponding vertices in <m>G_1</m>.\n"
"              Similarly, any proper vertex coloring of <m>G_2</m> corresponds to a proper vertex coloring of <m>G_1</m>.\n"
"              Thus the smallest number of colors needed to properly color <m>G_1</m> cannot be smaller than the smallest number of colors needed to properly color <m>G_2</m>,\n"
"              and vice-versa, so the chromatic numbers must be equal.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:774
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        If a planar graph <m>G</m> with <m>7</m> vertices divides the plane into 8 regions,\n"
"        how many edges must <m>G</m> have?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:780
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        <m>G</m> has <m>13</m> edges,\n"
"        since we need <m>7 - e + 8 = 2</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:789
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider the graph below:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><image><description>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:800
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A graph with six vertices.  Two vertices are aligned in a center column, and are adjacent to each other.  Each is also adjacent to each of the other four vertices.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:809
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Does the graph have an Euler trail or circuit?\n"
"              Explain.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:816
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Is the graph planar?\n"
"              Explain.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:823
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Is the graph bipartite?\n"
"              Complete?\n"
"              Complete bipartite?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:831
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              What is the chromatic number of the graph?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:842
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The graph does have an Euler trail, but not an Euler circuit.\n"
"              There are exactly two vertices with odd degree.\n"
"              The path starts at one and ends at the other.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:850
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The graph is planar.\n"
"              Even though as it is drawn with edges crossing,\n"
"              it is easy to redraw it without edges crossing.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:858
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The graph is not bipartite\n"
"              (there is an odd cycle),\n"
"              nor complete.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:866
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The chromatic number of the graph is 3.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:877
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For each part below, say whether the statement is true or false.\n"
"        Explain why the true statements are true,\n"
"        and give counterexamples for the false statements.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:884
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Every bipartite graph is planar.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:890
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Every bipartite graph has chromatic number 2.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:896
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Every bipartite graph has an Euler trail.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:902
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Every vertex of a bipartite graph has even degree.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:908
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A graph is bipartite if and only if the sum of the degrees of all the vertices is even.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:919
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              False.\n"
"              For example, <m>K_{3,3}</m> is not planar.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:926
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              True.\n"
"              The graph is bipartite, so it is possible to divide the vertices into two groups with no edges between vertices in the same group.\n"
"              Thus we can color all the vertices of one group red and the other group blue.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:934
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              False. <m>K_{3,3}</m> has 6 vertices with degree 3 and so contains no Euler trail.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:940
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              False. <m>K_{3,3}</m> again.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:946
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              False.\n"
"              The sum of the degrees of all vertices is even for <em>all</em>\n"
"              graphs so this property does not imply that the graph is bipartite.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:959
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider the statement, <q>If a graph is planar,\n"
"        then it has an Euler trail.</q>\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:983
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Is it possible for the contrapositive to be false?\n"
"              If it was, what would that tell you?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:997
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Is the converse of the statement true or false?\n"
"              Prove your answer.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:1009
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If a graph has an Euler trail, then it is planar.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/gt-conc.ptx:1015
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If a graph does not have an Euler trail, then it is not planar.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

