#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\tIn 1653, Blaise Pascal, concerned with questions that would lay the foundation of probability theory, collected several facts about a triangular array of numbers in his "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><pubtitle>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "Treatise on Arithmetical Triangle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:197
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\tThis arrangement of numbers appeared as early as the 10th century in China, India, and Persia.\n"
"\t\t\tThe Chinese and Persian treatment of the triangle was in service of what we would now consider algebra: finding <m>n</m>th roots, essentially solving polynomial equations.\n"
"\t\t\tThe numbers in the triangle appear as solutions to counting problems in Indian texts: from six <em>tastes</em>, how many combinations of one, or two, or three,...\n"
"\t\t\tcan you make?  European mathematicians in the 14th century presented the triangle as a table of <term>figurate numbers</term> (numbers that can be arranged in a geometric shape), which were themselves the centerpiece of the work of Pythagoras and his followers.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:206
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSo what is this remarkable triangle that holds the secrets of so many different mathematical problems?  Behold, Pascal's triangle:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><figure><caption>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:209
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pascal's triangle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><figure><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:240
#, no-wrap
msgid "The first 17 rows of Pascal's triangle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><figure><image><description><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:243
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe first 17 rows of Pascal's triangle.\n"
"\t\t\t\tA triangular array of hexagons, each row containing one more hexagon that the row above it.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn each hexagon is an integer: 1's on the border of the triangle, and every integer inside the triangle the sum of the two integers above it.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:252
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tSpend some time gazing at the beauty of this triangle.\n"
"\t\tWhat do you notice?  What do you wonder?  Look specifically at the 5th row (we call the 1 on the top row 0, so row 5 is 1, 5, 10, 10, 5, 1).\n"
"\t\tHow do the numbers in this row relate to the numbers above them?  Notice that <m>5 = 1+4</m> and <m>10 = 4+6</m>.\n"
"\t\tDoes this occur anywhere else in the triangle?\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:259
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tIndeed, every number in the triangle is the <em>sum of the two numbers above it</em>.\n"
"\t\tLet's take this as our <em>definition</em> of Pascal's triangle.\n"
"\t\tWe can then generate as many rows of the triangle as we like.\n"
"\t\tIt is this additive definition that was used in China and Persia to find <m>n</m>th roots, and we will briefly mention this use at the end of this section.\n"
"\t\tHowever, we are interested in counting questions, so our main goal now is to observe how the numbers of Pascal's triangle are answers to a variety of counting questions.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:267
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tHere are some apparently different discrete objects we can count:\n"
"\t\tlattice paths, bit strings, subsets, and pizzas.\n"
"\t\tWe will give an example of each type of counting problem\n"
"\t\t(and say what these things even are).\n"
"\t\tAs we will see, the numbers in Pascal's triangle are the answers to all of these questions.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:275
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tBefore we jump in, a little bit of notation.  Let's give each number in Pascal's triangle a name, based on its position.  Think of each number as being in a row and a column: rows are counted down, starting at 0, and columns are counted in from the left, also starting at 0.  The entry in row <m>n</m> and column <m>k</m> will be denoted <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m>.  For example, the <m>\\binom{6}{3} = 20</m>, since that is the value in row 6, column 3.  For reasons that will become clear soon, we pronounce <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m> as <q><m>n</m> <term>choose</term> <m>k</m>.</q>  We can rewrite the triangle with these names:\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:286
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tTriangular array of binomial coefficients.\n"
"\t\tEach lower row extends equally on both sides beyond the row above.\n"
"\t\tTop row contains 0 choose 0.\n"
"\t\tSecond row contains 1 choose 0 and 1 choose 1 (from left to right).\n"
"\t\tThird row contains 2 choose 0, 2 choose 1, and 2 choose 2.\n"
"\t\tBelow that the row contains 3 choose 0, 3 choose 1, 3 choose 2, and 3 choose 3.\n"
"\t\tThe bottom row contains 4 choose 0 through 4 choose 4.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:299
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lattice Paths"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:302
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer lattice"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:303
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThe <term>integer lattice</term> is the set of all points in the Cartesian plane for which both the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> coordinates are integers.\n"
"\t\tIf you like to draw graphs on graph paper,\n"
"\t\tthe lattice is the set of all the intersections of the grid lines.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:309
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tA <term>lattice path</term>\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:311
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tis one of the shortest possible paths connecting two points on the lattice,\n"
"\t\tmoving only horizontally and vertically.\n"
"\t\tFor example,\n"
"\t\there are three possible lattice paths from the point <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(3,2)</m>:\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA grid of 12 dots arranged in a 4-wide by 3-high rectangle.\n"
"\t\t\tThe lower-left dot is labeled (0,0).\n"
"\t\t\tThe top right dot is labeled (3,2).\n"
"\t\t\tA bold line runs from the bottom left dot to the third dot on the bottom row, then turns up and runs to the top dot in that column, then turns right and runs to the top-right dot.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:349
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA grid of 12 dots arranged in a 4-wide by 3-high rectangle.\n"
"\t\t\tThe lower-left dot is labeled (0,0).\n"
"\t\t\tThe top right dot is labeled (3,2).\n"
"\t\t\tA bold line runs from the bottom up to the top left dot, and then to the top-right dot.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:368
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA grid of 12 dots arranged in a 4-wide by 3-high rectangle.\n"
"\t\t\tThe lower-left dot is labeled (0,0).\n"
"\t\t\tThe top right dot is labeled (3,2).\n"
"\t\t\tA bold line runs from the bottom left dot to the second dot on the bottom row, then turns up and runs to the next dot above it, then turns right and runs to the middle right dot, and finally turns up and runs to the top-right dot.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:377
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tNotice that to ensure the path is the <em>shortest</em> possible, each move must be either to the right or up.\n"
"\t\tAdditionally, in this case, no matter what path we take, we must make three steps right and two steps up.\n"
"\t\tNo matter in what order we make these steps, there will always be five steps.\n"
"\t\tThus each path has <term>length</term> five.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:385
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThe counting question we will ask is this: <em>how many</em> lattice paths are there between <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>(3,2)</m>?  In this case, drawing all the paths wouldn't take too long.\n"
"\t\tOr we could list each path as a string of <q>directions</q> such as <m>xxyyx</m>, <m>yyxxx</m>, or <m>xyxxy</m>, which correspond to the three paths drawn above, where an <m>x</m> means travel one unit in the <m>x</m> direction, and similarly for <m>y</m>.\n"
"\t\tWe would get the following ten paths:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:391
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tWhen the distance between starting and stopping points is larger, we will want to find a more efficient way to count the paths.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:403
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tLet's take what we learned from the rook paths (which are, gasp, actually lattice paths).\n"
"\t\tConsider the lattice shown below:\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:420
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tA grid of 12 dots arranged in a 4-wide by 3-high rectangle.\n"
"\t\t\tThe lower-left dot is labeled (0,0).\n"
"\t\t\tThe top right dot is labeled (3,2).\n"
"\t\t\tThe dot on the top row directly to the left of (3,2) is labeled A; the dot directly below (3,2) is labeled B.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:429
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAny lattice path from (0,0) to (3,2) must pass through exactly one of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.\n"
"\t\tThe point <m>A</m> is 4 steps away from (0,0) and two of them are in the <m>x</m> direction.\n"
"\t\tThe last step is also in the <m>x</m> direction, so the paths from (0,0) to (3,2) that pass through <m>A</m> are exactly the six strings we listed above that end in an <m>x</m>.\n"
"\t\tFor the paths that pass through point <m>B</m>, the last step will be in the <m>y</m> direction, so the paths from (0,0) to (3,2) that pass through <m>B</m> are exactly the four strings we listed above that end in a <m>y</m>.\n"
"\t\tSo the total number of paths to (3,2) is just <m>6+4</m>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:437
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThe general observation here is that to find the number of paths that start at <m>(0,0)</m> and end at <m>(m,n)</m>, we can find the number of paths to the point directly to the left of the endpoint, <m>(m-1,n)</m> and add the number of paths to the point directly below the endpoint, <m>(m,n-1)</m>.\n"
"\t\tThis is exactly the same way that Pascal's triangle is generated!  Indeed, if we rotate the lattice appropriately, so the point <m>(0,0)</m> is at the top of the triangle and the axes along the sides of the triangle, we see that the numbers in Pascal's triangle give us exactly the number of paths to each lattice point.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:442
#, no-wrap
msgid "Integer lattice overlayed on Pascal's triangle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:488
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tTo make this observation helpful for actually finding the number of paths from the origin to a given point, we note that it is the <em>length</em> of the path that determines the <em>row</em> of Pascal's triangle, and the number of steps in the <m>y</m> direction that says how far into the triangle we are -- the <em>column</em> of Pascal's triangle.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:494
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow many lattice paths are there from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(4,7)</m>?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:499
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\tThe length of these paths is <m>4+7 = 11</m>.  Look at the 11th row of Pascal's triangle: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:499
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".  Count to the 7th position (remembering that the 1 is in position 0) which gives us <m>\\binom{11}{7} = 330</m> different paths.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:506 source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:537
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bit Strings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:511
#, no-wrap
msgid "bit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:512
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t<q>Bit</q> is short for <q>binary digit,</q>\n"
"\t\tso a <term>bit string</term>\n"
"\t\tis a string of binary digits.\n"
"\t\tThe <term>binary digits</term> are simply the numbers 0 and 1.\n"
"\t\tAll of the following are bit strings:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:519 source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:608
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:679 source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:437
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:448 source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:566
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:578
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:527
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThe number of bits (0's or 1's) in the string is the\n"
"\t\t<term>length</term> of the string;\n"
"\t\tthe strings above have lengths 4, 1, 4, and 10 respectively.\n"
"\t\tWe also can ask how many of the bits are 1's.\n"
"\t\tThe number of 1's in a bit string is the\n"
"\t\t<term>weight</term> of the string;\n"
"\t\tthe weights of the above strings are 2, 0, 4, and 5 respectively.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><idx><see>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:539
#, no-wrap
msgid "bit string"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:548
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tAn <term><m>n</m>-bit string</term>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tis a bit string of length <m>n</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThat is, it is a string containing <m>n</m> symbols,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\teach of which is a bit, either 0 or 1.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:557
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tThe <term>weight</term>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:559
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tof a bit string is the number of 1's in it.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:573
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t<m>\\B^n_k</m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:575
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\B^n_k</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li><notation><description>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:576
#, no-wrap
msgid "the set of length <m>n</m> bit strings with weight <m>k</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p><ul><li>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:578
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tis the set of all <m>n</m>-bit strings of weight <m>k</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:586
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tFor example,\n"
"\t\tthe elements of the set <m>\\B^3_2</m> are the bit strings 011, 101, and 110.\n"
"\t\tThose are the only strings containing three bits, exactly two of which are 1's.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:592
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThe counting questions: How many 5-bit strings have weight 3?\n"
"\t\tIn other words,\n"
"\t\twe are asking for the cardinality <m>|\\B^5_3|</m>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:598
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tLet's just list them and see how many there are.  \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tGreat.  Ten of them. Actually, I have a confession: I didn't type all of these from scratch.  Instead I just modified the list of 10 lattice paths from (0,0) to (3,2) that we found earlier.  Each <m>x</m> became a 1 and each <m>y</m> became a 0.  After all, any lattice path with length <m>n</m> that requires <m>k</m> steps in the <m>x</m> direction can be represented by a string of <m>n</m> symbols of two types, with <m>k</m> of those symbols being of one type.  Whether we call the two symbols <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> or we call them <m>1</m> and <m>0</m> will not change <em>how many</em> strings we get. \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:607
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tIt is not surprising then that the same relationship between Pascal's triangle and lattice paths holds for bit strings.  Look at the 10 strings above.  The first row contains all the bit strings of <m>\\B^5_3</m> that end in a 0.  Before that ending 0, we have a string in <m>\\B^4_3</m>, since it must have length 4 and weight 3 (the ending 0 increases the length, but not the weight).  The second row contains all the bit strings of <m>\\B^5_3</m> that end in a 1.  Before that ending 1, we have a string in <m>\\B^4_2</m>, since it must have length 4 and weight 2 (the ending 1 increases the length and the weight).  So the number of 5-bit strings of weight 3 is the sum of the number of 4-bit strings of weight 3 and the number of 4-bit strings of weight 2.  In symbols: \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:613
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tNow we have two good reasons to believe that Pascal's triangle tells us the number of bit strings of a given weight: There is a one-to-one correspondence between lattice paths and bit strings, and the same recursive relationship holds for bit strings as it does for generating Pascal's triangle.  So we can now use the triangle to count bit strings.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:619
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow many 11-bit strings have weight 5?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:624
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThere will be <m>\\binom{11}{5}</m> such strings.  From Pascal's triangle, we see that <m>\\binom{11}{5} = 462</m>\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:664
#, no-wrap
msgid "Subsets and Pizzas"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:668
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tA <term>subset</term> of a set <m>A</m> is any set all of whose elements are also in <m>A</m>.  Think of starting with the set <m>A</m> and removing some (or none or all) of its elements: the resulting set is a subset of <m>A</m>.\n"
"\t\t(More information about sets can be found in <xref po4a-id=0> and <xref po4a-id=1>.)\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:672
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tSuppose we look at the set <m>A = \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\tHow many subsets of <m>A</m> contain exactly 3 elements?\n"
"\t\tLet's list them all:\n"
"\t\t\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:683
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAgain, we see there are ten.  In fact, we have listed them in the same order as we listed the ten 5-bit strings of weight 3 and the ten lattice paths from (0,0) to (3,2).  Wait, does this even make sense?  In what way is a subset the same as a bit-string?\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:687
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThink of each bit in a bit string as representing one of the elements in a set.  The set <m>A</m> has five elements, so we need five bits to represent a subset of <m>A</m>.  If the bit in position <m>n</m> is a 0, that means we do <em>not</em> include <m>n</m> in our subset, while a 1 in that position tells us that <m>n</m> is in the subset.  Three 1's means we have said, <q>yes</q> to three elements.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:693
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tWhich subsets of <m>\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}</m> correspond to the bit strings below?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:701
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHere we are not fixing the weight of the strings, so our subsets will not all have the same size.  Here is the correspondence:\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:706
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>101011</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:706
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{1,3,5,6\\}</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:709
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>001000</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:709
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{3\\}</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:712
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>111111</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:712
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:715
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>000000</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:715 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:267
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:272
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\emptyset</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:719
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe last subset is the <term>empty set</term>: the set that contains no elements (we could have also written <m>\\{\\}</m>).  This is a subset of <em>every</em> set!\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><remark><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:727
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tWhat we have done here is give a <em>bijection</em> between the set of 5-bit strings of weight 3 and the set of 3-element subsets of <m>A</m>.  A <term>bijection</term> is a function <m>f: X \\to Y</m> such that each element of <m>Y</m> is the image of exactly one element from <m>X</m>.  You can prove that if there is a bijection between two sets, then they have the same number of elements.  This is a common counting technique we will use in the upcoming sections.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:733
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThis example illustrates that, once again, Pascal's triangle can give us the answer to a counting question.  The number of <m>k</m>-element subsets of a set with <m>n</m> elements is the same as the number of <m>n</m>-bit strings of weight <m>k</m>, and that is the number in row <m>n</m>, column <m>k</m> of the triangle: <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:739
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow many subsets of the set <m>\\{a,b,c,d,e,f,g\\}</m> have exactly 4 elements?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:744
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe set contains 7 elements, so the number of 4-element subsets is the same as the number of 7-bit strings of weight 4, namely <m>\\binom{7}{4} = 35</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:750
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAt this point I'm sure we are all getting pretty hungry, so let's get some pizza.  But which pizza shall we order?  Let's not overdo it and just choose three toppings from the ten available.  How many different pizzas can we order? \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:754
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAha!  So that's why we care about counting subsets!!  Each pizza choice is nothing more than a 3-element subset of the set of 10 toppings.  We now know how to count this: <m>\\binom{10}{3} = 120</m> different pizzas.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:758
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tWhat if we want an Italian soda with dinner?  Let's say we want to add two different flavored syrups from the 13 available.  How many different sodas are possible?  This is just the number of 2-element subsets of a set with 13 elements: <m>\\binom{13}{2} = 78</m>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:762
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    This is why we pronounce <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m> as <q><m>n</m> choose <m>k</m></q>.  It is the number of ways to choose <m>k</m> items from a collection of <m>n</m> items, since choosing <m>k</m> elements is exactly how you build a <m>k</m>-element subset.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:766
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tWe can view counting lattice paths as choosing <m>k</m> out of <m>n</m> things: Of the <m>n</m> steps on the path, we choose <m>k</m> of them to be in the <m>x</m> direction.  Bit strings can also be thought of in this way: Of the <m>n</m> bits in the string, we choose <m>k</m> of them to be 1's.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:771
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tWe can now answer all sorts of real-world counting problems, as long as they are really nothing more than asking for the number of subsets of a set.  Pascal's triangle contains all these answers.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:775
#, no-wrap
msgid "Counting Subsets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:777
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe number of <m>k</m>-element subsets of a set with <m>n</m> elements is the number in row <m>n</m>, column <m>k</m> of Pascal's triangle: <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m>,\n"
"\t\t\twhich we read as <q><m>n</m> choose <m>k</m>.</q>  This is the number of ways to choose <m>k</m> items from a collection of <m>n</m> items.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:845
#, no-wrap
msgid "Algebra?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:848
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tEarlier we said that one of the original uses for Pascal's triangle was to solve problems in <em>algebra</em>.  What does counting subsets (or bit strings or lattice paths) have to do with algebra?\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:852
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tSuppose you expand the binomial expression <m>(x+1)^6</m> (i.e., multiply the binomial <m>x+1</m> by itself six times).  This can be tedious to do by hand, but a computer algebra system such as SageMath can do this easily.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sage><input>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:857
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\texpand((x+1)^6)\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sage><output>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:860
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tx^6 + 6*x^5 + 15*x^4 + 20*x^3 + 15*x^2 + 6*x + 1\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:865
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tDo the coefficients look familiar?  Consider the 6th row of Pascal's triangle:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:868
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tWhy are these the coefficients?\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:875
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tModify the SageMath code above to expand <m>(x+1)^{10}</m>.  What is the coefficient of <m>x^6</m>?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:878
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t<var po4a-id=0>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><setup><var>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:882
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t<var>\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><setup><var><condition><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:886
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tCorrect.  The coefficient of <m>x^6</m> is 210, which also happens to be <m>\\binom{10}{6}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><setup><var><condition><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:893
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tNo, that is not the coefficient of <m>x^6</m>.  Try executing the SageMath cell above after changing the exponent to 10.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><exercise><setup>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:897
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t</var>\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:902
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\tTo see why this is more than just a coincidence, let's look at the expansion of <m>(x+y)^3</m> and do it very carefully.  We are really multiplying out "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:902
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".  This means we must distribute the binomials, which looks like the following. (We will use a different typeface for each version of the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> to keep track of where everything comes from.)\n"
"\t\t\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:910
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tThis repeated distribution results in a sum of terms, each the product of three variables.  We see that each term is the result of <em>choosing</em> either the <m>x</m> or the <m>y</m> from each of the binomials.  For example, the term <m>x\\y\\X</m> is the result of choosing the <m>x</m> from the first binomial, the <m>\\y</m> from the second, and the <m>\\X</m> from the third.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:915
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tSay we want to find the coefficient of the <m>x^2y</m> therm.  We collect like terms, collecting all the terms in which we have chosen <m>x</m> two times (and <m>y</m> the other one time).  Alternatively, the <m>x^2y</m> term comes from all the strings with two <m>x</m> and one <m>y</m>, just like a bit string or lattice path.  No matter how you think of it, the result is that we have <m>\\binom{3}{2} = 3</m> terms with the form <m>x^2y</m>.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:919
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tHopefully it is clear that this generalizes to the expansion of <m>(x+y)^n</m> for any positive integer <m>n</m>.  This is known as the <em>binomial theorem.</em>\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:923
#, no-wrap
msgid "Binomial Theorem"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:927
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe <m>n</m>th row of Pascal's triangle gives the coefficients of the expansion of <m>(x+y)^n</m>.  That is, for any positive integer <m>n</m>,\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-pascal.ptx:930
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"\t\t\t\tso the coefficient of <m>x^ky^{n-k}</m> is <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
