"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:40
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tA standard deck of playing cards contains 52 cards.  There are four suits: clubs, diamonds, hearts, and spades.  Each suit contains cards of 13 values: Ace, 2, 3,..., 10, Jack, Queen, and King.  \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:43
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe call the hearts and diamonds <term>red cards</term> (with clubs and spades the <term>black cards</term>).  In each suit, the Jack, Queen, and King are called <term>face cards</term> (the others are call <term>number cards</term>).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSuppose you pick two cards from a deck.  How many different combinations will have the first card be a red card and the second card be a face card?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:54
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t<em>Combinatorics</em> is about counting, but looking at the word, we see it really deals with how things <em>combine</em>.\n"
"\t\t\tIn this section, we will consider two very different ways that we can combine the outcomes we count in combinatorics problems.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tA restaurant offers 8 appetizers and 14 entrées.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHow many choices do you have if:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:68
#, no-wrap
