#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "What are <em>Outcomes</em>?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tBefore we start counting, a quick note about how we ask counting problems.\n"
"\t\t\tOften counting problems are asked terms of how many ways something can happen.\n"
"\t\t\tThat is, there is some <term>event</term> that can result in different <term>outcomes</term>, and it is the different outcomes that are counted.\n"
"\t\t\tAlternatively, we can take a less <q>active</q> view by asking for the number of elements in a set, such as the set of bit strings of a particular length and weight.\n"
"\t\t\tHowever, this too could be rephrased as a question about an event: if you randomly select one of the bit strings (from the set of bit strings), how many things can happen?\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:146
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tSo counting the elements in a set or counting the number of outcomes of an event is not really different.  Let's think of every counting problem as asking for the number of elements in a <em>set</em> of <em>outcomes</em>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:150
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAt the most basic level, counting is not hard at all: Just write all the outcomes in a numbered list and see how many numbers you use.\n"
"\t\t\tLet me tell you about my bow tie collection.\n"
"\t\t\tI have,\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:156
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tA purple bow tie.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:162
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tA green bow tie.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:168
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tA striped bow tie.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:174
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tA paisley bow tie.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:178
#, no-wrap
msgid "Obviously this example is quite outdated; only four bow ties?  What sort of mathematician do you take me for?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:178
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tHow many bow ties do I have (how many ways can I select a bow tie)?  Well, the largest number in my numbered list is 4, so I have four bow ties.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/> Of course, as we saw in the previous section, we can use shortcuts to avoid listing out all of the outcomes, such as using Pascal's triangle to find the number of items in our list.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWhere counting begins to get interesting is when we want to make a new list that combines two or more lists we have already counted.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example, suppose that in addition to my list of four bow ties, I had a list of seven pairs of novelty socks. I could ask how many choices I have if I wanted to wear either a cool bow tie or a pair of novelty socks.\n"
"\t\t\tOkay, combine my two lists to make a new list with 11 items: Perhaps first list the four bow ties and then keep counting (starting with 5 for the first pair of socks) until you have listed the seven additional items, ending at 11.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:188
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIn this example, we have combined our two sets of outcomes (picking a bow tie and picking a pair of socks) to get a new set of outcomes (picking a bow tie or pair of socks).\n"
"\t\t\tThat resulting set of outcomes contained 11 elements.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:193
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNow consider the question: How many choices do I have if I want to wear both a bow tie and a pair of novelty socks?  Again, I need to combine my two sets of outcomes to get a new set of outcomes.\n"
"\t\t\tBut this new set of outcomes will contain 28 elements.\n"
"\t\t\tHow did I know that I should multiply the number of elements in each of my original sets, instead of adding them?  We are once again combining outcomes: The resulting set of outcomes that contains 28 elements that are all outcomes consisting of a bow tie and a pair of socks.\n"
"\t\t\tNow read the previous paragraph!  They sound very close to each other!\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:200
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t  The difference between the two scenarios is subtle.\n"
"\t\t\tThere are two ways we can think of combining outcomes:\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:205
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe can combine the <em>sets</em> of outcomes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:210
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\tWe can combine the <em>outcomes</em> in the sets.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:214
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tBeing able to distinguish between these operations is a key skill in combinatorics.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:220
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSuppose your refrigerator door has some magnetic letters and numerals on it: There are five letters (A, B, C, D, and E) and three numerals (1, 2, and 3).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tYour brother says that if you combine these sets of magnets, you will get a collection of 8 magnets.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tYour sister says that you can combine these magnets in 15 ways.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:225
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          What sets of outcomes are your siblings thinking of?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:231
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tYour brother is thinking of combining the sets of outcomes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe set of outcomes for the letters is <m>\\{A, B, C, D, E\\}</m> and the set of outcomes for the numerals is <m>\\{1, 2, 3\\}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe set of outcomes for the combined set of magnets is <m>\\{A, B, C, D, E, 1, 2, 3\\}</m>, which contains 8 elements.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:237
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tYour sister is thinking of combining the outcomes themselves.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe set of outcomes is <m>\\{A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3, C1, C2, C3, D1, D2, D3, E1, E2, E3\\}</m>, which contains 15 elements.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:245
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Sum and Product Principles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:247
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tLet's carefully write down the rules we can use to combine sets of outcomes and outcomes in sets.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><principle><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:252
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sum Principle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><principle><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:256
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIf an event <m>A</m> results in <m>m</m> outcomes, and event <m>B</m> results in <m>n</m> <em>disjoint</em> outcomes, then the event <q><m>A</m> or <m>B</m></q> results in <m>m + n</m> outcomes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:263
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIt is important that the events be <term>disjoint</term>:\n"
"\t\t\ti.e., that there is no way for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> to both happen at the same time.\n"
"\t\t\tFor example,\n"
"\t\t\ta standard deck of 52 cards contains <m>26</m> red cards and <m>12</m> face cards.\n"
"\t\t\tHowever, the number of ways to select a card which is either red or a face card is not <m>26 + 12 = 38</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThis is because there are 6 cards which are both red and face cards.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:275
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many two-letter <q>words</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tstart with either A or B? (A <term>word</term>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tis just a sequence of letters;\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tit doesn't have to be English, or even pronounceable.)\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:284
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tFirst, how many two-letter words start with A? We just need to select the second letter,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\twhich can be accomplished in 26 ways.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo there are 26 words starting with A.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThere are also 26 words that start with B.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tTo select a word that starts with either A or B, we can pick the word from the first 26 or the second 26, for a total of 52 words.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:292
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe have two sets of outcomes: the words starting with A and the words starting with B.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe create a new set of outcomes by combining the sets to get the set of words that start with either A or B, and by the sum principle, this new set contains 52 outcomes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:299
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe often use the principle when the sets of outcomes need to be counted using other counting techniques.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:305
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many <m>10</m>-bit strings of weight <m>6</m> start with either <m>11</m> or <m>00</m>?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:310
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe learned how to count bit strings with a fixed length and weight in <xref po4a-id=0>.  The total number of <m>10</m>-bit strings of weight <m>6</m> is <m>\\binom{10}{6} = 210</m> (where that value was found using <xref po4a-id=1>Pascal's triangle</xref>).  However, we want to count just those that start with <m>11</m> or <m>00</m> (and not those that start with <m>10</m> or <m>01</m>).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:314
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSince there are no bit strings that start with both <m>11</m> and <m>00</m>, we can apply the sum principle.  We will compute the size of each set of outcomes and then add the results.  \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:318
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many <m>10</m>-bit strings of weight <m>6</m> start with <m>11</m>?  If the string starts with <m>11</m>, then of the remaining eight bits, there must be four 1's, so that the total weight is <m>6</m>.  This is just like asking for the number of <m>8</m>-bit strings with weight 4, so there are <m>\\binom{8}{4} = 70</m> such strings.\tSimilarly, there are <m>\\binom{8}{6} = 28</m> strings that start with <m>00</m> (all six 1's must be in the remaining 8 bits).  By the sum principle, the total number of strings we want to count is <m>70 + 28 = 98</m>. \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:324
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe can also use the sum principle <em>indirectly</em> in a way that might be called the <em>subtraction principle</em> (but isn't).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many <m>10</m>-bit strings of weight <m>6</m> have at least one 1 in their first three bits?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:335
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThere are lots of different ways a string can have at least one 1 in its first three bits: It could start with 100, 010, 001, 101, 110, 011, or 111.  We could count the number of strings that start with each of them and then apply the sum principle a bunch of times.  But let's not.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:339
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSuppose the number of strings we want to count is <m>x</m>.  Let <m>y</m> be the number of <m>10</m>-bit strings of weight 6 that <em>do not</em> have a 1 in any of their first three bits.  What is <m>x + y</m>?  This is the number of <m>10</m>-bit strings of weight 6 in total, which is <m>\\binom{10}{6} = 210</m>.  It is also easy to find <m>y</m>: Bit strings with length 10 and weight 6 that start with 000 must end in a bit string with length 7 and weight 6.  There are <m>\\binom{7}{6} = 7</m> of these.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:343
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThus, we have\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:346
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tso <m>x = 210 - 7 = 203</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:353
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWhile we didn't want to use the sum principle multiple times in the previous example, we could have.  The sum principle works with more than two events.\n"
"\t\t\tSay, in addition to your four bow ties and seven pairs of socks,\n"
"\t\t\tyou could also pick one of five belt buckles.\n"
"\t\t\tHow many choices do you have now?\n"
"\t\t\tYou would have <m>4+7+5 = 16</m> options.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:363
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many two-letter words start with one of the 5 vowels?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:369
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThere are 26 two-letter words starting with A, another 26 starting with E, and so on.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tBy the sum principle, the total number of words will then be <m>26+26+26+26+26 = 130</m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOf course it would be easier to just multiply <m>5\\cdot 26</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:399
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNote that in the previous example,\n"
"\t\t\twhen using the sum principle on a bunch of outcome sets of the same size, it is quicker to multiply.\n"
"\t\t\tLet's take advantage of this observation and state it as a new principle.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><principle><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:406
#, no-wrap
msgid "Product Principle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><principle><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:410
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIf event <m>A</m> can occur in <m>m</m> ways,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tand each possibility for <m>A</m> allows for exactly <m>n</m> ways for event <m>B</m>,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tthen the event <q><m>A</m> and <m>B</m></q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tcan occur in <m>m \\cdot n</m> ways.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:419
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A nice way to picture the product principle is to think of the outcomes as <q>levels</q> in a tree. To illustrate this, consider the counting question of <xref po4a-id=0>: How many ways can you select a letter from <m>\\{A, B, C, D, E\\}</m> followed by a numeral from <m>\\{1, 2, 3\\}</m>? Here is a tree picture that shows what is going on.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><shortdescription>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:423
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tree of outcomes for two symbol sequences where the first symbol is A-E and the second is 1-3."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:438
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Each leaf of the tree corresponds to an outcome.  The tree shows that the three outcomes of the second level are repeated five times, once for each outcome of the first level.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:444
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many two-letter words start with one of the 5 vowels?  This time use the product principle to answer the question.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:450
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow can we think of selecting a two-letter word as a sequence of two events that both happen?  Event <m>A</m> is <q>selecting the vowel that starts the word.</q>  Event <m>B</m> is <q>selecting the second letter for the word.</q>  By the product principle, there are <m>5 \\cdot 26 = 130</m> ways for the event <q><m>A</m> and <m>B</m></q> to occur.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:456
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOf course the solutions to <xref po4a-id=0> and <xref po4a-id=1> are the same, but the outcomes we are combining and the ways we are combining them are different.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:460
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWhen applying the sum principle, we combined five disjoint sets of outcomes.\n"
"\t\t\tOne such set of outcomes was,\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:465
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tand another was,\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:468
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\tThat is, each set of outcomes was a set of 2-letter words, starting with a different vowel.\n"
"\t\t\tThe sum principle combined these five sets to create a new set that contained exactly the same elements in the original sets, just all together in one set.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:474
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOn the other hand, when we applied the product principle, we combined the outcomes in two sets.\n"
"\t\t\tThe first set was,\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:479
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tand the second set was,\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:482
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\tThe product principle combines the elements in those sets to make a set of new outcomes (outcomes not present in any of the sets being combined).\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:487
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tAs with the sum principle, we often use the product principle to define sets of outcomes that first need to be counted carefully.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:493
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many lattice paths from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(10,10)</m> pass through the point <m>(4,7)</m>?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:498
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can count lattice paths with <xref po4a-id=0>Pascal's triangle</xref>, but how do we ensure that the path passes through a particular point?  Well, any path that passes through <m>(4,7)</m> must first go from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(4,7)</m>, and then complete its journey with a path from <m>(4,7)</m> to <m>(10,10)</m>. \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:501
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe number of paths from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(4,7)</m> is <m>\\binom{11}{4} = 330</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:505
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe number of paths from <m>(4,7)</m> to <m>(10,10)</m> is <m>\\binom{9}{3} = 84</m>.  (The paths have length 9 and with 6 steps in the <m>x</m> direction.)\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:509
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNow combine these with the... wait, what principle?  To get a path from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(10,10)</m> that passes through <m>(4,7)</m>, we need to first select a path from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(4,7)</m> and then <em>add on</em> a path from <m>(4,7)</m> to <m>(10,10)</m>.  So is this the sum principle???\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:513
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNo!  Remember that the sum principle combines the two sets of outcomes.  If we applied the sum principle, we would get a larger set containing the <m>330 + 84</m> paths that are contained in each individual set.  None of these are paths we want to count.  We want to combine the different elements in our sets of outcomes in every possible combination.  That is why the product principle is correct here.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:517
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThus, the total number of paths we want to count is <m>330 \\cdot 84 = 27720</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:523
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe product principle generalizes to more than two events as well.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:529
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many license plates can you make out of three letters followed by three numerical digits?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:535
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHere we have six events: the first letter, the second letter, the third letter, the first digit, the second digit, and the third digit.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe first three events can each happen in 26 ways; the last three can each happen in 10 ways.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tSo the total number of license plates will be <m>26\\cdot 26\\cdot 26 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10</m>, using the product principle.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:541
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tDoes this make sense? Think about how we would pick a license plate.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many choices would we have? First, we need to pick the first letter.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThere are 26 choices.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tNow for each of those, there are 26 choices for the second letter: 26 second letters with first letter A, 26 second letters with first letter B, and so on.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe add 26 to itself 26 times.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tOr quicker: there are <m>26 \\cdot 26</m> choices for the first two letters.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:550
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tNow for each choice of the first two letters, we have 26 choices for the third letter.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThat is, 26 third letters for the first two letters AA, 26 choices for the third letter after starting AB, and so on.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThere are <m>26 \\cdot 26</m> of these <m>26</m> third letter choices, for a total of <m>(26\\cdot26)\\cdot 26</m> choices for the first three letters.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAnd for each of these <m>26\\cdot26\\cdot26</m> choices of letters, we have a bunch of choices for the remaining digits.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:557
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIn fact, there are going to be exactly 1000 choices for the numbers.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWe can see this because there are 1000 three-digit numbers (000 through 999).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThis is 10 choices for the first digit, 10 for the second, and 10 for the third.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThe product principle says we multiply: <m>10\\cdot 10 \\cdot 10 = 1000</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:564
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tAll together, there were <m>26^3</m> choices for the three letters and <m>10^3</m> choices for the numbers, so we have a total of <m>26^3 \\cdot 10^3</m> choices of license plates.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:570
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThe previous example had an answer that used exponents.  So is there an <term>exponential principle</term> we should learn?  We don't separate one out as there really isn't anything different in the way we think of constructing our outcome sets.  But we will often see the product principle applied to the same event repeated multiple times.  Here are two examples.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:576
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tSuppose you roll a 12-sided die seven times, recording the number that appears after each roll.  How many sequences of seven rolls are possible?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:581
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tEach roll can result in 12 different outcomes, so by the product principle, the total number of sequences is <m>12^7</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:589
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many different pizzas can you make if you can choose from 10 toppings and you can have any number of toppings on your pizza?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:594
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tIf we want to apply the product principle, we must think of the compound event of picking a pizza as the result of combining some number of individual events in every possible combination.  What are those events?  There are multiple answers to this question, but the following is the most useful.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:598
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tFirst, decide whether you want to include anchovies on your pizza or not.  This event could result in two outcomes (yes or no to the anchovies).  Second, decide whether you want black olives.  Then Canadian bacon, then diced tomatoes, etc.  Each event has two outcomes, and we must combine all ten of these outcomes to create a single outcome corresponding to a single pizza.  Thus, by the product principle, there are <m>2^{10}</m> possible pizzas.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tBy the way, another approach to this problem is to <q>code</q> each pizza as a bit-string, where a 1 means the topping is included and a 0 means it is not.  Then the number of pizzas is the number of bit-strings of length 10.  Each bit in the string has two possible values, so there are <m>2^{10}</m> 10-bit strings.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:608
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tCareful: <q>and</q> doesn't mean <q>times.</q> For example, how many playing cards are both red and a face card? Not <m>26 \\cdot 12</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThe answer is 6, and we needed to know something about cards to answer that question.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:615
#, no-wrap
msgid "Counting functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:620
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many functions <m>f:\\{1,2,3,4,5\\} \\to \\{a,b,c,d\\}</m> are there?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:626
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tRemember that a function sends each element of the domain to exactly one element of the codomain.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tTo determine a function,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\twe just need to specify the image of each element in the domain.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhere can we send 1?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThere are 4 choices.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhere can we send 2?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tAgain, 4 choices.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat we have here is 5 <q>events</q>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(picking the image of an element in the domain)\n"
"\t\t\t\t\teach of which can happen in 4 ways\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(the choices for that image).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tThus there are <m>4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4 = 4^5</m> functions.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:641
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThis is more than just an example of how we can use the product principle in a particular counting question.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat we have here is a general interpretation of certain applications of the product principle using rigorously defined mathematical objects:\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tfunctions.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tWhenever we have a counting question that asks for the number of outcomes of a repeated event,\n"
"\t\t\t\t\twe can interpret that as asking for the number of functions from <m>\\{1,2,\\ldots, n\\}</m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(where <m>n</m> is the number of times the event is repeated)\n"
"\t\t\t\t\tto <m>\\{1,2,\\ldots,k\\}</m>\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t(where <m>k</m> is the number of ways that event can occur).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:655
#, no-wrap
msgid "Combining Principles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:658
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tLet's return to this section's <em>Investigate!</em> question: How many ways can you select two cards from a standard deck of 52, so that the first one is a red card and the second one is a face card? \n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:662
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThis looks a little like the product principle since the outcomes consist of pairs of cards.  There are 26 red cards that are outcomes for the first event, and 12 face cards that are outcomes for the second event.  However, the answer is not <m>26 \\cdot 12</m>.\n"
"\t\t\tThe problem is that while there are 26 ways for the first card to be selected, it is not the case that <em>for each</em> of those, there are 12 ways to select the second card.\n"
"\t\t\tIf the first card was both red and a face card, then there would be only 11 choices for the second card.  \t\t\t\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:668
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tIn <xref po4a-id=0> we will explore some ways to adjust for counting problems when events are not disjoint, but we can solve our card problem now if we are careful.  Think about the entire set of two-card outcomes.  Can we split these outcomes into two disjoint sets (as to apply the sum principle) such that each set can be formed using the product principle?\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:672
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tOf all the outcomes, we first count those that start with a red, non-face card.  Of the 26 red cards, there are 6 that are face cards, so there are 20 red, non-face cards.  For each of these, there are 12 face cards that can be selected as the second card.  By the product principle, the number of two-card outcomes starting with a red, non-face card is <m>20 \\cdot 12 = 240</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:676
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tNow, what outcomes have we not yet counted?  It is exactly the two-card outcomes that start with a red face card.  There are 6 cards we could start with, and for each there are 11 choices for the second card.  So the number of two-card outcomes starting with a red face card is <m>6 \\cdot 11 = 66</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:680
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tFinally, apply the sum principle to these two disjoint sets of outcomes to see that the total number of two-card outcomes is <m>240 + 66 = 306</m>.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tWe conclude this section with two more examples of how you can use both the sum and product principles in a single counting problem.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:690
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many lattice paths from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(9,9)</m> pass through either <m>(2,6)</m> or <m>(6,2)</m>?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:695
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWe are quite fortunate that no paths from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(9,9)</m> pass through both <m>(2,6)</m> and <m>(6,2)</m>.  This means we can apply the sum principle to the two sets of outcomes: the paths that pass through <m>(2,6)</m> and the paths that pass through <m>(6,2)</m>.  We will apply the product principle to determine the number of paths in each of these disjoint sets of outcomes.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:699
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe paths from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(9,9)</m> that pass through <m>(2,6)</m> are each the concatenation of paths from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(2,6)</m> and paths from <m>(2,6)</m> to <m>(9,9)</m>.  There are <m>\\binom{8}{2} = 28</m> paths for the first part and <m>\\binom{10}{7} = 120</m> paths for the second part, so applying the product principle gives us <m>28 \\cdot 120 = 3360</m> paths that pass through <m>(2,6)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThe paths from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(9,9)</m> that pass through <m>(6,2)</m> can similarly be calculated as\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:707
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t(and upon reflection, it is not surprising that the two numbers are the same, since each path that passes through <m>(2,6)</m> can be reflected to create a path that passes through <m>(6,2)</m>).\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:710
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tThus, the total number of paths that pass through either <m>(2,6)</m> or <m>(6,2)</m> is <m>3360 + 3360 = 6720</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:718
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tHow many two-digit numbers, using only the digits <m>\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}</m> have the <em>sum</em> of their digits even?\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:723
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tFirst think about the set of outcomes.  Some two-digit numbers that have the sum of their digits even are <m>11, 13, 15, 22, 24, 31, \\ldots</m>.  Okay, maybe it isn't all that difficult to just list them all (and this would be a nice way to check our work).  Let's try to use the sum and product principles anyway.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:727
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tWhat do we notice about all these numbers?  It looks like both digits must be even or both digits must be odd, to make the sum even.  We can consider these two cases, counting each case with the product principle.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combine-outcomes.ptx:731
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tCounting <q>odd-odd</q> numbers: three choices for the first digit and three choices for the second digit.  So there are <m>3 \\cdot 3 = 9</m> odd-odd numbers.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
