#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:268
#, no-wrap
msgid "Counting Sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:272
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" A <term>permutation</term> is a (possible) rearrangement of objects.\n"
"\t\tFor example,\n"
"\t\tthere are 6 permutations of the letters <em>a, b, c</em>:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:277
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tIn terms of our discrete structures, each permutation is really a <em>sequence</em> or <em>tuple</em> of a fixed length.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:282
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tWe know that we have\n"
"\t\tthem all listed above "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:283
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"there are 3 choices for which letter we put first, then 2 choices\n"
"\t\tfor which letter comes next, which leaves only 1 choice for the last letter.\n"
"\t\tThe multiplicative\n"
"\t\tprinciple says we multiply <m>3\\cdot 2 \\cdot 1</m>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:292
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow many sequences (permutations) are there of the\n"
"\t\t\t\tletters <em>a, b, c, d, e, f</em>?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:299
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tWe do NOT want to try to list all of the length 6 sequences of these letters.\n"
"\t\t\t\tHowever, if we did, we would need to pick a letter to write down first.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThere are\n"
"\t\t\t\t6 choices for that letter.\n"
"\t\t\t\tFor each choice of the first letter, there are 5 choices for the\n"
"\t\t\t\tsecond letter (we cannot repeat the first letter; we are rearranging letters and only have\n"
"\t\t\t\tone of each), and for each of those, there are 4 choices for the third, 3 choices for the\n"
"\t\t\t\tfourth, 2 choices for the fifth, and finally only 1 choice for the last letter.\n"
"\t\t\t\tSo there are <m>6\n"
"\t\t\t\t\\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 720</m> permutations of the 6 letters.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:314
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\tA piece of notation is helpful here: <m>n!</m>, read <q><m>n</m> factorial</q>, "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:316
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tis the product of all positive integers less than or equal to <m>n</m> (for reasons of convenience,\n"
"\t\twe also define 0! to be 1).\n"
"\t\tSo the number of permutations of 6 letters, as seen in the previous\n"
"\t\texample is <m>6! = 6\\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1</m>.\n"
"\t\tThis generalizes:\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:324
#, no-wrap
msgid "Permutations of <m>n</m> Elements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><idx><h><m>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:325 source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:419
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:422 source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:458
#: source/sec_addtops-numbth.ptx:425
#, no-wrap
msgid "n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:329
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tThere are <m>n! = n\\cdot (n-1)\\cdot (n-2)\\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 2\\cdot 1</m> permutations of <m>n</m> (distinct) elements.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:334
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Counting\n"
"\t\tBijective Functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:344
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow many functions <m>f:\\{1,2,\\ldots,8\\} \\to \\{1,2,\\ldots, 8\\}</m> are <em>\n"
"\t\t\t\tbijective</em>?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:351
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tRemember what it means for a function to be bijective:\n"
"\t\t\t\tEach element in the codomain must be the image of exactly one element of the domain.\n"
"\t\t\t\tUsing\n"
"\t\t\t\ttwo-line notation, we could write one of these bijections as\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:358
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\tWhat we are really doing is just rearranging the elements of the codomain, so we are\n"
"\t\t\t\tcreating a permutation of 8 elements.\n"
"\t\t\t\tIn fact, <q>permutation</q> is another term used to\n"
"\t\t\t\tdescribe bijective functions from a finite set to itself.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:366
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tIf you believe this, then you\n"
"\t\t\t\tsee the answer must be <m>8! = 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot\\cdots\\cdot 1 = 40320</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tYou can see this\n"
"\t\t\t\tdirectly as well: For each element of the domain, we must pick a distinct element of the\n"
"\t\t\t\tcodomain to map to.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThere are 8 choices for where to send 1, then 7 choices for where to\n"
"\t\t\t\tsend 2, and so on.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWe multiply using the multiplicative principle.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tSometimes we do not want to permute all of the letters/numbers/elements we are given.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:391
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow many four-letter <q>\n"
"\t\t\t\twords</q> can you make from the letters <em>a</em> through <em>g</em>, with no repeated letters?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:398
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThis is just like the problem of permuting four letters, only now we have more choices for each\n"
"\t\t\t\tletter.\n"
"\t\t\t\tFor the first letter, there are 7 choices.\n"
"\t\t\t\tFor each of those, there are 6 choices\n"
"\t\t\t\tfor the second letter.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThen there are 5 choices for the third letter and 4 choices for the\n"
"\t\t\t\tlast letter.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThe total number of words is <m>7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 = 840</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:409
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThis is not <m>7!</m> because we never multiplied by 3, 2, or 1.\n"
"\t\t\t\tWe could write it using <m>7!</m> though, if we cancel the 3, 2, and 1.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThus we could write the answer as "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><idx><h><m>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:418 source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:421
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:458
#, no-wrap
msgid "k"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:419
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>k</m>-permutation of <m>n</m> elements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:424
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tIn general, we can ask how many permutations exist of <m>k</m>\n"
"\t\tobjects choosing those objects from a larger collection of <m>n</m> objects.\n"
"\t\t(In the example above, <m>k\n"
"\t\t= 4</m>, and <m>n = 7</m>.) We write this number <m>P(n,k)</m> and sometimes call it a <term><m>k</m>-permutation\n"
"\t\tof <m>n</m> elements</term>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:432
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tFrom the example above, we see that to compute <m>P(n,k)</m> we must\n"
"\t\tapply the multiplicative principle to <m>k</m> numbers, starting with <m>n</m> and counting backwards.\n"
"\t\tFor example\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:441
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tNotice that <m>P(10,4)</m>\n"
"\t\tstarts out looking like <m>10!</m>, but we stop after 7.\n"
"\t\tWe can formally account for this <q>stopping</q>\n"
"\t\tby dividing away the part of the factorial we do not want:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:452
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tCareful: The factorial in the denominator is not <m>4!</m> but rather <m>(10-4)!</m>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:456
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>\n"
"\t\tk</m>-permutations of <m>n</m> elements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><definition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:465
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t<m>P(n,k)</m> is the number of <term><m>k</m>-permutations of <m>n</m> elements</term>,\n"
"\t\t\tthe number of ways to <em>arrange</em>\n"
"\t\t\t<m>k</m> objects chosen from <m>n</m> distinct objects.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:475
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThe number of <m>k</m>-permutations of <m>n</m> elements is\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:484
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tNote that when <m>n = k</m>, we have <m>P(n,n) = \\frac{n!}{(n-n)!} = n!</m> (since we defined <m>0!</m>\n"
"\t\tto be 1).\n"
"\t\tThis makes sense "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:486
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"we already know <m>n!</m> gives the number of permutations of\n"
"\t\tall <m>n</m> objects.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:491
#, no-wrap
msgid "Counting injective functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:497
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow many functions <m>f:\\{1,2,3\\} \\to \\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\}</m> are <em>injective</em>?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:503
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tNote that it doesn't make sense to ask for the number of <em>bijections</em> here, as there are\n"
"\t\t\t\tnone (because the codomain is larger than the domain, there are no surjections).\n"
"\t\t\t\tBut for a\n"
"\t\t\t\tfunction to be injective, we just can't use an element of the codomain more than once.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:510
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tWe need to pick an element from the codomain to be the image of 1.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThere are 8 choices.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThen\n"
"\t\t\t\twe need to pick one of the remaining 7 elements to be the image of 2.\n"
"\t\t\t\tFinally, one of the\n"
"\t\t\t\tremaining 6 elements must be the image of 3.\n"
"\t\t\t\tSo the total number of functions is <m>8\\cdot 7\n"
"\t\t\t\t\\cdot 6 = P(8,3)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:521
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tWhat this demonstrates in general is that the number of\n"
"\t\t\t\tinjections <m>f:A \\to B</m>, where <m>\\card{A} = k</m> and <m>\\card{B} = n</m>, is <m>P(n,k)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:530
#, no-wrap
msgid "Counting Sets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:533
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tLet's consider another way to count sequences: First count sets, then arrange them.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:539
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tYour basketball team has 12 players.  Assuming everyone can play every position, how many ways can you choose 5 players to be on the court at the same time?\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><fn>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:544
#, no-wrap
msgid "I'm told the five positions are called point guard, shooting guard, small forward, power forward, and center.  Who knew?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:544
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tThis question is actually too vague.  Do we mean how many ways can we select five players?  Or do we mean how many ways can we pick five players to fill the five positions?<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>  Let's answer both of these questions.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:547
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tFirst, if we just want to select five out of the 12 players, that is just like picking five out of 12 pizza toppings (although less delicious).  We know that there are <m>\\binom{12}{5}</m> ways to do this, and from <xref po4a-id=0>Pascal's triangle</xref> we know that this is 792.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:550
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\tOn the other hand, if we wanted to pick five players for the five different positions,... well, we could start by picking one of the 792 different <em>sets</em> of five players, and then permute them into the five positions.  Of the five players on the court, we pick one of the five to be the point guard, then one of the remaining four to be the shooting guard, and so on.  This gives us <m>5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 = 5!</m> ways to arrange the players.  So the total number of ways to pick five players for the five positions is "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:553
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\tWait. We could have found that number directly.  Without choosing the five players first, we have 12 choices for the point guard, then 11 choices for the shooting guard, and so on.  So the total number of ways to pick five players for the five positions is simply the permutation "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:553
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".  Thank goodness that is the same answer!\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:559
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThe example above illustrates a second way to compute the number of <m>k</m>-permutations of <m>n</m> elements: First select which <m>k</m> elements will be in the permutation, then count how many ways there are to arrange them. Once you have selected the set of <m>k</m> objects, we know there are <m>k!</m> ways to arrange (permute) them. But how do you select <m>k</m> objects from the <m>n</m>? You have <m>n</m> objects, and you need to <em>choose</em> <m>k</m> of them. You can do that in <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m> ways.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:563
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tUsing the multiplicative principle to combine the two steps, we get another formula for <m>P(n,k)</m>:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:570
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tThis is HUGE!\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:574
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tWe have a closed formula\n"
"\t\tfor <m>P(n,k)</m> already. We can substitute that in:\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:582
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tIf we then divide both sides\n"
"\t\tby <m>k!</m>, we get a closed formula for <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><theorem><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:587
#, no-wrap
msgid "Closed Formula for <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:607
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAnother name for the collections of things that <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m> counts is <term>combinations</term>.  We say that <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m> counts the number of <m>k</m>-element combinations of <m>n</m> elements.  Sometimes we even use the notation <m>C(n,k)</m> instead of <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m>.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:611
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tClearly combinations and permutations are very closely related. The formulas to count each are very similar; there is just an extra <m>k!</m> in the denominator of <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m>.  That extra <m>k!</m> accounts for the fact that <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m> does not distinguish between the different orders that the <m>k</m> objects can appear in.  We are just selecting (or choosing) the <m>k</m> objects, not arranging them.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:627
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tYou decide to have a dinner party.\n"
"\t\t\t\tEven though you are incredibly popular and have 14 different friends,\n"
"\t\t\t\tyou only have enough chairs to invite 6 of them.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:636
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHow many choices do you have for which 6 friends to invite?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:642
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tWhat if you need to decide not only which friends to invite but also where to seat them along your long table? How many choices do you have then?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:654
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tYou must simply choose 6 friends from a group of 14. This can be done in <m>\\binom{14}{6}</m> ways. We can find this number either by using Pascal's triangle or the closed formula: <m>\\frac{14!}{8!\\cdot 6!} = 3003</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:660
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHere you must count all the ways you can permute 6 friends chosen from a group of 14. So the answer is <m>P(14, 6)</m>, which can be calculated as <m>\\frac{14!}{8!} = 2162160</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:664
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tNotice that we can think of this counting problem as a question about counting functions: How many injective functions are there from your set of 6 chairs to your set of 14 friends (the functions are injective because you can't have a single chair go to two of your friends).\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:671
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow are these numbers related? Notice that <m>P(14,6)</m> is <em>much</em> larger than <m>\\binom{14}{6}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t\tThis makes sense. <m>\\binom{14}{6}</m> picks 6 friends, but <m>P(14,6)</m> arranges the 6 friends as well as picks them. In fact, we can say exactly how much larger <m>P(14,6)</m> is. In both counting problems we choose 6 out of 14 friends. For the first one, we stop there, at 3003 ways. But for the second counting problem, each of those 3003 choices of 6 friends can be arranged in exactly <m>6!</m> ways. So now we have <m>3003\\cdot 6!</m> choices and that is exactly <m>2162160</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:676
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\tAlternatively, look at the first problem another way. We want to select 6 out of 14 friends, but we do not care about the order they are selected in. To select 6 out of 14 friends, we might try this: "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:680
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\t\t\tThis is a reasonable guess, since we have 14 choices for the first guest, then 13 for the second, and so on. But the guess is wrong (in fact, that product is exactly <m>2162160 = P(14,6)</m>). It distinguishes between the different orders in which we could invite the guests. To correct for this, we could divide by the number of different arrangements of the 6 guests (so that all of these would count as just one outcome). There are precisely <m>6!</m> ways to arrange 6 guests, so the correct answer to the first question is "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tNote that another way to write this is\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:687
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\t\t\twhich is what we had originally.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:694
#, no-wrap
msgid "\t\tPerhaps <q>combination</q> is a misleading label.  We don't mean it like a combination lock (where the order would definitely matter).  Perhaps a better metaphor is a combination of flavors "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:694
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" you just need to decide which flavors to combine, not the order in which to combine them.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:698
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tSo how do you know when to use a combination and when to use a permutation?  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><title>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:702
#, no-wrap
msgid "Does order matter?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:704
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAlmost every source you look at about combinations and permutations will make some variation of the following claim.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:709
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\tUse a permutation when the order matters, and use a combination when the order does not matter.\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:714
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tWhat does this even mean? And why is it an absolutely awful way to distinguish between the two counting techniques?  Let's explore with an example.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:718
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tSuppose you are proposing a new lottery game.  In the game, five numbered balls will be randomly shot out of the machine that holds balls numbered 1 to 50.  If a player correctly picks the five numbers that come out, they win the jackpot.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:722
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tOf course, we want to know the odds of winning this lottery game, which requires us to know how many different outcomes there are.  The balls fly out of the machine randomly, so one result might be to get the balls\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:725
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tIf you were holding the ticket that had numbers <m>5, 17, 26, 33, 47</m>, do you expect to have won?  Most lottery games would say you have, since you have the same numbers, just in a different order.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:730
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tIn this sense, the <em>order</em> of the numbers does not matter.  A better way to say this is that <em>any</em> order of the same numbers is considered the same outcome, and is counted only once.  So really, we are counting the <em>sets</em> of five numbers, not the <em>sequences</em> of five numbers.  Each outcome is a combination, so the number of outcomes should be <m>C(52,5) = \\binom{52}{5}</m>.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:734
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tOn the other hand, if we must pick the numbers in the same order they come out of the machine, then we are really matching a sequence of numbers to a sequence of numbers.  The number of sequences is <m>P(52,5)</m>.  In this case, we would say that the order matters.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:738
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tOkay, so far so good.  But what about the following example? \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:744
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tHow many three letter <q>words</q> are there in which \n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:748
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tthe letters appear in alphabetical order?  \n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:753
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\tthe letters in the word can come in any order?\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:762
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tDoes <q>order matter</q> for the first question?  In some sense, absolutely!  We only want to count words where the letters are in the correct order.  But from the standard combination/permutation sense of order mattering, it does not.  \n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:766
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tMuch better would be to ask ourselves whether we should represent each outcome as a set of letters or a sequence of letters.  It is true that words are a sequence of letters, but are we counting all possible sequences?  Nope, just one sequence for each set of letters.  Ah, yes, set of letters.  Each outcome corresponds exactly to one set of three letters.  We are counting sets, so we count combinations.  The number of outcomes is <m>C(26,3) = \\binom{26}{3}</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:770
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\tFor the second question, it might look like the order shouldn't matter.  Well, it doesn't matter to the machine spitting out the letters, but it would matter if we were trying to match the letters.  No, let's think about it like this: Are we counting every possible sequence of letters?  Yes!  So these are permutations, and the number of outcomes is <m>P(26,3)</m>.\n"
"\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:776
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAnother deceptive use of order appears when counting bit strings.  Recall that an <m>n</m>-bit string of weight <m>k</m> is a string of <m>n</m> bits (0s and 1s) in which <m>k</m> of the bits are 1s.  For example, the 4-bit strings of weight 2 are\n"
"\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:779
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"\t\tDoes order matter for bit strings? Of course it does!  It is the only thing that matters.  All of the strings above contain exactly the same number of 0s and 1s; they are only distinguished by the order of the bits.  \n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:784
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tAnd yet, the number of <m>n</m>-bit strings of weight <m>k</m> is <m>\\binom{n}{k}</m> and NOT a permutation.  What is going on here?\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:788
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tNothing is broken; we are just not thinking about the right objects about which to consider the order.  When we <em>choose</em> <m>k</m> out of <m>n</m> things for a bit string, it is the <em>positions</em> we are choosing (to fill with 1s, say).  It does not matter in what order we choose those positions, just what set of positions we choose.  That is, the bit string 1001 is the result of choosing positions 1 and 4 to put 1s into.  If we chose positions 4 and then 1, we would get the same bit string.\t\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_counting-combperm.ptx:792
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\tTo summarize, the question of whether order matters can lead us astray when deciding between a combination and a permutation.  Instead, we should decide whether the outcomes we are counting are sets or sequences.  If we are counting sets, think combination.  If we are counting sequences, think permutation.\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

