#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-multisets.ptx:60
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We know how to solve lots of types of counting problems now.\n"
"      If each outcome in a set of outcomes we are counting can be represented by a sequence, we count it as a permutation (if the terms in the sequence don't repeat) or use the product principle (if they do).\n"
"      If we are counting outcomes for which we don't distinguish between different arrangements of the terms (if order doesn't matter) then we think of the outcome as a set and count it as a combination.\n"
"      However, sets never allow an element to be repeated; each element is either in a set or not.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_counting-multisets.ptx:67
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      So we have a glaring hole in our counting repertoire.\n"
"      What if we want to count outcomes that are collections of terms for which we do not distinguish between the order the terms appear in, but can contain terms more than once?  In other words, how can we complete the following table?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

