#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:8
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you have a huge box of animal crackers containing plenty of each of 10 different animals.\n"
"        For the counting questions below,\n"
"        carefully examine their similarities and differences, and then give an answer.\n"
"        The answers are all one of the following:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:24
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many animal parades containing 6 crackers can you line up?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:30
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many animal parades of 6 crackers can you line up so that the animals appear in alphabetical order?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:36
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many ways could you line up 6 different animals in alphabetical order?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:42
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many ways could you line up 6 different animals if they can come in any order?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:48
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many ways could you give 6 children one animal cracker each?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:54
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many ways could you give 6 children one animal cracker each so that no two kids get the same animal?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:60
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many ways could you give out 6 giraffes to 10 kids?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:66
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Write a question about giving animal crackers to kids that has the answer <m>{10\\choose 6}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      With all the different counting techniques we have mastered in this last chapter,\n"
"      it might be difficult to know when to apply which technique.\n"
"      Indeed, it is very easy to get mixed up and use the wrong counting method for a given problem.\n"
"      You get better with practice.\n"
"      As you practice, you start to notice some trends that can help you distinguish between types of counting problems.\n"
"      Here are some suggestions that you might find helpful when deciding how to tackle a counting problem and checking whether your solution is correct.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:85
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Remember that you are counting the number of items in some\n"
"            <em>list of outcomes</em>.\n"
"            Write down part of this list.\n"
"            Write down an element in the middle of the list "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:88
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" how are you deciding whether your element really is in the list?\n"
"            Could you get this element more than once using your proposed answer?\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:95
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            If generating an element on the list involves selecting something\n"
"            (for example,\n"
"            picking a letter or picking a position to put a letter, etc.),\n"
"            can the things you select be repeated?\n"
"            Remember, permutations and combinations select objects from a set\n"
"            <em>without</em> repeats.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:106
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Does order matter?\n"
"            Be careful here, and be sure you know what your answer really means.\n"
"            We usually say that order matters when you get different outcomes when the same objects are selected in different orders.\n"
"            Combinations and <q>sticks and stones</q>\n"
"            are used when order <em>does not</em> matter.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:119
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            There are four possibilities when it comes to order and repeats.\n"
"            If order matters and repeats are allowed,\n"
"            the answer will look like <m>n^k</m>.\n"
"            If order matters and repeats are not allowed, we have <m>P(n,k)</m>.\n"
"            If order doesn't matter and repeats are allowed, use sticks and stones.\n"
"            If order doesn't matter and repeats are not allowed, use <m>{n\\choose k}</m>.\n"
"            But be careful: this only applies when you are selecting things,\n"
"            and you should make sure you know exactly what you are selecting before determining which case you are in.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:132
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Think about how you would represent your counting problem in terms of sets or functions.\n"
"            We know how to count different sorts of sets and different types of functions.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:139
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            As we saw with combinatorial proofs,\n"
"            you can often solve a counting problem in more than one way.\n"
"            Do that, and compare your numerical answers.\n"
"            If they don't match, something is amiss.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:149
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      While we have covered many counting techniques,\n"
"      we have really only scratched the surface of the large subject of\n"
"      <em>enumerative combinatorics</em>.\n"
"      There are mathematicians doing original research in this area even as you read this.\n"
"      Counting can be really hard.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_counting-conc.ptx:157
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In the next chapter,\n"
"      we will approach counting questions from a very different direction,\n"
"      and in doing so,\n"
"      answer infinitely many counting questions at the same time.\n"
"      We will create <em>sequences</em>\n"
"      of answers to related questions.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

