#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:317
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A <term>ternary</term> string is a sequence of 0's, 1's, and 2's.  Just like a bit string, but with three symbols.  \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:321
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Let's call a ternary string <em>good</em> provided it never contains a 2 followed immediately by a 0.  Let <m>G_n</m> be the number of good strings of length <m>n</m>.  For example, <m>G_1 = 3</m>, and <m>G_2 = 8</m> (since of the 9 ternary strings of length 2, only one is not good).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:325
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Find, with justification, a recursive formula for <m>G_n</m>, and use it to compute <m>G_5</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider three cases: The last digit is a 0, a 1, or a 2.  Two of these should be easy to count, but strings ending in 0 cannot be proceeded by a 2, so they require a little more work.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:339
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider bit strings with length <m>l</m> and weight <m>k</m>\n"
"        (so strings of <m>l</m> 0's and 1's, including <m>k</m> 1's).\n"
"        We know how to count the number of these for a fixed <m>l</m> and <m>k</m>.\n"
"        Now, we will count the number of strings for which the <em>sum</em>\n"
"        of the length and the weight is fixed.\n"
"        For example,\n"
"        let's count all the bit strings for which <m>l+k = 11</m>.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:350
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Find examples of these strings of different lengths.\n"
"              What is the longest string possible?\n"
"              What is the shortest?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:358
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              How many strings are there of each of these lengths.\n"
"              Use this to count the total number of strings\n"
"              (with sum 11).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:366
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The other approach: Let <m>n = l+k</m> vary.\n"
"              How many strings have sum <m>n = 1</m>?\n"
"              How many have sum <m>n = 2</m>?\n"
"              And so on.\n"
"              Find and explain a recurrence relation for the sequence <m>(a_n)</m> that gives the number of strings with sum <m>n</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:376
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Describe what you have found above in terms of <xref po4a-id=0>Pascal's triangle</xref>.\n"
"              What pattern have you discovered?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-basics.ptx:391
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        When bees play chess,\n"
"        they use a hexagonal board like the one shown below.\n"
"        The queen bee can move one space at a time either directly to the right or angled up-right or down-right\n"
"        (but can never move leftwards).\n"
"        How many different paths can the queen take from the top left hexagon to the bottom right hexagon?\n"
"        Explain your answer, and how this relates to the previous question.\n"
"        (As an example,\n"
"        there are three paths to get to the second hexagon on the bottom row.)\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

