msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:35
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          For each of the patterns of dots below, draw the next pattern in the sequence.\n"
"          Describe the rate of growth of the number of dots in the patterns.\n"
"          Then guess a recursive definition and a closed formula for the number of dots in the <m>n</m>th pattern.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:54
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A sequence of three dot patterns, starting with a single dot (labeled n = 0), then the same dot with four new dots extending into an X (labeled n = 1) and finally the same pattern but with four new dots extending onto the limbs of the X (labeled n = 2).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:78
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A sequence of three dot patterns, labeled n = 0, n = 1, and n = 2, from left to right.\n"
"              The first pattern contains two dots, on above the other.\n"
"              The second pattern can be viewed as each of the dots from the first pattern splitting into 3, forming two triangles (so six dots total).\n"
"              The third patter again takes each dot from the second pattern and splits them into three, arranged as triangles (so 18 dots in total).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:102
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A sequence of four dot patters, labeled n = 1 through n = 4.\n"
"              The first pattern is a single dot.\n"
"              The second adds a row of two dots below the single dot (so three dots in a triangle).\n"
"              The third pattern adds a row of three dots, creating six dots arranged in a triangle.\n"
"              Finally we add four dots below the previous six, still forming a triangle, this time of 10 dots.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:114
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Our goal is to find closed formulas for sequences.\n"
"      Our primary strategy will be to first determine how the sequence is changing from term to term.\n"
"      This will lead to a recurrence relation for the sequence, and from that recurrence relation, we will find a closed formula.\n"
"      We start with two types of sequences that are particularly common and useful: arithmetic and geometric sequences.\n"
"      Along the way, we will explore some techniques for solving recurrence relations.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

