#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:317 source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:359
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:97
#, no-wrap
msgid "Arithmetic Sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:320
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Suppose you start a business selling prints of mathematical art.\n"
"      In week zero, you sell two prints.\n"
"      Each week after that, you sell four more prints than you did the previous week.\n"
"      How many prints will you sell in the <m>n</m>th week?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:327
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We can easily compute the first few terms of the sequence: <m>2, 6, 10, 14,\\ldots</m>.\n"
"      How do I know this is correct?  From the problem, we see that to get from one term to the next, we must add 4.\n"
"      It is clear then that the recurrence relation for the sequence is\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:332
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      The <em>rate of growth</em> for the sequence is the constant <m>4</m> since the <em>difference</em> between any two terms is 4 (note, we could write the recurrence relation as <m>a_n - a_{n-1} = 4</m>).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:337
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We call sequences with a <em>constant rate of change</em> <term>arithmetic sequences</term>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:341
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Now let's find a closed formula for our sequence.\n"
"      The first term is <m>a_0 = 2</m>.\n"
"      To get <m>a_1</m>, we add <m>4</m>.\n"
"      The next term requires us to add <m>4</m> again, which means we have added <m>4</m> to our initial term twice.\n"
"      Then we add <m>4</m> again, for a total of three times for <m>a_3</m>.\n"
"      In fact, to get <m>a_n</m>, we will have added <m>4</m> to <m>a_0</m> a total of <m>n</m> times.\n"
"      Thus, the closed formula for the sequence is\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:354
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This works for any arithmetic sequence.\n"
"      That is, any sequence with a constant difference will have a <em>linear</em> closed formula, where the <q>slope</q> of the linear function is the common difference.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:366
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        If the terms of a sequence differ by a constant, we say the sequence is <term>arithmetic</term>.\n"
"        If the initial term (<m>a_0</m>) of the sequence is <m>a</m> and the <term>common difference</term> is <m>d</m>, then we have,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:371
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Recursive definition: <m>a_n = a_{n-1} + d</m> with <m>a_0 = a</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:375
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Closed formula: <m>a_n = a + dn</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:380
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      As we did for our example above, for the recursive definition, we need to specify <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"      Then we need to express <m>a_n</m> in terms of <m>a_{n-1}</m>.\n"
"      If we call the first term <m>a</m>, then <m>a_0 = a</m>.\n"
"      For the recurrence relation, by the definition of an arithmetic sequence, the difference between successive terms is some constant, say <m>d</m>.\n"
"      So <m>a_n - a_{n-1} = d</m>, or in other words,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:391
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Let's now argue why the closed formula is correct.  One way we could do this is by using a technique sometimes called <term>telescoping</term> (a name which hopefully we become meaningful momentarily).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:395
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We write the recurrence relation in its <em>difference</em> form, <m>a_n - a_{n-1} = d</m> for all terms starting with <m>a_1</m> and going up to <m>a_n</m>.  This gives the following:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:403
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      Now we add all <m>n</m> equations together.  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:408
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      On the right-hand side, we have added <m>d</m> to itself <m>n</m> times, so the sum is <m>d\\cdot n</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:411
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      On the left-hand side, we get the sum:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:414
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      But look what happens when we regroup and cancel like terms:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:418
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      The sum <em>telescopes</em> down to be nice and compact for easy storage.  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:422
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Putting the two sides together gives us "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:422
#, no-wrap
msgid " which becomes "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:422
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" as we claimed.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:426
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The telescoping we did above is useful in other contexts (see <xref po4a-id=0>), but now that we have established a general form of the closed formula, we can apply it to any arithmetic sequence.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:432
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Find recursive definitions and closed formulas for the arithmetic sequences below.\n"
"          Assume the first term listed is <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:439
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>2, 5, 8, 11, 14, \\ldots</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:443
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>50, 43, 36, 29, \\ldots</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:451
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          First we should check that these sequences really are arithmetic by taking differences of successive terms.\n"
"          Doing so will reveal the common difference <m>d</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:458
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>5-2 = 3</m>, <m>8-5 = 3</m>, etc. To get from each term to the next, we add three, so <m>d = 3</m>. The recursive definition is therefore <m>a_n = a_{n-1} + 3</m> with <m>a_0 = 2</m>. The closed formula is <m>a_n = 2 + 3n</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:463
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Here the common difference is <m>-7</m>, since we add <m>-7</m> to 50 to get 43, and so on.\n"
"                Thus we have a recursive definition of <m>a_n = a_{n-1} - 7</m> with <m>a_0 = 50</m>.\n"
"                The closed formula is <m>a_n = 50 - 7n</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:476 source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:513
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:222
#, no-wrap
msgid "Geometric Sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:479
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      What about sequences like <m>3, 6, 12, 24, 48, \\ldots</m>? This is not arithmetic because the difference between terms is not constant.\n"
"      However, the <em>ratio</em> between successive terms is constant: <m>\\frac{6}{3} = \\frac{12}{6} = \\frac{24}{12} = \\cdots = 2</m>\n"
"      We call such sequences <term>geometric</term>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:485
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Recognizing that the sequence is geometric lets us easily write down a recursive definition.  <m>a_n = 2 a_{n-1}</m>, with <m>a_0 = 3</m>.  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:489
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A closed formula is also not difficult to reason out.  How do we get the term <m>a_3</m> for example?  We start with <m>3</m>, then multiply by 2 to get <m>a_1</m>, multiply by <m>2</m> again to get <m>a_2</m>, and multiply by <m>2</m> a third time to get <m>a_3</m>.  So we multiplied <m>3</m> by <m>2</m> a total of three times, or <m>a_3 = 3\\cdot 2^3</m>.  It looks like <m>a_n = 3\\cdot 2^n</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:493
#, no-wrap
msgid "      In general, the recursive definition for the geometric sequence with initial term <m>a</m> and common ratio <m>r</m> will be "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:493
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      To get the next term we multiply the previous term by <m>r</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:498
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      For the general closed formula, we could try something like telescoping again, although we would need to cancel fractions.  Instead, let's illustrate another technique for solving recurrence relations called <term>iteration</term>. The idea here is that we work our way up to <m>a_n</m> and notice the pattern. \n"
"      Write\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:507
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      We must multiply the first term <m>a</m> by <m>r</m> a number of times, <m>n</m> times to be precise.\n"
"      We get <m>a_n = a\\cdot r^{n}</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:520
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A sequence is called <term>geometric</term> if the ratio between successive terms is constant.\n"
"        Suppose the initial term <m>a_0</m> is <m>a</m> and the <term>common ratio</term> is <m>r</m>.\n"
"        Then we have,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:526
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Recursive definition: <m>a_n = ra_{n-1}</m> with <m>a_0 = a</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:530
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Closed formula: <m>a_n = a\\cdot r^{n}</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:537
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Find the recursive and closed formula for the geometric sequences below.\n"
"          Again, the first term listed is <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:541
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>3, 6, 12, 24, 48, \\ldots</m>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:545
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>27, 9, 3, 1, 1/3, \\ldots</m>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:553
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Start by checking that these sequences really are geometric by dividing each term by its previous term.\n"
"          If this ratio really is constant, we will have found <m>r</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:557
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>6/3 = 2</m>, <m>12/6 = 2</m>, <m>24/12 = 2</m>, etc. Yes, to get from any term to the next, we multiply by <m>r = 2</m>. So the recursive definition is <m>a_n = 2a_{n-1}</m> with <m>a_0 = 3</m>. The closed formula is <m>a_n = 3\\cdot 2^{n}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:562
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                The common ratio is <m>r = 1/3</m>.\n"
"                So the sequence has recursive definition <m>a_n = \\frac{1}{3}a_{n-1}</m> with <m>a_0 = 27</m> and closed formula <m>a_n = 27\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}^{n}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:572
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Geometric sequences are those which have a growth rate that is <em>proportional</em> to the sequence itself.\n"
"      Just like you might have seen in calculus, it is exactly the exponential functions that have this property.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:577
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In the examples and formulas above, we assumed that the <em>initial</em> term was <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"      If your sequence starts with <m>a_1</m>, you can easily find the term that would have been <m>a_0</m> and use that in the formula.\n"
"      For example, if we want a formula for the sequence <m>2, 5, 8,\\ldots</m> and insist that <m>2= a_1</m>, then we can find <m>a_0 = -1</m> (since the sequence is arithmetic with common difference 3, we have <m>a_0 + 3 = a_1</m>).\n"
"      Then the closed formula will be <m>a_n = -1 + 3n</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><remark><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:585
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        If you look at other sources, you might find that their closed formulas for arithmetic and geometric sequences differ from ours.\n"
"        Specifically, you might find the formulas <m>a_n = a +(n-1)d</m> (arithmetic) and <m>a_n = a\\cdot r^{n-1}</m> (geometric).\n"
"        Which is correct? Both! In our case, we take <m>a</m> to be <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"        If instead we had <m>a_1</m> as our initial term, we would get the (slightly more complicated) formulas you find elsewhere.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:594
#, no-wrap
msgid "Beyond Arithmetic and Geometric Sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:596
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Look at the sequence <m>(T_n)_{n\\ge 1}</m> which starts <m>1, 3, 6, 10, 15,\\ldots</m>.\n"
"      These are called the <term>triangular numbers</term>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:599
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      since they represent the number of dots in an equilateral triangle\n"
"      (think of how you arrange 10 bowling pins:\n"
"      a row of 4 plus a row of 3 plus a row of 2 and a row of 1).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:624
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A sequence of four dot patters, showing the first four triangular numbers: the patterns contain 1, 3, 6, and 10 dots, arranged in triangles with base 1, 2, 3, and 4, respectively.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:631
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Is this sequence arithmetic?\n"
"      No, since <m>3-1 = 2</m> and <m>6-3 = 3 \\ne 2</m>,\n"
"      so there is no common difference.\n"
"      Is the sequence geometric?\n"
"      No. <m>3/1 = 3</m> but <m>6/3 = 2</m>,\n"
"      so there is no common ratio.\n"
"      What to do?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:641
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Notice that the <em>differences</em> between terms <em>do</em> form an arithmetic sequence:\n"
"      <m>2, 3, 4, 5, 6,\\ldots</m>.  In other words, the rate of change of this sequence is arithmetic: <m>T_n - T_{n-1} = n</m>, which immediately gives us the recurrence relation <m>T_n = T_{n-1} + n</m>. \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:646
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Another way to think of this is that the <m>n</m>th term of the sequence <m>(T_n)</m> is the <em>sum</em>\n"
"      of the first <m>n</m> terms in the sequence <m>1,2,3,4,5,\\ldots</m>.\n"
"      Thus <m>(T_n)</m> is the\n"
"      <term>sequence of partial sums</term>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:651
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      of the sequence <m>1,2,3,\\ldots</m>\n"
"      (<em>partial</em> sums because we are not taking the sum of all infinitely many terms).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:656
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This should become clearer if we expand the recurrence relation to write the triangular numbers like this:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:664
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      We are really using <em>iteration</em> here.  We could also have seen this by using telescoping, taking <m>T_0 = 0</m>:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:672
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      Summing these equations, the right-hand side becomes <m>1+2+3+\\cdots + n</m>; the left-hand side cancels to leave just <m>T_n - T_0 = T_n</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:690
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This insight is usually attributed to Carl Friedrich Gauss,\n"
"      one of the greatest mathematicians of all time,\n"
"      who discovered it as a child when his unpleasant elementary teacher thought he would keep the class busy by requiring them to compute the lengthy sum.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:679
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If we know how to add up the terms of an arithmetic sequence,\n"
"      we can find a closed formula for a sequence whose differences are the terms of that arithmetic sequence.\n"
"      Consider how we could find the sum of the first 100 positive integers\n"
"      (that is, <m>T_{100}</m>).\n"
"      Instead of adding them in order,\n"
"      we regroup and add <m>1+100 = 101</m>.\n"
"      The next pair to combine is <m>2+99 = 101</m>.\n"
"      Then <m>3+98 = 101</m>.\n"
"      Keep going.\n"
"      This gives 50 pairs which each add up to <m>101</m>,\n"
"      so <m>T_{100} = 101\\cdot 50 = 5050</m>.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:696
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In general, using this same sort of regrouping,\n"
"      we find that <m>T_n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>.\n"
"      Incidentally,\n"
"      this is exactly the same as <m>{n+1 \\choose 2}</m>,\n"
"      which makes sense if you think of the triangular numbers as counting the number of handshakes that take place at a party with <m>n+1</m> people:\n"
"      the first person shakes <m>n</m> hands,\n"
"      the next shakes an additional <m>n-1</m> hands and so on.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:707
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The point of all of this is that some sequences,\n"
"      while not arithmetic or geometric,\n"
"      can be interpreted as the sequence of partial sums of arithmetic and geometric sequences.\n"
"      Luckily there are methods we can use to compute these sums quickly, which we will explore in the next two sections.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-growth.ptx:981 source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:935
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Match each formula on the left with the type of formula described on the right.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

