#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Arithmetic and Geometric Sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:10
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For the patterns of dots below,\n"
"        draw the next pattern in the sequence.\n"
"        Then give a recursive definition and a closed formula for the number of dots in the <m>n</m>th pattern.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:33
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A sequence of three dot patterns, starting with a single dot (labeled n = 0), then the same dot with four new dots extending into an X (labeled n = 1) and finally the same pattern but with four new dots extending onto the limbs of the X (labeled n = 2).\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:61
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A sequence of three dot patterns, labeled n = 0, n = 1, and n = 2, from left to right.  The first pattern contains two dots, on above the other.  The second pattern can be viewed as each of the dots from the first pattern splitting into 3, forming two triangles (so six dots total).  The third patter again takes each dot from the second pattern and splits them into three, arranged as triangles (so 18 dots all together).\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><investigation><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:86
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A sequence of four dot patters, labeled n = 1 through n = 4.  The first pattern is a single dot.  The second adds a row of two dots below the single dot (so three dots in a triangle).  The third pattern adds a row of three dots, creating six dots arranged in a triangle.  Finally we add four dots below the previous six, still forming a triangle, this time of 10 dots.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:93
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    We now turn to the question of finding closed formulas for particular types of sequences.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:103
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If the terms of a sequence differ by a constant,\n"
"      we say the sequence is <term>arithmetic</term>.\n"
"      If the initial term (<m>a_0</m>) of the sequence is <m>a</m> and the\n"
"      <term>common difference</term>\n"
"      is <m>d</m>, then we have,\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:111
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Recursive definition: <m>a_n = a_{n-1} + d</m> with <m>a_0 = a</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:115
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Closed formula: <m>a_n = a + dn</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:120
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    How do we know this?\n"
"    For the recursive definition,\n"
"    we need to specify <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"    Then we need to express <m>a_n</m> in terms of <m>a_{n-1}</m>.\n"
"    If we call the first term <m>a</m>, then <m>a_0 = a</m>.\n"
"    For the recurrence relation, by the definition of an arithmetic sequence,\n"
"    the difference between successive terms is some constant,\n"
"    say <m>d</m>.\n"
"    So <m>a_n - a_{n-1} = d</m>, or in other words,\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:135
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    To find a closed formula, first write out the sequence in general:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:146
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    We see that to find the <m>n</m>th term,\n"
"    we need to start with <m>a</m> and then add <m>d</m> a bunch of times.\n"
"    In fact, add it <m>n</m> times.\n"
"    Thus <m>a_n = a+dn</m>.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:155
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Find recursive definitions and closed formulas for the arithmetic sequences below.\n"
"        Assume the first term listed is <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:161
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>2, 5, 8, 11, 14, \\ldots</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:163
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>50, 43, 36, 29, \\ldots</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        First we should check that these sequences really are arithmetic by taking differences of successive terms.\n"
"        Doing so will reveal the common difference <m>d</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:176
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            <m>5-2 = 3</m>, <m>8-5 = 3</m>, etc.\n"
"            To get from each term to the next,\n"
"            we add three, so <m>d = 3</m>.\n"
"            The recursive definition is therefore <m>a_n = a_{n-1} + 3</m> with <m>a_0 = 2</m>.\n"
"            The closed formula is <m>a_n = 2 + 3n</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:185
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Here the common difference is <m>-7</m>,\n"
"              since we add <m>-7</m> to 50 to get 43, and so on.\n"
"              Thus we have a recursive definition of\n"
"              <m>a_n = a_{n-1} - 7</m> with <m>a_0 = 50</m>.\n"
"              The closed formula is <m>a_n = 50 - 7n</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:198
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    What about sequences like <m>2, 6, 18, 54, \\ldots</m>?\n"
"    This is not arithmetic because the difference between terms is not constant.\n"
"    However, the <em>ratio</em> between successive terms is constant.\n"
"    We call such sequences <term>geometric</term>.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:205
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    The recursive definition for the geometric sequence with initial term <m>a</m> and common ratio <m>r</m> is\n"
"    <m>a_n = a_{n-1}\\cdot r; a_0 = a</m>.\n"
"    To get the next term we multiply the previous term by <m>r</m>.\n"
"    We can find the closed formula like we did for the arithmetic progression.\n"
"    Write\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:216
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    We must multiply the first term <m>a</m> by <m>r</m> a number of times,\n"
"    <m>n</m> times to be precise.\n"
"    We get <m>a_n = a\\cdot r^{n}</m>.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:228
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A sequence is called <term>geometric</term>\n"
"      if the ratio between successive terms is constant.\n"
"      Suppose the initial term <m>a_0</m> is <m>a</m> and the\n"
"      <term>common ratio</term> is <m>r</m>.\n"
"      Then we have,\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:236
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Recursive definition: <m>a_n = ra_{n-1}</m> with <m>a_0 = a</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:240
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Closed formula: <m>a_n = a\\cdot r^{n}</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:247
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Find the recursive and closed formula for the geometric sequences below.\n"
"        Again, the first term listed is <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:251
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>3, 6, 12, 24, 48, \\ldots</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:253
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>27, 9, 3, 1, 1/3, \\ldots</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:259
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Start by checking that these sequences really are geometric by dividing each term by its previous term.\n"
"        If this ratio really is constant,\n"
"        we will have found <m>r</m>.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:265
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            <m>6/3 = 2</m>, <m>12/6 = 2</m>, <m>24/12 = 2</m>, etc.\n"
"            Yes, to get from any term to the next,\n"
"            we multiply by <m>r = 2</m>.\n"
"            So the recursive definition is <m>a_n = 2a_{n-1}</m> with <m>a_0 = 3</m>.\n"
"            The closed formula is <m>a_n = 3\\cdot 2^{n}</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:274
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The common ratio is <m>r = 1/3</m>.\n"
"              So the sequence has recursive definition <m>a_n = \\frac{1}{3}a_{n-1}</m> with\n"
"              <m>a_0 = 27</m> and closed formula <m>a_n = 27\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}^{n}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:285
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    In the examples and formulas above,\n"
"    we assumed that the <em>initial</em> term was <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"    If your sequence starts with <m>a_1</m>,\n"
"    you can easily find the term that would have been <m>a_0</m> and use that in the formula.\n"
"    For example, if we want a formula for the sequence\n"
"    <m>2, 5, 8,\\ldots</m> and insist that <m>2= a_1</m>,\n"
"    then we can find <m>a_0 = -1</m>\n"
"    (since the sequence is arithmetic with common difference 3, we have <m>a_0 + 3 = a_1</m>).\n"
"    Then the closed formula will be <m>a_n = -1 + 3n</m>.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><remark><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:298
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If you look at other textbooks or online,\n"
"      you might find that their closed formulas for arithmetic and geometric sequences differ from ours.\n"
"      Specifically, you might find the formulas\n"
"      <m>a_n = a +(n-1)d</m> (arithmetic) and <m>a_n = a\\cdot r^{n-1}</m>\n"
"      (geometric).\n"
"      Which is correct?\n"
"      Both!\n"
"      In our case, we take <m>a</m> to be <m>a_0</m>.\n"
"      If instead we had <m>a_1</m> as our initial term, we would get the\n"
"      (slightly more complicated)\n"
"      formulas you find elsewhere.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:314
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sums of Arithmetic and Geometric Sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:318
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Your neighborhood grocery store has a candy machine full of Skittles.\n"
"\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:323
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Suppose that the candy machine currently holds exactly 650 Skittles,\n"
"                and every time someone inserts a quarter,\n"
"                exactly 7 Skittles come out of the machine.\n"
"\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                      How many Skittles will be left in the machine after 20 quarters have been inserted?\n"
"                    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:336
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                      Will there ever be exactly zero Skittles left in the machine?\n"
"                      Explain.\n"
"                    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:346
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                What if the candy machine gives 7 Skittles to the first customer who put in a quarter, 10 to the second, 13 to the third, 16 to the fourth, etc.\n"
"                How many Skittles has the machine given out after 20 quarters are put into the machine?\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:353
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Now, what if the machine gives 4 Skittles to the first customer, 7 to the second, 12 to the third, 19 to the fourth, etc.\n"
"                How many Skittles has the machine given out after 20 quarters are put into the machine?\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:361
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Look at the sequence <m>(T_n)_{n\\ge 1}</m> which starts <m>1, 3, 6, 10, 15,\\ldots</m>.\n"
"        These are called the <term>triangular numbers</term>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:364
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        since they represent the number of dots in an equilateral triangle\n"
"        (think of how you arrange 10 bowling pins:\n"
"        a row of 4 plus a row of 3 plus a row of 2 and a row of 1).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:389
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A sequence of four dot patters, showing the first four triangular numbers: the patterns contain 1, 3, 6, and 10 dots, arranged in triangles with base 1, 2, 3, and 4, respectively.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:396
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Is this sequence arithmetic?\n"
"        No, since <m>3-1 = 2</m> and <m>6-3 = 3 \\ne 2</m>,\n"
"        so there is no common difference.\n"
"        Is the sequence geometric?\n"
"        No. <m>3/1 = 3</m> but <m>6/3 = 2</m>,\n"
"        so there is no common ratio.\n"
"        What to do?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:406
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Notice that the <em>differences</em> between terms <em>do</em> form an arithmetic sequence:\n"
"        <m>2, 3, 4, 5, 6,\\ldots</m>.\n"
"        This means that the <m>n</m>th term of the sequence <m>(T_n)</m> is the <em>sum</em>\n"
"        of the first <m>n</m> terms in the sequence <m>1,2,3,4,5,\\ldots</m>.\n"
"        We say that <m>(T_n)</m> is the\n"
"        <term>sequence of partial sums</term>\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:413
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        of the sequence <m>1,2,3,\\ldots</m>\n"
"        (<em>partial</em> sums because we are not taking the sum of all infinitely many terms).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:418
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This should become clearer if we write the triangular numbers like this:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p><fn>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:441
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This insight is usually attributed to Carl Friedrich Gauss,\n"
"        one of the greatest mathematicians of all time,\n"
"        who discovered it as a child when his unpleasant elementary teacher thought he would keep the class busy by requiring them to compute the lengthy sum.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:430
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        If we know how to add up the terms of an arithmetic sequence,\n"
"        we could find a closed formula for a sequence whose differences are the terms of that arithmetic sequence.\n"
"        Consider how we could find the sum of the first 100 positive integers\n"
"        (that is, <m>T_{100}</m>).\n"
"        Instead of adding them in order,\n"
"        we regroup and add <m>1+100 = 101</m>.\n"
"        The next pair to combine is <m>2+99 = 101</m>.\n"
"        Then <m>3+98 = 101</m>.\n"
"        Keep going.\n"
"        This gives 50 pairs which each add up to <m>101</m>,\n"
"        so <m>T_{100} = 101\\cdot 50 = 5050</m>.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:447
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        In general, using this same sort of regrouping,\n"
"        we find that <m>T_n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>.\n"
"        Incidentally,\n"
"        this is exactly the same as <m>{n+1 \\choose 2}</m>,\n"
"        which makes sense if you think of the triangular numbers as counting the number of handshakes that take place at a party with <m>n+1</m> people:\n"
"        the first person shakes <m>n</m> hands,\n"
"        the next shakes an additional <m>n-1</m> hands and so on.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:457
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The point of all of this is that some sequences,\n"
"        while not arithmetic or geometric,\n"
"        can be interpreted as the sequence of partial sums of arithmetic and geometric sequences.\n"
"        Luckily there are methods we can use to compute these sums quickly.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_seq-arithgeom.ptx:465 source/sec_seq-polynomial.ptx:206
#, no-wrap
msgid "Summing Arithmetic Sequences: Reverse and Add"
msgstr ""

