#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:53
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In <xref po4a-id=0> we saw that if a sequence has some sequence of differences that is constant, then the sequence has a polynomial closed formula.  Are there sequences that never have constant differences?  And what would their closed formulas look like?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Consider the Fibonacci sequence:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:60
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      If we look at the first differences, we get this sequence:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:64
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"      which is the Fibonacci sequence itself.  This is not surprising, since the Fibonacci sequence is defined by the recurrence relation <m>F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2}</m>.  That is saying precisely that to get the next turn of the sequence, we take the current term and add... a term in the sequence!  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Of course, if we take another difference, we will get the same sequence back, and again and again, so no <m>n</m>th differences will be constant.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Another sequence that has this behavior is the powers of 2:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:77
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      which has differences\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      We can also see this from the recurrence relation, since "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:81
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".  The rate of growth for this, and in fact any geometric sequence, is the sequence itself.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:85
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      If you have studied calculus, you may recall that the functions that have themselves (or close) as their rate of change (derivative, in the calculus context) are exactly the exponential functions.  Here too, geometric sequences, which have exponential closed formulas, have themselves as their rate of change.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:89
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In this section, we will explore sequences that are changing at a rate proportional to the sequence itself, and see how these all have an exponential closed formula, or some variation of that.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:115
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Consider the recurrence relation\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"              Since the <m>n</m>th term is given as a combination of the two previous terms, we will need two initial terms to determine the sequence.  Different initial terms will give different sequences.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:126
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              What sequence do you get if the initial conditions are <m>a_0 = 1</m>, <m>a_1 = 2</m>?  Give the first five terms (including 1 and 2).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
