#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:194
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Summing Geometric Sequences: Multiply, Shift, and\n"
"      Subtract"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:199
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose a candy machine dispenses candy in a geometric sequence by first giving 1 candy, then 2 candies, then 4, then 8, and so on.  How many candies will you have received in total after 10 turns of the machine?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:203
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We can create the sequence of partials sum as <m>1, 1+2, 1+2+4, 1+2+4+8, \\ldots</m> that gives\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:207
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This is not a geometric sequence, but is almost.  In fact, if we add 1 to each term, we get what sure looks like the geometric sequence <m>2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64,\\ldots</m>, so we might guess that the closed formula for the sequence of sums is <m>2^{n+1} - 1</m>.  If this is correct, then the answer to the candy question would be <m>2^{11} - 1 = 2047</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:211
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        More intriguing though is the observation that the sequence of partial sums of a geometric sequence is again geometric-ish.  Let's consider how to find the sum of a geometric sequence in general. \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:215
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We cannot just reverse and add as we did for the sum of an arithmetic sequence.\n"
"        Do you see why?\n"
"        The reason we got the same term added to itself many times is because there was a constant difference.\n"
"        So as we added that difference in one direction,\n"
"        we subtracted the difference going the other way, leaving a constant total.\n"
"        For geometric sums; we have a different technique.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:231
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Multiply each term by 2, the common ratio.\n"
"            We get <m>2S = 6 + 12 + 24 + \\cdots + 24576</m>.\n"
"            Now subtract: <m>2S - S = -3 + 24576 = 24573</m>.\n"
"            Since <m>2S - S = S</m>, we have our answer.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:240
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        To better see what happened in the above example, we can write it this way:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:271
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Then divide both sides by <m>-1</m> and we have the same result for <m>S</m>.\n"
"        The idea is, by multiplying the sum by the common ratio,\n"
"        each term becomes the next term.\n"
"        We shift over the sum to get the subtraction to mostly cancel out,\n"
"        leaving just the first term and the new last term.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:353
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            So <m>N = \\frac{46}{99}</m>.\n"
"            What have we done?\n"
"            We viewed the repeating decimal\n"
"            <m>0.464646\\ldots</m> as a sum of the geometric sequence\n"
"            <m>0.46, 0.0046, 0.000046, \\ldots</m> The common ratio is <m>0.01</m>.\n"
"            The only real difference is that we are now computing an\n"
"            <em>infinite</em> geometric sum,\n"
"            we do not have the extra <q>last</q> term to consider.\n"
"            Really, this is the result of taking a limit as we would in calculus when we compute\n"
"            <em>infinite</em> geometric sums.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:368
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        To summarize, we now can find a closed formula for a sequence <m>a_n</m> that has a rate of growth that is an exponential function: <m>a_n - a_{n-1} = b_n</m>, where <m>b_n</m> is a geometric sequence (i.e., an exponential function).  What sort of closed formula do we get here?  It's <em>another</em> exponential function!  \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:374
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Characteristic Root Technique"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:378
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Suppose we want to solve a recurrence relation expressed as a combination of the two previous terms,\n"
"      such as <m>a_n = a_{n-1} + 6a_{n-2}</m>.\n"
"      In other words,\n"
"      we want to find a function of <m>n</m> which satisfies <m>a_n - a_{n-1} - 6a_{n-2} = 0</m>.\n"
"      Think about how we build up this sequence iteratively.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:388
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Let's stop there and agree this is getting very complicated.  However, we do notice that in each step, we would, among other things,\n"
"      multiply a previous iteration by 6.\n"
"      So our closed formula would include <m>6</m> multiplied some number of times.\n"
"      Thus it is reasonable to guess the solution will contain parts that look geometric.\n"
"      Perhaps the solution will take the form <m>r^n</m> for some constant <m>r</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:396
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The nice thing is,\n"
"      we know how to check whether a formula is actually a solution to a recurrence relation:\n"
"      plug it in.\n"
"      What happens if we plug in <m>r^n</m> into the recursion above?\n"
"      We get\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:407
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Now solve for <m>r</m>:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:410
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"      so by factoring, <m>r = -2</m> or <m>r = 3</m>\n"
"      (or <m>r = 0</m>, although this does not help us).\n"
"      This tells us that <m>a_n = (-2)^n</m> is a solution to the recurrence relation,\n"
"      as is <m>a_n = 3^n</m>.\n"
"      Which one is correct?\n"
"      They both are, unless we specify initial conditions.\n"
"      Notice we could also have <m>a_n = (-2)^n + 3^n</m>.\n"
"      Or <m>a_n = 7(-2)^n + 4\\cdot 3^n</m>.\n"
"      In fact, for any <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>,\n"
"      <m>a_n = a(-2)^n + b 3^n</m> is a solution\n"
"      (try plugging this into the recurrence relation).\n"
"      To find the values of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>,\n"
"      use the initial conditions.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:427
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This points us in the direction of a more general technique for solving recurrence relations.\n"
"      Notice we will always be able to factor out the <m>r^{n-2}</m> as we did above.\n"
"      So we really only care about the other part.\n"
"      We call this other part the\n"
"      <term>characteristic equation</term>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:433
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      for the recurrence relation.\n"
"      We are interested in finding the roots of the characteristic equation,\n"
"      which are called (surprise) the\n"
"      <term>characteristic roots</term>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:440
#, no-wrap
msgid "Characteristic Roots"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:443
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Given a recurrence relation <m>a_n + \\alpha a_{n-1} + \\beta a_{n-2} = 0</m>, the\n"
"        <term>characteristic polynomial</term> is\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:449
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        giving the\n"
"        <term>characteristic equation</term>:\n"
"\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:459
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        If <m>r_1</m> and <m>r_2</m> are two distinct roots of the characteristic polynomial (i.e., solutions to the characteristic equation),\n"
"        then the solution to the recurrence relation is\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:463
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"        where <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are constants determined by the initial conditions.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:471
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Solve the recurrence relation\n"
"          <m>a_n = 7a_{n-1} - 10 a_{n-2}</m> with <m>a_0 = 2</m> and <m>a_1 = 3</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:477
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Rewrite the recurrence relation <m>a_n - 7a_{n-1} + 10a_{n-2} = 0</m>.\n"
"          Now form the characteristic equation:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:482
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          and solve for <m>x</m>:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:485
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"          so <m>x = 2</m> and <m>x = 5</m> are the characteristic roots.\n"
"          We therefore know that the solution to the recurrence relation will have the form\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:494
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          To find <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>,\n"
"          plug in <m>n =0</m> and <m>n = 1</m> to get a system of two equations with two unknowns:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:503
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Solving this system gives <m>a = \\frac{7}{3}</m> and\n"
"          <m>b = -\\frac{1}{3}</m>, so the solution to the recurrence relation is\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:513
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Perhaps the most famous recurrence relation is <m>F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2}</m>,\n"
"      which together with the initial conditions <m>F_0 = 0</m> and <m>F_1= 1</m> defines the Fibonacci sequence.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:516
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      But notice that this is precisely the type of recurrence relation on which we can use the characteristic root technique.\n"
"      When we do,\n"
"      the only thing that changes is that the characteristic equation does not factor,\n"
"      so we must use the quadratic formula to find the characteristic roots.\n"
"      In fact, doing so gives the third most famous irrational number,\n"
"      <m>\\varphi</m>, the <term>golden ratio</term>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:526
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Before leaving the characteristic root technique,\n"
"      we should think about what might happen when solving the characteristic equation.\n"
"      We have an example above in which the characteristic polynomial has two distinct roots.\n"
"      These roots can be integers, or perhaps irrational numbers\n"
"      (requiring the quadratic formula to find them).\n"
"      In these cases,\n"
"      we know what the solution to the recurrence relation looks like.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:536
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      However, it is possible for the characteristic polynomial to have only one root.\n"
"      This can happen if the characteristic polynomial factors as <m>(x - r)^2</m>.\n"
"      It is still the case that <m>r^n</m> would be a solution to the recurrence relation,\n"
"      but we won't be able to find solutions for all initial conditions using the general form <m>a_n = ar_1^n + br_2^n</m>,\n"
"      since we can't distinguish between <m>r_1^n</m> and <m>r_2^n</m>.\n"
"      We are in luck though:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:545
#, no-wrap
msgid "Characteristic Root Technique for Repeated Roots"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:549
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose the recurrence relation\n"
"        <m>a_n = \\alpha a_{n-1} + \\beta a_{n-2}</m> has a characteristic polynomial with only one root <m>r</m>.\n"
"        Then the solution to the recurrence relation is\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:555
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        where <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are constants determined by the initial conditions.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:560
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Notice the extra <m>n</m> in <m>bnr^n</m>.\n"
"      This allows us to solve for the constants <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> from the initial conditions.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:567
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Solve the recurrence relation\n"
"          <m>a_n = 6a_{n-1} - 9a_{n-2}</m> with initial conditions <m>a_0 = 1</m> and <m>a_1 = 4</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:573
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The characteristic polynomial is <m>x^2 - 6x + 9</m>.\n"
"          We solve the characteristic equation\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:578
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          by factoring:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:582
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          so <m>x =3</m> is the only characteristic root.\n"
"          Therefore we know that the solution to the recurrence relation has the form\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:587
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          for some constants <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.\n"
"          Now use the initial conditions:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:592
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"          Since <m>a = 1</m>, we find that <m>b = \\frac{1}{3}</m>.\n"
"          Therefore the solution to the recurrence relation is\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:603
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Although we will not consider examples more complicated than these,\n"
"      this characteristic root technique can be applied to much more complicated recurrence relations.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:606
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      For example,\n"
"      <m>a_n = 2a_{n-1} + a_{n-2} - 3a_{n-3}</m> has characteristic polynomial <m>x^3 - 2 x^2 - x + 3</m>.\n"
"      Assuming we see how to factor such a degree 3\n"
"      (or more)\n"
"      polynomial, we can easily find the characteristic roots and as such solve the recurrence relation\n"
"      (the solution would look like\n"
"      <m>a_n = ar_1^n + br_2^n + cr_3^n</m> if there were 3 distinct roots).  It is also possible that the characteristic roots are complex numbers.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:618
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      However, the characteristic root technique is only useful for solving recurrence relations in a particular form: <m>a_n</m> is given as a linear combination of some number of previous terms.  These recurrence relations are called <term>linear homogeneous recurrence relations with constant coefficients</term>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:620
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The <q>homogeneous</q> refers to the fact that there is no additional term in the recurrence relation other than a multiple of <m>a_j</m> terms.  For example, <m>a_n = 2a_{n-1} + 1</m> is <em>non-homogeneous</em> because of the additional constant 1.  There are general methods of solving such things, but we will not consider them here, other than through the use of telescoping or iteration described above.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:629
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Which of the following recurrence relations would be good candidates to try the characteristic root technique on?  Select all that apply\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:636
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>a_n = 3a_{n-1} + a_{n-2}</m>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:641
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Correct: <m>a_n</m> is written in terms of only multiple of previous terms.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:648
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>a_n = a_{n-1}+2a_{n-2} + 3a_{n-3}</m>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:653
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              This works too, although the characteristic polynomial will have degree 3, so finding characteristic roots will be difficult.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:660
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>a_{n} = \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2^n + \\frac{2}{3}(-1)^n</m>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:665
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              This looks like it is the closed formula that would result from a characteristic root technique application, but isn't a recursive formula.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:672
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>a_n = a_{n-1} + 3a_{n-2} + 5</m>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:677
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The addition of the constant makes it so we cannot use the characteristic root technique directly.  While there are methods for dealing with this, we have not considered them in this section.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-exponential.ptx:684
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>x^2 -3x - 1 = 0</m>\n"
"            "
