#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:146
#, no-wrap
msgid "Recursive Reasoning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:157
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We have seen that describing a sequence recursively can often be easier than describing the sequence with a closed formula.  We will now see how using similar recursive reasoning can help us prove statements using a proof technique called <term>mathematical induction</term>.  This style of proof is especially useful when the different instances of the statement (for different values of <m>n</m>, say) are related recursively.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:161
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      For example, suppose we wanted to prove a fact about all the terms in a sequence for which we have a recursive definition.  Consider the sequence <m>(a_n)_{n\\ge 0}</m> defined recursively by <m>a_n = 3a_{n-1} - 2</m> with <m>a_0 = 5</m>.  Could we prove that every term in this sequence is odd?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:165
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Let's start by writing out the first few terms of the sequence:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:168
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      So far, all these numbers look odd.  Will the next number be odd?  Of course, we could just compute it using the recurrence relation.  We would take <m>3\\cdot 109 - 2</m>.  We don't actually care <em>which</em> odd number this is, just that it is, in fact, odd.  We know it will be odd because the product of two odd numbers is odd, and subtracting 2 from an odd number results in an odd number.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:173
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Great, so <m>a_4</m> is odd.  Will <m>a_5</m> be odd too?  Yes, use the same argument as above: <m>a_5 = 3  a_4 - 2</m>.  We just convinced ourselves that <m>a_4</m> is odd (without finding its actual value), so <m>3 a_4</m> is odd, and 2 less than it will be odd too.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      What about <m>a_6</m>?  Do the same thing.  In fact, why are we using any particular number as the index?  If it is the same argument each time, we should be able to just give this argument once and say it always works.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:181
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Suppose we have found that <m>a_k</m> is odd (where <m>k</m> is some arbitrary natural number).  From this, we can find that <m>a_{k+1}</m> is odd, since <m>a_{k+1} = 3 a_k - 2</m>, and <m>3</m> times the odd number <m>a_{k}</m> will be odd, and subtracting 2 will result in an odd number.  Yay.  Let's put this all together as a proof.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:186
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We claim that for any <m>n \\ge 0</m>, the number <m>a_n</m> is odd, where <m>a_n = 3a_{n-1} - 2</m> and <m>a_0 = 5</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:190
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        When <m>n = 0</m>, the claim is true, since <m>a_0 = 5</m> is an odd number.  \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:194
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Further, we can prove that every larger <m>n</m> has <m>a_n</m> odd because as long as <m>a_k</m> is odd, so is <m>a_{k+1}</m> (since <m>a_{k+1} = 3a_{k} - 2</m>, and 3 times an odd number minus 2 is always odd).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:198
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Therefore <m>a_n</m> is odd for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:203
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Soon we will give a more rigid structure for proofs by induction, but the basic idea is exactly what we have above.  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:398
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formalizing Proofs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:400
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Induction can prove many statements that hold for all natural numbers, not just statements about sequences.  \n"
"      In particular,\n"
"      induction should be used when there is some way to go from one case to the next "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:402
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" when you can see how to always <q>do one more.</q>  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:406
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Thinking about how we write statements in logical symbols, we will use induction to prove statements of the form\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:409
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"      where the domain of discourse (the values of <m>n</m> we quantify over) has some least element. Say that domain of discourse is the natural numbers.  We are then proving this <em>sequence</em> of statements:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:416
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The way we do this with induction is to prove a <term>base case</term>, that <m>P(0)</m> is true (or <m>P(a)</m> where <m>a</m> is the least element of our domain of discourse).  Next, we prove the <term>inductive case</term>, that <m>P(k) \\imp P(k+1)</m> for all <m>k \\ge 0</m> (or <m>k \\ge a</m>).  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:420
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Together, these are enough to prove <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n</m>.  How do we know?  That is, why is this style of proof valid?  Well, let's convince ourselves that <m>P(3)</m> is true.  We know <m>P(0)</m> is true.  And because we know that <m>P(0) \\imp P(1)</m>, we then also know that <m>P(1)</m> is true.  Because <m>P(1) \\imp P(2)</m>, we then get that <m>P(2)</m> is true.  Finally, because <m>P(2) \\imp P(3)</m>, we have that <m>P(3)</m>.  There is nothing special about 3 here.  We could have gone up as far as we like, "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><alert>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:420
#, no-wrap
msgid "to any <m>n</m> value!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:424
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Think of a row of dominoes set up standing on their edges.\n"
"      We want to argue that in a minute,\n"
"      all the dominoes will have fallen.\n"
"      For this to happen, you will need to push the first domino.\n"
"      That is the base case.\n"
"      It will also have to be that the dominoes are close enough together that when any particular domino falls,\n"
"      it will cause the next domino to fall.\n"
"      That is the inductive case.\n"
"      If both of these conditions are met "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:433
#, no-wrap
msgid "      you push the first domino over, and each domino will cause the next to fall "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:434
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      then all the dominoes will fall.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:439
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Induction is powerful!\n"
"      Think how much easier it is to knock over dominoes when you don't have to push over each domino yourself.\n"
"      You just start the chain reaction\n"
"      and then rely on the relative nearness of the dominoes to take care of the rest.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:446
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      When writing a proof by induction, we will follow a standard style.  Writing in this style allows us to keep our ideas organized and might even help us formulate the proof.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:450
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Here is the general structure of a proof by mathematical induction:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:454
#, no-wrap
msgid "Induction Proof Structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><q>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:459
#, no-wrap
msgid "        Start by saying what the statement is that you want to prove: <q>Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:459
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".</q> To prove that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>, you must prove two facts:\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:466
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Base case: Prove that <m>P(0)</m> is true.\n"
"              You do this directly.\n"
"              This is often easy.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:476
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Inductive case: Prove that\n"
"              <m>P(k) \\imp P(k+1)</m> for all <m>k \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"              That is, prove that for any <m>k \\ge 0</m> if <m>P(k)</m> is true,\n"
"              then <m>P(k+1)</m> is true as well.\n"
"              This is the proof of an if\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:481
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" then\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:482
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" statement,\n"
"              so you can assume <m>P(k)</m> is true (<m>P(k)</m> is called the\n"
"              <em>inductive hypothesis</em>).\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:486
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            You must then explain why <m>P(k+1)</m> is also true, given that assumption.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:493
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Assuming you are successful on both parts above,\n"
"        you can conclude,\n"
"        <q>Therefore by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"        the statement <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.</q>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:501
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Sometimes the statement <m>P(n)</m> will only be true for values of <m>n \\ge 4</m>,\n"
"      for example, or some other value.\n"
"      In such cases, replace all the 0's above with 4's\n"
"      (or the other value).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:509
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Before attempting to prove a statement by mathematical induction, first think about <em>why</em> the statement is true using inductive reasoning.\n"
"      Explain why induction is the right thing to do,\n"
"      and roughly why the inductive case will work.\n"
"      Then, sit down and write out a careful,\n"
"      formal proof using the structure above.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:520
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Here are some examples of proof by mathematical induction.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:526
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Prove for each natural number <m>n \\ge 1</m> that <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:531
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          First, let's think inductively about this equation.\n"
"          In fact, we know this is true for other reasons\n"
"          (reverse and add comes to mind).\n"
"          But why might induction be applicable?\n"
"          The left-hand side adds up the numbers from 1 to <m>n</m>.\n"
"          If we know how to do that,\n"
"          adding just one more term (<m>n+1</m>) would not be that hard.\n"
"          For example, if <m>n = 100</m>,\n"
"          suppose we know that the sum of the first 100 numbers is <m>5050</m>\n"
"          (so <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 100 = 5050</m>, which is true).\n"
"          Now to find the sum of the first 101 numbers,\n"
"          it makes more sense to just add 101 to 5050,\n"
"          instead of computing the entire sum again.\n"
"          We would have <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 100 + 101 = 5050 + 101 = 5151</m>.\n"
"          In fact, it would always be easy to add just one more term.\n"
"          This is why we should use induction.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:550 source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:367
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Now the formal proof:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:555
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>.\n"
"            We will show that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all natural numbers <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:560
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Base case: <m>P(1)</m> is the statement\n"
"            <m>1 = \\frac{1(1+1)}{2}</m> which is clearly true.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:565
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Inductive case: Let <m>k \\ge 1</m> be a natural number.\n"
"            Assume (for induction) that <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"            That means <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + k = \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}</m>.\n"
"            We will prove that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true as well.\n"
"            That is, we must prove that <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + k + (k+1) = \\frac{(k+1)(k+2)}{2}</m>.\n"
"            To prove this equation,\n"
"            start by adding <m>k+1</m> to both sides of the inductive hypothesis:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:578
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Now, simplifying the right side we get:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:587
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Thus <m>P(k+1)</m> is true,\n"
"            so by the principle of mathematical induction, <m>P(n)</m> is true for all natural numbers <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:596
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Note that in the part of the proof where we proved <m>P(k+1)</m> from <m>P(k)</m>,\n"
"      we used the equation <m>P(k)</m>.\n"
"      This was the inductive hypothesis.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:600
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Seeing how to use the inductive hypotheses is usually straightforward when proving a fact about a sum like this.\n"
"      In other proofs, it can be less obvious where it fits in.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:607
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Prove that for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>,\n"
"          <m>6^n - 1</m> is a multiple of 5.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:613
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Again, start by understanding the dynamics of the problem.\n"
"          What does increasing <m>n</m> do?\n"
"          Let's try with a few examples.\n"
"          If <m>n = 1</m>, then yes, <m>6^1 - 1 = 5</m> is a multiple of 5.\n"
"          What does incrementing <m>n</m> to 2 look like?\n"
"          We get <m>6^2 - 1 = 35</m>, which again is a multiple of 5.\n"
"          Next, <m>n = 3</m>: But instead of just finding <m>6^3 - 1</m>,\n"
"          what did the increase in <m>n</m> do?\n"
"          We will still subtract 1, but now we are multiplying by another 6 first.\n"
"          Viewed another way,\n"
"          we are multiplying a number that is one more than a multiple of 5 by 6\n"
"          (because <m>6^2 - 1</m> is a multiple of 5, so <m>6^2</m> is one more than a multiple of 5).\n"
"          What do numbers that are one more than a multiple of 5 look like?\n"
"          They must have last digit 1 or 6.\n"
"          What happens when you multiply such a number by 6?\n"
"          It depends on the number, but in any case,\n"
"          the last digit of the new number must be a 6.\n"
"          And then if you subtract 1, you get last digit 5, so a multiple of 5.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:634
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The point is, every time we multiply by just one more six,\n"
"          we still get a number with last digit 6, so subtracting 1 gives us a multiple of 5.\n"
"          Now the formal proof:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:641
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement,\n"
"            <q><m>6^n - 1</m> is a multiple of 5.</q>\n"
"            We will prove that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:647
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Base case: <m>P(0)</m> is true:\n"
"            <m>6^0 -1 = 0</m>, which is a multiple of 5.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:652
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Inductive case: Let <m>k</m> be an arbitrary natural number.\n"
"            Assume, for induction, that <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"            That is, <m>6^k - 1</m> is a multiple of <m>5</m>.\n"
"            Then <m>6^k - 1 = 5j</m> for some integer <m>j</m>.\n"
"            This means that <m>6^k = 5j + 1</m>.\n"
"            Multiply both sides by <m>6</m>:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:664
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            But we want to know about <m>6^{k+1} - 1</m>,\n"
"            so subtract 1 from both sides:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:672
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Of course <m>30j+5 = 5(6j+1)</m>, so is a multiple of 5.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:676
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Therefore <m>6^{k+1} - 1</m> is a multiple of 5, or in other words,\n"
"            <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"            Thus, by the principle of mathematical induction <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:686
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We had to be a little bit clever (i.e., use some algebra) to locate the <m>6^k - 1</m> inside of\n"
"      <m>6^{k+1} - 1</m> before we could apply the inductive hypothesis.\n"
"      This is what can make inductive proofs challenging.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:692
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In the two examples above, we started with <m>n = 1</m> or <m>n = 0</m>.\n"
"      We can start later if we need to.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:699
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Prove that <m>n^2 \\lt 2^n</m> for all integers <m>n \\ge 5</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:704
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          First, the idea of the argument.\n"
"          What happens when we increase <m>n</m> by 1?\n"
"          On the left-hand side,\n"
"          we increase the base of the square and go to the next square number.\n"
"          On the right-hand side, we increase the power of 2.\n"
"          This means we double the number.\n"
"          So the question is,\n"
"          how does doubling a number relate to increasing to the next square?\n"
"          Think about what the difference of two consecutive squares looks like.\n"
"          We have <m>(n+1)^2 - n^2</m>.\n"
"          This factors:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:721
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          But doubling the right-hand side increases it by <m>2^n</m>,\n"
"          since <m>2^{n+1} = 2^n + 2^n</m>.\n"
"          When <m>n</m> is large enough, <m>2^n &gt; 2n + 1</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:727
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          What we are saying here is that each time <m>n</m> increases,\n"
"          the left-hand side grows by less than the right-hand side.\n"
"          So if the left-hand side starts smaller\n"
"          (as it does when <m>n = 5</m>),\n"
"          it will never catch up.\n"
"          Now the formal proof:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:737
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <m>n^2 \\lt 2^n</m>.\n"
"            We will prove <m>P(n)</m> is true for all integers <m>n \\ge 5</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:742
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Base case: <m>P(5)</m> is the statement <m>5^2 \\lt 2^5</m>.\n"
"            Since <m>5^2 = 25</m> and <m>2^5 = 32</m>,\n"
"            we see that <m>P(5)</m> is indeed true.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:748
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Inductive case: Let <m>k \\ge 5</m> be an arbitrary integer.\n"
"            Assume, for induction, that <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"            That is, assume <m>k^2 \\lt  2^k</m>.\n"
"            We will prove that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true,\n"
"            i.e., <m>(k+1)^2 \\lt  2^{k+1}</m>.\n"
"            To prove such an inequality,\n"
"            start with the left-hand side and work towards the right-hand side:\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:764
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Following the equalities and inequalities through,\n"
"            we get <m>(k+1)^2 \\lt 2^{k+1}</m>,\n"
"            in other words, <m>P(k+1)</m>.\n"
"            Therefore by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"            <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 5</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:776
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The previous example might remind you of the\n"
"      <em>racetrack principle</em> from calculus,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:779
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      which says that if <m>f(a) \\lt g(a)</m>,\n"
"      and <m>f'(x) \\lt g'(x)</m> for\n"
"      <m>x &gt;\n"
"      a</m>, then <m>f(x) \\lt g(x)</m> for <m>x &gt; a</m>.\n"
"      Same idea: the larger function is increasing more than the smaller function,\n"
"      so the larger function will stay larger.\n"
"      In discrete math, we don't have derivatives,\n"
"      so we look at differences.\n"
"      Thus induction is the way to go.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><title>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:791
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Warning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:794
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        With great power, comes great responsibility.\n"
"        Induction isn't magic.\n"
"        It seems very powerful to be able to assume <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"        After all, we are trying to prove <m>P(n)</m> is true, and the only difference is in the variable:\n"
"        <m>k</m> vs. <m>n</m>.\n"
"        Are we assuming that what we want to prove is true?\n"
"        Not really.\n"
"        We assume <m>P(k)</m> is true only for the sake of proving that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:805
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Still you might start to believe that you can prove anything with induction.\n"
"        Consider this incorrect <q>proof</q> that every Canadian\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:808
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        has the same eye color: Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement that any <m>n</m> Canadians have the same eye color. <m>P(1)</m> is true,\n"
"        since everyone has the same eye color as themselves.\n"
"        Now assume <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"        That is, assume that in any group of <m>k</m> Canadians,\n"
"        everyone has the same eye color.\n"
"        Now consider an arbitrary group of <m>k+1</m> Canadians.\n"
"        The first <m>k</m> of these must all have the same eye color,\n"
"        since <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"        Also, the last <m>k</m> of these must have the same eye color,\n"
"        since <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"        So in fact, everyone in the group must have the same eye color.\n"
"        Thus <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"        So by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"        <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:825
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Clearly something went wrong.\n"
"        The problem is that the proof that <m>P(k)</m> implies <m>P(k+1)</m> assumes that <m>k \\ge 2</m>.\n"
"        We have only shown <m>P(1)</m> is true.\n"
"        In fact, <m>P(2)</m> is false.  Try this: read through the previous paragraph again, substituting <m>1</m> for each <m>k</m>.  Can you spot the error in that argument?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:839
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Suppose you wanted to prove, using mathematical induction, that <m>1+3+5+\\cdots+2n-1 = n^2</m> for all values of <m>n \\ge 1</m>.  Which of the following would be an appropriate <em>first line</em> of the proof?  Select all that apply.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:846
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <q><m>1+3+5+\\cdots+2n-1 = n^2</m>.</q>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:851
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Correct.  Note in particular, we do not include the <q>for all <m>n \\ge 1</m></q> as part of the definition of <m>P(n)</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:858
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              For each <m>n \\ge 1</m>, let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement, <q>the sum of the first <m>n</m> odd numbers is <m>n^2</m>.</q>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:863
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              This is correct.  Note that saying that we define <m>P(n)</m> for each <m>n\\ge 1</m> is different from saying that <m>P(n)</m> includes <q>...for all <m>n\\ge 1</m>.</q>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:870
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Assume <m>1+3+\\cdots + 2n-1 = n^2</m> for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:875
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              This is what you are trying to prove, so you cannot assume it.  Later in the proof (in the inductive case) we will assume that <m>P(k)</m> is true for some arbitrary <m>k</m>, but this is not assuming it is true for all <m>n</m> at once.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:882
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement, <q><m>1+3+\\cdots+2n-1 = n^2</m> for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.</q>\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:887
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              This doesn't make sense: what would <m>P(3)</m> be?  That <m>1 + 3 + 5 = 3^2</m> for all <m>3 \\ge 1</m>??\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:894
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Since <m>P(1) = 1 = 1^2</m>, the base case is true.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:899
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Two problems here: first, you need to say what <m>P(n)</m> is.  Second, <m>P(1)</m> is a statement, so it cannot be equal to the number 1.  \n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-induction.ptx:908
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Suppose you wanted to prove that <m>P(n,3) \\ge \\binom{n}{3}</m> for all <m>n \\ge 4</m>.  Write the first line of a proof by induction. \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:12
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you are trying to prove, by mathematical induction, that a statement <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.  What would you attempt to prove in the <em>induction step</em> of the proof?  (Select all that apply.)\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            That assuming <m>P(k)</m> is true for an arbitrary <m>k \\ge 0</m>, we can prove that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:26
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            That <m>P(k)</m> implies <m>P(k+1)</m> for all <m>k \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:33
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            That assuming <m>P(k+1)</m> is true for an arbitrary <m>k \\ge 0</m>, we can prove that <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:40
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            That <m>P(k+1)</m> implies <m>P(k)</m> for all <m>k \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            That <m>P(k)</m> implies <m>P(k+1)</m> for at least one <m>k \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you wanted to prove the following statement:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:60
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"        What would the first line of a proof by induction be?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:68
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <q><m>2 + 4 + 6 + \\cdots + 2n = n(n+1)</m>.</q>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:75
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <q><m>2 + 4 + 6 + \\cdots + 2n = n(n-1)</m> for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.</q>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:82
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Assume <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:89
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>P(n) = 2 + 4 + 6 + \\cdots + 2n</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:96
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Suppose <m>P(n) = n(n+1)</m> for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:107 source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:161
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you were proving the following statement by mathematical induction:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:110
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"        What would you need to show to establish the base case?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Show that <m>P(1)</m> is true.  That is, show that <m>2 = 1(1+1)</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:125
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Show that <m>P(2)</m> is true.  That is, show that <m>2 + 4 = 2(2+1)</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:130
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Even though the sum starts with <m>2</m>, we need to consider the smallest <m>n</m> for which the statement <m>P(n)</m> is true.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:137
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Show that <m>P(1)</m> and <m>P(2)</m> are both true.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:144
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Show that <m>P(1)</m> implies <m>P(2)</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:151
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Nothing, since the sum already has more than <m>n = 1</m> terms.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:164
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"        What would the first line of the inductive case be?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:172
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Assume <m>P(k)</m> is true for some arbitrary <m>k \\ge 1</m>, that is, assume <m>2+4+6+\\cdots + 2k = k(k+1)</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:179
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Assume <m>P(k)</m> is true for all <m>k \\ge 1</m>, that is, assume <m>2+4+6+\\cdots + 2k = k(k+1)</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><feedback><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:184
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Assume <m>P(k)</m> and <m>P(k)</m> are both true for an arbitrary <m>k \\ge 1</m>; that is, assume <m>2+4+6+\\cdots + 2k = k(k+1)</m> and <m>2+4+6+\\cdots + 2k+ 2k+2) = (k+1)(k+2)</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:191
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Assume <m>P(k)</m> implies <m>P(k+1)</m> for an arbitrary <m>k \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:198
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Assume <m>P(k)</m> is true for some large <m>k \\ge 1</m>; say, assume <m>2 + 4 + 6 + \\cdots + 432 = 216(217)</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:209
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Arrange some of the statements below to create a correct proof by induction that the recurrence relation <m>a_n = 5a_{n-1} + 4</m>, with initial condition <m>a_0 = 0</m> has closed formula <m>a_n = 5^n - 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:215
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement, <q><m>a_n = 5^n - 1</m></q>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:221
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Note that <m>a_0 = 5^0 - 1 = 0</m>, so <m>P(0)</m> is true.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:226
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Now assume that <m>P(k)</m> is true for an arbitrary integer <m>k \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:231
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Then <m>a_k = 5^k - 1</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:236
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      By the recurrence relation, we have <m>a_{k+1} = 5 a_k + 4 = 5 (5^k -1) + 4</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:241
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This simplifies to <m>a_{k+1} = 5^{k+1} - 5 + 4 = 5^{k+1} - 1</m>, so <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:246
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Therefore, by the principle of mathematical induction, <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:251
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Now assume that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true for an arbitrary integer <m>k \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:256
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Then <m>a_{k+1} = 5a_k+4</m>, so <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:266
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Arrange some of the statements below to create a correct proof by induction that for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>, the number <m>14^n - 1</m> is a multiple of <m>13</m>. \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:272
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement, <q><m>14^n - 1</m> is a multiple of <m>13</m>.</q>\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:277
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Note that <m>14^1 - 1 = 13</m>, so this is definitely a multiple of <m>13</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:282
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Now assume that <m>P(k)</m> is true for an arbitrary integers <m>k \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:287
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Then <m>14^k - 1 = 13\\cdot j</m> for some integer <m>j</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:292
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Since <m>14^{k+1} - 1 = 14(14^k - 1) + 14 - 1 = 14(13\\cdot j) + 13</m>, we see that <m>14^{k+1} - 1</m> is a multiple of <m>13</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:297
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Thus <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:302
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Therefore, by the principle of mathematical induction, <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:307
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Note that <m>14^2 - 1 = (14-1)(14+1)</m> by difference of squares, so this is a multiple of 13.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:312
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Now assume that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:317
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Thus <m>14^k = \\frac{14^{k+1} - 1}{14}</m>, which must also be a multiple of 13.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:327
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Arrange some of the statements below to create a correct proof by induction that for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>, <m>1+1+2+3+5+\\cdots + F_n = F_{n+2} - 1</m>, where <m>F_n</m> is the <m>n</m>th Fibonacci number.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:333
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement, <q><m>1+1+2+3+5+\\cdots + F_n = F_{n+2} - 1</m>.</q>\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:339
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      For the base case, note that <m>P(1)</m> and <m>P(2)</m> are true, because <m>1 = 2-1</m> and <m>1+1 = 3-1</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:344
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Now assume that <m>P(k)</m> is true for an arbitrary integer <m>k \\ge 2</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:349
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      That is, assume <m>1+1+2+3 + 5+F_k = F_{k+2} - 1</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:354
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Then adding <m>F_{k+1}</m> to both sides, we get <m>1+1+2+3+5+\\cdots + F_k + F_{k+1} = F_{k+1} + F_{k+2} - 1</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:359
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        By the definition of Fibonacci numbers, <m>F_{k+1} + F_{k+2} = F_{k+3}</m>, so the right-hand side simplifies to <m>F_{k+3} - 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:364
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Thus <m>P(k+1)</m> is true, and therefore by the principle of mathematical induction, <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:369
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For the base case, note that <m>1+1+2 = 4 = F_{5}-1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:374
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Then by the inductive hypothesis, <m>1+1+2+3+\\cdots+F_{k+1} = F_{k+2} - 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-induction.ptx:379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Subtracting <m>F_{k+1}</m> from both sides gives us <m>P(k)</m>, which we also assumed to be true.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:10
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        On the way to the market,\n"
"        you exchange your cow for some magic dark chocolate espresso beans.\n"
"        These beans have the property that every night at midnight,\n"
"        each bean splits into two, effectively doubling your collection.\n"
"        You decide to take advantage of this, and each morning (around 8 am) you eat 5 beans.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Explain why it is true that <em>if</em>\n"
"              at noon on day <m>n</m> you have a number of beans ending in a 5, then at noon on day <m>n+1</m> you will still have a number of beans ending in a 5.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:26
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Why is the previous fact not enough to conclude that you will always have a number of beans ending in a 5?\n"
"              What additional fact would you need?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:33
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Assuming you have the additional fact in part (b),\n"
"              and have successfully proved the fact in part (a),\n"
"              how do you know that you will always have a number of beans ending in a 5?\n"
"              Illustrate what is going on by carefully explaining how the two facts above prove that you will have a number of beans ending in a 5 on\n"
"              <em>day 4</em> specifically.\n"
"              In other words, explain why induction works in this context.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:49
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If we have a number of beans ending in a 5, and we double it,\n"
"              we will get a number of beans ending in a 0\n"
"              (since <m>5\\cdot 2 = 10</m> ).\n"
"              Then if we subtract 5, we will once again get a number of beans ending in a 5.\n"
"              Thus, if on any day we have a number ending in a 5, the next day we will also have a number ending in a 5.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:59
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              If you don't <em>start</em> with a number of beans ending in a 5\n"
"              (on day 1),\n"
"              the above reasoning is still correct but not helpful.\n"
"              For example,\n"
"              if you start with a number ending in a 3, the next day you will have a number ending in a 1.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Part (b) is the base case, and part (a) is the inductive case.\n"
"              If on day 1 we have a number ending in a 5 (by part (b)),\n"
"              then on day 2 we will also have a number ending in a 5 (by part (a)).\n"
"              Then by part (a) again, we will have a number ending in a 5 on day 3.\n"
"              By part (a) again,\n"
"              this means we will have a number ending in a 5 on day 4.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:78
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The proof by induction would say that on <em>every</em>\n"
"              day we will have a number ending in a 5, and this works because we can start with the base case and\n"
"              then use the inductive case over and over until we get up to our desired <m>n</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:91
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Use induction to prove for all\n"
"        <m>n \\in \\N</m> that <m>\\d\\sum_{k=0}^n 2^k = 2^{n+1} - 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:99
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          We must prove that <m>1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + \\cdots +2^n = 2^{n+1} - 1</m> for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          Thus let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <m>1 + 2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + 2^n = 2^{n+1} - 1</m>.\n"
"          We will prove that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          First we establish the base case,\n"
"          <m>P(0)</m>, which claims that <m>1 = 2^{0+1} -1</m>.\n"
"          Since <m>2^1 - 1 = 2 - 1 = 1</m>,\n"
"          we see that <m>P(0)</m> is true.\n"
"          Now for the inductive case.\n"
"          Assume that <m>P(k)</m> is true for an arbitrary <m>k \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          That is, <m>1 + 2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + 2^k = 2^{k+1} - 1</m>.\n"
"          We must show that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true (i.e., that <m>1 + 2 + 2^2 + \\cdots + 2^{k+1} = 2^{k+2} - 1</m>).\n"
"          To do this, we start with the left-hand side of <m>P(k+1)</m> and work to the right-hand side:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:119
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Thus <m>P(k+1)</m> is true, so by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:130
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that <m>7^n - 1</m> is a multiple of 6 for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:137
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement,\n"
"          <q><m>7^n - 1</m> is a multiple of 6.</q>\n"
"          We will show <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          First we establish the base case, <m>P(0)</m>.\n"
"          Since <m>7^0 - 1 = 0</m>,\n"
"          and <m>0</m> is a multiple of 6, <m>P(0)</m> is true.\n"
"          Now for the inductive case.\n"
"          Assume <m>P(k)</m> holds for an arbitrary <m>k \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          That is, <m>7^k - 1</m> is a multiple of 6, or in other words,\n"
"          <m>7^k - 1 = 6j</m> for some integer <m>j</m>.\n"
"          Now consider <m>7^{k+1} - 1</m>:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:157
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore <m>7^{k+1} - 1</m> is a multiple of 6, or in other words,\n"
"          <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"          Therefore by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:170
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that <m>1 + 3 + 5 + \\cdots + (2n-1) = n^2</m> for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <m>1+3 +5 + \\cdots + (2n-1) = n^2</m>.\n"
"          We will prove that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"          First the base case, <m>P(1)</m>.\n"
"          We have <m>1 = 1^2</m> which is true,\n"
"          so <m>P(1)</m> is established.\n"
"          Now the inductive case.\n"
"          Assume that <m>P(k)</m> is true for some fixed arbitrary <m>k \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"          That is, <m>1 + 3 + 5 + \\cdots + (2k-1) = k^2</m>.\n"
"          We will now prove that <m>P(k+1)</m> is also true (i.e., that <m>1 + 3 + 5 + \\cdots + (2k+1) = (k+1)^2</m>).\n"
"          We start with the left-hand side of <m>P(k+1)</m> and work to the right-hand side:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:194
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Thus <m>P(k+1)</m> holds,\n"
"          so by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:207
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that <m>F_0 + F_2 + F_4 + \\cdots + F_{2n} = F_{2n+1} - 1</m> where <m>F_n</m> is the <m>n</m>th Fibonacci number.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:214
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <m>F_0 + F_2 + F_4 + \\cdots + F_{2n} = F_{2n+1} - 1</m>.\n"
"          We will show that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"          First the base case is easy because <m>F_0 = 0</m> and <m>F_1 = 1</m> so <m>F_0 = F_1 - 1</m>.\n"
"          Now consider the inductive case.\n"
"          Assume <m>P(k)</m> is true, that is,\n"
"          assume <m>F_0 + F_2 + F_4 + \\cdots + F_{2k} = F_{2k+1} - 1</m>.\n"
"          To establish <m>P(k+1)</m> we work from left to right:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:229
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore <m>F_0 + F_2 + F_4 + \\cdots + F_{2k+2} = F_{2k+3} - 1</m>,\n"
"          which is to say <m>P(k+1)</m> holds.\n"
"          Therefore by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:242
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that <m>2^n \\lt n!</m> for all <m>n \\ge 4</m>.\n"
"        (Recall, <m>n! = 1\\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\cdots\\cdot n</m>.)\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:250
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <m>2^n \\lt  n!</m>.\n"
"          We will show <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 4</m>.\n"
"          First, we check the base case and see that yes, <m>2^4 \\lt  4!</m>\n"
"          (as <m>16 \\lt  24</m>),\n"
"          so <m>P(4)</m> is true.\n"
"          Now for the inductive case.\n"
"          Assume <m>P(k)</m> is true for an arbitrary <m>k \\ge 4</m>.\n"
"          That is, <m>2^k \\lt  k!</m>.\n"
"          Now consider <m>P(k+1)</m>: <m>2^{k+1} \\lt  (k+1)!</m>.\n"
"          To prove this,\n"
"          we start with the left side and work to the right side.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:270
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore <m>2^{k+1} \\lt (k+1)!</m>, so we have established <m>P(k+1)</m>.\n"
"          Thus by the principle of mathematical induction <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 4</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:282
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove, by mathematical induction,\n"
"        that <m>F_0 + F_1 + F_2 + \\cdots + F_{n} = F_{n+2} - 1</m>,\n"
"        where <m>F_n</m> is the <m>n</m>th Fibonacci number\n"
"        (<m>F_0 = 0</m>,\n"
"        <m>F_1 = 1</m> and <m>F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2}</m>).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:295
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Zombie Euler and Zombie Cauchy,\n"
"        two famous zombie mathematicians,\n"
"        have just signed up for Myspace accounts.\n"
"        After one day, Zombie Cauchy has more followers than Zombie Euler.\n"
"        Each day after that,\n"
"        the number of new followers of Zombie Cauchy is exactly the same as the number of new followers of Zombie Euler\n"
"        (and neither lose any followers).\n"
"        Explain how a proof by mathematical induction can show that on every day after the first day, Zombie Cauchy will have more followers than Zombie Euler.\n"
"        That is, explain what the base case and inductive case are,\n"
"        and why they together prove that Zombie Cauchy will have more followers on the 4th day.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:313
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Find the largest number of points that it is impossible for a football team to get exactly, using just 3-point field goals and 7-point touchdowns\n"
"        (ignore the possibilities of safeties,\n"
"        missed extra points, and two-point conversions).\n"
"        Prove your answer is correct by mathematical induction.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:321
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        It is not possible to score exactly 11 points.  Can you prove that you can score <m>n</m> points for any <m>n \\ge 12</m>?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:329
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that the sum of <m>n</m> squares can be found as follows\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:341
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that the sum of the interior angles of a convex <m>n</m>-gon is\n"
"        <m>(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ</m>. (A convex <m>n</m>-gon is a polygon with <m>n</m> sides for which each interior angle is less than <m>180^\\circ</m>.)\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:347
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Start with <m>(k+1)</m>-gon, and divide it up into a <m>k</m>-gon and a triangle.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:356
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        What is wrong with the following\n"
"        <q>proof</q> of the <q>fact</q>\n"
"        that <m>n+3 = n+7</m> for all values of <m>n</m> (besides of course that the thing it is claiming to prove is false)?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:363
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement that <m>n + 3 = n + 7</m>.\n"
"          We will prove that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          Assume, for induction, that <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"          That is, <m>k+3 = k+7</m>.\n"
"          We must show that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"          Now since <m>k + 3 = k + 7</m>, add 1 to both sides.\n"
"          This gives <m>k + 3 + 1 = k + 7 + 1</m>.\n"
"          Regrouping <m>(k+1) + 3 = (k+1) + 7</m>.\n"
"          But this is simply <m>P(k+1)</m>.\n"
"          Thus by the principle of mathematical induction <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The only problem is that we never established the base case.\n"
"        Of course, when <m>n = 0</m>, <m>0+3 \\ne 0+7</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:388
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The proof in the previous problem does not work.\n"
"        But if we modify the <q>fact,</q>\n"
"        we can get a working proof.\n"
"        Prove that <m>n + 3 \\lt n + 7</m> for all values of <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        You can do this proof with algebra\n"
"        (without induction),\n"
"        but the goal of this exercise is to write out a valid induction proof.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:401
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement that <m>n + 3 \\lt n + 7</m>.\n"
"          We will prove that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          First, note that the base case holds: <m>0+3 \\lt 0+7</m>.\n"
"          Now assume for induction that <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"          That is, <m>k+3 \\lt k+7</m>.\n"
"          We must show that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"          Now since <m>k + 3 \\lt k + 7</m>, add 1 to both sides.\n"
"          This gives <m>k + 3 + 1 \\lt k + 7 + 1</m>.\n"
"          Regrouping <m>(k+1) + 3 \\lt (k+1) + 7</m>.\n"
"          But this is simply <m>P(k+1)</m>.\n"
"          Thus by the principle of mathematical induction <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:421
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Find the flaw in the following <q>proof</q> of the <q>fact</q>\n"
"        that <m>n \\lt 100</m> for every <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:427
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <m>n \\lt 100</m>.\n"
"          We will prove <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          First we establish the base case:\n"
"          when <m>n = 0</m>, <m>P(n)</m> is true, because <m>0 \\lt 100</m>.\n"
"          Now for the inductive step, assume <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"          That is, <m>k \\lt 100</m>.\n"
"          Now if <m>k \\lt 100</m>, then <m>k</m> is some number, like 80.\n"
"          Of course <m>80+1 = 81</m> which is still less than 100.\n"
"          So <m>k +1 \\lt 100</m> as well.\n"
"          But this is what <m>P(k+1)</m> claims,\n"
"          so we have shown that <m>P(k) \\imp P(k+1)</m>.\n"
"          Thus by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:446
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The problem here is that while <m>P(0)</m> is true,\n"
"        and while <m>P(k) \\imp P(k+1)</m> for\n"
"        <em>some</em> values of <m>k</m>,\n"
"        there is at least one value of <m>k</m>\n"
"        (namely <m>k = 99</m>)\n"
"        when that implication fails.\n"
"        For a valid proof by induction,\n"
"        <m>P(k) \\imp P(k+1)</m> must be true for all values of <m>k</m> greater than or equal to the base case.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:461
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        While the above proof does not work\n"
"        (it better not since the statement it is trying to prove is false!)\n"
"        we can prove something similar.\n"
"        Prove that there is a strictly increasing sequence <m>a_1, a_2, a_3, \\ldots</m> of numbers\n"
"        (not necessarily integers)\n"
"        such that <m>a_n \\lt 100</m> for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        (By <term>strictly increasing</term>\n"
"        we mean <m>a_n \\lt a_{n+1}</m> for all <m>n</m>.\n"
"        So each term must be larger than the last.)\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:474
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For the inductive step, you can assume you have a strictly increasing sequence up to <m>a_k</m> where <m>a_k \\lt 100</m>.  Now you just need to find the next term <m>a_{k+1}</m> so that <m>a_{k} \\lt a_{k+1} \\lt 100</m>.  What should <m>a_{k+1}</m> be?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:483
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        What is wrong with the following\n"
"        <q>proof</q> of the <q>fact</q>\n"
"        that for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>,\n"
"        the number <m>n^2 + n</m> is odd?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:491
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement\n"
"          <q><m>n^2 + n</m> is odd.</q>\n"
"          We will prove that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          Suppose, for induction, that <m>P(k)</m> is true,\n"
"          that is, that <m>k^2 + k</m> is odd.\n"
"          Now consider the statement <m>P(k+1)</m>.\n"
"          Now <m>(k+1)^2 + (k+1) = k^2 + 2k + 1 + k + 1 = k^2 + k + 2k + 2</m>.\n"
"          By the inductive hypothesis,\n"
"          <m>k^2 + k</m> is odd, and of course <m>2k + 2</m> is even.\n"
"          An odd plus an even is always odd,\n"
"          so therefore <m>(k+1)^2 + (k+1)</m> is odd.\n"
"          Therefore by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:510
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We once again failed to establish the base case:\n"
"        When <m>n = 0</m>, <m>n^2 + n = 0</m> which is even, not odd.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:519
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Now give a valid proof\n"
"        (by induction,\n"
"        even though you might be able to do so without using induction)\n"
"        of the statement, <q>For all <m>n \\in \\N</m>,\n"
"        the number <m>n^2 + n</m> is even.</q>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:528
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For the inductive case, you will need to show that <m>(k+1)^2 + (k+1)</m> is even.  Factor this out, and locate the part of it that is <m>k^2 + k</m>.  What have you assumed about that quantity?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:538
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that there is a sequence of positive real numbers\n"
"        <m>a_0, a_1, a_2, \\ldots</m> such that the partial sum\n"
"        <m>a_0 + a_1 + a_2 + \\cdots + a_n</m> is strictly less than <m>2</m> for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"        Hint: Think about how you could define what <m>a_{k+1}</m> is to make the induction argument work.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:546
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This is similar to <xref po4a-id=0>, although there you were showing that a sequence had all its terms less than some value, and here you are showing that the sum is less than some value.  But the partial sums forms a sequence, so this is actually very similar.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:557
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Use induction to prove that if <m>n</m> people all shake hands with each other,\n"
"        that the total number of handshakes is <m>\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:563
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We have already proved this without using induction, but looking at it inductively sheds light onto the problem (and is fun).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:566
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The question you need to answer to complete the inductive step is, how many new handshakes take place when a person <m>k+1</m> enters the room?  Why does adding this give you the correct formula?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:577
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Use induction to prove that <m>\\d\\sum_{k=0}^n {n \\choose k} = 2^n</m>.\n"
"        That is, the sum of the <m>n</m>th row of Pascal's triangle is <m>2^n</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:583
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Here's the idea:\n"
"        Since every entry in Pascal's triangle is the sum of the two entries above it,\n"
"        we can get the <m>k+1</m>st row by adding up all the pairs of entry from the <m>k</m>th row.\n"
"        But doing this uses each entry on the <m>k</m>th row twice.\n"
"        Thus each time we drop to the next row, we double the total.\n"
"        Of course, row 0 has sum <m>1 = 2^0</m>\n"
"        (the base case).\n"
"        Now try to make this precise with a formal induction proof.\n"
"        You will use the fact that\n"
"        <m>{n \\choose k} = {n-1 \\choose k-1} + {n-1 \\choose k}</m> for the inductive case.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:601
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Use induction to prove <m>{4 \\choose 0} + {5 \\choose 1} + {6 \\choose 2} + \\cdots + {4+n \\choose n} = {5+n \\choose n}</m>.\n"
"        (This is an example of the hockey stick theorem.)\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:607
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        To see why this works, try it on a copy of Pascal's triangle.\n"
"        We are adding up the entries along a diagonal,\n"
"        starting with the 1 on the left-hand side of the 4th row.\n"
"        Suppose we add up the first 5 entries on this diagonal.\n"
"        The claim is that the sum is the entry below and to the left of the last of these 5 entries.\n"
"        Note that if this is true, and we instead add up the first 6 entries,\n"
"        we will need to add the entry one spot to the right of the previous sum.\n"
"        But these two together give the entry below them,\n"
"        which is below and left of the last of the 6 entries on the diagonal.\n"
"        If you follow that, you can see what is going on.\n"
"        But it is not a great proof.\n"
"        A formal induction proof is needed.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:627
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Use the product rule for logarithms (<m>\\log(ab) = \\log(a) + \\log(b)</m>) to prove,\n"
"        by induction on <m>n</m>,\n"
"        that <m>\\log(a^n) = n \\log(a)</m>,\n"
"        for all natural numbers <m>n \\ge 2</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:635
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The idea here is that if we take the logarithm of <m>a^n</m>,\n"
"        we can increase <m>n</m> by 1 if we multiply by another <m>a</m>\n"
"        (inside the logarithm).\n"
"        This results in adding 1 more <m>\\log(a)</m> to the total.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:643
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement <m>\\log(a^n) = n \\log(a)</m>.\n"
"          The base case, <m>P(2)</m> is true,\n"
"          because <m>\\log(a^2) = \\log(a\\cdot a) = \\log(a) + \\log(a) = 2\\log(a)</m>,\n"
"          by the product rule for logarithms.\n"
"          Now assume, for induction, that <m>P(k)</m> is true.\n"
"          That is, <m>\\log(a^k) = k\\log(a)</m>.\n"
"          Consider <m>\\log(a^{k+1})</m>.\n"
"          We have\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:653
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"          with the last equality due to the inductive hypothesis.\n"
"          But this simplifies to <m>(k+1) \\log(a)</m>,\n"
"          establishing <m>P(k+1)</m>.\n"
"          Therefore by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 2</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:669
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Let <m>f_1, f_2,\\ldots, f_n</m> be differentiable functions.\n"
"        Prove, using induction, that\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:677
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        You may assume <m>(f+g)' = f' + g'</m> for any differentiable functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:682
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        You are allowed to assume the base case.\n"
"        For the inductive case,\n"
"        group all but the last function together as one sum of functions, and\n"
"        then apply the usual sum of derivatives rule,\n"
"        and then the inductive hypothesis.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:695
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose <m>f_1, f_2, \\ldots, f_n</m> are differentiable functions.\n"
"        Use mathematical induction to prove the generalized product rule:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        You may assume the product rule for two functions is true.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:708
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For the inductive step, we know by the product rule for two functions that\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:715
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Then use the inductive hypothesis on the first summand,\n"
"        and distribute.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:725
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        In <xref po4a-id=0> we proved that the following is a valid deduction rule:\n"
"\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:731
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P\n"
"              \\imp Q</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:737
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>Q \\imp R</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:741
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>\n"
"              \\therefore</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:744
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P \\imp R</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:750
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Now use mathematical induction to prove you can chain together any number of statements like this.  That is, prove for any <m>n</m> that the following is a valid deduction rule:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:755
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P_1 \\imp P_2</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:760
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P_2\n"
"              \\imp P_3</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:766
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>\\vdots</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:771
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P_{n-1}\n"
"              \\imp P_n</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:776
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>\\therefore</m>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:778
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<m>P_1\n"
"\\imp P_n</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-induction.ptx:786
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        You can inductively assume that from the first <m>n-2</m> implications you\n"
"        can deduce <m>P_1 \\imp P_{n-1}</m>. Then you can use a truth table to verify that this simplified deduction rule is valid. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><title>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Strong Induction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:21
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tExplain the difference between proof by induction and proof by strong induction.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><objectives><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:27
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\tUse strong induction to prove statements.\n"
"\t\t\t\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:38
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Start with a square piece of paper.\n"
"        You want to cut this square into smaller squares, leaving no waste\n"
"        (every piece of paper you end up with must be a square).\n"
"        Obviously it is possible to cut the square into 4 squares.\n"
"        You can also cut it into 9 squares.\n"
"        It turns out you can cut the square into 7 squares\n"
"        (although not all the same size).\n"
"        What other numbers of squares could you end up with?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:49
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Sometimes, to prove that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true,\n"
"      it would be helpful to know that <m>P(k)</m>\n"
"      <em>and</em> <m>P(k-1)</m> <em>and</em>\n"
"      <m>P(k-2)</m> are all true.  This is certainly the case when proving something about a recurrence relation that is given as a combination of two previous terms.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:58
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Prove that <m>2^n</m> is a solution to the recurrence relation <m>a_n = 5a_{n-1} - 6a_{n-2}</m> with initial conditions <m>a_0 = 1</m> and <m>a_1 = 2</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:64
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement,\n"
"            <q><m>a_n = 2^n</m>.</q>  We will show this is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Base cases: <m>a_0 = 2^0 = 1</m> and <m>a_1 = 2^1 = 2</m> both agree with the initial conditions.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Inductive case:  Let <m>k \\ge 2</m> be arbitrary.  Assume <m>P(k)</m> and <m>P(k-1)</m> are both true.  That is, assume <m>a_k = 2^k</m> and <m>a_{k-1} = 2^{k-1}</m>.  We will show that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.  Consider <m>a_{k+1}</m>.  We have\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"            Therefore, by the principle of mathematical induction, <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:85
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Well, almost the principle of mathematical induction.  Is what we did okay?  \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:91
#, no-wrap
msgid "  There are also times when we might want to go even farther back to use an assumption that <m>P(j)</m> is true for <m>j</m> much smaller than <m>k+1</m>.  This is the idea behind <term>strong induction</term> and the topic of this short section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Consider the following puzzle:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><introduction><blockquote><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:112
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      You have a rectangular chocolate bar,\n"
"      made up of <m>n</m> identical squares of chocolate.\n"
"      You can take such a bar and break it along any row or column.\n"
"      How many times will you have to break the bar to reduce it to <m>n</m> single chocolate squares?\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:119
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    At first, this question might seem impossible.\n"
"      Perhaps we meant to ask for the <em>smallest</em> number of breaks needed.\n"
"      Let's investigate.\n"
"  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose you started with a <m>1\\times 3</m> bar.  How many breaks would you need to reduce it to single squares?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:137
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          If you had a <m>1\\times 4</m> bar, how many breaks are required?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          If you had 4 squares arranged in a <m>2\\times 2</m> square, your first break would require you to break the chocolate into two <m>1 \\times 2</m> bars.  Then each of these would require <var po4a-id=0> more break(s), for a total of <var po4a-id=1> breaks to go from the <m>2\\times 2</m> to single squares.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:150
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A <m>6</m>-square bar could either be a <m>1 \\times 6</m> bar, requiring <var po4a-id=0> breaks, or a <m>2 \\times 3</m> bar. \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:154
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          There are two ways to proceed now.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:158
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Break the bar into two <m>1 \\times 3</m> bars, each requiring <var po4a-id=0> more breaks, for a total of <var po4a-id=1> breaks.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:163
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Break the bar into a <m>1\\times 2</m> bar and a <m>2 \\times 2</m> bar.  The <m>1 \\times 2</m> bar takes <var po4a-id=0> more break(s) and the <m>2 \\times 2</m> bar takes <var po4a-id=1> more break(s), for a total of <var po4a-id=2> breaks.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:173
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Based on the above data, what should our conjecture be for the number of breaks to reduce an <m>n</m>-square bar to single squares, in terms of <m>n</m>?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:176
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          It will take <var po4a-id=0> breaks to reduce an <m>n</m>-square bar to single squares.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:183
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Do we believe this?  Suppose you used one break to reduce the bar into two smaller bars, with <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> squares respectively.  If the conjecture is correct, how many more breaks will it take to reduce the size <m>a</m> bar?\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:190
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          How many more breaks will it take to reduce the size <m>b</m> bar?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:196
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          How many breaks is this all together, in terms of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, including the initial break?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><worksheet><exercise><webwork><task><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:202
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          But what is <m>a+b</m>?  We got <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> by breaking the <m>n</m> squares in two pieces, so <m>a+b = </m><var po4a-id=0>.  This gives us a total number of breaks as <var po4a-id=1>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:213
#, no-wrap
msgid "Divide and Conquer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:216
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Think of recursive definitions as instructions for building a ladder.  You can build the ladder as tall as you like because you have instructions for building the next rung, as long as you are standing on the rung before it.\n"
"\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:221
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Induction is the corresponding proof technique.  To prove that you can climb the ladder as high as you like, you prove that you can step onto the ladder (the base case) and then prove that, from any rung, you can get to the next rung (the inductive step).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:225
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        More specifically, suppose you were trying to prove that you can get to rung 4 on the ladder.  You have successfully proved that you can get to rung 1, and that from any rung, you can get to the next.  So you can get to rung 1, and from 1 you can get to 2.  From 2 you can get to 3, and from 3 you can get to 4.  Therefore, you can get to 4.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:229
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        But notice that along the way, you know you have visited rungs 1 through 3.  We might as well assume that we have visited all the rungs below the next one we are trying to reach.  This is the idea behind <term>strong induction</term>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:233
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A better ladder metaphor for strong induction is to think of ladders as things we can stack on top of each other.  We want to argue that it is possible to climb 20 rungs of a ladder.  Let's divide that into two smaller ladders, say a 12-rung ladder and an 8-rung ladder.  We can assume that we can climb both of these since 20 is the least size we are not yet convinced of.  Well, put those two ladders together, and you get <m>12+8 = 20</m> rungs.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:237
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We better climb down from our shaky metaphor before we hurt ourselves.  Let's look at a formal definition of strong induction.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:241
#, no-wrap
msgid "Strong Induction Proof Structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><q>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:245
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Start by saying what we want to prove:\n"
"          <q>Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:246
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".</q>\n"
"          Then establish two facts:\n"
"  \n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:252
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Base case: Prove that <m>P(0)</m> is true.  (Perhaps also prove other needed base cases.)\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:260
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Inductive case: Assume <m>P(j)</m> is true for all <m>j \\le k</m>.\n"
"                Prove that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:269
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Conclude, <q>Therefore, by strong induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\gt 0</m>.</q>\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><fn>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:276
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Technically,\n"
"      strong induction does not require you to prove a separate base case.\n"
"      This is because when proving the inductive case,\n"
"      you must show that <m>P(0)</m> is true,\n"
"      assuming <m>P(k)</m> is true for all <m>k \\lt 0</m>.\n"
"      But this is not any help so you end up proving <m>P(0)</m> anyway.\n"
"      To be on the safe side, we will always include the base case separately.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:275
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Of course, it is acceptable to replace 0 with a larger base case if needed.<placeholder type=\"fn\" id=\"0\"/>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:286
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        To illustrate strong induction, let's return to the chocolate bar problem.  \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proposition><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:293
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Given an <m>n</m>-square rectangular chocolate bar,\n"
"                it always takes <m>n-1</m> breaks to reduce the bar to single squares.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:301
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement,\n"
"        <q>It takes <m>n-1</m> breaks to reduce a <m>n</m>-square chocolate bar to single squares.</q>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:306
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Base case: Consider <m>P(2)</m>.\n"
"        The squares must be arranged into a <m>1\\times 2</m> rectangle,\n"
"        and we require <m>2-1 = 1</m> breaks to reduce this to single squares.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:312
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Inductive case:\n"
"        Fix an arbitrary <m>k\\ge 2</m> and assume <m>P(j)</m> is true for all <m>j \\le k</m>.\n"
"        Consider a <m>(k+1)</m>-square rectangular chocolate bar.\n"
"        Break the bar once along any row or column.\n"
"        This results in two chocolate bars,\n"
"        say of sizes <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> .\n"
"        That is, we have an <m>a</m>-square rectangular chocolate bar,\n"
"        a <m>b</m>-square rectangular chocolate bar, and <m>a+b = k+1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:323
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We also know that <m>a \\le k</m> and <m>b \\le k</m>,\n"
"        so by our inductive hypothesis,\n"
"        <m>P(a)</m> and <m>P(b)</m> are true.\n"
"        To reduce the <m>a</m>-square bar to single squares takes <m>a-1</m> breaks;\n"
"        to reduce the <m>b</m>-square bar to single squares takes <m>b-1</m> breaks.\n"
"        Doing this results in our original bar being reduced to single squares.\n"
"        All together it took the initial break,\n"
"        plus the <m>a-1</m> and <m>b-1</m> breaks,\n"
"        for a total of "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:331
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" breaks.\n"
"        Thus <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:336
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Therefore, by strong induction,\n"
"        <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 2</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:342
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Here is a more mathematically relevant example:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:348
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Prove that any natural number greater than 1 is either prime\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:350
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          or can be written as the product of primes.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:355
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          First, the idea:\n"
"          If we take some number <m>n</m>, maybe it is prime.\n"
"          If so, we are done.\n"
"          If not, then it is composite,\n"
"          so it is the product of two smaller numbers.\n"
"          Each of these factors is smaller than <m>n</m>\n"
"          (but at least 2),\n"
"          so we can repeat the argument with these numbers.\n"
"          We have reduced to a smaller case.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:372
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement,\n"
"            <q><m>n</m> is either prime or can be written as the product of primes.</q>\n"
"            We will prove <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 2</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:378
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Base case: <m>P(2)</m> is true because <m>2</m> is indeed prime.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:382
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Inductive case: assume <m>P(j)</m> is true for all <m>j \\le k</m>.\n"
"            We want to show that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"            That is, we want to show that <m>k+1</m> is either prime or is the product of primes.\n"
"            If <m>k+1</m> is prime, we are done.\n"
"            If not, then <m>k+1</m> has more than 2 divisors,\n"
"            so we can write <m>k+1 = m_1 \\cdot m_2</m>,\n"
"            with <m>m_1</m> and <m>m_2</m> less than <m>k+1</m>\n"
"            (and greater than 1).\n"
"            By the inductive hypothesis,\n"
"            <m>m_1</m> and <m>m_2</m> are each either prime or can be written as the product of primes.\n"
"            In either case,\n"
"            we have that <m>m_1\\cdot m_2 = k+1</m> can be written as the product of primes.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><proof><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:397
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Thus by strong induction,\n"
"            <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 2</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:406
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Whether you use regular induction or strong induction depends on the statement you want to prove.\n"
"      If you wanted to be safe, you could always use strong induction.\n"
"      It really is <em>stronger</em>,\n"
"      so can accomplish everything <q>weak</q> induction can.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:411
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      That said, using regular induction is often easier since there is only one place you can use the induction hypothesis.\n"
"      There is also something to be said for\n"
"      <em>elegance</em> in proofs.\n"
"      If you can prove a statement using simpler tools, it is nice to do so.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:439
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          True or false: To prove the inductive case of a proof by strong induction, you should assume <m>P(k+1)</m> is true and prove that <m>P(j)</m> is true for all <m>j \\le k</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><feedback><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:444
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          This is backwards.  You still prove the next case, you just get to assume all previous cases (not just the one previous case).\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:452
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Which of the following claims about the relationship between proof by induction and proof by strong induction are true?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:459
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Any proof by induction can be written as a proof by strong induction.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:466
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Any proof by strong induction can be written as a proof by induction.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:473
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Strong induction is <q>stronger</q> because the base case is stronger.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><reading-questions><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/sec_seq-strong-induction.ptx:480
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Strong induction is <q>stronger</q> because the inductive hypothesis is stronger.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:9
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Suppose you are trying to prove, by strong induction, that a statement <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 0</m>.  What would you attempt to prove in the <em>induction step</em> of the proof?  (Select all that apply.)\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          That assuming <m>P(j)</m> is true for all <m>j \\le k</m>, for an arbitrary <m>k \\ge 0</m>, we can prove that <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:23
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          That <m>(P(0) \\wedge P(1) \\wedge \\cdots \\wedge P(k))</m> implies <m>P(k+1)</m> for all <m>k \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:30
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          That assuming <m>P(k+1)</m> is true for an arbitrary <m>k \\ge 0</m>, we can prove that <m>P(j)</m> is true for all <m>j \\le k</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:37
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          That <m>P(k+1)</m> implies <m>P(j)</m> for all <m>j \\le k</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><choices><choice><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:44
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          That <m>P(k-2)</m> implies <m>P(k+1)</m> for at least one <m>k \\ge 0</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:54
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A simpler version of the chocolate bar problem is as follows:  Suppose you have a chocolate bar that is <m>n</m> squares long.  You can break the chocolate bar into two pieces by making a single straight break across the bar.  No matter where you make the breaks, you will break the chocolate bar into <m>n</m> pieces by making <m>n-1</m> breaks.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Arrange some of the following statements in the correct order to form a proof of this claim by strong induction.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement that a chocolate bar that is <m>n</m> squares long can be broken into <m>n</m> pieces by making <m>n-1</m> breaks.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:68
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        <m>P(1)</m> is true because a chocolate bar that is 1 square long is already in one piece.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Assume that <m>P(j)</m> is true for all <m>j \\le k</m> for an arbitrary <m>k \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:78
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider a chocolate bar that is <m>k+1</m> squares long.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:83
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Anywhere you break this bar will result in two smaller bars, say of length <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>. \n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:88
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Since <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are no more than <m>k</m>, it will be possible to break these smaller bars into single squares using <m>a-1</m> and <m>b-1</m> breaks, respectively.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:93
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The total number of breaks is therefore <m>a-1 + b-1 + 1 = a+b -1</m>, which is <m>k+1-1 = k</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><blocks><block><p>
#: source/practice/seq-strong-induction.ptx:98
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore, by the principle of strong induction, <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:11
#, no-wrap
