#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:46
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove that every positive integer is either a power of 2 or can be written as the sum of distinct powers of 2.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:51
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        The proof will be by strong induction.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:56
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement,\n"
"          <q><m>n</m> is either a power of 2 or can be written as the sum of distinct powers of 2.</q>\n"
"          We will show that <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:62
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Base case: <m>1 = 2^0</m> is a power of 2, so <m>P(1)</m> is true.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:66
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Inductive case: Suppose <m>P(k)</m> is true for all <m>k \\lt n</m>.\n"
"          Now if <m>n</m> is a power of 2, we are done.\n"
"          If not, let <m>2^x</m> be the largest power of 2 strictly less than <m>n</m>.\n"
"          Consider <m>n - 2^x</m>,\n"
"          which is a smaller number, in fact smaller than both <m>n</m> and <m>2^x</m>.\n"
"          Thus <m>n-2^x</m> is either a power of 2 or can be written as the sum of distinct powers of 2, but none of them are going to be <m>2^x</m>,\n"
"          so together with <m>2^x</m> we have written <m>n</m> as the sum of distinct powers of 2.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><proof><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:76
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Therefore, by the principle of (strong) mathematical induction,\n"
"          <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:88
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove, using strong induction,\n"
"        that every natural number is either a Fibonacci number or can be written as the <em>sum</em>\n"
"        of <em>distinct</em> Fibonacci numbers.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:95
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        As with the previous question, we will want to subtract something from <m>n</m> in the inductive step.  There we subtracted the largest power of 2 less than <m>n</m>.  So what should you subtract here?\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:98
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Note that you will still need to take care here that the sum you get from the inductive hypothesis, together with the number you subtracted, will be a sum of <em>distinct</em> Fibonacci numbers.  In fact, you could prove that the Fibonacci numbers in the sum are non-consecutive!\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        We have previously proved that for any tree, the number of edges is always one less than the number of vertices.  That is, a tree with <m>v</m> vertices and <m>e</m> edges satisfies <m>v = e+1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:111
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Give an alternate proof of this fact using strong induction on the number of vertices.  Do so by taking a non-leaf vertex and <q>splitting</q> it into two vertices, each belonging to a separate tree.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:120
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose that a particular real number <m>x</m> has the property that <m>x + \\frac{1}{x}</m> is an integer.\n"
"        Prove that <m>x^n + \\frac{1}{x^n}</m> is an integer for all natural numbers <m>n</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-strong-induction.ptx:126
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        You will need to use strong induction.\n"
"        For the inductive case, try multiplying <m>\\left (x^k + \\frac{1}{x^{k}}\\right)\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)</m>, and collect which terms together are integers.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

