#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-conc.ptx:37
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In this chapter, we explored sequences and mathematical induction. At first, these might not seem entirely related, but there is a link: recursive reasoning. When we have many cases (maybe infinitely many), it is often easier to describe a particular case by saying how it relates to other cases, instead of describing it from scratch. For sequences, we can describe the <m>n</m>th term in the sequence by saying how it is related to the <em>previous</em> term. When showing a statement involving the variable <m>n</m> is true for all values of <m>n</m>, we can describe why the case for <m>n = k</m> is true based on why the case for <m>n = k-1</m> is true.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-conc.ptx:41
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      While thinking of problems recursively is often easier than thinking of them absolutely\n"
"      (at least after you get used to thinking in this way),\n"
"      our ultimate goal is to move beyond this recursive description.\n"
"      For sequences, we want to find <em>closed formulas</em>\n"
"      for the <m>n</m>th term of the sequence.\n"
"      For proofs, we want to know that the statement is true for a particular <m>n</m>\n"
"      (not only under the assumption that the statement is true for the previous value of <m>n</m>).\n"
"      In this chapter, we saw some methods for moving from recursive descriptions to absolute descriptions.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-conc.ptx:55
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            If the terms of a sequence increase by a constant difference or constant ratio\n"
"            (these are both recursive descriptions),\n"
"            then the sequence is arithmetic or geometric, respectively,\n"
"            and we have closed formulas for each of these based on the initial terms and common difference or ratio.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-conc.ptx:64
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            If the terms of a sequence increase at a polynomial rate\n"
"            (that is, if the differences between terms form a sequence with a polynomial closed formula),\n"
"            then the sequence is given by a polynomial closed formula\n"
"            (of degree one more than the sequence of differences).\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-conc.ptx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            If the terms of a sequence increase at an exponential rate,\n"
"            then we expect the closed formula for the sequence to be exponential.\n"
"            These sequences often have relatively nice recursive formulas,\n"
"            and the <em>characteristic root technique</em>\n"
"            allows us to find the closed formula for these sequences.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_seq-conc.ptx:83
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            If we want to prove that a statement is true for all values of <m>n</m>\n"
"            (greater than some first small value),\n"
"            and we can describe why the statement for <m>n = k</m> implies the statement for <m>n = k+1</m>,\n"
"            then the <em>principle of mathematical induction</em>\n"
"            gives us that the statement is true for all values of <m>n</m>\n"
"            (greater than the base case).\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_seq-conc.ptx:95
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Throughout the chapter we tried to understand <em>why</em>\n"
"      these facts listed above are true.\n"
"      In part, that is what proofs,\n"
"      by induction or not, attempt to accomplish:\n"
"      They explain why mathematical truths are, in fact, truths.\n"
"      As we develop our ability to reason about mathematics,\n"
"      it is a good idea to make sure that the methods of our reasoning are sound.\n"
"      The branch of mathematics that deals with deciding whether reasoning is good or not is\n"
"      <em>mathematical logic</em>,\n"
"      the subject of the next chapter.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

