msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:298
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove the following statements by mathematical induction:\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:301
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>n! \\lt n^n</m> for <m>n \\ge 2</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:304
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            <m>\\d\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2} + \\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3} +\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}+\\cdots + \\frac{1}{n\\cdot(n+1)} = \\d\\frac{n}{n+1}</m> for all <m>n \\in \\Z^+</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:308
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            <m>4^n - 1</m> is a multiple of 3 for all <m>n \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:313
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <em>greatest</em> amount of postage you <em>cannot</em>\n"
"              make exactly using 4 and 9 cent stamps is 23 cents.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:320
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Every even number squared is divisible by 4.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:331
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>(n+1)^{n+1} &gt; (n+1) \\cdot n^{n}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:337
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              This should be similar to the other sum proofs.\n"
"              The last bit comes down to adding fractions.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:344
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Write <m>4^{k+1} - 1 = 4\\cdot 4^k - 4 + 3</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:350
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              One 9-cent stamp is 1 more than two 4-cent stamps,\n"
"              and seven 4-cent stamps is 1 more than three 9-cent stamps.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:357
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Be careful to actually use induction here.\n"
"              The base case: <m>2^2 = 4</m>.\n"
"              The inductive case:\n"
"              Assume <m>(2n)^2</m> is divisible by 4, and consider <m>(2n+2)^2 = (2n)^2 + 4n + 4</m>.\n"
"              This is divisible by 4 because <m>4n +4</m> clearly is,\n"
"              and by our inductive hypothesis, so is <m>(2n)^2</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:373
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove <m>1^3 + 2^3 + 3^3 + \\cdots + n^3 = \\left(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\right)^2</m> holds for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>,\n"
"        by mathematical induction.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        This is a straight-forward induction proof.\n"
"        Note that you will need to simplify\n"
"        <m>\\left(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\right)^2 + (n+1)^3</m> and get <m>\\left(\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}\\right)^2</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:389
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Suppose <m>a_0 = 1</m>,\n"
"        <m>a_1 = 1</m> and <m>a_n = 3a_{n-1} - 2a_{n-2}</m>.\n"
"        Prove, using strong induction,\n"
"        that <m>a_n = 1</m> for all <m>n</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:397
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        There are two base cases <m>P(0)</m> and <m>P(1)</m>.\n"
"        Then, for the inductive case,\n"
"        assume <m>P(k)</m> is true for all <m>k \\lt n</m>.\n"
"        This allows you to assume <m>a_{n-1} = 1</m> and <m>a_{n-2} = 1</m>.\n"
"        Apply the recurrence relation.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:412
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Prove using induction that every set containing <m>n</m> elements has <m>2^n</m> different subsets for any <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:417
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Let <m>P(n)</m> be the statement,\n"
"        <q>Every set containing <m>n</m> elements has <m>2^n</m> different subsets.</q>\n"
"        We will show <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><u>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:421
#, no-wrap
msgid "Base case"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:421
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
": Any set with 1 element <m>\\{a\\}</m> has exactly 2 subsets:\n"
"        the empty set and the set itself.\n"
"        Thus the number of subsets is <m>2= 2^1</m>.\n"
"        Thus <m>P(1)</m> is true.\n"
"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><u>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:426
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inductive case"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/seq-conc.ptx:426
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
": Suppose <m>P(k)</m> is true for some arbitrary <m>k \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"        Thus every set containing exactly <m>k</m> elements has <m>2^k</m> different subsets.\n"
"        Now consider a set containing <m>k+1</m> elements:\n"
"        <m>A = \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_k, a_{k+1}\\}</m>.\n"
"        Any subset of <m>A</m> must either contain <m>a_{k+1}</m> or not.\n"
"        In other words, a subset of <m>A</m> is just a subset of\n"
"        <m>\\{a_1, a_2,\\ldots,\n"
"        a_k\\}</m> with or without <m>a_{k+1}</m>.\n"
"        Thus there are <m>2^k</m> subsets of <m>A</m> which contain <m>a_{k+1}</m> and another <m>2^{k+1}</m> subsets of <m>A</m> which do not contain <m>a^{k+1}</m>.\n"
"        This gives a total of <m>2^k + 2^k = 2\\cdot 2^k = 2^{k+1}</m> subsets of <m>A</m>.\n"
"        But our choice of <m>A</m> was arbitrary,\n"
"        so this works for any subset containing <m>k+1</m> elements,\n"
"        so <m>P(k+1)</m> is true.\n"
"        Therefore, by the principle of mathematical induction,\n"
"        <m>P(n)</m> is true for all <m>n \\ge 1</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <chapter><title>
#: source/ch_structures.ptx:4
#, no-wrap
msgid "Discrete Structures Revisited"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <chapter><introduction><p>
#: source/ch_structures.ptx:8
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Throughout the previous chapters, we have seen many examples of discrete structures and their properties and uses.  The following sections are  self-contained overviews of each of the main structures we have explored. These can either be studied prior to other material in the book or as a review and reference after the fact. \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:7
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Note that this section contains a lot of detail on what we can say about sets.  If you have never seen anything about unions or Venn diagrams, it is worth reading this carefully.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:10
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The most fundamental objects we will use in our studies\n"
"      (and really in all of math)\n"
"      are\n"
"      <em>sets</em>.\n"
"      Much of what follows might be review,\n"
"      but it is very important that you are fluent in the language of set theory.\n"
"      Most of the notation we use below is standard,\n"
"      although some might be a little different than what you have seen before.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:21
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      For us, a <term>set</term> \n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:23
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      will simply be an unordered collection of objects.\n"
"      Two examples:\n"
"      We could consider the set of all actors who have played\n"
"      <em>The Doctor</em> on <em>Doctor Who</em>,\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:28
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      or the set of natural numbers between 1 and 10 inclusive.\n"
"      In the first case, Tom Baker is an element\n"
"      (or member)\n"
"      of the set, while Idris Elba,\n"
"      among many others, is not an element of the set.\n"
"      Also, the two examples are of different sets.\n"
"      Two sets are equal exactly if they contain the exact same elements.\n"
"      For example,\n"
"      the set containing all of the vowels in the Declaration of Independence is precisely the same set as the set of vowels in the word\n"
"      <q>questionably</q> (namely, all of them);\n"
"      we do not care about order or repetitions,\n"
"      just whether the element is in the set or not.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:44
#, no-wrap
msgid "Notation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We need some notation to make talking about sets easier.\n"
"      Consider,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:55
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This is read,\n"
"      <q><m>A</m> is the set containing the elements 1, 2, and 3.</q>\n"
"      We use curly braces <q><m>\\{,~~ \\}</m></q>\n"
"      to enclose elements of a set.\n"
"      Some more notation:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:66
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The symbol <q><m>\\in</m></q> is read <q>is in</q> or\n"
"      <q>is an element of.</q>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Thus the above means that <m>a</m> is an element of the set containing the letters <m>a</m>,\n"
"      <m>b</m>,\n"
"      and <m>c</m>.\n"
"      Note that this is a true statement.\n"
"      It would also be true to say that <m>d</m> is not in that set:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Be warned: We write <q><m>x \\in A</m></q>\n"
"      when we wish to express that one of the elements of the set <m>A</m> is <m>x</m>.\n"
"      For example, consider the set,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:89
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This is a strange set, to be sure.\n"
"      It contains four elements:\n"
"      the number 1, the letter b, the set <m>\\{x,y,z\\}</m>, and the empty set\n"
"      <m>\\emptyset = \\{ \\}</m>, the set containing no elements.\n"
"      Is <m>x</m> in <m>A</m>?\n"
"      The answer is no.\n"
"      None of the four elements in <m>A</m> are the letter <m>x</m>,\n"
"      so we must conclude that <m>x \\notin A</m>.\n"
"      Similarly, consider the set <m>B = \\{1,b\\}</m>.\n"
"      Even though the elements of <m>B</m> are elements of <m>A</m>,\n"
"      we cannot say that the <em>set</em>\n"
"      <m>B</m> is one of the elements of <m>A</m>.\n"
"      Therefore <m>B \\notin A</m>.\n"
"      (Soon we will see that <m>B</m> is a\n"
"      <em>subset</em> of <m>A</m>,\n"
"      but this is different from being an\n"
"      <em>element</em> of <m>A</m>.)\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:109
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We have described the sets above by listing their elements.\n"
"      Sometimes this is hard to do,\n"
"      especially when there are a lot of elements in the set\n"
"      (perhaps infinitely many).\n"
"      For instance,\n"
"      if we want <m>A</m> to be the set of all even natural numbers,\n"
"      would could write,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"      but this is a little imprecise.\n"
"      A better way would be\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Let's look at this carefully.  First, there are some new symbols to digest: <q><m>\\N</m></q> is the symbol usually used to denote the <term>natural numbers</term>, which we will take to be the set <m>\\{0, 1, 2, 3, \\ldots\\}</m>.   Next, the colon, <q>:</q>, is read <em>such that</em>; it separates the elements that are in the set from the condition that the elements in the set must satisfy.\n"
"      \n"
"      So putting this all together, we would read the set as, <q>the set of all <m>x</m> in the natural numbers, such that there exists some <m>n</m> in the natural numbers for which <m>x</m> is twice <m>n</m>.</q> In other words, the set of all natural numbers that are even.  Here is another way to write the same set. \n"
"      \n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:137
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Note: Sometimes mathematicians use <m>|</m> or\n"
"      <m>\\backepsilon</m> for the <q>such that</q>\n"
"      symbol instead of the colon.\n"
"      Also, there is a fairly even split between mathematicians about whether <m>0</m> is an element of the natural numbers, so be careful there.  \n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:145
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This notation is usually called <term>set builder notation</term>.\n"
"      It tells us how to <em>build</em> a set by telling us precisely the condition elements must meet to gain access\n"
"      (the condition is the logical statement after the\n"
"      <q><m>\\st</m></q> symbol).  Reading and comprehending sets written in this way takes practice.\n"
"      Here are some more examples:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:155
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Describe each of the following sets both in words and by listing out enough elements to see the pattern.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{x \\st x + 3 \\in \\N\\}</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:162
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{x \\in \\N \\st x + 3 \\in \\N\\}</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:164
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{x \\st x \\in \\N \\text{ or } -x \\in \\N\\}</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:166
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{x \\st x \\in \\N \\text{ and } -x \\in \\N\\}</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:175
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                This is the set of all numbers that are 3 less than a natural number (i.e., that if you add 3 to them,\n"
"                you get a natural number).\n"
"                The set could also be written as <m>\\{-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, \\ldots\\}</m>\n"
"                (note that 0 is a natural number, so\n"
"                <m>-3</m> is in this set because <m>-3 + 3 = 0</m>).\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:185
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                This is the set of all natural numbers that are 3 less than a natural number.\n"
"                So here we just have <m>\\{0, 1, 2,3 \\ldots\\}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:192
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                This is the set of all integers\n"
"                    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:195
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                (positive and negative whole numbers, written <m>\\Z</m>).\n"
"                In other words, <m>\\{\\ldots, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, \\ldots\\}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:202
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Here we want all numbers <m>x</m> such that <m>x</m> and <m>-x</m> are natural numbers.\n"
"                There is only one: 0.\n"
"                So we have the set <m>\\{0\\}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:213
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      There is also a subtle variation on set builder notation.  While the condition is generally given after the <q>such that</q>, sometimes it is hidden in the first part.  Here is an example.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:219
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          List a few elements in the sets below and describe them in words.  The set <m>\\Z</m> is the set of <term>integers</term>; positive and negative whole numbers.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:223
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>A = \\{x \\in \\Z \\st x^2 \\in \\N\\}</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:228
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>B = \\{x^2 \\st x \\in \\N\\}</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:239
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                The set of integers that pass the condition that their square is a natural number.  Well, every integer, when you square it, gives you a non-negative integer, so a natural number.  Thus <m>A = \\Z = \\{\\ldots, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, \\ldots\\}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Here we are looking for the set of all <m>x^2</m>s where <m>x</m> is a natural number.  So this set is simply the set of perfect squares.  <m>B = \\{0, 1, 4, 9, 16, \\ldots\\}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:248
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Another way we could have written this set, using more strict set builder notation, would be as <m>B = \\{x \\in \\N \\st x = n^2 \\text{ for some } n \\in \\N\\}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:257
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We already have a lot of notation, and there is more yet.\n"
"      Below is a handy chart of symbols.\n"
"      Some of these will be discussed in greater detail as we move forward.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:263
#, no-wrap
msgid "Special sets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:269
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>empty set</term> is the set that contains no elements.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:273
#, no-wrap
msgid "the empty set"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:295 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:301
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\N</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:297
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The set of natural numbers.\n"
"              That is, <m>\\N = \\{0, 1, 2, 3\\ldots\\}</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:302
#, no-wrap
msgid "the set of natural numbers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:312 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:320
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\Z</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:314
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The set of\n"
"              integers.\n"
"              That is, <m>\\Z = \\{\\ldots, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, \\ldots\\}</m>.\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:321
#, no-wrap
msgid "the set of integers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:328 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:333
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\Q</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:330
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The set of rational numbers.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:334
#, no-wrap
msgid "the set of rational numbers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:344 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:348
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\R</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:346
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The set of real numbers.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:349
#, no-wrap
msgid "the set of real numbers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:358 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:363
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\pow(A)</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:360
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>power set</term> of any set <m>A</m> is the set of all subsets of <m>A</m>.\n"
"                  "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:364
#, no-wrap
msgid "the power set of <m>A</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:367
#, no-wrap
msgid "power set"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:375
#, no-wrap
msgid "Set Theory Notation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:379 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:385
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{, \\}</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:381
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              We use these <term>braces</term> to enclose the elements of a set.\n"
"              So <m>\\{1,2,3\\}</m> is the set containing 1, 2, and 3.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:386
#, no-wrap
msgid "braces, to contain set elements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:393 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:399
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\st</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:395
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>\\{x \\st x &gt; 2\\}</m> is the set of all <m>x</m> <term>such that</term>\n"
"              <m>x</m> is greater than 2.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:400
#, no-wrap
msgid "<q>such that</q>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:407 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:414
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\in</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:409
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>2 \\in \\{1,2,3\\}</m> asserts that 2 is\n"
"              <term>an element of</term>\n"
"              the set <m>\\{1,2,3\\}</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:415
#, no-wrap
msgid "<q>is an element of</q>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><title>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:422
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\not\\in</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:424
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>4 \\notin \\{1,2,3\\}</m> because 4\n"
"              <term>is not an element of</term>\n"
"              the set <m>\\{1,2,3\\}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:431 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:438
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\subseteq</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:433
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\subseteq B</m> asserts that\n"
"              <term><m>A</m> is a subset of <m>B</m></term>:\n"
"              Every element of <m>A</m> is also an element of <m>B</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:439
#, no-wrap
msgid "<q>is a subset of</q>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:446 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:454
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\subset</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:448
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\subset B</m> asserts that\n"
"              <term><m>A</m> is a proper subset of <m>B</m></term>:\n"
"              Every element of <m>A</m> is also an element of <m>B</m>,\n"
"              but <m>A \\ne B</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:455
#, no-wrap
msgid "<q>is a proper subset of</q>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:462 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:468
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\cap</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:464
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\cap B</m> is the <term>intersection of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></term>:\n"
"              the set containing all elements that are elements of both <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:469
#, no-wrap
msgid "set intersection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:478 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:484
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\cup</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:480
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\cup B</m> is the <term>union of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></term>:\n"
"              the set containing all elements that are elements of <m>A</m> or <m>B</m> or both.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:485
#, no-wrap
msgid "set union"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:494 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:500
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\times</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:496
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\times B</m> is the <term>Cartesian product of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></term>:\n"
"              the set of all ordered pairs <m>(a,b)</m> with <m>a \\in A</m> and <m>b \\in B</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:501 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:504
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cartesian product"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:509 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:515
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\setminus</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:511
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<m>A \\setminus B</m> is <term>set difference between <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></term>:\n"
"              the set containing all elements of <m>A</m> that are not elements of <m>B</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:516
#, no-wrap
msgid "set difference"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:524 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:530
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\bar{A}</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:526
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>complement of <m>A</m></term>\n"
"              is the set of everything that is not an element of <m>A</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:531
#, no-wrap
msgid "the complement of <m>A</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:539 source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:546
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\card{A}</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:541
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>cardinality\n"
"              (or size)\n"
"              of <m>A</m></term> is the number of elements in <m>A</m>.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p><dl><li><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:547
#, no-wrap
msgid "cardinality (size) of <m>A</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:564
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Find the cardinality of each set below.\n"
"\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:567
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>A = \\{3,4,\\ldots, 15\\}</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:569
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>B = \\{n \\in \\N \\st 2 \\lt n \\le 200\\}</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:572
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  <m>C = \\{n \\le 100 \\st n \\in \\N \\text{ and for some } m \\in \\N, (n = 2m+1)\\}</m>.\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:580
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Find two sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> for which <m>|A| = 5</m>,\n"
"              <m>|B| = 6</m>, and <m>|A\\cup B| = 9</m>.\n"
"              What is <m>|A \\cap B|</m>?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:588
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Find sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> with <m>|A| = |B|</m> such that\n"
"              <m>|A\\cup B| = 7</m> and <m>|A \\cap B| = 3</m>.\n"
"              What is <m>|A|</m>?\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:596
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Let <m>A = \\{1,2,\\ldots, 10\\}</m>.\n"
"              Define <m>\\mathcal{B}_2 = \\{B \\subseteq A \\st |B| = 2\\}</m>.\n"
"              Find <m>|\\mathcal{B}_2|</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><investigation><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:603
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            For any sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>,\n"
"            define <m>AB = \\{ab \\st a\\in A \\text{ and } b \\in B\\}</m>.\n"
"            If <m>A = \\{1,2\\}</m> and <m>B = \\{2,3,4\\}</m>, what is <m>|AB|</m>?\n"
"            What is <m>|A \\times B|</m>?\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:614
#, no-wrap
msgid "Relationships between Sets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:617
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We have already said what it means for two sets to be equal:\n"
"      They have exactly the same elements.\n"
"      Thus, for example,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:626
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      (Remember, the order the elements are written down in does not matter.) Also,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:630
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      since these are all ways to write the set containing the first three positive integers\n"
"      (how we write them doesn't matter, just what they are).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:635
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      What about the sets <m>A = \\{1, 2, 3\\}</m> and <m>B = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}</m>?\n"
"      Clearly <m>A \\ne B</m>,\n"
"      but notice that every element of <m>A</m> is also an element of <m>B</m>.\n"
"      Because of this we say that <m>A</m> is a <em>subset</em>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:640
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      of <m>B</m>, or in symbols,\n"
"      <m>A \\subset B</m> or <m>A \\subseteq B</m>.\n"
"      Both symbols are read <q>is a subset of.</q>\n"
"      The difference is that sometimes we want to say that <m>A</m> is either equal to or is a subset of <m>B</m>,\n"
"      in which case we use <m>\\subseteq</m>.\n"
"      This is analogous to the difference between <m>\\lt</m> and <m>\\le</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:651
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>A = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}</m>,\n"
"          <m>B = \\{2, 4, 6\\}</m>, <m>C = \\{1, 2, 3\\}</m>, and <m>D = \\{7, 8, 9\\}</m>.\n"
"          Determine which of the following are true, false, or meaningless.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:658
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>A \\subset B</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:660
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>B \\subset A</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:662
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>B \\in C</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:664
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\emptyset \\in A</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:666
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\emptyset \\subset A</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:668
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>A \\lt D</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:670
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>3 \\in C</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:672
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>3 \\subset C</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:674
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\{3\\} \\subset C</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:683
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                False.\n"
"                For example, <m>1\\in A</m> but <m>1 \\notin B</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:690
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                True.\n"
"                Every element in <m>B</m> is an element in <m>A</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:697
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                False.\n"
"                The elements in <m>C</m> are 1, 2, and 3.\n"
"                The <em>set</em> <m>B</m> is not equal to 1, 2, or 3.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:705
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                False. <m>A</m> has exactly 6 elements,\n"
"                and none of them are the empty set.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:712
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                True.\n"
"                Everything in the empty set (nothing) is also an element of <m>A</m>.\n"
"                Notice that the empty set is a subset of every set.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:720
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Meaningless.\n"
"                A set cannot be less than another set.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:727
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                True. <m>3</m> is one of the elements of the set <m>C</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:733
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Meaningless. <m>3</m> is not a set,\n"
"                so it cannot be a subset of another set.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:740
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                True. <m>3</m> is the only element of the set <m>\\{3\\}</m>,\n"
"                and is an element of <m>C</m>,\n"
"                so every element in <m>\\{3\\}</m> is an element of <m>C</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:751
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In the example above, <m>B</m> is a subset of <m>A</m>.\n"
"      You might wonder what other sets are subsets of <m>A</m>.\n"
"      If you collect all these subsets of <m>A</m> into a new set,\n"
"      we get a set of sets.\n"
"      We call the set of all subsets of <m>A</m> the <term>power set</term>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:757
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      of <m>A</m>, and write it <m>\\pow(A)</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:763
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>A = \\{1,2,3\\}</m>.\n"
"          Find <m>\\pow(A)</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:769
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>\\pow(A)</m> is a set of sets,\n"
"          all of which are subsets of <m>A</m>.\n"
"          So\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:778
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Notice that while <m>2 \\in A</m>,\n"
"          it is wrong to write <m>2 \\in \\pow(A)</m> since none of the elements in <m>\\pow(A)</m> are numbers!\n"
"          On the other hand, we do have\n"
"          <m>\\{2\\} \\in \\pow(A)</m> because <m>\\{2\\} \\subseteq A</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:785
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          What does a subset of <m>\\pow(A)</m> look like?\n"
"          Notice that <m>\\{2\\} \\not\\subseteq \\pow(A)</m> because not everything in <m>\\{2\\}</m> is in <m>\\pow(A)</m>.\n"
"          But we do have <m>\\{ \\{2\\} \\} \\subseteq \\pow(A)</m>.\n"
"          The only element of <m>\\{\\{2\\}\\}</m> is the set <m>\\{2\\}</m>, which is also an element of <m>\\pow(A)</m>.\n"
"          We could take the collection of all subsets of <m>\\pow(A)</m> and call that <m>\\pow(\\pow(A))</m>.\n"
"          Or even the power set of that set of sets of sets.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:796
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Another way to compare sets is by their <em>size</em>.\n"
"      Notice that in the example above,\n"
"      <m>A</m> has 6 elements, and <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>,\n"
"      and <m>D</m> all have 3 elements.\n"
"      The size of a set is called the set's\n"
"      <term>cardinality</term>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:803
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We would write <m>|A| = 6</m>,\n"
"      <m>|B| = 3</m>, and so on.\n"
"      For sets that have a finite number of elements,\n"
"      the cardinality of the set is simply the number of elements in the set.\n"
"      Note that the cardinality of <m>\\{ 1, 2, 3, 2, 1\\}</m> is 3.\n"
"      We do not count repeats\n"
"      (in fact, <m>\\{1, 2, 3, 2, 1\\}</m> is exactly the same set as <m>\\{1, 2, 3\\}</m>).\n"
"      There are sets with infinite cardinality,\n"
"      such as <m>\\N</m>, the set of rational numbers\n"
"      (written <m>\\mathbb Q</m>),\n"
"      the set of even natural numbers,\n"
"      and the set of real numbers\n"
"      (<m>\\mathbb R</m>).\n"
"      It is possible to distinguish between different infinite cardinalities,\n"
"      but that is beyond the scope of this text.\n"
"      For us, a set will either be infinite or finite;\n"
"      if it is finite,\n"
"      then we can determine its cardinality by counting elements.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:831
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Find the cardinality of <m>A = \\{23, 24, \\ldots, 37, 38\\}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:837
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Find the cardinality of <m>B = \\{1, \\{2, 3, 4\\}, \\emptyset\\}</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:843
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                If <m>C = \\{1,2,3\\}</m>, what is the cardinality of <m>\\pow(C)</m>?\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:854
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Since <m>38 - 23 = 15</m>,\n"
"                we can conclude that the cardinality of the set is <m>|A| = 16</m>\n"
"                (you need to add one since 23 is included).\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:862
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Here <m>|B| = 3</m>.\n"
"                The three elements are the number 1, the set <m>\\{2,3,4\\}</m>,\n"
"                and the empty set.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:870
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                We wrote out the elements of the power set <m>\\pow(C)</m> above,\n"
"                and there are 8 elements\n"
"                (each of which is a set).\n"
"                So <m>\\card{\\pow(C)} = 8</m>. (You might wonder if there is a relationship between <m>\\card{A}</m> and\n"
"                <m>\\card{\\pow(A)}</m> for all sets <m>A</m>.\n"
"                This is a good question that we explore in <xref po4a-id=0>.)\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:885
#, no-wrap
msgid "Operations on Sets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:889
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Is it possible to add two sets?\n"
"      Not really, however there is something similar.\n"
"      If we want to combine two sets to get the collection of objects that are in either set,\n"
"      then we can take the <term>union</term>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:894
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      of the two sets.\n"
"      Symbolically,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:898
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
",\n"
"      read, <q><m>C</m> is the union of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>,</q>\n"
"      means that the elements of <m>C</m> are exactly the elements that are either an element of <m>A</m> or an element of <m>B</m>\n"
"      (or an element of both).\n"
"      For example,\n"
"      if <m>A = \\{1, 2, 3\\}</m> and <m>B = \\{2, 3, 4\\}</m>,\n"
"      then <m>A \\cup B = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:908
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The other common operation on sets is <term>intersection</term>.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:910
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We write,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:914
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      and say,\n"
"      <q><m>C</m> is the intersection of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>,</q>\n"
"      when the elements in <m>C</m> are precisely those both in <m>A</m> and in <m>B</m>.\n"
"      So if <m>A = \\{1, 2, 3\\}</m> and <m>B = \\{2, 3, 4\\}</m>,\n"
"      then <m>A \\cap B = \\{2, 3\\}</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:922
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Often when dealing with sets,\n"
"      we will have some understanding as to what <q>everything</q> is.\n"
"      Perhaps we are only concerned with natural numbers.\n"
"      In this case we would say that our\n"
"      <term>universe</term> is <m>\\N</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:928
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Sometimes we denote this universe by <m>\\U</m>.\n"
"      Given this context,\n"
"      we might wish to speak of all the elements that are\n"
"      <em>not</em> in a particular set.\n"
"      We say <m>B</m> is the <term>complement</term>\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:934
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      of <m>A</m>, and write,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:938
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      when <m>B</m> contains every element not contained in <m>A</m>.\n"
"      So, if our universe is <m>\\{1, 2,\\ldots, 9, 10\\}</m>,\n"
"      and <m>A = \\{2, 3, 5, 7\\}</m>,\n"
"      then <m>\\bar A = \\{1, 4, 6, 8, 9,10\\}</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:945
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Of course we can perform more than one operation at a time.\n"
"      For example, consider\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:953
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This is the set of all elements that are both elements of <m>A</m> and not elements of <m>B</m>.\n"
"      What have we done?\n"
"      We've started with <m>A</m> and removed all of the elements that were in <m>B</m>.\n"
"      Another way to write this is the\n"
"      <term>set difference</term>:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:966
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      It is important to remember that these operations (union,\n"
"      intersection,\n"
"      complement, and difference) on sets produce other sets.\n"
"      Don't confuse these with the symbols from the previous section\n"
"      (element of and subset of). <m>A \\cap B</m> is a set,\n"
"      while <m>A \\subseteq B</m> is true or false.\n"
"      This is the same difference as between <m>3 + 2</m>\n"
"      (which is a number)\n"
"      and <m>3 \\le 2</m>\n"
"      (which is false).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:981
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>A = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}</m>,\n"
"          <m>B = \\{2, 4, 6\\}</m>, <m>C = \\{1, 2, 3\\}</m>, and <m>D = \\{7, 8, 9\\}</m>.\n"
"          If the universe is <m>\\U = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 10\\}</m>, find:\n"
"\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:986
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>A \\cup B</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:988
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>A \\cap B</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:990
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>B \\cap C</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:992
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>A \\cap D</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:994
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\bar{B \\cup C}</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:996
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>A \\setminus B</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:998
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>(D \\cap \\bar C) \\cup \\bar{A \\cap B}</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1000
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\emptyset \\cup C</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1002
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\emptyset \\cap C</m>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1010
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\cup B = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\} = A</m> since everything in <m>B</m> is already in <m>A</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1014
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\cap B = \\{2, 4, 6\\} = B</m> since everything in <m>B</m> is in <m>A</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1018
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>B \\cap C = \\{2\\}</m> as the only element of both <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> is 2.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1022
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\cap D = \\emptyset</m> since <m>A</m> and <m>D</m> have no common elements.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1026
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>\\bar{B \\cup C} = \\{5, 7, 8, 9, 10\\}</m>.\n"
"              First we find that <m>B \\cup C = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 6\\}</m>, and\n"
"              then we take everything not in that set.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1032
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>A \\setminus B = \\{1, 3, 5\\}</m> since the elements 1, 3, and 5 are in <m>A</m> but not in <m>B</m>.\n"
"              This is the same as <m>A \\cap \\bar B</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1037
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>(D \\cap \\bar C) \\cup \\bar{A \\cap B} = \\{1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10\\}</m>.\n"
"              The set contains all elements that are either in <m>D</m> but not in <m>C</m> (i.e., <m>\\{7,8,9\\}</m>),\n"
"              or not in both <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> (i.e., <m>\\{1,3,5,7,8,9,10\\}</m>).\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1043
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>\\emptyset \\cup C = C</m> since nothing is added by the empty set.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1047
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>\\emptyset \\cap C = \\emptyset</m> since nothing can be both in a set and in the empty set.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1055
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Having notation like this is useful.  We will often want to add or remove elements from sets, and our notation allows us to do so precisely.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1061
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          If <m>A = \\{1,2,3\\}</m>, then we can describe the set we get by adding the number 4 as <m>A \\cup \\{4\\}</m>.  If we want to express the set we get by removing the number 2 from <m>A</m>, we can do so by writing <m>A \\setminus \\{2\\}</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1065
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Careful though.  If you add an element to the set, you get a new set!  So you would have <m>B = A \\cup \\{4\\}</m> and then correctly say that <m>B</m> contains 4, but <m>A</m> does not.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1071
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      There is one more way to combine sets that will be useful for us:\n"
"      the <term>Cartesian product</term>,\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1074
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      <m>A \\times B</m>.\n"
"\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1077
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>A\\times B</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1078
#, no-wrap
msgid "the Cartesian product of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1081
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This sounds fancy but is nothing you haven't seen before.\n"
"      When you graph a function in calculus,\n"
"      you graph it in the Cartesian plane.\n"
"      This is the set of all ordered pairs of real numbers <m>(x,y)</m>.\n"
"      We can do this for <em>any</em> pair of sets,\n"
"      not just the real numbers with themselves.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1090
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Put another way,\n"
"      <m>A \\times B = \\{(a,b) \\st a \\in A \\text{ and } b \\in B\\}</m>.\n"
"      The first coordinate comes from the first set, and the second coordinate comes from the second set.\n"
"      Sometimes we will want to take the Cartesian product of a set with itself,\n"
"      and this is fine:\n"
"      <m>A \\times A = \\{(a,b) \\st a, b \\in A\\}</m>\n"
"      (we might also write <m>A^2</m> for this set).\n"
"      Notice that in <m>A \\times A</m>,\n"
"      we still want <em>all</em> ordered pairs,\n"
"      not just the ones where the first and second coordinate are the same.\n"
"      We can also take products of 3 or more sets,\n"
"      getting ordered triples, or quadruples, and so on.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Let <m>A = \\{1,2\\}</m> and <m>B = \\{3,4,5\\}</m>.\n"
"          Find <m>A \\times B</m> and <m>A \\times A</m>.\n"
"          How many elements do you expect to be in <m>B \\times B</m>?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1114
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>A \\times B = \\{(1,3), (1,4), (1,5), (2,3), (2,4), (2,5)\\}</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>A \\times A = A^2 = \\{(1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2)\\}</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1122
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>|B\\times B| = 9</m>.\n"
"          There will be 3 pairs with first coordinate <m>3</m>,\n"
"          three more with first coordinate <m>4</m>,\n"
"          and a final three with first coordinate <m>5</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1132
#, no-wrap
msgid "Venn Diagrams"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1137
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      There is a very nice visual tool we can use to represent operations on sets.\n"
"      A <term>Venn diagram</term> displays sets as intersecting circles.\n"
"      We can shade the region we are talking about when we carry out an operation.\n"
"      We can also represent the cardinality of a particular set by putting the number in the corresponding region.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1152
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two overlapping circles enclosed in a rectangular box.  Circles labeled A and B."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1163
#, no-wrap
msgid "Three overlapping circles enclosed in a rectangle.  The circle labeled A is in the top left, the circle labeled B is in the top right, the circle labeled C is in the bottom center.  The circles intersect to create seven interior regions: one inside all circles, three inside just a pair of circles, and three inside only one circle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Each circle represents a set.\n"
"      The rectangle containing the circles represents the universe.\n"
"      To represent combinations of these sets,\n"
"      we shade the corresponding region.\n"
"      For example, we could draw <m>A \\cap B</m> as:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1189
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two overlapping circles enclosed in a rectangular box.  Circles labeled A and B. The overlapping region inside both circles is shaded light gray."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1195
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Here is a representation of <m>A \\cap \\bar B</m>,\n"
"      or equivalently <m>A \\setminus B</m>:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1215
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two overlapping circles enclosed in a rectangular box.  Circles labeled A and B. The region inside circle A but outside circle B is shaded light gray."
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1221
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A more complicated example is <m>(B \\cap C) \\cup (C \\cap \\bar A)</m>,\n"
"      as seen below.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Three overlapping circles enclosed in a rectangle.  The circle labeled A is in the top left, the circle labeled B is in the top right, the circle labeled C is in the bottom center.  The circles intersect to create seven interior regions: one inside all circles, three inside just a pair of circles, and three inside only one circle.  Three of the seven regions are shaded light gray: the region in C alone, the region in just C and B, and the center region in all three circles.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-sets.ptx:1251
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Notice that the shaded regions above could also be arrived at in another way.\n"
"      We could have started with all of <m>C</m> and\n"
"      then excluded the region where <m>C</m> and <m>A</m> overlap outside of <m>B</m>.\n"
"      That region is <m>(A \\cap C) \\cap \\bar B</m>.\n"
"      So the above Venn diagram also represents <m>C \\cap \\bar{\\left((A\\cap C)\\cap \\bar B\\right)}</m>.\n"
"      So using just the picture, we have determined that\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:35
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Find the least element of the following sets, if there is one.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:39
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>\\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} : n^2 - <var po4a-id=0> \\ge <var po4a-id=1>\\}</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>\\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} : n^2 - <var po4a-id=0> \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:55
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>\\{n^2+<var po4a-id=0> : n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>\\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} : n = k^2 + <var po4a-id=0> \\text{ for some } k \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}</m>\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><statement><instruction>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:71
#, no-wrap
msgid "(if there is no least element, enter 'none')"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:78
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                This is the set <m>\\{<var po4a-id=0>, <var po4a-id=1>, <var po4a-id=2>, \\ldots\\}</m> since we need each element to be a natural number whose square is at least <var po4a-id=3> more than <var po4a-id=4>. Since <m><var po4a-id=5>^2 - <var po4a-id=6> = <var po4a-id=7></m> but <m><var po4a-id=8>^2 - <var po4a-id=9> = <var po4a-id=10></m> we see that the first such natural number is <var po4a-id=11>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:83
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                We get the same set as the previous part, and the smallest non-negative number for which <m>n^2 - <var po4a-id=0></m> is a natural number is <var po4a-id=1>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:86
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Note that if we didn't specify <m>n \\ge 0</m> by saying that <m>n \\in \\mathbb N</m> then any integer less than <m>-<var po4a-id=0></m> would also be in the set, so there would not be a least element.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:91
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                This is the set <m>\\{<var po4a-id=0>, <var po4a-id=1>, <var po4a-id=2>, <var po4a-id=3>, \\ldots\\}</m>, namely the set of numbers that are the <em>result</em> of squaring and adding <var po4a-id=4> to a natural number. (<m>0^2 + <var po4a-id=5> = <var po4a-id=6></m>, <m>1^2 + <var po4a-id=7> = <var po4a-id=8></m>, <m>2^2 + <var po4a-id=9> = <var po4a-id=10></m> and so on.) Thus the least element of the set is <var po4a-id=11>.  \n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:96
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Now we are looking for natural numbers that are equal to taking some natural number, squaring it and adding <var po4a-id=0>. That is, <m>\\{<var po4a-id=1>, <var po4a-id=2>, <var po4a-id=3>, <var po4a-id=4>, \\ldots\\}</m>, the same set as the previous part. So again, the least element is <var po4a-id=5>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><webwork><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:154
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Find the following cardinalities.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

