msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:762
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Find an example of a set <m>A</m> with <m>|A| = 3</m> which contains only other sets and has the\n"
"        following property: For all sets <m>B \\in A</m>, we also have <m>B \\subseteq A</m>. Explain why\n"
"        your example works. (FYI: Sets that have this property are called <term>transitive</term>.) "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:776
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Consider the sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, where <m>A = \\{3, |B|\\}</m> and <m>B = \\{1, |A|, |B|\\}</m>.\n"
"        What are the sets? "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:780
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" We need to be a little careful here. If <m>B</m>\n"
"        contains 3 elements, then <m>A</m> contains just the number 3 (listed twice). So that would\n"
"        make <m>|A| = 1</m>, which would make <m>B = \\{1, 3\\}</m>, which only has 2 elements. Thus <m>|B| \\ne\n"
"        3</m>. This means that <m>|A| = 2</m>, so <m>B</m> contains at least the elements 1 and 2. Since <m>\\card{B}\n"
"        \\ne 3</m>, we must have <m>\\card{B} = 2</m>, which agrees with the definition of <m>B</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:787
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Therefore it must be that <m>A = \\{2,3\\}</m> and <m>B = \\{1, 2\\}</m>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:795
#, no-wrap
msgid "        Explain why there is no set <m>A</m> which satisfies <m>A = \\{2, \\card{A}\\}</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><hint><p>
#: source/exercises/intro-sets.ptx:799
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        It looks like you should be able to define the set <m>A</m> like this. But consider the two\n"
"        possible values for <m>\\card{A}</m>. "
msgstr ""

