#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:8
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This section contains many details about functions, much of which should be familiar from non-discrete contexts.\n"
"      As you read, consider how the way we talk about functions here might be different from what you know from other areas of mathematics.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:17
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      A <term>function</term> is a rule that assigns each input exactly one output.\n"
"      We call the output the <term> image</term> of the input.\n"
"      The set of all inputs for a function is called the <term>domain</term>.\n"
"      The set of all allowable outputs is called the <term>codomain</term>.\n"
"      We would write <m>f:X \\to Y</m> to describe a function with name <m>f</m>, domain <m>X</m>, and codomain <m>Y</m>.\n"
"      This does not tell us <em>which</em> function <m>f</m> is though.\n"
"      To define the function, we must describe the rule.\n"
"      This is often done by giving a formula to compute the output for any input (although this is certainly not the only way to describe the rule).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:28
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      For example, consider the function <m>f:\\N \\to \\N</m> defined by <m>f(x) = x^2 + 3</m>.\n"
"      Here the domain and codomain are the same set (the natural numbers).\n"
"      The rule: Take your input, multiply it by itself, and add 3.\n"
"      This works because we can apply this rule to every natural number (every element of the domain) and the result is always a natural number (an element of the codomain).\n"
"      Notice though that not every natural number is actually an output (there is no way to get 0, 1, 2, 5, etc.).\n"
"      The set of natural numbers that <em>are</em> outputs is called the <term>range</term>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:35
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      of the function (in this case, the range is <m>\\{3, 4, 7, 12, 19, 28, \\ldots\\}</m>, all the natural numbers that are 3 more than a perfect square).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:39
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The key thing that makes a rule a <em> function</em> is that there is <em>exactly one</em> output for each input.\n"
"      That is, it is important that the rule be a good rule.\n"
"      What output do we assign to the input 7? There can only be one answer for any particular function.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The following are all examples of functions:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:51
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>f:\\Z \\to \\Z</m> defined by <m>f(n) = 3n</m>. The domain and codomain are both the set of integers. However, the range is only the set of integer multiples of 3.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>g: \\{1,2,3\\} \\to \\{a,b,c\\}</m> defined by <m>g(1) = c</m>, <m>g(2) = a</m>, and <m>g(3) = a</m>. The domain is the set <m>\\{1,2,3\\}</m>, the codomain is the set <m>\\{a,b,c\\}</m> and the range is the set <m>\\{a,c\\}</m>. Note that <m>g(2)</m> and <m>g(3)</m> are the same element of the codomain. This is okay since each element in the domain still has only one output.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>h:\\{1,2,3,4\\} \\to \\N</m> defined by the table:\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:72
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:395
#, no-wrap
msgid " 4"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>h(x)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid " 12"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:85
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Here the domain is the finite set <m>\\{1,2,3,4\\}</m>, and the codomain is the set of natural numbers, <m>\\N</m>.\n"
"                At first you might think this function is the same as <m>f</m> defined above.\n"
"                It absolutely is not.\n"
"                Even though the rule is the same, the domain and codomain are different, so these are two different functions.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:99
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Just because you can describe a rule in the same way you would write a function does not mean that the rule is a function.\n"
"          The following are NOT functions.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:104
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>f:\\N \\to \\N</m> defined by <m>f(n) = \\frac{n}{2}</m>. The reason this is not a function is because not every input has an output. Where does <m>f</m> send 3? The rule says that <m>f(3) = \\frac{3}{2}</m>, but <m> \\frac{3}{2}</m> is not an element of the codomain.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><introduction><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:110
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Consider the rule that matches each person to their phone number.\n"
"                If you think of the set of people as the domain and the set of phone numbers as the codomain, then this is not a function, since some people have two phone numbers.\n"
"                Switching the domain and codomain sets doesn't help either, since some phone numbers belong to multiple people (assuming some households still have landlines when you are reading this).\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:123
#, no-wrap
msgid "Describing Functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      It is worth making a distinction between a function and its description.\n"
"      The function is the abstract mathematical object that in some way exists whether or not anyone ever talks about it.\n"
"      But when we <em>do</em> want to talk about the function, we need a way to describe it.\n"
"      A particular function can be described in multiple ways.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><h><em>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:134
#, no-wrap
msgid "rule of four"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:135
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Some calculus textbooks talk about the <em>Rule of Four</em>, that every function can be described in four ways: algebraically (a formula), numerically (a table), graphically, or in words.\n"
"      In discrete math, we can still use any of these to describe functions, but we can also be more specific since we are primarily concerned with functions that have <m>\\N</m> or a finite subset of <m>\\N</m> as their domain.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:141
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Describing a function graphically usually means drawing the graph of the function: plotting the points on the plane.\n"
"      We can do this and might get a graph like the following for a function <m>f:\\{1,2,3\\} \\to \\{1,2,3\\}</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:156
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Graph of the first quadrant, including axes and a rectangular grid.\n"
"        Points on grid at (1,2), (2, 1) and (3,3).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:162
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      It would be absolutely WRONG to connect the dots or try to fit them to some curve.\n"
"      There are only three elements in the domain.\n"
"      A curve would mean that the domain contains an entire interval of real numbers.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:168
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Here is another way to represent that same function:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:181
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Two rows containing the numbers 1, 2, and 3, from left to right.\n"
"        Arrows from the top 1 to bottom 2, from the top 2 to bottom 1, and top 3 straight down to bottom 3.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:187
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      This shows that the function <m>f</m> sends 1 to 2, 2 to 1, and 3 to 3: Just follow the arrows.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:191
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The arrow diagram used to define the function above can be very helpful in visualizing functions.\n"
"      We will often be working with functions with <em>finite</em> domains, so this kind of picture is often more useful than a traditional graph of a function.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:196
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Note that for finite domains, finding an algebraic formula that gives the output for any input is often impossible.\n"
"      Of course we could use a piecewise-defined function, like\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:200
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      This describes exactly the same function as above, but we can all agree is a ridiculous way of doing so.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:205
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Since we will so often use functions with small domains and codomains, let's adopt some notation to describe them.\n"
"      All we need is some clear way of denoting the image of each element in the domain.\n"
"      In fact, writing a table of values would work perfectly:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:212
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m> x</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:213
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:391
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:402 source/sec_addtops-numbth.ptx:1212
#, no-wrap
msgid "0"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:221
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:401
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>f(x)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:222
#, no-wrap
msgid " 3"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:233
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We simplify this further by writing this as a <q>matrix</q> with each input directly over its output:\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:236
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"      Note this is just notation and not the same sort of matrix you would find in a linear algebra class (it does not make sense to do operations with these matrices, or row reduce them, for example).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:241
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      One advantage of the two-line notation over the arrow diagrams is that it is harder to accidentally define a rule that is not a function using two-line notation.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:247
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Which of the following diagrams represent a function? Let <m>X = \\{1,2,3,4\\}</m> and <m>Y = \\{a,b,c,d\\}</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:263
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Picture labeled f:X to Y with two rows of numbers and letters and arrows between the rows.\n"
"              Top row contains the numbers 1, 2, 3, and 4.\n"
"              Bottom row contains letters a, b, c, and d.\n"
"              Arrows point from 1 to d, 2 to a, 3 to c, and 4 to d.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:283
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Picture labeled g:X to Y with two rows of numbers and letters and arrows between the rows.\n"
"              Top row contains the numbers 1, 2, 3, and 4.\n"
"              Bottom row contains letters a, b, c, and d.\n"
"              Arrows point from 1 to d, 2 to a, 3 to a, and 4 to b.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:304
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Picture labeled f:X to Y with two rows of numbers and letters and arrows between the rows.\n"
"              Top row contains the numbers 1, 2, 3, and 4.\n"
"              Bottom row contains letters a, b, c, and d.\n"
"              Arrows point from 2 to a and also 2 to c, 3 to d, and 4 to b.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:315
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m> f</m> is a function. So is <m>g</m>. There is no problem with an element of the codomain not being the image of any input, and there is no problem with <m>a</m> from the codomain being the image of both 2 and 3 from the domain. We could use our two-line notation to write these as\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:319
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:993
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          .\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:323
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          However, <m>h</m> is NOT a function.\n"
"          In fact, it fails for two reasons.\n"
"          First, the element 1 from the domain has not been mapped to any element from the codomain.\n"
"          Second, the element 2 from the domain has been mapped to more than one element from the codomain (<m>a</m> and <m>c</m>).\n"
"          Note that either one of these problems is enough to make a rule not a function.\n"
"          In general, neither of the following mappings are functions:\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:343
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Three black dots in a row above and two white dots in a row below.\n"
"              An arrow points from the left black dot to the left white dot and from the right black dot to the right white dot.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:362
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Three black dots in a row above and four white dots in a row below.\n"
"              Two arrows point from the left black dot to each of the two left-most white dots.\n"
"              Each of the other black dots have an arrow pointing down to the white dots below and slightly to the right of them.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:370
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          It might also be helpful to think about how you would write the two-line notation for <m>h</m>.\n"
"          We would have something like:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:374
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"          There is nothing under 1 (bad), and we needed to put more than one thing under 2 (very bad).\n"
"          With a rule that is actually a function, the two-line notation will always <q>work.</q>\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:382
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We will also be interested in functions with domain <m>\\N</m>.\n"
"      Here two-line notation is no good, but describing the function algebraically is often possible.\n"
"      Even tables are a little awkward since they do not describe the function completely.\n"
"      For example, consider the function <m>f:\\N \\to \\N</m> given by the table below.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:397
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:408
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>\\ldots</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:403
#, no-wrap
msgid " 1"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:406
#, no-wrap
msgid "16"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><tabular><row><cell>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:407
#, no-wrap
msgid "25"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:413
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Have I given you enough entries for you to be able to determine <m>f(6)</m>? You might guess that <m>f(6) = 36</m>, but there is no way for you to <em>know</em> this for sure.\n"
"      Maybe I am being a jerk and intended <m>f(6) = 42</m>.\n"
"      In fact, for every natural number <m>n</m>, there is a function that agrees with the table above, but for which <m>f(6) = n</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:419
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Okay, suppose I really did mean for <m>f(6) = 36</m>, and in fact, for the rule that you think is governing the function to actually be the rule.\n"
"      Then I should say what that rule is.\n"
"      <m>f(n) = n^2</m>.\n"
"      Now there is no confusion possible.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:426
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Giving an explicit formula that calculates the image of any element in the domain is a great way to describe a function.\n"
"      We will say that these explicit rules are <term>closed formulas</term> for the function.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:432
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      There is another very useful way to describe functions whose domain is <m>\\N</m>, that rely specifically on the structure of the natural numbers.\n"
"      We can define a function <em>recursively</em>!\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:439
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Consider the function <m>f:\\N \\to \\N</m> given by <m>f(0) = 0</m> and <m>f(n+1) = f(n) + 2n+1</m>.\n"
"          Find <m>f(6)</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:446
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          The rule says that <m>f(6) = f(5) + 11</m> (we are using <m>6 = n+1</m> so <m>n = 5</m>).\n"
"          We don't know what <m>f(5)</m> is though.\n"
"          Well, we know that <m>f(5) = f(4) + 9</m>.\n"
"          So we need to compute <m>f(4)</m>, which will require knowing <m> f(3)</m>, which will require knowing<m>f(2)</m>,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:449
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" will it ever end?\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:453
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Yes! This process will always end because we have <m>\\N</m> as our domain, so there is a least element.\n"
"          And we gave the value of <m>f(0)</m> explicitly, so we are good.\n"
"          We might decide to work up to <m>f(6)</m> instead of working down from <m>f(6)</m>:\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:467
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          It looks like this recursively defined function is the same as the explicitly defined function <m>f(n) = n^2</m>.\n"
"          Is it? Later we will prove that it is.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:474
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Recursively defined functions are often easier to create from a <q>real-world</q> problem, because they describe how the values of the functions are changing.\n"
"      However, this comes with a price.\n"
"      It is harder to calculate the image of a single input, since you need to know the images of other (previous) elements in the domain.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:480
#, no-wrap
msgid "Recursively Defined Functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:486
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        For a function <m>f:\\N \\to \\N</m>, a <term>recursive definition</term> consists of an <term>initial condition</term> together with a <term>recurrence relation</term>.\n"
"        The initial condition is the explicitly given value of <m>f(0)</m>.\n"
"        The recurrence relation is a formula for <m>f(n+1)</m> in terms of <m>f(n)</m> (and possibly <m>n</m> itself).\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:495
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Give recursive definitions for the functions described below.\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:499
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>f:\\N \\to \\N</m> gives the number of snails in your terrarium <m>n</m> years after you built it, assuming you started with 3 snails and the number of snails doubles each year.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:505
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>g:\\N \\to \\N</m> gives the number of push-ups you do <m>n</m> days after you started your push-ups challenge, assuming you could do 7 push-ups on day 0 and you can do 2 more push-ups each day.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:511
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>h:\\N \\to \\N</m> defined by <m>h(n) = n!</m>. Recall that <m>n! = 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot (n-1)\\cdot n</m> is the product of all numbers from <m>1</m> through <m>n</m>. We also define <m>0! = 1</m>.\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:523
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                The initial condition is <m>f(0) = 3</m>.\n"
"                To get <m>f(n+1)</m>, we would double the number of snails in the terrarium the previous year, which is given by <m>f(n)</m>.\n"
"                Thus <m>f(n+1) = 2f(n)</m>.\n"
"                The full recursive definition contains both of these and would be written,\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:529
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:540
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:552 source/sec_addtops-numbth.ptx:934
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:535
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                We are told that on day 0 you can do 7 push-ups, so <m>g(0) = 7</m>.\n"
"                The number of push-ups you can do on day <m>n+1</m> is 2 more than the number you can do on day <m>n</m>, which is given by <m>g(n)</m>.\n"
"                Thus\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:546
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Here <m>h(0) = 1</m>.\n"
"                To get the recurrence relation, think about how you can get <m>h(n+1) = (n+1)!</m> from <m>h(n) = n!</m>.\n"
"                If you write out both of these as products, you see that <m>(n+1)!</m> is just like <m>n!</m> except that you have one more term in the product, an extra <m>n+1</m>.\n"
"                So we have,\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:563
#, no-wrap
msgid "Surjections, Injections, and Bijections"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:569
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We now turn to investigating special properties functions might or might not possess.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:573
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In the examples above, you may have noticed that sometimes there are elements of the codomain that are not in the range.\n"
"      When this sort of thing <em>does not</em> happen (that is, when everything in the codomain is in the range), we say the function is <term>onto</term>\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:575
#, no-wrap
msgid " surjection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:576
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      or that the function maps the domain <em>onto</em> the codomain.\n"
"      This terminology should make sense: The function puts the domain (entirely) on top of the codomain.\n"
"      The fancy math term for an onto function is a <term>surjection</term>,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:580
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      and we say that an onto function is a <term>surjective</term> function.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:584
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:707
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In pictures:\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:603
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Four black dots in a top row and three white dots in a bottom row.\n"
"          Arrows point from the top dots to the bottom dots below them: the two white dots on the left have one arrow each point to them.\n"
"          The right-most white dot has two arrows pointing to it, coming from two different black dots.\n"
"          The image is labeled with the word \"Surjective\"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:627
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Four black dots in a top row and three white dots in a bottom row.\n"
"          Arrows point from the top dots to the bottom dots below them: the two white dots on the left and right have two arrows each pointing to them.\n"
"          The center white dot has no arrows pointing to it.\n"
"          The image is labeled with the words \"Not surjective\"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:641
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Which functions are surjective (i.e., onto)?\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:644
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:772
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>f:\\Z \\to \\Z</m> defined by <m>f(n) = 3n</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:648
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:776
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>g: \\{1,2,3\\} \\to \\{a,b,c\\}</m> defined by <m>g = \\begin{pmatrix}1 \\amp 2 \\amp 3 \\\\ c \\amp a \\amp a \\end{pmatrix}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:653
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:781
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                <m>h:\\{1,2,3\\} \\to \\{1,2,3\\}</m> defined as follows:\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p><ol><li><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:666
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:794
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  Two rows containing the numbers 1, 2, and 3, from left to right.\n"
"                  Arrows from the top 1 to bottom 2, from the top 2 to bottom 1, and top 3 straight down to bottom 3.\n"
"                "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:679
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m> f</m> is not surjective. There are elements in the codomain that are not in the range. For example, no <m>n \\in \\Z</m> gets mapped to the number 1 (the rule would say that <m>\\frac{1}{3}</m> would be sent to 1, but <m>\\frac{1}{3}</m> is not in the domain). In fact, the range of the function is <m>3\\Z</m> (the integer multiples of 3), which is not equal to <m>\\Z</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:683
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>g</m> is not surjective. There is no <m>x \\in \\{1,2,3\\}</m> (the domain) for which <m>g(x) = b</m>, so <m>b</m>, which is in the codomain, is not in the range. Notice that there is an element from the codomain <q>missing</q> from the bottom row of the matrix.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:687
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>h</m> is surjective. Every element of the codomain is also in the range. Nothing in the codomain is missed.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:695
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      To be a function, a rule cannot assign a single element of the domain to two or more different elements of the codomain.\n"
"      However, we have seen that the reverse <em>is</em> permissible: A function might assign the same element of the codomain to two or more different elements of the domain.\n"
"      When this <em>does not</em> occur (that is, when each element of the codomain is the image of at most one element of the domain), then we say the function is <term>one-to-one</term>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><idx><see>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:698
#, no-wrap
msgid "injection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:699
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Again, this terminology makes sense: We are sending at most one element from the domain to one element from the codomain.\n"
"      One input to one output.\n"
"      The fancy math term for a one-to-one function is an <term>injection</term>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We call one-to-one functions <term>injective</term> functions.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:727
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Four black dots in a top row and five white dots in a bottom row.\n"
"          Arrows point from the top dots to the bottom dots below them.\n"
"          Each black dot has an arrow pointing to a different white dot.\n"
"          The center white dot has no arrow pointing to it.\n"
"          The image is labeled with the word \"Injective\"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><sidebyside><image><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:753
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Four black dots in a top row and five white dots in a bottom row.\n"
"          Arrows point from the top dots to the bottom dots below them.\n"
"          Two of the black dots have an arrow pointing to a different white dot.\n"
"          The other two black dots have arrows pointing to the same white dot.\n"
"          Two white dots have no arrows pointing to them.\n"
"          The image is labeled with the words \"Not injective\"\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:769
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Which functions are injective (i.e., one-to-one)?\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:807
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m> f</m> is injective. Each element in the codomain is assigned to at <em>most</em> one element from the domain. If <m>x</m> is a multiple of three, then only <m>x/3</m> is mapped to <m>x</m>. If <m>x</m> is not a multiple of 3, then there is no input corresponding to the output <m>x</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:811
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>g</m> is not injective. Both inputs <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> are assigned the output <m>a</m>. Notice that there is an element from the codomain that appears more than once on the bottom row of the matrix.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p><ol><li>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:815
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>h</m> is injective. Each output is only an output once.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:823
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Be careful: <q>surjective</q> and <q>injective</q> are NOT opposites.\n"
"      You can see in the two examples above that there are functions that are surjective but not injective, injective but not surjective, both, or neither.\n"
"      In the case when a function is both one-to-one and onto (an injection and surjection), we say the function is a <term>bijection</term>,\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:827
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      or that the function is a <term>bijective</term> function.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:831
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      To illustrate the contrast between these two properties, consider a more formal definition of each, side by side.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><title>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:835
#, no-wrap
msgid "Injective vs. Surjective"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:838
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A function is <term>injective</term> provided every element of the codomain is the image of <em>at most</em> one element from the domain.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><assemblage><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:842
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        A function is <term>surjective</term> provided every element of the codomain is the image of <em>at least</em> one element from the domain.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:847
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Notice both properties are determined by what happens to elements of the codomain: They could be repeated as images, or they could be <q>missed</q> (not be images).\n"
"      Injective functions do not have repeats but might or might not miss elements.\n"
"      Surjective functions do not miss elements, but might or might not have repeats.\n"
"      The bijective functions are those that do not have repeats and do not miss elements.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><title>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:855
#, no-wrap
msgid "Image and Inverse Image"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:860
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      When discussing functions, we have notation for talking about an element of the domain (say <m>x</m>) and its corresponding element in the codomain (we write <m>f(x)</m>, which <em>is</em> the image of <m>x</m>).\n"
"      Sometimes we will want to talk about all the elements that are images of some subset of the domain.\n"
"      It would also be nice to start with some element of the codomain (say <m>y</m>) and talk about which element or elements (if any) from the domain it is the image of.\n"
"      We could write <q> those <m>x</m> in the domain such that <m>f(x) = y</m>,</q> but this is a lot of writing.\n"
"      Here is some notation to make our lives easier.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:869
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      To address the first situation, what we are after is a way to describe the <em>set</em> of images of elements in some subset of the domain.\n"
"      Suppose <m>f:X \\to Y</m> is a function and that <m>A \\subseteq X</m> is some subset of the domain (possibly all of it).\n"
"      We will use the notation <m>f(A)</m> to denote the <term>image of <m>A</m> under <m>f</m></term>, namely the set of elements in <m>Y</m> that are the image of elements from <m>A</m>.\n"
"      That is, <m>f(A) = \\{f(a) \\in Y \\st a \\in A\\}</m>.\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:874
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>f(A)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:876
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          the image of <m>A</m> under <m>f</m>\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:884
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      We can do this in the other direction as well.\n"
"      We might ask which elements of the domain get mapped to a particular set in the codomain.\n"
"      Let <m>f:X \\to Y</m> be a function and suppose <m>B \\subseteq Y</m> is a subset of the codomain.\n"
"      Then we will write <m> f\\inv(B)</m> for the <term>inverse image of <m>B</m> under <m>f</m></term>, namely the set of elements in <m> X</m> whose image are elements in <m>B</m>.\n"
"      In other words, <m>f\\inv(B) = \\{x \\in X \\st f(x) \\in B\\}</m> .\n"
"      "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><usage>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:890
#, no-wrap
msgid "<m>f\\inv(B)</m> "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p><notation><description>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:892
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          the inverse image of <m>B</m> under <m>f</m>\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:898
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Often we are interested in the element(s) whose image is a particular element <m>y</m> of in the codomain.\n"
"      The notation above works: <m>f\\inv(\\{y\\})</m> is the set of all elements in the domain that <m>f</m> sends to <m>y</m>.\n"
"      It makes sense to think of this as a set: there might not be anything sent to <m>y</m> (if <m>y</m> is not in the range), in which case <m>f\\inv(\\{y\\}) = \\emptyset</m>.\n"
"      Or <m>f</m> might send multiple elements to <m>y</m> (if <m>f</m> is not injective).\n"
"      As a notational convenience, we usually drop the set braces around the <m>y</m> and write <m>f\\inv(y)</m> instead for this set.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:907
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      WARNING: <m> f\\inv(y)</m> is not an inverse function! Inverse functions only exist for bijections, but <m> f\\inv(y)</m> is defined for any function <m>f</m>.\n"
"      The point: <m>f\\inv(y)</m> is a <em>set</em>, not an <em> element</em> of the domain.\n"
"      This is just sloppy notation for <m>f\\inv(\\{y\\})</m>.\n"
"      To help make this distinction, we would call <m>f\\inv(y)</m> the <term>complete inverse image of <m>y</m> under <m>f</m></term>.\n"
"      It is not the image of <m>y</m> under <m>f\\inv</m> (since the function <m>f\\inv</m> might not exist).\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:917
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Consider the function <m>f:\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\} \\to \\{a,b,c,d\\}</m> given by\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:920
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
".\n"
"          Find <m>f(\\{1,2,3\\})</m>, <m>f\\inv(\\{a,b\\})</m>, and <m>f\\inv(d)</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:927
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>f(\\{1,2,3\\}) = \\{a,b\\}</m> since <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are the elements in the codomain to which <m>f</m> sends <m> 1</m>, <m>2</m>, and <m>3</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:931
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>f\\inv(\\{a,b\\}) = \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}</m> since these are exactly the elements that <m>f</m> sends to <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:935
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          <m>f\\inv(d) = \\emptyset</m> since <m>d</m> is not in the range of <m>f</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:943
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Consider the function <m>g:\\Z \\to \\Z</m> defined by <m>g(n) = n^2 + 1</m>.\n"
"          Find <m>g(1)</m> and <m>g(\\{1\\})</m>.\n"
"          Then find <m> g\\inv(1)</m>, <m>g\\inv(2)</m>, and <m>g\\inv(3)</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:951
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Note that <m>g(1) \\ne g(\\{1\\})</m>.\n"
"          The first is an element: <m>g(1) = 2</m>.\n"
"          The second is a set: <m>g(\\{1\\}) = \\{2\\}</m> .\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:957
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          To find <m>g\\inv(1)</m>, we need to find all integers <m>n</m> such that <m>n^2 + 1 = 1</m>.\n"
"          Clearly only 0 works, so <m>g\\inv(1) = \\{0\\}</m> (note that even though there is only one element, we still write it as a set with one element in it).\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:962
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          To find <m> g\\inv(2)</m>, we need to find all <m>n</m> such that <m>n^2 + 1 = 2</m>.\n"
"          We see <m>g\\inv(2) = \\{-1,1\\}</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:967
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Finally, if <m>n^2 + 1 = 3</m>, then we are looking for an <m>n</m> such that <m>n^2 = 2</m>.\n"
"          There are no such integers so <m>g\\inv(3) = \\emptyset</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:974
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Since <m>f\\inv(y)</m> is a set, it makes sense to ask for <m>\\card{f\\inv(y)}</m>, the number of elements in the domain that map to <m>y</m>.\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><statement><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:980
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          Find a function <m>f:\\{1,2,3,4,5\\} \\to \\N</m> such that <m>\\card{f\\inv(7)} = 5</m>.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><example><solution><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:986
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          There is only one such function.\n"
"          We need five elements of the domain to map to the number <m>7 \\in \\N</m>.\n"
"          Since there are only five elements in the domain, all of them must map to 7.\n"
"          So\n"
"          "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><title>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1000
#, no-wrap
msgid "Function Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1003
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        Here is a summary of all the main concepts and definitions we use when working with functions.\n"
"        "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1010
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A <term>function</term> is a rule that assigns each element of a set, called the <term>domain</term>, to exactly one element of a second set, called the <term>codomain</term>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1017
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              Notation: <m>f:X \\to Y</m> is our way of saying that the function is called <m>f</m>, the domain is the set <m>X</m>, and the codomain is the set <m>Y</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1025
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              To specify the rule for a function with small domain, use <term>two-line notation</term> by writing a matrix with each output directly below its corresponding input, as in:\n"
"              "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1029
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              .\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1036
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>f(x) = y</m> means the element <m>x</m> of the domain (input) is assigned to the element <m>y</m> of the codomain. We say <m>y</m> is an output. Alternatively, we call <m>y</m> the <term>image of <m>x</m> under <m>f</m></term> .\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1043
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>range</term> is a subset of the codomain.\n"
"              It is the set of all elements that are assigned to at least one element of the domain by the function.\n"
"              That is, the range is the set of all outputs.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1052
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A function is <term>injective</term> (an <term>injection</term> or <term> one-to-one</term>) if every element of the codomain is the image of <term>at most</term> one element from the domain.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1059
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A function is <term> surjective</term> (a <term>surjection</term> or <term>onto</term>) if every element of the codomain is the image of <term>at least</term> one element from the domain.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1066
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              A <term>bijection</term> is a function that is both an injection and surjection.\n"
"              In other words, if every element of the codomain is the image of <term>exactly one</term> element from the domain.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1074
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>image</term> of an element <m>x</m> in the domain is the element <m>y</m> in the codomain that <m>x</m> is mapped to.\n"
"              That is, the image of <m>x</m> under <m>f</m> is <m>f(x)</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1083
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>complete inverse image</term> of an element <m>y</m> in the codomain, written <m>f\\inv(y)</m>, is the <em>set</em> of all elements in the domain that are assigned to <m>y</m> by the function.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1090
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>image</term> of a subset <m>A</m> of the domain is the set <m>f(A) = \\{f(a) \\in Y \\st a \\in A\\}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <section><subsection><paragraphs><p><ul><li><p>
#: source/sec_structures-functions.ptx:1097
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              The <term>inverse image</term> of a subset <m>B</m> of the codomain is the set <m>f\\inv(B) = \\{x \\in X \\st f(x) \\in B\\}</m>.\n"
"            "
msgstr ""

