#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:524
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:714
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Is <m>f</m> surjective? Prove your\n"
"        answer. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:535
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:725
#, no-wrap
msgid "              <m>f</m> is injective. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><proof><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:539
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:729
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                Let <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> be elements of the domain <m>\\Z</m>. Assume <m>f(x) = f(y)</m>. If <m>x</m>\n"
"                and <m>y</m> are both even, then <m>f(x) = x+1</m> and <m>f(y) = y+1</m>. Since <m>f(x) = f(y)</m>,\n"
"                we have <m>x + 1 = y + 1</m> which implies that <m>x = y</m>. Similarly, if <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>\n"
"                are both odd, then <m>x - 3 = y-3</m> so again <m>x = y</m>. The only other possibility is\n"
"                that <m>x</m> is even and <m>y</m> is odd (or vice-versa). But then <m>x + 1</m> would be odd,\n"
"                and <m>y - 3</m> would be even, so it cannot be that <m>f(x) = f(y)</m>. Therefore if <m>f(x)\n"
"                = f(y)</m> we then have <m>x = y</m>, which proves that <m>f</m> is injective. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:552
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:742
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              <m>\n"
"              f</m> is surjective. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p><ol><li><proof><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:556
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:746
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Let <m>y</m> be an element of the codomain <m>\\Z</m>. We will show\n"
"                there is an element <m>n</m> of the domain (<m>\\Z</m>) such that <m>f(n) = y</m>. There are\n"
"                two cases: First, if <m>y</m> is even, then let <m>n = y+3</m>. Since <m>y</m> is even, <m>n</m>\n"
"                is odd, so <m>f(n) = n-3 = y+3-3 = y</m> as desired. Second, if <m>y</m> is odd, then let <m>n\n"
"                = y-1</m>. Since <m>y</m> is odd, <m>n</m> is even, so <m>f(n) = n+1 = y-1+1 = y</m> as\n"
"                needed. Therefore <m>f</m> is surjective. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:573
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:763
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        At the end of the semester a teacher assigns letter grades to each of her students.\n"
"        Is this a function?\n"
"        If so, what sets make up the domain and codomain,\n"
"        and is the function injective,\n"
"        surjective, bijective, or neither?\n"
