#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:637
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:827
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" Let <m>f:X \\to Y</m> be a\n"
"        function and <m>A \\subseteq X</m> be a finite subset of the domain. What can you say about the\n"
"        relationship between <m>\\card{A}</m> and <m>\\card{f(A)}</m>? Consider both the general case and\n"
"        what happens when you know <m>f</m> is injective, surjective, or bijective. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:644
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:834
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        In general, <m>\\card{A} \\ge \\card{f(A)}</m>, since you cannot get more outputs than you have\n"
"        inputs (each input goes to exactly one output), but you could have fewer outputs if the\n"
"        function is not injective. If the function is injective, then <m>\\card{A} = \\card{f(A)}</m>,\n"
"        although you can have equality even if <m>f</m> is not injective (it must be injective <em>\n"
"        restricted</em> to <m>A</m>). "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><statement><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:654
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:844
#, no-wrap
