" Let <m>f:X \\to Y</m> be a function\n"
"        and <m>B \\subseteq Y</m> be a finite subset of the codomain. What can you say about the\n"
"        relationship between <m>\\card{B}</m> and <m>\\card{f\\inv(B)}</m>? Consider both the general case\n"
"        and what happens when you know <m>f</m> is injective, surjective, or bijective. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:661
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:851
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        In general, there is no relationship between <m>\\card{B}</m> and <m>\\card{f\\inv(B)}</m>. This is\n"
"        because <m>B</m> might contain elements that are not in the range of <m>f</m>, so we might even\n"
"        have <m>f\\inv(B) = \\emptyset</m>. On the other hand, there might be lots of elements from the\n"
"        domain that all get sent to a few elements in <m>B</m>, making <m>f\\inv(B)</m> larger than <m>B</m>. "
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <exercises><exercise><solution><p>
#: source/practice/intro-functions.ptx:667
#: source/exercises/intro-functions.ptx:857
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        More specifically, if <m>f</m> is injective, then <m>\\card{B} \\ge \\card{f\\inv(B)}</m> (since every\n"
"        element in <m>B</m> must come from at most one element from the domain). If <m>f</m> is\n"
"        surjective, then <m>\\card{B} \\le \\card{f\\inv(B)}</m> (since every element in <m>B</m> must come\n"
"        from at least one element of the domain). Thus if <m>f</m> is bijective then <m>\\card{B} =\n"
"        \\card{f\\inv(B)}</m>. "
msgstr ""

